(window.COURSES=window.COURSES||{})["qa"]=JSON.parse("{\"id\":\"qa\",\"name\":\"Software Quality Assurance\",\"short\":\"Quality\",\"code\":\"22H211-CS-EN-T1\",\"by\":\"Prof. Dr. Arabi Keshk, Prof. Mahmoud Gadallah\",\"lectures\":[{\"n\":1,\"title\":\"Introduction to Software Testing\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"What is software testing?\",\"pts\":[\"Software testing is an <b>incremental and iterative</b> process for detecting defects and verifying that a software system meets its specified requirements. It is an <b>integral part of the SDLC</b> and identifies defects, flaws or errors in the application.\",\"It ensures <b>quality, reliability and user satisfaction</b>. Finding bugs early saves time and cost and increases customer confidence.\",\"<b>Miller's goal of testing</b>: \\\"The general aim of testing is to <b>affirm the quality</b> of software systems by <b>systematically exercising</b> the software in <b>carefully controlled circumstances</b>.\\\"\",\"<b>6 objectives of testing</b>:<br>(1) ensure the solution meets the <b>business requirements</b>, which enhances customer confidence<br>(2) catch bugs, errors and defects<br>(3) ensure the system is <b>stable and ready</b> for use<br>(4) identify <b>areas of weakness</b><br>(5) establish the <b>degree of quality</b><br>(6) determine <b>user acceptability</b>.\",\"Benefits to the organization: saves time and money (defects found early), stable product with less downtime, customer satisfaction.\"]},{\"h\":\"Evolution of software testing (Gelperin & Hetzel)\",\"table\":[[\"Period\",\"Orientation\",\"Meaning\"],[\"Until 1956\",\"Debugging-oriented\",\"Testing meant debugging\"],[\"1957–1978\",\"Demonstration-oriented\",\"Ensure the software meets its specifications\"],[\"1979–1982\",\"Destruction-oriented\",\"Tests designed to find errors (Myers' era)\"],[\"1983–1987\",\"Evaluation-oriented\",\"Introduced verification and validation\"],[\"1988–2000\",\"Prevention-oriented\",\"Prevent defects through early test design and planning\"]],\"pts\":[\"<b>Deve Gelperin and William C. Hetzel</b> classified software testing based on the <b>goals and phases of testing</b>, as follows:\"]},{\"h\":\"What is a bug? (5 conditions)\",\"pts\":[\"A <b>bug</b> is an error in a program that may produce an incorrect or undesired result or stop it from working correctly. In testing, a bug means <b>anything that affects quality</b>. Other names: defect, fault, problem, error, incident, anomaly, failure, variance, inconsistency.\",\"The conditions can be applied to a calculator to understand them better. We know that the calculator should perform the operations of <b>addition, subtraction, multiplication and division</b>.\",\"<b>1.</b> The software does <b>not do</b> what the specification says it should. <i>Calculator: the + key does not work.</i>\",\"<b>2.</b> The software does what the specification says it should <b>not</b> do (opposite behaviour). <i>Calculator crashes/freezes.</i>\",\"<b>3.</b> The software does something the specification <b>does not mention</b>. <i>Calculator has an extra \\\"square\\\" function not in the spec.</i>\",\"<b>4.</b> The software does not do something it <b>should (mandatory) do</b> although the spec may not mention it. <i>Weak battery → wrong answers.</i>\",\"<b>5.</b> The software is <b>difficult to understand</b>, has cumbersome steps, or is <b>slow</b> (customer's perspective). <i>Buttons too small, lights too flashy to read the answer.</i>\",\"Condition 5 brings the <b>customer perspective</b>: it occurs whenever the performance of the software is not acceptable to the user. The <b>feedback of the tester</b> becomes important, as it brings out the negatives even before the product reaches the customer.\"]},{\"h\":\"Types of bugs and why they occur\",\"table\":[[\"Bug type\",\"Examples\"],[\"Conceptual\",\"Wrong syntax, misspelled keywords, flawed design logic\"],[\"Math\",\"Divide-by-zero, overflow, rounding errors\"],[\"Logical\",\"Infinite loops, wrong logic, incorrect conditions\"],[\"Resource\",\"Buffer overflow, access violations, uninitialized variables\"],[\"Co-programming\",\"Deadlocks, race conditions, concurrency issues\"],[\"Team working\",\"Outdated documentation, mismatched files, incorrect linking\"]]},{\"h\":\"Reasons for bugs and the cost of bugs\",\"pts\":[\"<b>7 reasons</b>:<br>(1) <b>Human factor</b>: mistakes in logic or syntax<br>(2) <b>Communication failure</b> between teams<br>(3) <b>Unrealistic development timeframe</b><br>(4) <b>Poor design logic</b> (inadequate research, misunderstood feasibility)<br>(5) <b>Poor coding practices</b> (inefficient methods and <b>faulty tools</b>)<br>(6) <b>Lack of skilled testing</b><br>(7) <b>Change requests</b> at the last minute.\",\"Explanations:<br>• <b>Communication failure</b>: miscommunication between teams leads to errors in design or development.<br>• <b>Unrealistic development timeframe</b>: tight deadlines cause poor design and testing compromises.<br>• <b>Lack of skilled testing</b>: unskilled testers fail to detect hidden issues.<br>• <b>Change requests</b>: last-minute requirement or tool changes introduce new defects.\",\"<b>Cost of fixing depends on when the bug is found</b>:<br>• <b>Requirement stage</b>: <b>low</b> cost; easy to correct early errors.<br>• <b>Coding stage</b>: <b>moderate</b> cost; the developer can fix it easily if caught early.<br>• <b>Integration</b>: <b>higher</b> (developer + engineers).<br>• <b>Testing</b>: <b>very costly</b> (developers, engineers, managers, delays).<br>• <b>Production</b>: <b>extremely costly</b> (reputation, financial or legal penalties).\",\"Real example: the <b>Patriot missile failure (Gulf War, 1991)</b> was caused by a <b>timing bug</b> and led to the death of <b>28 soldiers</b>.\",\"It is a real-world example of how a <b>single software bug</b> can have a catastrophic result.\"]},{\"h\":\"Software development models: Waterfall\",\"pts\":[\"<b>1.3 Software development models</b>: the software development life cycle consists of several phases, from the project's conception to its completion. Different models such as <b>Waterfall, V, Spiral, RAD, Prototyping and Agile</b> are used depending on project requirements. The testing approach also varies according to the nature of each model.\",\"<b>Waterfall</b> (one of the oldest, sequential): System Engineering → Analysis → Design → Coding → Testing → Maintenance.\",\"The Waterfall process starts at the <b>system level</b> and is followed by phases like analysis, design, coding, testing and maintenance, as depicted in Figure 1.1.\",\"Figure 1.1 (Waterfall Model): six hexagons step down like a staircase: <b>System Engineering → Analysis → Design → Coding → Testing → Maintenance</b>, each joined to the next by an arrow. A vertical line rises from Maintenance on the right and is joined by two-headed arrows to every earlier phase (Testing, Coding, Design, Analysis, System Engineering), so each later phase can feed back to the earlier ones.\",\"<b>The six phases</b>:<br>• <b>System Engineering</b>: defines both <b>software and hardware requirements</b>.<br>• <b>Analysis</b>: feasibility study, goals, performance and interface requirements.<br>• <b>Design</b>: translates requirements into software structure, such as database design, to guide coding and ensure quality.<br>• <b>Coding</b>: developers write the program code based on the design specifications.<br>• <b>Testing</b>: the software is tested to detect bugs and verify that it meets the stated requirements.<br>• <b>Maintenance</b>: fixes issues and updates the software as new needs arise.\",\"<b>Advantages</b>: simple, structured, clear documentation, early requirements, easy management.<br><b>Drawback</b>: errors made in early phases can carry over to later stages, causing delays and increased costs, since problems must be <b>fixed at multiple points</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-waterfall\",\"qa-waterfall-flow\"],\"figH\":{\"qa-waterfall\":\"Figure 1.1: the Waterfall Model, phase by phase\",\"qa-waterfall-flow\":\"Waterfall in action: how the work flows down the phases, and what an early error costs\"}},{\"h\":\"Spiral model\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Spiral</b>: iterative, overcomes waterfall limits. Four phases repeated in cycles: <b>Planning → Risk analysis → Design engineering → Customer evaluation (testing & evaluation)</b>. Usually <b>2 or 3 prototypes</b> before the final product.\",\"In the Spiral model the phases are repeated in cycles <b>until all issues are resolved</b>. Each prototype goes through the <b>full cycle</b> to identify and fix problems.\",\"<b>The four phases</b>:<br>• <b>Planning</b>: defines specifications, objectives, constraints and strategies; all the alternatives that help in developing a cost-effective project are analyzed and all possible risks involved in the project development are identified.<br>• <b>Risk analysis</b>: identifies possible risks and evaluates cost-effective solutions.<br>• <b>Design engineering</b>: the actual software is developed iteratively.<br>• <b>Testing & evaluation</b>: customers test the product and provide feedback to improve it; during this cycle all the phases concentrate on the feedback received from the customer and the testing team to resolve the drawbacks and bugs found in each prototype.\",\"Figure 1.2 (Spiral Model): <br>• <b>Quadrants</b>: two axes split the figure into four: <b>Plan</b> (top left), <b>Risk Analysis</b> (top right), <b>Design</b> (bottom right) and <b>Testing</b> (bottom left).<br>• <b>Axes</b>: the vertical axis points up to <b>Cumulative cost</b>, the horizontal axis points left to <b>Review</b>, and a curved <b>Progress</b> arrow at the top shows the spiral turning clockwise and widening outward.<br>• <b>Left of the vertical axis</b> (inside out): Requirements plan, Concept of operation, Development plan, Test plan, Release.<br>• <b>Right of it, upper part</b>: Risk analysis on each loop, with a shaded wedge widening into <b>Prototype 1, Prototype 2 and Operational Prototype</b>.<br>• <b>Right of it, lower part</b>: Concept of requirements, Requirements, Draft, Detailed design, Code, Integration, Test, Verification & Validation (twice) and Implementation; the spiral ends with an arrow at Release.\",\"<b>Advantages</b>: manages risks early, flexible, customer feedback, prototyping, suits complex projects.<br><b>Drawback</b>: the main drawback of the model is the amount of time taken to complete the iterations, which can increase costs. Although it improves problem-solving and customer involvement, the Spiral Model can be time-consuming and costly due to multiple iterations and <b>continuous testing</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-spiral\"],\"figH\":{\"qa-spiral\":\"Figure 1.2: the Spiral Model, quadrant by quadrant\"}},{\"h\":\"V-Model\",\"pts\":[\"Integrates testing into <b>every stage</b> of the SDLC, with early testing starting from the requirements phase. Development goes <b>high level → low level</b> (left side, down); testing goes <b>low level → high level</b> (right side, up).\",\"It ensures continuous quality checks throughout the process. The <b>salient aspect</b> of the V model is that it portrays distinct testing levels and illustrates how each level addresses a different stage of the lifecycle. The development activities begin with defining the business requirements. The model is shaped like a \\\"V\\\": the <b>left side</b> represents development activities (from business requirements to detailed design) and the <b>right side</b> represents the corresponding <b>testing phases</b> (from unit testing to acceptance testing).\",\"<b>Test levels</b>:<br>• <b>Unit</b>: individual modules.<br>• <b>Integration</b>: interactions between modules.<br>• <b>System</b>: the whole system meets design and performance.<br>• <b>Acceptance</b>: done by the customer to confirm business requirements.\",\"During the <b>unit design stage</b>, the individual programs or modules of the entire software are specified, and the test cases for unit testing are developed based on them. Test cases are constructed to check various aspects of the software: these checks test the actual program structure or whether the software functions as per the specification.\",\"Example (calculator): test code logic of calculations, memory per module, how modules are linked, overall efficiency.\",\"<b>Advantage</b>: early verification and validation reduce defects <b>and improve quality</b>.<br><b>Drawback</b>: less effective when requirements are incomplete or badly documented; not suitable for every project.\"]},{\"h\":\"V-Model mapping (figure)\",\"table\":[[\"Left side (development)\",\"Plan produced\",\"Right side (testing)\"],[\"Requirements specifications\",\"Acceptance test plan\",\"Acceptance test\"],[\"Functional specifications\",\"System test plan\",\"System test\"],[\"System design\",\"Integration test plan\",\"Integration test\"],[\"Unit design\",\"Test cases\",\"Unit test\"],[\"Code (bottom of the V)\",\"–\",\"–\"]],\"pts\":[\"Figure 1.3 (V Model): the boxes form a V. Down the left arm (arrows pointing down): Requirements Specifications → Functional Specifications → System Design → Unit Design → <b>Code</b> at the bottom. Up the right arm (arrows pointing up): Code → Unit Test → Integration Test → System Test → Acceptance Test. Dashed arrows run across from each left box to its partner on the right, labelled with the plan: <b>Acceptance Test Plan, System Test Plan, Integration Test Plan</b>; between Unit Design and Unit Test the label is <b>Test Cases</b>.\"],\"fig\":\"qa-vmodel\",\"figH\":{\"qa-vmodel\":\"Figure 1.3: the V Model, each development stage and the test level that checks it\"}},{\"h\":\"RAD model\",\"pts\":[\"<b>RAD</b> (Rapid Application Development): built from business, project-management and SRS requirements. A prototype is matched against requirements; if there is a gap, another prototype is built. <b>Linear sequential</b> with an <b>extremely short cycle</b> and <b>reusable components</b>.\",\"When the requirements are well understood and defined, the RAD process enables the development team to develop the final product in a <b>shorter period</b>.\",\"<b>RAD phases</b>: Requirements planning → Design (prototype) → Construction → Testing &amp; handover.<br>• <b>Requirements planning</b>: collecting project requirements and creating an outline.<br>• <b>Design phase</b>: building a prototype that includes all requirements and necessary changes.<br>• <b>Construction phase</b>: developing and improving prototypes using RAD tools until the customer is satisfied.<br>• <b>Testing and handover</b>: using reusable components to reduce testing time before delivering the final product.\",\"<b>Advantages</b>: fast development with prototypes, flexible to changes, involves customer feedback, reduces risk, and <b>ensures high quality</b>.<br><b>Disadvantages</b>: a missing reusable component can make the project fail; only for small, simple projects, not complex ones.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-rad\"],\"figH\":{\"qa-rad\":\"RAD in action: from the requirements to a prototype to the handover\"}},{\"h\":\"Agile model, XP and TDD\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Agile</b>: most widely used model today. <b>Incremental</b>, short cycles, small releases, constant feedback and <b>continuous collaboration</b> of customers, developers and testers. Approaches: <b>DSDM, SCRUM, XP</b> (XP most popular).\",\"Unlike traditional sequential models like Waterfall or Spiral, Agile is designed to handle <b>complex projects with continuous changes</b> by responding quickly and efficiently. It relies on <b>constant feedback</b> to divide work into small, testable parts that are refined based on user input. Each release builds on the previous one and is thoroughly tested to fix bugs early. The continuous collaboration between customers, developers and testers ensures that requirements are met accurately.<br>DSDM = <b>Dynamic Systems Development Method</b>; XP = <b>Extreme Programming</b>.\",\"<b>XP</b>: programmers work in <b>pairs</b> (one codes, one reviews/tests). <b>TDD</b> = write test cases <b>before</b> code, in five steps:\",\"TDD, a main XP practice, writes test cases before coding <b>to ensure quality and reduce errors</b>.<br>(1) <b>Create the test code</b>: the developer writes automated test scripts before writing the actual program code; these tests define what the software should do.<br>(2) <b>Write or modify the functional code</b>: the developer then writes the actual code to pass the predefined test cases; the code is adjusted until it meets all test requirements.<br>(3) <b>Create additional tests</b>: the tester adds more tests with different inputs based on the complexity of the module.<br>(4) <b>Test the functional code</b>: the functional code is tested repeatedly using the created test cases until all tests pass successfully.<br>(5) <b>Refactor the code</b>: the developer improves the code to make it easier to maintain, extend and update; this step removes duplicate or unused code and reduces complexity.\",\"Figure 1.4 (Agile Model): five boxes stacked top to bottom, joined by downward arrows: Create the test code → Write or modify the function code → Create additional tests → Test the functional code → Refactor the code. A loop arrow goes from <b>Test the functional code</b> back up to <b>Write or modify the function code</b>.\",\"In summary, in the Agile approach, <b>testers play a key role</b> in ensuring software quality and efficiency through continuous testing and feedback.\",\"<b>Advantages</b>: flexible, feedback, fast delivery, better quality.<br><b>Disadvantages</b>: needs active customer involvement, hard for large teams, scope creep.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-agile\",\"qa-tdd\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-agile\":1,\"qa-tdd\":4},\"figH\":{\"qa-agile\":\"Agile in action: short cycles and small releases with constant feedback\",\"qa-tdd\":\"Figure 1.4: the Agile Model, the five steps of test-driven development (TDD)\"}},{\"h\":\"Summary\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Summary of the models</b>:<br>• Waterfall is sequential.<br>• <b>V and Spiral</b> use testing as an integral part, so they are more efficient than waterfall.<br>• Agile is considered to be the <b>most advanced and efficient</b> type of software development model.<br>• Extreme Programming (XP) is a method developed based on the agile model, which uses test driven development (TDD) to develop highly efficient software.\",\"Summary (1.4): software testing demonstrates that a program performs the intended functions correctly; the goal of software testing is to ensure that the product is error free. Detecting bugs is the most important part of a software testing process; these bugs can be an error in the program or issues that affect the quality of the software. Bugs can prove to be very costly: they take time to resolve, affecting the project timelines and cost, and they also result in losses, as the company may have to compensate the customer with money or by replacing the defective product. They can also damage the reputation of the company.\",\"Summary (1.4): depending on the <b>kind of error or the reason for error</b>, bugs are classified into various types such as bugs due to conceptual error, math bugs, logical bugs, resource bugs, co-programming bugs and team working bugs.\",\"Summary (1.4): bugs in software can occur due to various reasons such as <b>human error, lack of communication, tight time lines, improper design logic, inefficient coding practices and unskilled testers</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-models-compare\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-models-compare\":0},\"figH\":{\"qa-models-compare\":\"Comparing the models: where testing happens in each one\"}}],\"cards\":[[\"Software testing (definition)\",\"An incremental and iterative process for detecting defects and verifying that a system meets specified requirements.\"],[\"Miller's aim of testing\",\"To affirm the quality of software systems by systematically exercising the software in carefully controlled circumstances.\"],[\"1957–1978\",\"Demonstration-oriented testing: ensure software meets its specifications.\"],[\"1979–1982\",\"Destruction-oriented testing: tests designed to find errors (Myers).\"],[\"1983–1987\",\"Evaluation-oriented: introduced verification and validation.\"],[\"1988–2000\",\"Prevention-oriented: prevent defects with early test design and planning.\"],[\"Math bugs\",\"Divide-by-zero, overflow, rounding errors.\"],[\"Co-programming bugs\",\"Deadlocks, race conditions, concurrency issues.\"],[\"Team working bugs\",\"Outdated documentation, mismatched files, incorrect linking.\"],[\"Cheapest stage to fix a bug\",\"Requirement stage. Production stage is extremely costly.\"],[\"Patriot missile (1991)\",\"A timing bug caused the death of 28 soldiers.\"],[\"Spiral phases\",\"Planning, risk analysis, design engineering, customer evaluation (repeated in cycles).\"],[\"V-model: acceptance test plan is written at\",\"The requirements specifications stage.\"],[\"RAD disadvantages\",\"A missing reusable component can cause failure; only for small, simple projects.\"],[\"TDD steps\",\"Create test code, write functional code, create additional tests, test functional code, refactor.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"List the objectives of software testing.\",\"1) Ensure the solution meets business requirements (customer confidence).<br>2) Catch bugs, errors and defects.<br>3) Ensure the system is stable and ready.<br>4) Identify areas of weakness.<br>5) Establish the degree of quality.<br>6) Determine user acceptability.\"],[\"State the five conditions that mean a bug exists, with the calculator example.\",\"1) Does not do what the spec says (+ key does not work).<br>2) Does what the spec says it should not (crashes).<br>3) Does something the spec does not mention (extra square function).<br>4) Does not do something it should although not mentioned (wrong answers on weak battery).<br>5) Hard to understand, cumbersome or slow (buttons too small, lights too flashy).\"],[\"Explain how the cost of a bug depends on when it is found.\",\"Requirement stage: low cost. Coding: moderate. Integration: higher, needs developers and engineers. Testing: very costly, involves managers and delays the project. Production: extremely costly, damages reputation and may cause financial or legal penalties (e.g. Patriot missile 1991).\"],[\"Compare Waterfall and Spiral.\",\"Waterfall is linear and sequential, testing comes near the end, simple and well documented, but early errors carry over and are expensive. Spiral is iterative with planning, risk analysis, engineering and customer evaluation in each cycle, uses 2 to 3 prototypes, manages risk early, but is time-consuming and costly.\"],[\"Explain how test cases are developed at every stage in the V-model.\",\"Each development stage on the left produces a test plan for the matching test level on the right: requirements to acceptance test plan, functional specs to system test plan, system design to integration test plan, unit design to unit test cases. So testing is planned early and in parallel with development.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"Demonstration-oriented testing was followed during which period?\",\"o\":[[\"1957–1978\",\"Correct. Demonstration-oriented testing ensured the software met its specifications.\"],[\"1979–1982\",\"That is destruction-oriented testing (Myers' era).\"],[\"1983–1987\",\"That is evaluation-oriented (verification and validation).\"],[\"1988–2000\",\"1988–2000 is the prevention-oriented period, when defects were prevented through early test design and planning, not demonstration (L1, Evolution of testing).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"Which reason for bugs covers errors caused by incorrect usage of logic or syntax?\",\"o\":[[\"Communication failure\",\"Communication failure is miscommunication between teams (e.g. developers misreading requirements), not a developer's own logic or syntax mistake.\"],[\"Human factor\",\"Correct. Developers may make mistakes in logic or syntax.\"],[\"Lack of skilled testing\",\"Lack of skilled testing means testers miss hidden issues; it explains why bugs survive, not why logic or syntax errors are made.\"],[\"Unrealistic timeframe\",\"An unrealistic development timeframe is about tight deadlines pushing rushed work; the reason named for logic/syntax mistakes is the human factor.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"During which stage of the V-model is the acceptance test plan developed?\",\"o\":[[\"Requirements specifications\",\"Correct. The top-left stage produces the acceptance test plan.\"],[\"Functional specifications\",\"The functional specifications stage produces the system test plan, one level below the requirements stage on the left side of the V (L1, V-model mapping).\"],[\"System design\",\"The system design stage produces the integration test plan, not the acceptance test plan (L1, V-model mapping).\"],[\"Integration test\",\"This is on the testing side, not where plans are written.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"\\\"The year 1988–2000 followed a prevention-oriented approach.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture lists 1988–2000 as prevention-oriented: focused on preventing defects through early test design and planning. (‡ The summary sheet's key marks this False, which contradicts the slide.)\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture table clearly says 1988–2000 = prevention-oriented.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Summary T/F\"},{\"q\":\"A calculator shows a \\\"square\\\" function that is not mentioned in the product specification. Which bug condition is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Condition 1: does not do what the spec says\",\"Condition 1 is failing to do what the spec says, like the + key not working; the square function is extra behaviour, not missing behaviour.\"],[\"Condition 2: does what the spec says it should not\",\"Condition 2 is doing what the spec says it should not (the calculator crashing or freezing); an unmentioned extra feature is condition 3.\"],[\"Condition 3: does something the spec does not mention\",\"Correct. Extra, unspecified behaviour is condition 3.\"],[\"Condition 5: hard to use or slow\",\"Condition 5 is the customer's usability view.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which bug type includes deadlocks and race conditions?\",\"o\":[[\"Resource bugs\",\"Resource bugs are buffer overflow, access violations, uninitialized variables.\"],[\"Logical bugs\",\"Logical bugs are infinite loops and wrong conditions.\"],[\"Co-programming bugs\",\"Correct. Concurrency problems are co-programming bugs.\"],[\"Team working bugs\",\"Those are outdated documentation and mismatched files.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"At which stage is fixing a bug EXTREMELY costly?\",\"o\":[[\"Requirement\",\"The requirement stage is the cheapest place to fix a bug: low cost, easy to correct early errors (‡ not on the slides: at this stage only documents change; no code, testers or customers are involved) (L1, Cost of bugs).\"],[\"Coding\",\"Fixing a bug during coding has moderate cost; only the developer is involved and nothing has shipped (L1, Cost of bugs).\"],[\"Testing\",\"The testing stage is very costly (developers, engineers, managers and delays), but production is the extremely costly stage (L1, Cost of bugs).\"],[\"Production\",\"Correct. It damages reputation and may bring financial or legal penalties.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Which waterfall phase defines BOTH software and hardware requirements?\",\"o\":[[\"Analysis\",\"Analysis covers feasibility, goals, performance and interface requirements.\"],[\"System engineering\",\"Correct. It defines software and hardware requirements needed for development.\"],[\"Design\",\"Design translates requirements into structure.\"],[\"Maintenance\",\"Maintenance fixes and updates after delivery.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"How many prototypes are typically created in the Spiral model before the final product?\",\"o\":[[\"None\",\"The Spiral model is built around prototypes: each cycle of planning, risk analysis, engineering and evaluation produces one, so none is wrong.\"],[\"Exactly one\",\"The Spiral model repeats its four phases in several cycles, so it builds more than one prototype; the lecture says usually 2 or 3.\"],[\"Two or three\",\"Correct. The Spiral model repeats planning → risk analysis → design engineering → customer evaluation in cycles, usually producing 2 or 3 prototypes before the final product (L1, Spiral model).\"],[\"Ten or more\",\"Ten or more prototypes would make the Spiral model, already time-consuming and costly, impractical; the lecture says usually 2 or 3.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In Test-Driven Development (XP), what is written FIRST?\",\"o\":[[\"The functional code\",\"Functional code comes second, written to pass the tests.\"],[\"The automated test code\",\"Correct. TDD writes tests before code: step 1 is create the test code, then write the functional code to pass it, add tests, test, and refactor (L1, XP and TDD).\"],[\"The user manual\",\"A user manual is not one of the five TDD steps; TDD is about writing automated tests before the functional code.\"],[\"The refactored code\",\"Refactoring is step 5, the last TDD step, done after the functional code passes its tests (L1, XP and TDD).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which model is only suitable for small and simple projects and fails if a reusable component is missing?\",\"o\":[[\"Spiral\",\"Spiral suits large, complex projects because it manages risk early; its drawback is being time-consuming and costly, not missing reusable components.\"],[\"V-model\",\"The V-model's drawback is incomplete requirements.\"],[\"RAD\",\"Correct. These are exactly RAD's two disadvantages.\"],[\"Agile\",\"Agile handles complex projects with changing requirements; its drawbacks are needing active customer involvement, large teams and scope creep.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"What is the main focus of Preventive Maintenance?\",\"o\":[[\"Redesigning the entire user interface.\",\"Improving or redesigning working software is perfective maintenance.\"],[\"Fixing immediate runtime errors.\",\"Fixing faults that have already happened is corrective maintenance, the opposite of acting before errors appear.\"],[\"Moving data from legacy databases to cloud storage.\",\"Fitting software or data to a new environment is adaptive maintenance (migration).\"],[\"Modifying software to detect and correct latent faults before they become actual errors.\",\"Correct. Preventive maintenance modifies software to detect and correct latent faults before they become effective faults (ISO/IEC 14764). The lectures only describe Maintenance as the last Waterfall phase, which \\\"fixes issues and updates the software as new needs arise\\\" (L1).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Upgrading a software application to make it compatible with a new version of an operating system is an example of:\",\"o\":[[\"Perfective Maintenance\",\"Perfective maintenance adds or improves features or performance; the environment changing is not its reason.\"],[\"Corrective Maintenance\",\"Corrective maintenance fixes faults that have appeared; nothing is broken here.\"],[\"Preventive Maintenance\",\"Preventive maintenance fixes latent faults before they become errors; it is not triggered by a new environment.\"],[\"Adaptive Maintenance\",\"Correct. Adaptive maintenance changes software so it keeps working in a changed environment: a new operating system, hardware, database or regulations. Making an application compatible with a new OS version is the textbook example (ISO/IEC 14764).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of Software Maintenance?\",\"o\":[[\"Stopping the software from running on older machines.\",\"The opposite: adaptive maintenance keeps software working when the environment changes.\"],[\"Writing the initial application code from scratch.\",\"That is the Coding phase, before Testing and Maintenance (L1, Waterfall).\"],[\"Modifying software, improve performance, or adapt it to a changed environment.\",\"Correct. Maintenance is the last Waterfall phase, after delivery: it \\\"fixes issues and updates the software as new needs arise\\\" (L1). The IEEE definition (IEEE 1219, ISO/IEC 14764): modification of a software product after delivery to correct faults, to improve performance or other attributes, or to adapt it to a modified environment.\"],[\"Designing the system architecture during the requirement gathering phase.\",\"That is the early Analysis and Design phases, long before maintenance (L1, Waterfall).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Which stage of SDLC typically consumes the maximum cost and effort?\",\"o\":[[\"Perfective Maintenance\",\"Correct. The options are maintenance types, and maintenance is the costliest stage, so the question asks which type takes most of it. Lientz, Swanson and Tompkins (1978): 60.3% perfective, 18.2% adaptive, 17.4% corrective, 4.1% other; the rounded textbook version is 50% perfective, 25% adaptive, 21% corrective, 4% preventive.\"],[\"Adaptive Maintenance\",\"About 18-25% of maintenance effort, well below perfective (Lientz and Swanson).\"],[\"Preventive Maintenance\",\"The smallest share, about 4% (Lientz and Swanson). This is the quiz's key, which goes against the standard sources.\"],[\"Corrective Maintenance\",\"About 17-21%: most effort goes into changing working software, not fixing faults (Lientz and Swanson).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Perfective maintenance is mainly concerned with:\",\"o\":[[\"Fixing hidden software crashes.\",\"Fixing faults is corrective maintenance; catching latent faults early is preventive.\"],[\"Restructuring code to make it easier to read without changing its functionality.\",\"Refactoring for readability is preventive maintenance in ISO/IEC 14764 (it improves maintainability); older IEEE wording puts maintainability under perfective, but the main concern of perfective is performance and features.\"],[\"Porting code to a new computer hardware platform.\",\"Fitting a new hardware platform is adaptive maintenance.\"],[\"Enhancing system performance, execution speed, or adding new features.\",\"Correct. Perfective maintenance improves software that already works: better performance and speed, or new features users ask for. It takes the largest share of maintenance effort (Lientz and Swanson).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of software maintenance is performed to fix bugs and errors discovered after the product is released?\",\"o\":[[\"Corrective Maintenance\",\"Correct. Corrective maintenance fixes faults (bugs and errors) found after the software is delivered, usually reported by users (ISO/IEC 14764).\"],[\"Perfective Maintenance\",\"Perfective maintenance improves performance or adds features; nothing is broken.\"],[\"Preventive Maintenance\",\"Preventive maintenance fixes latent faults before they become errors; here the bugs have already appeared.\"],[\"Adaptive Maintenance\",\"Adaptive maintenance fits the software to a changed environment (new OS or hardware).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"According to Miller, the general aim of testing is to:\",\"o\":[[\"Prove that the software contains no bugs at all\",\"Miller's goal is to affirm quality, not to prove the absence of bugs.\"],[\"Fix every defect as soon as the code is written\",\"Fixing defects is debugging; Miller's aim is about affirming quality through controlled testing.\"],[\"Affirm the quality of software by systematically exercising it in carefully controlled circumstances\",\"Correct. This is Miller's goal of testing as quoted in the lecture.\"],[\"Run the software randomly in uncontrolled conditions\",\"Miller stresses <b>systematic</b> exercising in <b>carefully controlled</b> circumstances, the opposite of random use.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT one of the six objectives of testing listed in the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"Write the functional code of the system\",\"Correct. Writing code is development, not a testing objective.\"],[\"Identify areas of weakness\",\"Identifying areas of weakness is objective 4 of the six objectives of testing, so it IS on the lecture's list (L1, Objectives of testing).\"],[\"Establish the degree of quality\",\"Establishing the degree of quality is objective 5 of the six objectives of testing, so it IS on the list (L1, Objectives of testing).\"],[\"Determine user acceptability\",\"Determining user acceptability is objective 6, the last of the six objectives of testing, so it IS on the list (L1, Objectives of testing).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A file manager's specification says files must <b>never</b> be deleted without asking for confirmation. During testing, the app deletes a file immediately with no confirmation. Which bug condition is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Condition 3: the software does something the specification does not mention\",\"The spec does mention this behaviour (it forbids it), so it is not unmentioned behaviour.\"],[\"Condition 5: the software is difficult to understand or slow\",\"Condition 5 is the customer's usability/speed view, not forbidden behaviour.\"],[\"Condition 4: the software does not do something it should do although the spec does not mention it\",\"Here the spec explicitly covers the behaviour, so condition 4 does not apply.\"],[\"Condition 2: the software does what the specification says it should not do\",\"Correct. The spec forbids deleting without confirmation and the software does exactly that.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A program computes an average by dividing a total by a count, and it crashes when the count is 0. It also shows 0.30000000004 instead of 0.3. Which type of bug are these?\",\"o\":[[\"Resource bugs\",\"Resource bugs are buffer overflows, access violations and uninitialized variables.\"],[\"Math bugs\",\"Correct. Divide-by-zero and rounding errors are the lecture's examples of math bugs.\"],[\"Co-programming bugs\",\"Co-programming bugs are deadlocks, race conditions and concurrency issues.\"],[\"Team working bugs\",\"Team working bugs are outdated documentation, mismatched files and incorrect linking.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In Gelperin and Hetzel's evolution of testing, which orientation <b>introduced verification and validation</b>?\",\"o\":[[\"Destruction-oriented (1979–1982)\",\"Destruction-oriented testing designed tests to find errors (Myers' era).\"],[\"Evaluation-oriented (1983–1987)\",\"Correct. The lecture says evaluation-oriented testing introduced verification and validation.\"],[\"Debugging-oriented (until 1956)\",\"In that period testing simply meant debugging.\"],[\"Prevention-oriented (1988–2000)\",\"Prevention-oriented testing focused on early test design and planning to prevent defects.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Order these stages from the CHEAPEST to the MOST expensive place to find and fix a bug, as given in the lecture.\",\"o\":[[\"Coding → Requirement → Testing → Integration → Production\",\"Requirement is the cheapest stage, and integration comes before testing in cost.\"],[\"Requirement → Integration → Coding → Production → Testing\",\"Coding is moderate (cheaper than integration) and production is the most expensive.\"],[\"Production → Testing → Integration → Coding → Requirement\",\"This is the reverse order: production is the most expensive, not the cheapest.\"],[\"Requirement → Coding → Integration → Testing → Production\",\"Correct. Low → moderate → higher → very costly → extremely costly.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In the V-model, which test plan is produced during the <b>System design</b> stage on the left side?\",\"o\":[[\"Integration test plan\",\"Correct. System design ↔ integration test plan ↔ integration test.\"],[\"Acceptance test plan\",\"That comes from the requirements specifications stage.\"],[\"System test plan\",\"That comes from the functional specifications stage.\"],[\"Unit test cases\",\"Unit test cases come from the unit design stage at the bottom of the left side, not from system design (L1, V-model mapping).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"In the V-model, development on the left side goes from low level to high level, and testing on the right side goes from high level to low level.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"It is the other way round: development goes high → low level (down the left side) and testing goes low → high level (up the right side).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Development goes from high level to low level, and testing goes from low level (unit) to high level (acceptance).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In XP's Test-Driven Development, which step comes immediately AFTER \\\"write/modify the functional code\\\"?\",\"o\":[[\"Create the test code\",\"That is step 1, before the functional code is written.\"],[\"Refactor the code\",\"Refactoring is step 5, the last step; after writing the functional code you first create additional tests and run them (L1, TDD).\"],[\"Create additional tests\",\"Correct. The order is: create test code → write/modify functional code → create additional tests → test the functional code → refactor.\"],[\"Test the functional code\",\"That is step 4; additional tests are created first (step 3).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"\\\"The Patriot missile failure in the 1991 Gulf War was caused by a timing bug and led to the death of 28 soldiers.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture gives this as a real example of the cost of bugs.\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture states exactly this: a timing bug, 28 soldiers killed.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Software testing is described in the lecture as a(n) ……… process for detecting defects and verifying that a system meets its specified requirements.\",\"o\":[[\"Incremental and iterative\",\"Correct. The lecture defines testing as an incremental and iterative process and an integral part of the SDLC. (L1, What is software testing?)\"],[\"One-time and final\",\"Wrong. Testing is not done once at the end; it is incremental and iterative and part of the whole SDLC.\"],[\"Purely sequential\",\"Wrong. \\\"Sequential\\\" describes the Waterfall model, not the definition of testing.\"],[\"Random and uncontrolled\",\"Wrong. Miller says testing exercises software systematically in carefully controlled circumstances.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In Gelperin and Hetzel's classification, the period until 1956, when testing simply meant debugging, is called:\",\"o\":[[\"Demonstration-oriented\",\"Wrong. Demonstration-oriented is 1957–1978: ensure the software meets its specifications.\"],[\"Debugging-oriented\",\"Correct. Until 1956 testing was debugging-oriented. (L1, Evolution of software testing)\"],[\"Destruction-oriented\",\"Wrong. Destruction-oriented is 1979–1982, when tests were designed to find errors (Myers).\"],[\"Prevention-oriented\",\"Wrong. Prevention-oriented is 1988–2000, preventing defects with early test design and planning.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: Destruction-oriented testing (1979–1982), the era of Myers, designed tests to find errors.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The table gives 1979–1982 as destruction-oriented: tests designed to find errors (Myers' era). (L1, Evolution of software testing)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. The statement matches the table exactly, so it is true.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Who classified software testing based on the goals and phases of testing?\",\"o\":[[\"Miller\",\"Wrong. Miller gave the general aim of testing (affirm the quality...), not the historical classification.\"],[\"Myers\",\"Wrong. Myers is only named as the era of destruction-oriented testing (1979–1982).\"],[\"Deve Gelperin and William C. Hetzel\",\"Correct. Gelperin and Hetzel classified testing by its goals and phases. (L1, Evolution of software testing)\"],[\"Miller and Myers\",\"Wrong. Neither made the classification; it belongs to Gelperin and Hetzel.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"A program reads a buffer past its end and uses a variable that was never initialized. Which type of bug is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Math bugs\",\"Wrong. Math bugs are divide-by-zero, overflow and rounding errors.\"],[\"Logical bugs\",\"Wrong. Logical bugs are infinite loops, wrong logic and incorrect conditions.\"],[\"Team working bugs\",\"Wrong. Team working bugs are outdated documentation, mismatched files and incorrect linking.\"],[\"Resource bugs\",\"Correct. Buffer overflow, access violations and uninitialized variables are resource bugs. (L1, Types of bugs)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A loop whose exit condition is written wrongly never ends. This is an example of:\",\"o\":[[\"Logical bugs\",\"Correct. Infinite loops, wrong logic and incorrect conditions are logical bugs. (L1, Types of bugs)\"],[\"Conceptual bugs\",\"Wrong. Conceptual bugs are wrong syntax, misspelled keywords and flawed design logic.\"],[\"Co-programming bugs\",\"Wrong. Co-programming bugs are deadlocks, race conditions and concurrency issues.\"],[\"Resource bugs\",\"Wrong. Resource bugs are buffer overflow, access violations and uninitialized variables.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A program will not compile because a keyword is misspelled. According to the lecture's table, this bug type is:\",\"o\":[[\"Math\",\"Wrong. Math bugs are divide-by-zero, overflow and rounding errors.\"],[\"Conceptual\",\"Correct. Wrong syntax and misspelled keywords are conceptual bugs. (L1, Types of bugs)\"],[\"Team working\",\"Wrong. Team working bugs are outdated documentation, mismatched files and incorrect linking.\"],[\"Logical\",\"Wrong. Logical bugs are infinite loops and incorrect conditions, not misspelled keywords.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Two developers use different versions of the same source file, and the project documentation is out of date. Which bug type is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Co-programming\",\"Wrong. Co-programming bugs are deadlocks, race conditions and concurrency issues.\"],[\"Resource\",\"Wrong. Resource bugs are buffer overflow, access violations and uninitialized variables.\"],[\"Team working\",\"Correct. Outdated documentation, mismatched files and incorrect linking are team working bugs. (L1, Types of bugs)\"],[\"Conceptual\",\"Wrong. Conceptual bugs are wrong syntax, misspelled keywords and flawed design logic.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"A calculator gives wrong answers when its battery gets weak, although the specification never mentions the battery. Which bug condition is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Condition 1: does not do what the spec says\",\"Wrong. Condition 1 is a stated function that fails, like the + key not working.\"],[\"Condition 2: does what the spec says it should not\",\"Wrong. Condition 2 is forbidden behaviour such as crashing or freezing.\"],[\"Condition 3: does something the spec does not mention\",\"Wrong. Condition 3 is an extra unmentioned feature, like the \\\"square\\\" function.\"],[\"Condition 4: does not do something it should, although the spec may not mention it\",\"Correct. Working correctly on a weak battery is mandatory even if unmentioned; this is the lecture's example of condition 4. (L1, What is a bug?)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A word processor's specification says the Save button stores the document, but clicking Save does nothing. Which bug condition is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Condition 1: the software does not do what the specification says it should\",\"Correct. A function required by the spec does not work, like the calculator's + key. (L1, What is a bug?)\"],[\"Condition 3: the software does something the specification does not mention\",\"Wrong. Condition 3 is an extra unmentioned feature, not a missing required one.\"],[\"Condition 4: the software does not do something it should, although unmentioned\",\"Wrong. Condition 4 is about things not in the spec; here Save is explicitly in the spec.\"],[\"Condition 5: the software is difficult to understand or slow\",\"Wrong. Condition 5 is about usability and speed from the customer's view.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: Bug condition 5 brings in the customer perspective: it occurs whenever the performance of the software is not acceptable to the user.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture says condition 5 is the customer perspective, and the tester's feedback brings out these negatives before the product reaches the customer. (L1, What is a bug?)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. This is exactly how the lecture describes condition 5.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A team is forced to finish a system in half the needed time, so design and testing are compromised. Which reason for bugs is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Communication failure\",\"Wrong. Communication failure is miscommunication between teams.\"],[\"Unrealistic development timeframe\",\"Correct. Tight deadlines cause poor design and testing compromises. (L1, Reasons for bugs)\"],[\"Change requests\",\"Wrong. Change requests are last-minute requirement or tool changes.\"],[\"Human factor\",\"Wrong. Human factor means mistakes in logic or syntax.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Inefficient coding methods and faulty tools belong to which reason for bugs?\",\"o\":[[\"Poor design logic\",\"Wrong. Poor design logic is inadequate research or misunderstood feasibility.\"],[\"Lack of skilled testing\",\"Wrong. That reason is about unskilled testers failing to find hidden issues.\"],[\"Poor coding practices\",\"Correct. Poor coding practices include inefficient methods and faulty tools. (L1, Reasons for bugs)\"],[\"Communication failure\",\"Wrong. Communication failure is miscommunication between teams.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: Fixing a bug found during integration is cheaper than fixing the same bug found during coding.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong. Coding-stage cost is moderate; integration is higher because it involves the developer and engineers.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The order is Requirement (low), Coding (moderate), Integration (higher), Testing (very costly), Production (extremely costly). (L1, Cost of bugs)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Fixing a bug at this stage is very costly because it involves developers, engineers and managers and delays the project:\",\"o\":[[\"Requirement stage\",\"Wrong. The requirement stage is the cheapest: early errors are easy to correct.\"],[\"Coding stage\",\"Wrong. At coding the cost is moderate; the developer can fix it easily.\"],[\"Production stage\",\"Wrong. Production is extremely costly because of reputation and financial or legal penalties.\"],[\"Testing stage\",\"Correct. At testing, fixing is very costly: developers, engineers, managers and delays. (L1, Cost of bugs)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"What is the main drawback of the Waterfall model according to the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"Errors made in early phases carry over to later stages and must be fixed at multiple points\",\"Correct. Early errors carry over, causing delays and higher cost. (L1, Waterfall)\"],[\"The time taken to complete many iterations\",\"Wrong. That is the main drawback of the Spiral model.\"],[\"A missing reusable component can make the project fail\",\"Wrong. That is a disadvantage of RAD.\"],[\"It needs active customer involvement and suffers scope creep\",\"Wrong. These are disadvantages of Agile.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which Waterfall phase covers the feasibility study, goals, and performance and interface requirements?\",\"o\":[[\"System Engineering\",\"Wrong. System Engineering defines both software and hardware requirements.\"],[\"Analysis\",\"Correct. Analysis is the feasibility study, goals, performance and interface requirements. (L1, Waterfall)\"],[\"Design\",\"Wrong. Design translates requirements into a software structure such as database design.\"],[\"Maintenance\",\"Wrong. Maintenance fixes issues and updates the software as new needs arise.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the Spiral model, which phase comes immediately after Planning in each cycle?\",\"o\":[[\"Design engineering\",\"Wrong. Design engineering is the third phase, after risk analysis.\"],[\"Customer evaluation\",\"Wrong. Customer evaluation (testing & evaluation) is the last phase of the cycle.\"],[\"Risk analysis\",\"Correct. The cycle is Planning → Risk analysis → Design engineering → Customer evaluation. (L1, Spiral model)\"],[\"Coding\",\"Wrong. Coding is a Waterfall phase, not one of the four Spiral phases.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: The main drawback of the Spiral model is the amount of time taken to complete its iterations, which can increase costs.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture says the Spiral model can be time-consuming and costly due to multiple iterations and continuous testing. (L1, Spiral model)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. This is exactly the drawback the lecture gives for Spiral.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In the V-model, the test plan created at the Functional specifications stage is the:\",\"o\":[[\"Acceptance test plan\",\"Wrong. The acceptance test plan comes from the requirements specifications.\"],[\"Integration test plan\",\"Wrong. The integration test plan comes from the system design stage.\"],[\"Unit test cases\",\"Wrong. Test cases for unit testing come from the unit design stage.\"],[\"System test plan\",\"Correct. Functional specifications pair with the system test plan and the system test. (L1, V-Model mapping)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In the V-model, which test level is performed by the customer to confirm the business requirements?\",\"o\":[[\"Acceptance testing\",\"Correct. Acceptance testing is done by the customer to confirm business requirements. (L1, V-Model)\"],[\"System testing\",\"Wrong. System testing checks that the whole system meets design and performance.\"],[\"Integration testing\",\"Wrong. Integration testing checks interactions between modules.\"],[\"Unit testing\",\"Wrong. Unit testing checks individual modules.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: The V-model is less effective when requirements are incomplete or badly documented.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. This is the V-model drawback given in the lecture; it is also not suitable for every project. (L1, V-Model)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. The lecture lists exactly this as the V-model's drawback.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"……… is a linear sequential model with an extremely short development cycle that uses reusable components.\",\"o\":[[\"Waterfall\",\"Wrong. Waterfall is sequential but has no short cycle or reusable components.\"],[\"RAD\",\"Correct. RAD is linear sequential with an extremely short cycle and reusable components. (L1, RAD model)\"],[\"Spiral\",\"Wrong. Spiral is iterative with risk analysis and 2 or 3 prototypes.\"],[\"V-model\",\"Wrong. The V-model integrates testing into every stage; it is not described as short-cycle.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which RAD phase uses reusable components to reduce testing time before delivering the final product?\",\"o\":[[\"Requirements planning\",\"Wrong. Requirements planning collects requirements and creates an outline.\"],[\"Design phase\",\"Wrong. The design phase builds a prototype with all requirements and changes.\"],[\"Testing and handover\",\"Correct. Testing and handover uses reusable components to cut testing time before delivery. (L1, RAD model)\"],[\"Construction phase\",\"Wrong. Construction develops and improves prototypes with RAD tools until the customer is satisfied.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In Agile, the approach DSDM stands for:\",\"o\":[[\"Dynamic Software Design Model\",\"Wrong. This is not the lecture's expansion.\"],[\"Distributed System Development Method\",\"Wrong. The \\\"D\\\" is Dynamic, not Distributed.\"],[\"Defect Search and Detection Method\",\"Wrong. DSDM is a development method, not a bug-finding method.\"],[\"Dynamic Systems Development Method\",\"Correct. DSDM = Dynamic Systems Development Method; the others are SCRUM and XP. (L1, Agile model)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: In XP (Extreme Programming), programmers work in pairs: one writes the code while the other reviews and tests it.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture describes XP pair programming this way. (L1, XP and TDD)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. The lecture says exactly this about XP.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which TDD step removes duplicate or unused code and reduces complexity?\",\"o\":[[\"Refactor the code\",\"Correct. Refactoring makes the code easier to maintain, extend and update by removing duplicate or unused code. (L1, XP and TDD)\"],[\"Create the test code\",\"Wrong. This first step writes automated tests that define what the software should do.\"],[\"Create additional tests\",\"Wrong. Here the tester adds more tests with different inputs.\"],[\"Test the functional code\",\"Wrong. This step reruns the tests until all pass.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: In the lecture's summary, Waterfall is considered the most advanced and efficient software development model.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong. Waterfall is described only as sequential; Agile is the most advanced and efficient.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The summary says Agile is the most advanced and efficient; V and Spiral are more efficient than waterfall. (L1, Summary)\"]],\"a\":1}]},{\"n\":2,\"title\":\"Black Box Testing\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Software testing terms\",\"table\":[[\"Term\",\"Meaning\",\"Example\"],[\"Bug / Defect\",\"Flaw/error causing unintended results\",\"Login button not working\"],[\"Verification\",\"\\\"Are we building the product right?\\\"\",\"Code reviews, design checks\"],[\"Validation\",\"\\\"Are we building the right product?\\\"\",\"User acceptance testing\"],[\"Quality Assurance (QA)\",\"Process-focused, preventive\",\"Process audits, checklists\"],[\"Quality Control (QC)\",\"Product-focused, corrective\",\"Unit and system testing\"],[\"Test Plan\",\"Document with test objectives and scope\",\"Describes login testing scope\"],[\"Test Case\",\"Input, conditions and expected output\",\"Invalid password → \\\"Error\\\" message\"],[\"Unit Testing\",\"Smallest testable component\",\"Function or module test\"],[\"Integration Testing\",\"Tests interfaces between modules\",\"Payment module connects to billing\"],[\"System Testing\",\"Tests complete system behaviour\",\"Ensure that specifications are met\"],[\"Acceptance Testing\",\"Performed by user/customer\",\"Software product meets the requirements\"],[\"Agile Testing\",\"Early customer-focused testing in agile cycles\",\"Sprint-based validation\"]]},{\"h\":\"Black-box (functional) testing\",\"pts\":[\"Also called <b>behavioral testing</b>. Focuses on functionality <b>without knowing the internal logic</b>. The tester analyses <b>inputs and expected outputs</b> and checks the software meets requirements.\",\"Figure (Black-box): an <b>Input</b> arrow points down into a closed grey box labelled <b>Software</b>, and an <b>output</b> arrow points down out of it; the tester sees only the input and the output.\",\"<b>Advantages</b>:<br>• no need to understand the code<br>• quick test-case development<br>• helps <b>expose ambiguities in specifications</b><br>• suitable for user-perspective (GUI) testing.\",\"<b>Disadvantages</b>:<br>• cannot test all possible inputs<br>• hard to design cases if requirements are unclear<br>• bugs in complex areas may go undetected.\",\"<b>ATM example</b>: the tester acts as a customer and checks functions through the GUI (display changes when the card is detected, password masking, navigating from the main menu to a function) without knowing the internal logic.\",\"<b>Static testing</b> = testing something that is <b>not running</b> (examining and reviewing it).<br><b>Dynamic testing</b> = running and using the software.\"],\"fig\":\"qa-blackbox\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-blackbox\":1},\"figH\":{\"qa-blackbox\":\"Black-box testing: input in, output out, the inside stays closed\"}},{\"h\":\"Static vs dynamic black-box testing\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Static black-box</b>: testing <b>without executing</b> the software; focuses on the <b>specification</b>.<br>• Purpose: ensure completeness and appropriateness.<br>• Key document: the specification (e.g. user manual) from early SDLC.<br>• Goal: bug-free, clear, user-focused documentation.\",\"<b>Dynamic black-box</b>: testing by <b>executing</b> the software with data, without knowing the inside code (customer view, also called <b>exploratory</b>/behavioral testing). You should have the specification.\",\"Figure (Dynamic black-box): <b>Inputs</b> → a dark box marked <b>?</b> and labelled <b>Software</b> → <b>Outputs</b>. A callout pointing at the inputs says <b>Exploratory Testing</b>, and the caption under it is <b>behavioral testing</b>.\",\"Steps to begin DBB testing:<br>(1) have the documents/specifications<br>(2) define test cases.<br>Example (Windows calculator addition): 0+0=0, 0+1=1, 254+1=255, 255+1=256, 256+1=257.\",\"<b>Start with test-to-pass</b> (confirm it works normally with standard input) <b>then test-to-fail</b> (push it to its limits with extreme inputs).\"],\"fig\":\"qa-dynamic-bb\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-dynamic-bb\":2},\"figH\":{\"qa-dynamic-bb\":\"Dynamic black-box testing: run the software, watch only inputs and outputs, then try the calculator cases\"}},{\"h\":\"Test-to-pass vs test-to-fail\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Test-to-pass</b>: assure the software <b>minimally works</b> (\\\"kindness dealing\\\"); test in normal cases using boundaries, sub-boundaries and default data. Car example: drive it normally before crash-testing it. Don't be surprised to find bugs even in test-to-pass.\",\"Figure (Car example): \\\"Use test-to-pass to reveal bugs before you test-to-fail.\\\" On the left, a driver waves from a car driven normally (<b>Test-to-pass</b>); on the right, a dented car with a cracked windscreen speeds off with smoke behind it (<b>Test-to-fail</b>).\",\"<b>Test-to-fail</b>: find weaknesses by using <b>abnormal cases</b> (forcing an error).\",\"Error messages: saving to a disk with no disk inserted. The spec says it should show an error, so it looks like test-to-pass, but you are also forcing an error, so it is test-to-fail. <b>In the end it is probably both.</b>\"],\"fig\":\"qa-pass-fail\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-pass-fail\":1},\"figH\":{\"qa-pass-fail\":\"The car example: test-to-pass before test-to-fail\"}},{\"h\":\"Equivalence partitioning (EP)\",\"pts\":[\"EP groups test cases into categories and <b>reduces the number of test cases without lowering test quality</b>. An equivalence class (partition) is a set of test cases that <b>tests the same thing or reveals the same bug</b>, so testing <b>one case from each class</b> is enough.\",\"<b>Objective</b>: identify test cases that produce <b>similar results</b>. Since test cases in the same class are equivalent, testing <b>one case from each class</b> is enough to detect bugs.\",\"<b>Benefits</b>: greatly reduces the number of test cases; saves time and effort while still ensuring good coverage.\",\"Equivalence partitioning is the process of <b>methodically reducing the huge set of possible test cases into a much smaller one</b>. <b>Partition</b>: a set of test cases that test the same thing.\",\"Classes: <b>valid</b> (satisfy the condition) and <b>invalid</b> (do not satisfy it). Example: 1+1, 1+2… are one partition; 1+999999999999999999999999999 is another (it may overflow).\",\"<b>Driving license example</b>: age above 18 and at most 60 is eligible. Classes: valid = 18 to 60; invalid = below 18; invalid = above 60 → <b>3 classes</b>.\",\"<b>Program example</b>: accepts <b>4 to 10 inputs</b>, each a <b>5-digit integer ≥ 10,000</b>.<br>Number of inputs:<br>• less than 4 (invalid, test 3)<br>• 4–10 (valid, test 4, 7, 10)<br>• more than 10 (invalid, test 11).\",\"Partition the system inputs and outputs into <b>'equivalence sets'</b>.\",\"Input values 10,000–99,999 → partitions <b>&lt;10,000</b>, <b>10,000–99,999</b>, <b>&gt;99,999</b>.<br><b>6 test cases</b>:<br>• 00000 (invalid special value)<br>• 09999 (invalid left boundary)<br>• 10000 (valid left boundary)<br>• 50000 (valid mid)<br>• 99999 (valid right boundary)<br>• 100000 (invalid right boundary).\",\"Figure (EP example): two partition bars.<br>• <b>Number of input values</b>: Less than 4 (Invalid) | Between 4 and 10 (Valid) | More than 10 (Invalid), with arrows at <b>3, 4, 7, 10, 11</b>.<br>• <b>Input values</b>: Less than 10000 (Invalid) | Between 10000 and 99999 (Valid) | More than 99999 (Invalid), with arrows at <b>9999 (BV), 10000, 50000 (Mid-point), 99999, 100000 (BV)</b>. A red \\\"Valid\\\" line spans the middle partition of each bar.\"],\"fig\":\"qa-ep-boundary\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-ep-boundary\":9},\"figH\":{\"qa-ep-boundary\":\"Try it: find the partition and the boundary values of any input\"}},{\"h\":\"Data testing and boundary conditions\",\"pts\":[\"Software = <b>data</b> (keyboard input, mouse clicks, disk files, printouts…)<br>+ <b>program</b> (executable flow, transitions, logic, computations).<br>Goal: reduce test cases using partitions based on <b>boundary conditions, sub-boundary conditions, nulls and bad data</b>.\",\"Examples of data:<br>• the words you type into a word processor<br>• the numbers entered into a spreadsheet<br>• the picture printed by your photo software<br>• the backup files stored on your floppy disk<br>• the data being sent by your modem over the phone lines.\",\"<b>Boundary conditions</b>: situations at the <b>edge of the planned operational limits</b>. Programming is susceptible to problems at its edges (like the edge of a cliff).\",\"Figure 5.5: \\\"A software boundary is much like the edge of a cliff\\\" (a person walking with a dog right at the edge of a cliff).\",\"<b>Boundary examples</b>:<br>• Text field 1–255 chars: valid 1, 255 (and 254); invalid <b>0 and 256</b>.<br>• CD-R: very small file, file at the disc limit, empty file, file too large.<br>• Print multiple pages per sheet: 1, the maximum, 0 and max+1.<br>• 9-digit ZIP: 00000-0000, 99999-9999, one digit more or less.\",\"<b>Bugs increase at the boundaries</b>, but testing the edges only may not be sufficient, so we must use equivalence partitions to test all data in, out of and at the boundary.<br>• Test: data that works, data that does not work, data at the boundary.<br>• Floppy example: very small file, very large file that fits, empty file, file too large.\",\"Floppy disk program (write and read from a floppy disk): boundary = <b>max file, min file</b>. <b>Searching for boundaries in software is a very important task.</b>\"],\"fig\":\"qa-cliff\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-cliff\":3},\"figH\":{\"qa-cliff\":\"Figure 5.5: a software boundary is like the edge of a cliff\"}},{\"h\":\"State testing\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Software state</b>: the condition or mode the software is currently in (e.g. Paint's pencil state vs airbrush state). <b>State testing</b> verifies the program's logic through its states and the <b>transitions</b> between them.\",\"A <b>state transition map</b> (from the spec or drawn by you with boxes, arrows, circles) shows:<br>(1) each unique state<br>(2) the input/condition that moves from one state to the next<br>(3) conditions set and output produced when a state is entered or exited.<br>The state transition map may be in the specification, or we may make it. We can use any diagram that <b>all the team accept</b> (e.g. boxes, arrows, circles).<br>Draw it <b>from the user's view</b>.\",\"Figure 5.10 (state transition diagrams can be drawn using different techniques), the same password example drawn two ways.<br>• <b>Boxes and arrows</b>: Idle → (Esc Key Pressed / Display Password Box) → <b>Waiting for password</b>; Incorrect Password / Clear the field loops back to Waiting for password; Correct Password / Initiate Destruct leaves it.<br>• <b>Circles</b>: Idle (double circle) → Esc Key Pressed → <b>Wait for Password</b> → Correct Password; Incorrect Password loops back to Wait for Password.<br>Remember: create your map from the <b>user's view</b> of the software.\",\"<b>5 ways to reduce states/transitions to test</b>:<br>(1) visit each state at least once<br>(2) test the most common state-to-state transitions<br>(3) test the least common paths<br>(4) test all error states and returning from them<br>(5) test random transitions.\",\"Check all <b>state variables</b>.<br>Paint's startup state:<br>• window as last shown<br>• same size as last time<br>• blank drawing area<br>• tool box/color box/status bar shown<br>• pencil selected<br>• black on white<br>• document \\\"untitled\\\".\",\"Figure .11 (<b>The Windows Paint opening screen in the startup state</b>): a window titled \\\"untitled - Paint\\\" with the menu File, Edit, View, Image, Colors, Help; the tool box on the left with the <b>pencil</b> tool selected; a blank white drawing area with the pencil cursor; the color box at the bottom (black foreground on white background) and the status bar \\\"For Help, click Help Topics on the Help Menu\\\" with the cursor position 194,155.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-paint-states\",\"qa-password-states\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-password-states\":2,\"qa-paint-states\":5},\"figH\":{\"qa-paint-states\":\"Try it: Paint's pencil and airbrush states, and its startup state\",\"qa-password-states\":\"Figure 5.10: one password state diagram drawn two ways\"}},{\"h\":\"Worked example: media player state diagram\",\"table\":[[\"From state\",\"Input\",\"To state\"],[\"OFF\",\"play\",\"PLAY\"],[\"OFF\",\"fast forward\",\"FAST FORWARD\"],[\"PLAY\",\"stop\",\"OFF\"],[\"PLAY\",\"fast forward\",\"FAST PLAY\"],[\"FAST FORWARD\",\"stop\",\"OFF\"],[\"FAST FORWARD\",\"play\",\"PLAY\"],[\"FAST PLAY\",\"stop\",\"PLAY\"],[\"FAST PLAY\",\"fast forward\",\"FAST FORWARD\"]],\"pts\":[\"A media player has three operations: <b>play, fast forward and fast play</b>. Play and fast forward are activated using the play and fast forward buttons respectively. These operations can be cancelled using the <b>stop</b> button. The media player's operation is specified using the state transition diagram (states OFF, PLAY, FAST FORWARD, FAST PLAY) shown in the table below.\"],\"fig\":\"qa-media-player\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-media-player\":0},\"figH\":{\"qa-media-player\":\"Try it: press the media player's buttons and visit every state\"}},{\"h\":\"Media player answers\",\"pts\":[\"<b>All states at least once</b> (minimum = 1 test case): &lt;Off&gt; play → fast forward → fast forward (visits OFF, PLAY, FAST PLAY, FAST FORWARD).\",\"<b>All 8 transitions at least once</b> (2 test cases): TC1 &lt;Off&gt; play → fast forward → fast forward → stop; TC2 &lt;Off&gt; fast forward → play → fast forward → stop → stop.\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Verification\",\"Are we building the product right? (code reviews, design checks)\"],[\"Validation\",\"Are we building the right product? (user acceptance testing)\"],[\"QA vs QC\",\"QA: process-focused, preventive. QC: product-focused, corrective.\"],[\"Test plan\",\"Document with test objectives and scope.\"],[\"Test case\",\"Input, conditions and expected output.\"],[\"Black-box testing is also called\",\"Behavioral (functional) testing.\"],[\"Static testing\",\"Examining and reviewing something that is not running.\"],[\"Test-to-pass\",\"Assure the software minimally works using normal cases (\\\"kindness dealing\\\").\"],[\"Test-to-fail\",\"Find weaknesses by forcing errors with abnormal/extreme inputs.\"],[\"Equivalence partition\",\"A set of test cases that tests the same thing or reveals the same bug.\"],[\"Boundary conditions\",\"Situations at the edge of the planned operational limits of the software.\"],[\"Text field 1 to 255 characters: invalid boundary tests\",\"0 and 256 characters.\"],[\"5-digit input 10000 to 99999: the 6 test cases\",\"00000, 09999, 10000, 50000, 99999, 100000.\"],[\"Software state\",\"The condition or mode the software is currently in.\"],[\"State transition map contains\",\"Each unique state, the input that moves between states, conditions/output on entry or exit.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Compare test-to-pass and test-to-fail. Is an error-message test one or the other?\",\"Test-to-pass checks the software minimally works using normal input, boundaries and default data. Test-to-fail tries to break it using abnormal cases. Always start with test-to-pass. An error-message test (saving with no disk) is required by the spec (test-to-pass) but forces an error (test-to-fail), so it is probably both.\"],[\"A field accepts a 5-digit integer from 10,000 to 99,999. Give the equivalence partitions and test cases.\",\"Partitions: less than 10,000 (invalid), 10,000 to 99,999 (valid), more than 99,999 (invalid). Test cases: 00000 invalid special value, 09999 invalid left boundary, 10000 valid left boundary, 50000 valid mid, 99999 valid right boundary, 100000 invalid right boundary.\"],[\"What are the five ways to reduce the number of states and transitions to test?\",\"Visit each state at least once; test the most common transitions; test the least common paths; test all error states and returning from them; test random state transitions.\"],[\"List the advantages and disadvantages of black-box testing.\",\"Advantages: no need to understand code, quick test-case development, exposes ambiguities in specs, suits GUI/user-view testing. Disadvantages: cannot test all inputs, hard if requirements are unclear, bugs in complex areas may go undetected.\"],[\"Does equivalence partitioning reduce test cases without compromising quality? Explain.\",\"Yes. Inputs in one class are processed the same way, so one test per class detects the same bugs as testing every value. This greatly reduces the number of cases and saves time while keeping good coverage.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"The process that confirms that software meets its <b>specification</b> is:\",\"o\":[[\"Verification\",\"Correct. Verification = \\\"are we building the product right?\\\", checked against the spec.\"],[\"Validation\",\"Validation checks the user's needs (\\\"the right product\\\").\"],[\"Design\",\"Design is a development phase, not a checking process.\"],[\"Requirements\",\"Requirements are what we check against, not the checking process.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"The process that confirms that software meets the <b>user's requirement</b> is:\",\"o\":[[\"Validation\",\"Correct. Validation = \\\"are we building the right product?\\\" (e.g. UAT).\"],[\"Verification\",\"Verification checks against the specification.\"],[\"Design\",\"Design is a development activity that defines the software structure; it does not check the product against anything. Checking against user needs is validation.\"],[\"Requirements\",\"Requirements are what the product is checked against, not the checking process itself; confirming the user's requirement is met is validation.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"The program shall accept an input value of integer &gt; 9000. Determine the test case.\",\"o\":[[\"9000\",\"A valid test (invalid boundary: must be rejected), but not the only one.\"],[\"9001\",\"A valid test (first valid value), but not the only one.\"],[\"9999\",\"A valid test (a normal valid value), but not the only one.\"],[\"All answers\",\"Correct. 9000 is the invalid boundary, 9001 the valid boundary and 9999 a valid value: all are useful BVA/EP test cases.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of software testing refers to testing the <b>running</b> software?\",\"o\":[[\"Requirement testing\",\"\\\"Requirement testing\\\" is not one of the lecture's terms; the lecture splits testing into static (not running) and dynamic (running) testing.\"],[\"Static testing\",\"Static testing examines something that is NOT running.\"],[\"Analysis testing\",\"\\\"Analysis testing\\\" is not a lecture term; testing the running software is dynamic testing, examining it without running is static.\"],[\"Dynamic testing\",\"Correct. Dynamic testing means running and using the software.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Which testing helps expose ambiguities or inconsistencies in the specifications and is carried out from a user's perspective?\",\"o\":[[\"White box testing\",\"White box looks at code, not the user's view.\"],[\"Automation testing\",\"Automation testing describes how tests are run (with tools), not a user-perspective technique; exposing specification ambiguities is a black-box advantage.\"],[\"Manual testing\",\"Manual testing only says tests are run by hand; it can be black- or white-box. The lecture lists exposing specification ambiguities under black-box.\"],[\"Black box testing\",\"Correct. Black-box testing works only from inputs, expected outputs and the spec, so it exposes ambiguities in the specification and suits user-perspective (GUI) testing (L2, Black-box advantages).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"Which dynamic technique pushes the software to its limits to find the bugs that occur under extreme conditions?\",\"o\":[[\"Test to pass\",\"Test-to-pass only assures the software minimally works with normal input (\\\"kindness dealing\\\"); pushing it to its limits is test-to-fail.\"],[\"Test to fail\",\"Correct. It forces errors using extreme or abnormal input.\"],[\"Data testing\",\"Data testing partitions the input data (boundaries, sub-boundaries, nulls, bad data) to reduce test cases; it is not defined as pushing the software to its limits.\"],[\"State testing\",\"State testing verifies the program's logic through its states and the transitions between them, not behaviour under extreme input.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the boundary tests for the field <b>Day</b> in DD/MM/YYYY.\",\"o\":[[\"29\",\"Upper boundary for February in a leap year, but not the only one.\"],[\"28\",\"Upper boundary for February, but not the only one.\"],[\"All the above\",\"Correct. The last day depends on the month: 28, 29, 30 and 31 are all boundaries.\"],[\"30, 31\",\"Boundaries for other months, but 28 and 29 are also needed.\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"A document with test objectives and scope is called a:\",\"o\":[[\"Random test\",\"A random test is a way of choosing test input (e.g. random transitions), not a document that sets out test objectives and scope.\"],[\"Test case\",\"A test case is input, conditions and expected output.\"],[\"Test plan\",\"Correct (the summary spells it \\\"test plane\\\").\"],[\"Dynamic test\",\"Dynamic testing is a type of testing (running the software), not a document; the document with objectives and scope is the test plan.\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"A text entry field allows 1 to 255 characters. Which are the INVALID boundary tests?\",\"o\":[[\"1 and 255\",\"1 and 255 satisfy the 1–255 rule, so they are the VALID boundary tests; the invalid ones lie just outside the range.\"],[\"0 and 256\",\"Correct. One below the minimum and one above the maximum.\"],[\"254 and 255\",\"254 and 255 are both inside 1–255, so both are valid tests; invalid boundaries are just outside the range: 0 and 256.\"],[\"128 only\",\"128 is a middle value of the valid range, not a boundary at all, and it is valid; the invalid boundaries are 0 and 256.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Driving license: an individual above 18 and at most 60 is eligible. How many equivalence classes?\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"One class would hold only the valid ages 18–60; EP also needs the two invalid classes, below 18 and above 60, giving 3.\"],[\"2\",\"There are two invalid classes, plus one valid.\"],[\"3\",\"Correct. Between 18 and 60 (valid), below 18 (invalid), above 60 (invalid).\"],[\"6\",\"6 is the number of test cases in the 5-digit example, not classes here.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In the media-player example, what is the minimum number of test cases that visits every state at least once?\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"Correct. Off → play → fast forward → fast forward visits all four states.\"],[\"2\",\"Two are needed to exercise every transition, not every state.\"],[\"4\",\"Four test cases (one per state) are not needed: a single sequence off → play → fast forward → fast forward already visits all four states.\"],[\"8\",\"8 is the number of transitions in the media-player diagram, not test cases; one sequence visits every state.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Static black-box testing mainly focuses on:\",\"o\":[[\"Running the code with extreme data\",\"Running the code with extreme data is dynamic test-to-fail; static black-box testing does not execute the software at all.\"],[\"The specification (e.g. user manual)\",\"Correct. It tests without executing and aims for complete, clear documentation.\"],[\"Internal data structures\",\"Examining internal data structures needs knowledge of the code, which is white-box testing; static black-box testing reviews the specification.\"],[\"Server load\",\"Server load is checked by running the system under stress (dynamic performance testing), not by reviewing the specification.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"The program shall accept an input value of integer &gt; 9000. Determine the invalid test case for this program.\",\"o\":[[\"9000\",\"Correct. With \\\"> 9000\\\" the valid class starts at 9001, so 9000 is the invalid boundary: the value just outside the range, which must be rejected. Lecture 2's example is the same: for \\\"≥ 10,000\\\", 09999 is the invalid left boundary and 10000 the valid left boundary (L2, Equivalence partitioning, Boundary value analysis).\"],[\"9999\",\"9999 is greater than 9000, so it is a valid input from the accepted class (L2).\"],[\"10000\",\"10000 is greater than 9000, so it is a valid input (L2).\"],[\"All answers\",\"9999 and 10000 are valid, so not every option is an invalid test case.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of software testing refers to testing the running software\",\"o\":[[\"Static testing\",\"The opposite: static testing examines and reviews software that is not running (L2).\"],[\"Requirement testing\",\"Not a lecture term; L2 splits testing into static (not running) and dynamic (running).\"],[\"Dynamic testing\",\"Correct. Lecture 2: \\\"Static testing = testing something that is not running (examining and reviewing it). Dynamic testing = running and using the software.\\\"\"],[\"Analysis testing\",\"Not a lecture term; \\\"Analysis\\\" is only a Waterfall/Spiral phase in L1, not a type of testing.\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The process is confirmed that software meets the customer's Requirement\",\"o\":[[\"Design\",\"Design is a development activity that defines the structure; it does not check the product.\"],[\"Requirements\",\"Requirements are what the product is checked against, not the checking process.\"],[\"Validation\",\"Correct. Lecture 2: validation = \\\"Are we building the right product?\\\", checking against what the customer needs (e.g. user acceptance testing); verification = \\\"Are we building the product right?\\\", checking against the specification. The 2025/26 final keyed the same question as Validation.\"],[\"Verification\",\"Verification checks against the specification; checking against the customer's requirement is validation (L2).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The process is confirmed that software meets its specification\",\"o\":[[\"Validation\",\"Validation checks against the customer's requirements; checking against the specification is verification (L2).\"],[\"Design\",\"Design is a development activity; it does not confirm anything against the spec.\"],[\"Requirements\",\"Requirements are what is checked against, not the checking process.\"],[\"Verification\",\"Correct. Lecture 2: verification = \\\"Are we building the product right?\\\", checking against the specification (code reviews, design checks). In the V-model, the left side going down (requirements spec to code) is verification (L1, L2).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Document with test objectives and scope is called\",\"o\":[[\"Test plane\",\"Correct. Lecture 2's table of testing terms: \\\"Test Plan: document with test objectives and scope\\\" (example: describes the login testing scope). \\\"Test plane\\\" is the quiz's spelling of test plan.\"],[\"Random test\",\"A random test is a way of choosing inputs, not a document.\"],[\"Dynamic test\",\"Dynamic testing is a type of testing (running the software), not a document (L2).\"],[\"Test case\",\"A test case is one test: input, conditions and expected output; the test plan sets the objectives and scope for all of them.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Identify the boundary tests in order to test the field Day in DD/MM/YYYY.\",\"o\":[[\"30, 31\",\"Boundaries for 30- and 31-day months, but February's 28 and 29 are missing.\"],[\"All answers\",\"Correct. Boundary value analysis tests the edges of each valid range (L2). The last valid day depends on the month and year: 28 (February), 29 (February in a leap year), 30 (April, June, September, November), 31 (the other months). All of them are boundaries to test.\"],[\"28\",\"Only February's boundary in a normal year.\"],[\"29\",\"Only February's boundary in a leap year.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Which type of software testing that not running, examining and reviewing it\",\"o\":[[\"Dynamic testing\",\"The opposite: dynamic testing runs and uses the software (L2).\"],[\"Static testing\",\"Correct. Lecture 2: \\\"Static testing = testing something that is not running (examining and reviewing it).\\\"\"],[\"integration testing\",\"Integration testing runs the combined modules, so it is dynamic (L3).\"],[\"System testing\",\"System testing runs the whole system, so it is dynamic (L1, V-model).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"A password must be <b>6 to 12 characters</b> long. Which set of lengths picks exactly ONE representative from each equivalence class?\",\"o\":[[\"6, 12\",\"Both values are in the same valid class; the two invalid classes are not tested.\"],[\"8, 10, 12\",\"All three are from the valid class, so the invalid classes are missed.\"],[\"3, 15\",\"These cover only the two invalid classes; the valid class is missing.\"],[\"3, 8, 15\",\"Correct. 3 is in the invalid \\\"fewer than 6\\\" class, 8 in the valid 6–12 class and 15 in the invalid \\\"more than 12\\\" class.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A \\\"quantity\\\" field accepts whole numbers from <b>1 to 99</b>. Using boundary value analysis as in the lecture, which set gives the valid AND invalid boundary tests?\",\"o\":[[\"1, 50, 99\",\"50 is a mid value; the invalid boundaries 0 and 100 are missing.\"],[\"0, 1, 99, 100\",\"Correct. 1 and 99 are the valid edges; 0 and 100 are just outside (like 0 and 256 for the 1–255 text field).\"],[\"0, 100\",\"These are only the invalid boundaries; the valid edges 1 and 99 are missing.\"],[\"2, 98\",\"These are inside the range and are not the edges.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A program accepts integers from <b>100 to 999</b>. Following the lecture's 10,000–99,999 example, which value is the <b>invalid left boundary</b> test?\",\"o\":[[\"100\",\"100 is the lowest value the program accepts, so it is the VALID left boundary (like 10000 in the lecture); the invalid one is just below it.\"],[\"1000\",\"1000 is the invalid RIGHT boundary (just above 999).\"],[\"99\",\"Correct. It is just below the lowest valid value, like 09999 in the lecture's example.\"],[\"550\",\"550 is a valid middle value (like 50000 in the lecture's example), not a boundary; the invalid left boundary is 99.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"When starting dynamic black-box testing, which approach should be applied FIRST?\",\"o\":[[\"Test-to-pass\",\"Correct. First confirm it minimally works with normal input, then push it with test-to-fail.\"],[\"Test-to-fail\",\"Test-to-fail comes after test-to-pass, like crash-testing a car only after driving it normally.\"],[\"Static black-box testing\",\"Static testing does not run the software, so it is not a dynamic black-box approach.\"],[\"Debugging\",\"Debugging fixes bugs; it is not a dynamic black-box test approach.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"Trying to save a file to a disk drive with no disk inserted, where the spec says an error message must appear, is purely a test-to-pass case.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The lecture says it looks like test-to-pass, but you are also forcing an error, so in the end it is probably both.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. It checks a specified error message (test-to-pass) but also forces an error (test-to-fail), so it is probably both.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT one of the lecture's 5 ways to reduce the number of states and transitions to test?\",\"o\":[[\"Test every possible sequence of transitions exhaustively\",\"Correct. The point is to REDUCE the number of tests; exhaustive testing is not one of the 5 ways.\"],[\"Visit each state at least once\",\"Visiting each state at least once is way 1 of the lecture's 5 ways to reduce states and transitions, so it IS on the list.\"],[\"Test all error states and returning from them\",\"Testing all error states and returning from them is way 4 of the 5 ways, so it IS on the list.\"],[\"Test the least common paths\",\"Testing the least common paths is way 3 of the 5 ways (after each state once and the most common transitions), so it IS on the list.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which of these does a state transition map NOT need to show?\",\"o\":[[\"Each unique state the software can be in\",\"Each unique state the software can be in is item 1 of what a state transition map shows, so the map does need it.\"],[\"The input or condition that moves the software from one state to the next\",\"The input or condition that moves the software from one state to the next is item 2 of the map (the arrows), so the map needs it.\"],[\"The source code line that implements each transition\",\"Correct. The map is drawn from the user's view; code lines belong to white-box testing.\"],[\"Conditions set and output produced when a state is entered or exited\",\"The conditions set and output produced when a state is entered or exited is item 3 of the map, so the map needs it.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In the lecture's media player, you start in OFF and press: <b>fast forward → play → fast forward → stop</b>. Which state are you in at the end?\",\"o\":[[\"OFF\",\"Stop from FAST PLAY goes to PLAY, not OFF (only stop from PLAY or FAST FORWARD goes to OFF).\"],[\"PLAY\",\"Correct. OFF → FAST FORWARD → PLAY → FAST PLAY, and stop from FAST PLAY returns to PLAY.\"],[\"FAST PLAY\",\"You were in FAST PLAY before the last input; stop moves you out of it.\"],[\"FAST FORWARD\",\"Play from FAST FORWARD moved you to PLAY in the second step and you never returned.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Process audits and checklists are examples of which term from the lecture's table?\",\"o\":[[\"Quality Control (QC)\",\"QC is product-focused and corrective; its examples are unit and system testing.\"],[\"Validation\",\"Validation asks \\\"are we building the right product?\\\", e.g. user acceptance testing.\"],[\"Integration testing\",\"Integration testing checks interfaces between modules.\"],[\"Quality Assurance (QA)\",\"Correct. QA is process-focused and preventive; its examples are process audits and checklists.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Which is a DISADVANTAGE of black-box testing according to the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"The tester must understand the source code\",\"The opposite: not needing to understand the code is an advantage.\"],[\"It cannot test all possible inputs\",\"Correct. This is listed among the black-box disadvantages.\"],[\"Test cases take a long time to develop\",\"The lecture lists quick test-case development as an advantage.\"],[\"It cannot be done from the user's perspective\",\"Black-box testing is suitable for user-perspective (GUI) testing.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Code reviews and design checks, which ask \\\"Are we building the product right?\\\", are examples of:\",\"o\":[[\"Validation\",\"Wrong. Validation asks \\\"Are we building the right product?\\\" (user acceptance testing).\"],[\"Quality Control (QC)\",\"Wrong. QC is product-focused and corrective, e.g. unit and system testing.\"],[\"Verification\",\"Correct. Verification: building the product right (code reviews, design checks). (L2, Software testing terms)\"],[\"Acceptance Testing\",\"Wrong. Acceptance testing is performed by the user/customer on the product.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: QA is product-focused and corrective, while QC is process-focused and preventive.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong. The two are swapped.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. QA is process-focused and preventive (audits, checklists); QC is product-focused and corrective (unit and system testing). (L2, Software testing terms)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Invalid password → 'Error' message\\\" lists an input, conditions and an expected output. In the lecture's table this is a:\",\"o\":[[\"Test plan\",\"Wrong. A test plan is a document with test objectives and scope.\"],[\"Test case\",\"Correct. A test case is input, conditions and expected output. (L2, Software testing terms)\"],[\"Bug / Defect\",\"Wrong. A bug is a flaw causing unintended results, like a login button not working.\"],[\"Validation\",\"Wrong. Validation checks we are building the right product for the user.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Checking that the payment module connects correctly to the billing module is:\",\"o\":[[\"Integration testing\",\"Correct. Integration testing tests the interfaces between modules (payment to billing). (L2, Software testing terms)\"],[\"Unit testing\",\"Wrong. Unit testing tests the smallest testable component, a single function or module.\"],[\"System testing\",\"Wrong. System testing tests the complete system behaviour against the specifications.\"],[\"Acceptance testing\",\"Wrong. Acceptance testing is performed by the user/customer.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Black-box testing is also called:\",\"o\":[[\"Structural testing\",\"Wrong. The lecture never calls it structural; it focuses on functionality without the internal logic.\"],[\"Static testing\",\"Wrong. Static testing is examining something that is not running; black-box can be static or dynamic.\"],[\"Unit testing\",\"Wrong. Unit testing is a test level for the smallest component.\"],[\"Behavioral testing\",\"Correct. Black-box (functional) testing is also called behavioral testing. (L2, Black-box testing)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: In the ATM example, the black-box tester checks password masking and menu navigation through the GUI without knowing the internal logic.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The tester acts as a customer and checks functions through the GUI only. (L2, Black-box testing)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. This is exactly the lecture's ATM example.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: Static testing means running and using the software with data.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong. Running and using the software is dynamic testing.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Static testing is testing something that is not running (examining and reviewing it). (L2, Black-box testing)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Dynamic black-box testing, done from the customer's view without knowing the code, is also called:\",\"o\":[[\"Static testing\",\"Wrong. Static testing does not execute the software.\"],[\"Exploratory testing\",\"Correct. Dynamic black-box is also called exploratory (behavioral) testing. (L2, Static vs dynamic black-box)\"],[\"State testing\",\"Wrong. State testing checks the program's states and transitions; it is one technique, not another name.\"],[\"Verification\",\"Wrong. Verification asks whether we build the product right, e.g. code reviews.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Test-to-pass is described in the lecture as:\",\"o\":[[\"Forcing errors with abnormal cases\",\"Wrong. That is test-to-fail.\"],[\"Reviewing the user manual without running the software\",\"Wrong. That is static black-box testing.\"],[\"\\\"Kindness dealing\\\": assuring the software minimally works with normal cases\",\"Correct. Test-to-pass uses normal cases, boundaries, sub-boundaries and default data. (L2, Test-to-pass vs test-to-fail)\"],[\"Visiting every state at least once\",\"Wrong. That is one of the ways to reduce states in state testing.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: Since test-to-pass uses only normal input, it can never reveal bugs.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong. The lecture warns not to be surprised to find bugs even in test-to-pass.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. \\\"Use test-to-pass to reveal bugs before you test-to-fail\\\"; bugs can appear even in test-to-pass. (L2, Test-to-pass vs test-to-fail)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"An equivalence class (partition) is:\",\"o\":[[\"A set of test cases that tests the same thing or reveals the same bug\",\"Correct. So testing one case from each class is enough. (L2, Equivalence partitioning)\"],[\"A situation at the edge of the planned operational limits\",\"Wrong. That is the definition of a boundary condition.\"],[\"The condition or mode the software is currently in\",\"Wrong. That is a software state.\"],[\"A document with test objectives and scope\",\"Wrong. That is a test plan.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: Equivalence partitioning reduces the number of test cases without lowering test quality.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. It methodically reduces the huge set of test cases to a much smaller one while still ensuring good coverage. (L2, Equivalence partitioning)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. The lecture states exactly this benefit.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A program accepts <b>2 to 8</b> inputs. Following the lecture's \\\"4 to 10 inputs\\\" example, which numbers of inputs should be tested?\",\"o\":[[\"2 and 8 only\",\"Wrong. This tests only the valid edges and misses both invalid classes and a middle value.\"],[\"0, 2, 8, 10\",\"Wrong. The invalid tests should be just outside the range (1 and 9), and a middle valid value is missing.\"],[\"1, 5, 9\",\"Wrong. One per class is not what the example does; it also tests the valid boundaries 2 and 8.\"],[\"1, 2, 5, 8, 9\",\"Correct. Like 3, 4, 7, 10, 11: one below (invalid), the lower edge, a middle value, the upper edge (valid), one above (invalid). (L2, EP program example)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A field accepts a 4-digit integer from <b>1000 to 9999</b>. Following the lecture's 6-test-case method, which set is correct?\",\"o\":[[\"1000, 5000, 9999\",\"Wrong. These are only the valid tests; the special value and both invalid boundaries are missing.\"],[\"0999, 1000, 9999, 10000\",\"Wrong. The special value 0000 and a valid mid value are missing.\"],[\"0000, 0999, 1000, 5000, 9999, 10000\",\"Correct. Special value, invalid left, valid left, valid mid, valid right, invalid right, as in 00000, 09999, 10000, 50000, 99999, 100000. (L2, EP input values)\"],[\"0000, 1001, 5000, 9998, 10000\",\"Wrong. The boundaries are the edge values 1000 and 9999, and the invalid left 0999 is missing.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In the lecture's 10,000 to 99,999 example, the test case <b>00000</b> is the:\",\"o\":[[\"Invalid left boundary\",\"Wrong. The invalid left boundary is 09999.\"],[\"Invalid special value\",\"Correct. 00000 is the invalid special value. (L2, EP input values)\"],[\"Valid left boundary\",\"Wrong. The valid left boundary is 10000.\"],[\"Valid mid value\",\"Wrong. The valid mid value is 50000.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A print dialog allows 1 up to a maximum of 16 pages per sheet. Following the lecture's boundary example, which values should be tested?\",\"o\":[[\"0, 1, 16, 17\",\"Correct. Test 1, the maximum, 0 and max+1. (L2, Boundary examples)\"],[\"1 and 16 only\",\"Wrong. This misses the invalid values just outside the limits, 0 and 17.\"],[\"8 only\",\"Wrong. A middle value alone tests no boundary at all.\"],[\"−1, 0, 17, 18\",\"Wrong. These are all invalid; the valid edges 1 and 16 are missing.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Boundary conditions are:\",\"o\":[[\"Test cases that produce similar results\",\"Wrong. That is the objective of equivalence partitioning.\"],[\"The inputs that move the software from one state to another\",\"Wrong. Those are state transitions.\"],[\"Documents describing test objectives and scope\",\"Wrong. That is a test plan.\"],[\"Situations at the edge of the planned operational limits of the software\",\"Correct. Like the edge of a cliff; bugs increase at the boundaries. (L2, Boundary conditions)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Software consists of data and program. Which of the following belongs to the <b>program</b> part?\",\"o\":[[\"Keyboard input\",\"Wrong. Keyboard input is listed as data.\"],[\"Mouse clicks\",\"Wrong. Mouse clicks are listed as data.\"],[\"Executable flow, transitions, logic and computations\",\"Correct. The program part is the executable flow, transitions, logic and computations. (L2, Data testing)\"],[\"Disk files and printouts\",\"Wrong. Disk files and printouts are listed as data.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT listed in the lecture as a basis for partitioning data to reduce test cases?\",\"o\":[[\"Boundary conditions\",\"Wrong. Boundary conditions are listed.\"],[\"The most common state-to-state transitions\",\"Correct. That is a way to reduce state testing, not a data partition basis (boundary, sub-boundary, nulls, bad data). (L2, Data testing)\"],[\"Nulls\",\"Wrong. Nulls are listed.\"],[\"Bad data\",\"Wrong. Bad data is listed.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: In Paint, the pencil state and the airbrush state are examples of software states.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. A software state is the condition or mode the software is in, e.g. Paint's pencil vs airbrush state. (L2, State testing)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. The lecture uses exactly this example.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT part of Paint's startup state in the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"The airbrush tool is selected\",\"Correct. At startup the pencil is selected, not the airbrush. (L2, State variables)\"],[\"The drawing area is blank\",\"Wrong. A blank drawing area is part of the startup state.\"],[\"Colors are black on white\",\"Wrong. Black on white is part of the startup state.\"],[\"The document is named \\\"untitled\\\"\",\"Wrong. The document \\\"untitled\\\" is part of the startup state.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A state transition map should be drawn from the point of view of:\",\"o\":[[\"The programmer who wrote the code\",\"Wrong. The lecture says to create the map from the user's view.\"],[\"The database designer\",\"Wrong. Database design is not involved in the state map.\"],[\"The project manager\",\"Wrong. The manager's view is not mentioned.\"],[\"The user of the software\",\"Correct. \\\"Create your map from the user's view of the software.\\\" (L2, State testing)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Media player: you are in <b>FAST PLAY</b> and press <b>stop</b>. Which state do you go to?\",\"o\":[[\"OFF\",\"Wrong. stop leads to OFF only from PLAY and FAST FORWARD; from FAST PLAY it leads to PLAY.\"],[\"PLAY\",\"Correct. The table gives FAST PLAY + stop → PLAY. (L2, Media player)\"],[\"FAST FORWARD\",\"Wrong. FAST FORWARD is reached from FAST PLAY by fast forward, not stop.\"],[\"FAST PLAY\",\"Wrong. There is no stop self-loop on FAST PLAY.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Media player: starting in OFF, press <b>fast forward → play → fast forward → fast forward</b>. What is the final state?\",\"o\":[[\"PLAY\",\"Wrong. PLAY is reached after the second press; two more presses follow.\"],[\"FAST PLAY\",\"Wrong. FAST PLAY is reached after the third press; the last fast forward leaves it.\"],[\"FAST FORWARD\",\"Correct. OFF →ff→ FAST FORWARD →play→ PLAY →ff→ FAST PLAY →ff→ FAST FORWARD. (L2, Media player)\"],[\"OFF\",\"Wrong. No stop was pressed, so OFF is never reached after the start.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: In the media player, pressing play while in FAST FORWARD moves the player to FAST PLAY.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong. FAST FORWARD + play goes to PLAY; FAST PLAY is reached from PLAY by fast forward.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The table gives FAST FORWARD + play → PLAY. (L2, Media player)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the media player example, how many test cases does the lecture need to cover all 8 transitions at least once?\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"Wrong. One test case is enough only to visit every state, not every transition.\"],[\"2\",\"Correct. TC1 and TC2 together cover all 8 transitions. (L2, Media player answers)\"],[\"4\",\"Wrong. There are 4 states, but the transitions are covered with 2 test cases.\"],[\"8\",\"Wrong. 8 is the number of transitions, not the number of test cases needed.\"]],\"a\":1}]},{\"n\":3,\"title\":\"White Box Testing\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"What white-box testing does\",\"pts\":[\"Unit outline:<br>(1) <b>Static white-box</b>: examining design and code, formal review, coding standards and guidelines, code review checklist.<br>(2) <b>Dynamic white-box</b>: vs debugging, testing the pieces, data coverage, code coverage.\",\"White-box testing helps a tester:<br>(1) test <b>independent paths</b> within a unit or module<br>(2) test <b>logical correctness</b> (both the true and false conditions)<br>(3) test <b>loops</b>, specifically at their boundaries, and <b>check the operational boundary correctness</b><br>(4) test <b>internal data structures</b> to ensure their validity.\",\"Also called <b>glass box</b>, <b>structural</b>, <b>open box</b> or <b>clear box</b> testing, because it examines internal workings.\",\"<b>Static white-box</b> (examining the design and code) has 3 parts:<br>• <b>Formal Review</b> (peer review, walkthrough, inspection)<br>• <b>Coding Standards and Guidelines</b><br>• <b>Code Review Checklist</b>.\",\"Figure (Static White Box Testing tree): the top box <b>Static White Box Testing</b> has arrows down to three boxes: <b>Formal Review</b>, <b>Coding Standards and Guidelines</b> and <b>Code Review Checklist</b>.<br>From Formal Review, three arrows go to <b>Peer Review</b>, <b>Walkthrough</b> and <b>Inspection</b>.<br>A vertical label on the left reads \\\"Examining the Design and Code\\\".<br>Under the tree: \\\"For a successful formal review, 4 essential elements are required: 1- Identify Problems 2- Follow Rules 3- Prepare 4- Write a Report.\\\" The next slide repeats the same tree.\"],\"fig\":\"qa-static-wb\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-static-wb\":4},\"figH\":{\"qa-static-wb\":\"Static white-box testing: its three parts and the types of formal review\"}},{\"h\":\"Formal review\",\"pts\":[\"Formal meetings between programmers and testers to inspect the software's design and code.\",\"<b>4 essential elements</b>:<br>(1) <b>Identify problems</b> in design and code<br>(2) <b>Follow rules</b>: fixed set of rules, e.g. lines of code reviewed per day and time spent<br>(3) <b>Prepare</b>: every participant prepares and contributes<br>(4) <b>Write a report</b> summarizing results, made available to the development team.\",\"<b>Peer review</b>: a programmer who helped design the architecture/code, with other programmers or testers acting as reviewers.\",\"<b>Walkthrough</b>: the programmer who <b>wrote</b> the code presents it to a group of <b>5 or 6</b> programmers and testers. The presenter reads through the code line by line, or function by function, and <b>explains what each function and line of code means</b>. Relevant comments and queries are addressed during the walkthrough session.\",\"<b>Inspection</b>: the presenter is <b>not the real programmer</b>. Inspectors review the code from the <b>user's and tester's perspective</b>.\"]},{\"h\":\"Coding standards and guidelines\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Standards</b> are rules that <b>must be adhered to</b> (mandatory). <b>Guidelines</b> are instructions which <b>enable a person to follow a set of standards</b> (<b>not really mandatory</b>).\",\"<b>3 reasons</b> to follow them:<br>(1) <b>Reliability</b>: code written based on standards and guidelines is more reliable and secure than code that is not<br>(2) <b>Readability / Maintainability</b>: such code is easier to understand and maintain<br>(3) <b>Portability</b>: code must be portable enough to run on different hardware and different compilers.<br><b>When standards and guidelines are followed, it becomes easier for people to access the code.</b>\"]},{\"h\":\"Generic code review checklist\",\"table\":[[\"Error class\",\"Meaning\",\"What to check\"],[\"Data reference\",\"Variables, constants, arrays, strings, records not properly declared/initialized\",\"Uninitialized variables; subscripts within bounds; off-by-one errors; a variable used where a constant is better\"],[\"Data declaration\",\"Improper declaration of variables and constants\",\"Correct length/type/storage class; initialized properly and consistent with type; variables with similar names; declared but never used or used only once\"],[\"Computation\",\"Wrong calculations\",\"Mixed data types (int + float); same type different size (long + short); overflow mid-calculation; divisor can be zero\"],[\"Comparison\",\"Boundary conditions: &lt;, &gt;, =, ≠, true/false\",\"Using &lt; instead of &lt;=; correct Boolean operands (integer in a Boolean calculation)\"],[\"Control flow\",\"Improper behaviour of loops and control structures\",\"The slide lists no checks; it only says the errors come from improper behavior of loops and control structures in a language (‡ e.g. loops terminate, correct branching)\"],[\"Input/Output\",\"Reading files, keyboard/mouse input, writing to file or screen\",\"File and device handling\"]],\"pts\":[\"<b>Data reference errors</b> (slide wording): errors caused by the usage of variables, constants, arrays, strings or records which are not properly declared or initialized to use and refer to them. Check:<br>(a) if any un-initialized variables are referenced<br>(b) if the array and string subscript integer values are within the array's bounds or string dimension<br>(c) if there are any \\\"off-by-one\\\" errors in indexing operations or references to arrays<br>(d) if a variable is used where a constant would work better (<b>example: when checking the boundary of an array</b>).\",\"<b>Data declaration errors</b> occur due to improper declaration of variables and constants. Check:<br>(a) if the variables are assigned the correct length, type and storage class<br>(b) if a variable is <b>initialized at the time of declaration</b>, and also whether it is properly initialized and consistent with its type<br>(c) if there are any variables with similar names<br>(d) if there are any variables declared which are never referenced or just referenced once <b>(should be a constant)</b>.\",\"<b>Computation errors</b> appear due to errors in calculations, <b>where the expected results are not obtained due to false calculations</b>. Check:<br>(a) if any calculations use variables which have different data types (ex: adding integers and floating point numbers)<br>(b) if any calculations use variables which have the same data type but vary in size (ex: adding long integers to short integers)<br>(c) if overflow in the middle of a numeric calculation is possible<br>(d) if it is ever possible for a divisor to be zero.\",\"<b>Comparison errors</b> occur during boundary conditions such as 'less than, greater than, equal, not equal, and true or false'. Check:<br>(a) if the comparison is correct (EX: using &lt; instead of &lt;=)<br>(b) whether the operands of a Boolean operator are correct (EX: checking if an integer variable containing integer values is used in a Boolean calculation).\",\"• <b>Control flow errors</b> appear due to improper behavior of loops and control structures in a language.<br>• <b>Input/output errors</b> relate to errors such as reading from a file, accepting input from a keyboard or mouse, and writing to an output device such as a file or screen.\"]},{\"h\":\"Note on the \\\"zero is true\\\" slide\",\"pts\":[\"The Comparison Errors slide says: \\\"In the C language, zero is considered as true and non-zero is considered as false.\\\" The summary MCQ repeats it with answer \\\"C language\\\".\",\"<b>In real C it is the opposite</b>: 0 is false and any non-zero value is true. If the exam repeats the slide sentence, choose \\\"C language\\\". The slide's point is that mixing integers and Booleans is a source of comparison errors.\"]},{\"h\":\"Dynamic white-box testing\",\"pts\":[\"A <b>validation check</b>: running test cases with input values to ensure the application works according to the spec throughout execution.\",\"It covers:<br>(a) testing low-level functions, procedures, subroutines or libraries directly<br>(b) testing the whole program at the top level, adjusting test cases based on what we know of its operation<br>(c) reading variables and state information to check the tests do what they were designed to do, and forcing the software to do things that are hard to test normally.\",\"<b>Dynamic white-box testing vs debugging</b>: testing <b>finds</b> bugs; debugging <b>fixes</b> the bugs found during testing.\",\"<b>Testing the pieces</b> = unit and integration testing.<br>Integration testing strategies (figure): <b>Bottom-up</b>, <b>Top-down</b>, <b>Umbrella approach</b>.\",\"Figure (Unit and Integration Testing): a box <b>Integration testing</b> with a line that splits into three arrows down to <b>Bottom Up Testing</b>, <b>Top Down Testing</b> and <b>Umbrella Approach Testing</b>.\"],\"fig\":\"qa-integration\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-integration\":4},\"figH\":{\"qa-integration\":\"Integration testing: bottom-up, top-down and umbrella\"}},{\"h\":\"Data coverage and code coverage\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Data coverage</b>: data tracked completely through the software (values stored in variables, constants, arrays and data structures; keyboard and mouse input, files, screen input and output, and data sent to modems, networks and so on).<br>In the data coverage method, the code is divided into <b>data and states</b>, <b>relating to program flow</b>, which is similar to black-box testing, so it is easy to map white-box and black-box cases.<br>Types: <b>Data flow</b>, <b>Sub-boundaries</b>, <b>Error forcing</b>.\",\"<b>Code coverage</b> has 3 types: <b>Statement coverage</b>, <b>Path coverage</b> (branch), <b>Condition coverage</b>.\",\"Figure (4.2.4 Code Coverage): a box <b>Code Coverage</b> with one arrow that splits into three arrows to <b>Statement Coverage</b>, <b>Path Coverage</b> and <b>Condition Coverage</b>. The same figure appears on the statement coverage and path coverage slides.\",\"<b>Statement coverage</b>: every statement runs at least once. Example: 4 sequential PRINT lines, so one test runs all of them.\",\"Statement coverage code:<br><code>1: PRINT \\\"Good Morning\\\"</code><br><code>2: PRINT \\\"Be Positive\\\"</code><br><code>3: PRINT \\\"Today is a Great Day\\\"</code><br><code>4: END</code>\",\"<b>Path coverage</b>: every path (both branches of every IF). Example: IF Date$ = \\\"25-12-2008\\\" → PRINT \\\"MERRY CHRISTMAS\\\". 2 tests: Date = 25-12-2008 runs 1,2,3,4,5,6,7; any other date runs 1,2,5,6,7.\",\"Path coverage code:<br><code>1: PRINT \\\"Good Morning\\\"</code><br><code>2: IF Date$ = \\\"25-12-2008\\\" then</code><br><code>3: PRINT \\\"MERRY CHRISTMAS\\\"</code><br><code>4: END IF</code><br><code>5: PRINT \\\"The date id:\\\"; Date$</code><br><code>6: PRINT \\\"The time:\\\"; Time$</code><br><code>7: END</code>\"],\"fig\":\"qa-code-coverage\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-code-coverage\":2},\"figH\":{\"qa-code-coverage\":\"The three types of code coverage: statement, path and condition\"}},{\"h\":\"Condition coverage worked example\",\"code\":\"1: PRINT \\\"Good Morning\\\"\\n2: IF Date$ = \\\"25-12-2008\\\" AND Time$ = \\\"00:00:00\\\" THEN\\n3:    PRINT \\\"MERRY CHRISTMAS\\\"\\n4: END IF\\n5: PRINT \\\"The date is: \\\"; Date$\\n6: PRINT \\\"The time is: \\\"; Time$\\n7: END\",\"fig\":\"qa-condition-coverage\",\"figH\":{\"qa-condition-coverage\":\"Try it: run the four test cases for full condition coverage\"}},{\"h\":\"Test cases for full condition coverage\",\"table\":[[\"Date$\",\"Time$\",\"Lines executed\"],[\"25-12-2007\",\"11:11:11\",\"1, 2, 5, 6, 7\"],[\"25-12-2007\",\"00:00:00\",\"1, 2, 5, 6, 7\"],[\"25-12-2008\",\"11:11:11\",\"1, 2, 5, 6, 7\"],[\"25-12-2008\",\"00:00:00\",\"1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7\"]]},{\"h\":\"Why condition coverage needs more cases\",\"pts\":[\"Branch coverage needs only 2 tests (whole IF true once, false once). For <b>full condition coverage</b> (the slide's table is titled \\\"Test cases for a Full Condition Coverage\\\"), the lecture tests <b>every combination</b> of the two sub-conditions (Date part true/false with Time part true/false), giving the <b>4 test cases</b> in the table; only the last one (25-12-2008, 00:00:00) runs line 3.\",\"The exercise at the end (triangle problem) is solved in Lecture 4.\"]},{\"h\":\"Exercise: triangle problem\",\"pts\":[\"Exercise: Apply black box and white box on the triangle problem.\",\"Show <b>Function testing</b>, <b>statement</b>, <b>Branch or condition</b> and <b>Path</b> coverage of the triangle problem.\",\"Pseudocode:<br><code>read a,b,c;</code><br><code>type = \\\"scalene\\\";</code><br><code>if (a == b or a == c or b == c) type = \\\"isosceles\\\";</code><br><code>if (a == b and a == c) type = \\\"equilateral\\\";</code><br><code>if (a &gt;= b+c or b &gt;= a+c or c &gt;= a+b) type = \\\"not a triangle\\\";</code><br><code>if (a &lt;= 0 or b &lt;= 0 or c &lt;= 0) type = \\\"bad inputs\\\";</code><br><code>print type</code>\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Other names for white-box testing\",\"Glass box, structural, open box, clear box testing.\"],[\"3 parts of static white-box testing\",\"Formal review, coding standards and guidelines, code review checklist.\"],[\"4 essential elements of a formal review\",\"Identify problems, follow rules, prepare, write a report.\"],[\"3 types of formal review\",\"Peer review, walkthrough, inspection.\"],[\"Walkthrough\",\"The author presents the code line by line to a group of 5 or 6 programmers and testers.\"],[\"Inspection\",\"The presenter is not the author; inspectors review from the user's and tester's view.\"],[\"Standards vs guidelines\",\"Standards are mandatory rules; guidelines are instructions that help follow them and are not really mandatory.\"],[\"3 reasons for standards and guidelines\",\"Reliability, readability/maintainability, portability.\"],[\"Adding long integers to short integers\",\"A computation error.\"],[\"Using < instead of <=\",\"A comparison error.\"],[\"Off-by-one indexing\",\"A data reference error.\"],[\"Dynamic white-box testing vs debugging\",\"Testing finds bugs; debugging fixes them.\"],[\"Integration testing approaches (figure)\",\"Bottom-up, top-down, umbrella approach.\"],[\"Types of data coverage\",\"Data flow, sub-boundaries, error forcing.\"],[\"Types of code coverage\",\"Statement, path (branch), condition coverage.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Differentiate between peer review, walkthrough and inspection.\",\"Peer review: a programmer involved in the design or code, with other programmers or testers as reviewers. Walkthrough: the author presents the code line by line to 5 or 6 programmers and testers and answers questions. Inspection: someone other than the author presents; inspectors review from the user's and tester's perspective. It is the most formal.\"],[\"What are the six classes in the generic code review checklist? Give an example of each.\",\"Data reference (uninitialized variable, off-by-one); data declaration (wrong type or length, unused variable); computation (int plus float, long plus short, overflow, divide by zero); comparison (< instead of <=); control flow (improper loop behaviour); input/output (file read, keyboard input, writing to screen).\"],[\"Are debugging and dynamic white-box testing the same?\",\"No. Dynamic white-box testing runs test cases with inputs to find bugs, as a validation check. Debugging fixes the bugs found during testing. Both use knowledge of the code.\"],[\"Write test cases for full condition coverage of: IF Date$ = \\\"25-12-2008\\\" AND Time$ = \\\"00:00:00\\\" THEN PRINT \\\"MERRY CHRISTMAS\\\".\",\"(25-12-2007, 11:11:11) runs 1,2,5,6,7<br>(25-12-2007, 00:00:00) runs 1,2,5,6,7<br>(25-12-2008, 11:11:11) runs 1,2,5,6,7<br>(25-12-2008, 00:00:00) runs 1,2,3,4,5,6,7.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"Which of the following stages do inspections come under?\",\"o\":[[\"Formal review\",\"Correct. Formal review = peer review, walkthrough and inspection.\"],[\"Peer review\",\"Peer review is a sibling of inspection, not its parent.\"],[\"Code coverage\",\"Code coverage (statement, path, condition) belongs to dynamic white-box testing, which runs the code; inspections are a static formal review.\"],[\"Data coverage\",\"Data coverage (data flow, sub-boundaries, error forcing) is part of dynamic white-box testing; inspection is one of the three formal reviews.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"From the following, select the choice which is appropriate to standards and guidelines.\",\"o\":[[\"Scalability\",\"Scalability is not one of the 3 reasons for following standards and guidelines, which are reliability, readability/maintainability and portability (L3).\"],[\"Reliability\",\"Correct. The reasons are reliability, readability/maintainability and portability.\"],[\"Usability\",\"Usability is a user-facing quality, not one of the 3 reasons for standards and guidelines: reliability, readability/maintainability, portability (L3).\"],[\"Compatibility\",\"Not among the 3 reasons (portability is, compatibility is not).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"What are instructions to be followed which are <b>not really mandatory</b>?\",\"o\":[[\"Standards and guidelines\",\"This pairs guidelines with standards, but standards are rules that must be adhered to, so they are mandatory; only guidelines are not.\"],[\"Standards\",\"Standards are the mandatory rules that must be adhered to; the instructions that are not really mandatory are guidelines (L3).\"],[\"Guidelines\",\"Correct. Guidelines help you follow the standards but are not mandatory.\"],[\"Not all\",\"The lecture names one category exactly: guidelines are instructions that help follow the standards and are not really mandatory.\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"What are instructions to be followed which are <b>mandatory</b>?\",\"o\":[[\"Guidelines\",\"Guidelines only help a person follow the standards and are not really mandatory; the mandatory rules are the standards (L3).\"],[\"Standards\",\"Correct. Standards are rules that must be adhered to.\"],[\"Standards and guidelines\",\"Only standards are mandatory; guidelines are not really mandatory, so naming both is wrong (L3, Coding standards and guidelines).\"],[\"Not all\",\"The lecture gives a direct answer: standards are rules that must be adhered to, i.e. mandatory.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Formal review, coding standards and guidelines, and code review are phases of:\",\"o\":[[\"Static white box\",\"Correct. These are the three parts of static white-box testing.\"],[\"Dynamic white box\",\"Dynamic white-box covers testing the pieces, data coverage and code coverage.\"],[\"Static black box\",\"Static black-box testing reviews the specification (e.g. user manual) without code knowledge; formal review and code review examine the code itself.\"],[\"Dynamic black box\",\"Dynamic black-box runs the software without code knowledge.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"if (X&gt;1) and (Y=0) then A=A/X; the statement-coverage test case is:\",\"o\":[[\"All answers\",\"With AND both parts must be true; X=3,y=1 and X=2,y=1 have Y≠0 and skip A=A/X, so not all options work.\"],[\"X=3, y=1\",\"Y=1 makes (Y=0) false, so the AND condition is false and A=A/X never executes; statement coverage needs every statement run.\"],[\"X=2, y=1\",\"X&gt;1 holds but Y=1 makes (Y=0) false, so the AND fails and the statement A=A/X is skipped.\"],[\"X=2, y=0\",\"Correct. X&gt;1 and Y=0 are both true, so A=A/X runs and every statement executes.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"if (X=2) or (y&gt;1) then A=A+1; the statement-coverage test cases are:\",\"o\":[[\"X=2, y=0\",\"This one works (X=2 makes the OR true, so A=A+1 runs), but it is not the only one: the other two also make the condition true, so \\\"All answers\\\" is the full choice.\"],[\"X=2, y=2\",\"This works (both X=2 and y&gt;1 are true), but each of the other cases also runs A=A+1, so \\\"All answers\\\" is the right choice.\"],[\"X=1, y=2\",\"This works (y=2 &gt; 1 makes the OR true), but the other cases also run A=A+1, so \\\"All answers\\\" is the right choice.\"],[\"All answers\",\"Correct. With OR, each case makes the condition true, so A=A+1 executes in all three.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"if (X&gt;1) and (Y=0) then A=A/X; if (X=2) or (A&gt;1) then A=A+1; the statement-coverage test case is:\",\"o\":[[\"X=3, Y=0, A=1\",\"First IF true: A=1/3. Second: X≠2 and A=0.33 is not &gt;1, so A=A+1 is skipped.\"],[\"X=0, Y=0, A=4\",\"First IF false (X&gt;1 fails), so A=A/X is skipped.\"],[\"All answers\",\"Not all cases run both statements: X=0 skips A=A/X, and X=3,Y=0,A=1 gives A=1/3, so A=A+1 is skipped. Only X=2,Y=0,A=4 runs both.\"],[\"X=2, Y=0, A=4\",\"Correct. First IF true: A=4/2=2. Second IF: X=2 true, so A=3. Both statements run.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"The process of fixing the bugs found during the testing phase is called:\",\"o\":[[\"Static black box testing\",\"Static black-box testing reviews the specification without running the software; it neither runs tests nor fixes bugs.\"],[\"Debugging\",\"Correct. Testing finds bugs; debugging fixes them.\"],[\"Dynamic black box testing\",\"Dynamic black-box testing runs the software to FIND bugs without code knowledge; fixing the bugs found is debugging (L3).\"],[\"Dynamic white box testing\",\"Dynamic white-box testing finds bugs using code knowledge; the lecture separates it from debugging, which fixes them (L3).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"Adding long integers to short integers will cause:\",\"o\":[[\"Control flow errors\",\"Control flow errors are about loops and control structures behaving wrongly (e.g. loops not terminating), not mixing data sizes in a calculation.\"],[\"Computation errors\",\"Correct. Same type but different sizes is a computation-error check.\"],[\"Data reference errors\",\"Those concern uninitialized variables and array bounds.\"],[\"Comparison errors\",\"Comparison errors are about boundary operators (&lt; vs &lt;=) and Boolean operands; adding long to short integers is a computation error.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"The umbrella approach is a type of:\",\"o\":[[\"Integration testing\",\"Correct. The figure shows bottom-up, top-down and umbrella under integration testing.\"],[\"Top-down testing\",\"Top-down is one of the three integration approaches, alongside umbrella; umbrella is not a kind of top-down, both fall under integration testing.\"],[\"Bottom-up testing\",\"Bottom-up is one of the three integration approaches, alongside umbrella; umbrella falls under integration testing, not under bottom-up.\"],[\"Static testing\",\"Integration testing runs the combined pieces, part of dynamic white-box testing (testing the pieces), so umbrella is not static testing.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"A validation check that runs test cases with input values and uses knowledge of the code is:\",\"o\":[[\"Static black box testing\",\"Static black-box testing neither runs the software nor uses the code; it reviews the specification.\"],[\"Dynamic white box testing\",\"Correct. The lecture defines dynamic white-box testing as a validation check.\"],[\"Dynamic black box testing\",\"Dynamic black-box testing runs the software but without knowing the code; using code knowledge makes it white-box.\"],[\"Formal review\",\"A formal review is static white-box testing: people examine the design and code in a meeting without running test cases.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"\\\"Zero is considered as true and non-zero as false\\\" — the slide attributes this to which language?\",\"o\":[[\"Java\",\"The slide does not name Java; its Comparison Errors sentence names C. (In fact Java does not treat integers as Booleans at all.)\"],[\"C++\",\"The slide names only C, not C++. (In real C and C++ zero is false and non-zero is true, the opposite of the slide.)\"],[\"All the above\",\"The slide attributes the statement to C alone, so \\\"All the above\\\" does not match the slide's answer.\"],[\"C\",\"This is the slide/summary answer. Careful: in real C it is the opposite (0 = false, non-zero = true). Pick \\\"C\\\" only if the exam repeats the slide wording.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"In a walkthrough, who presents the code?\",\"o\":[[\"An independent inspector\",\"An independent presenter who is not the author is an inspection; in a walkthrough the author presents (L3, Formal review).\"],[\"The programmer who developed the code\",\"Correct. The author presents it to a team of 5 or 6.\"],[\"The customer\",\"Walkthroughs are internal meetings of programmers and testers; the customer is not there, and the author presents the code.\"],[\"The project manager\",\"The lecture names the presenter as the programmer who wrote the code, presenting to 5 or 6 programmers and testers, not a manager.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"The reason for adhering to standards and guidelines are\",\"o\":[[\"Portability\",\"A real reason, but only one of the three; reliability and maintainability are also listed (L3).\"],[\"Reliability\",\"A real reason, but only one of the three (L3).\"],[\"Maintainability\",\"A real reason (readability/maintainability), but only one of the three (L3).\"],[\"All answers\",\"Correct. Lecture 3 gives 3 reasons to follow coding standards and guidelines: (1) reliability, more reliable and secure code; (2) readability/maintainability; (3) portability, running on different hardware and compilers (L3, Coding standards and guidelines).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Code coverage have the next type\",\"o\":[[\"All answers\",\"Correct. Lecture 3: \\\"Code coverage has 3 types: Statement coverage, Path coverage (branch), Condition coverage.\\\" Condition coverage is on the slide, branch coverage is the slide's name for path coverage, and line coverage is the usual other name for statement coverage (every line runs at least once) (L3, Data coverage and code coverage).\"],[\"Condition coverage\",\"A real type, but only one of the three (L3).\"],[\"Line coverage\",\"A real type (statement coverage), but only one of the three (L3).\"],[\"Branch coverage\",\"A real type (path coverage), but only one of the three (L3).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following stages do inspections come under?\",\"o\":[[\"Code coverage\",\"Code coverage (statement, path, condition) is dynamic white-box testing, which runs the code; an inspection is a static review (L3).\"],[\"Data coverage\",\"Data coverage (data flow, sub-boundaries, error forcing) is dynamic white-box testing (L3).\"],[\"Formal review\",\"Correct. Lecture 3: static white-box testing splits into Formal Review, Coding Standards and Guidelines, and Code Review Checklist, and Formal Review branches into Peer Review, Walkthrough and Inspection (L3, Static white-box figure).\"],[\"Peer review\",\"Peer review is a sibling of inspection, one of the three formal reviews, not the stage above it (L3).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"What are instructions to be followed which are not really mandatory.\",\"o\":[[\"None of the above\",\"Guidelines fit the definition exactly (L3).\"],[\"Standards\",\"The opposite: standards are the mandatory rules (L3).\"],[\"Standards and Guidelines\",\"Standards are mandatory, so the pair does not fit \\\"not really mandatory\\\" (L3).\"],[\"Guidelines\",\"Correct. Lecture 3: \\\"Standards are rules that must be adhered to (mandatory). Guidelines are instructions which enable a person to follow a set of standards (not really mandatory).\\\"\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The meeting for inspection of the software's design and code is called\",\"o\":[[\"All the above\",\"Only formal review matches the definition (L3).\"],[\"Code review\",\"In L3, \\\"code review\\\" is the Code Review Checklist, a list of error types, not the meeting; and the meeting covers the design too.\"],[\"Data review\",\"Not a lecture term; data coverage is dynamic testing, not a meeting.\"],[\"Formal review\",\"Correct. Lecture 3 defines formal review as \\\"formal meetings between programmers and testers to inspect the software's design and code\\\" (L3, Formal review).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Data coverage have the next type\",\"o\":[[\"sub-boundaries\",\"A real type, but only one of the three (L3).\"],[\"Data flow\",\"A real type, but only one of the three (L3).\"],[\"Error forcing\",\"A real type, but only one of the three (L3).\"],[\"All answers\",\"Correct. Lecture 3: data coverage types are \\\"Data flow, Sub-boundaries, Error forcing.\\\" All three options are on the list (L3, Data coverage and code coverage).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"if(X=2) or (y&gt;1) then A=A+1; the statement coverage test cases are\",\"o\":[[\"X=2, y=0\",\"Works (X=2 makes the OR true), but so do the other two.\"],[\"X=1, y=2\",\"Works (y=2 > 1 makes the OR true), but so do the other two.\"],[\"X=2, y=2\",\"Works (both parts true), but so do the other two.\"],[\"All answers\",\"Correct. Statement coverage means every statement runs at least once (L3); the only statement is A=A+1, so the OR condition must be true. X=2,y=0 (X=2 true), X=1,y=2 (y>1 true) and X=2,y=2 (both true) each run it. The 2025/26 final had the same question, keyed \\\"All answers\\\".\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a strategy of integration testing?\",\"o\":[[\"Bottom-up testing\",\"Correct. Lecture 3: \\\"Integration testing strategies: Bottom-up, Top-down, Umbrella approach.\\\" In the figure, Integration testing splits into Bottom Up, Top Down and Umbrella Approach Testing (L3, Testing the pieces).\"],[\"Black box\",\"Black box is a testing approach (from the spec, without the code; L2), not an integration strategy.\"],[\"Bing Bang testing\",\"Not in the lecture's list. (Big Bang, putting all modules together at once, is a textbook integration strategy, but not on the slides.)\"],[\"Static white box testing\",\"Static white-box reviews design and code without running it; integration testing runs the combined pieces (L3).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Umbrella approach is a type of\",\"o\":[[\"Bottom up testing\",\"A sibling of the umbrella approach, not its parent (L3).\"],[\"Static testing\",\"Static testing does not run the code; integration testing runs the combined modules (L2, L3).\"],[\"Integration Testing\",\"Correct. Lecture 3's figure: Integration testing splits into Bottom Up Testing, Top Down Testing and Umbrella Approach Testing (L3, Testing the pieces).\"],[\"Top down testing\",\"A sibling of the umbrella approach, not its parent (L3).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The errors that caused due to the variables, constants, and strings are related to\",\"o\":[[\"Comparison Errors\",\"Comparison errors happen at boundary conditions (&lt; vs &lt;=, wrong Boolean operands) (L3).\"],[\"Control Flow Errors\",\"Control flow errors are loops and control structures behaving wrongly (L3).\"],[\"Computation Errors\",\"Computation errors are wrong calculations, e.g. mixing data types (L3).\"],[\"Data Reference Errors\",\"Correct. Lecture 3, Code Review Checklist: data reference errors are \\\"errors caused by the usage of variables, constants, arrays, strings or records which are not properly declared or initialized\\\" (e.g. uninitialized variables, subscripts out of bounds, off-by-one indexing).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Adding long integers to short integers will cause\",\"o\":[[\"Comparison Errors\",\"Comparison errors are about comparison operators and Boolean operands, not arithmetic (L3).\"],[\"Computation Errors\",\"Correct. Lecture 3, Code Review Checklist, computation errors check (b): calculations using variables of \\\"the same data type but vary in size (ex: adding long integers to short integers).\\\"\"],[\"Data Reference Errors\",\"Data reference errors are about undeclared or uninitialized variables and out-of-bounds indexes (L3).\"],[\"Control Flow Errors\",\"Control flow errors are about loops and control structures (L3).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"if (X&gt;1) and (Y=0) then A=A/X; if(X=2) or (A&gt;1) then A=A+1;the statement coverage test cases are\",\"o\":[[\"X=0,Y=0, A=4\",\"X=0 makes X>1 false, so A=A/X is skipped.\"],[\"X=2,Y=0, A=4\",\"Correct. Statement coverage needs both A=A/X and A=A+1 to run (L3). X=2,Y=0,A=4: X>1 and Y=0 are true, so A = 4/2 = 2; then X=2 is true, so A = 3. Both statements run. The 2025/26 final keyed the same question this way.\"],[\"All the above\",\"The other two each skip a statement.\"],[\"X=3,Y=0, A=1\",\"A = 1/3 ≈ 0.33; then X=2 is false and A>1 is false, so A=A+1 is skipped.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Formal review, Coding standard and guidelines, and Code review are phases of\",\"o\":[[\"Dynamic white box testing\",\"Dynamic white-box runs the code: testing the pieces, data coverage and code coverage (L3).\"],[\"Dynamic Black box testing\",\"Dynamic black-box runs the software without seeing the code, e.g. equivalence partitioning and boundary values (L2).\"],[\"Static Black box testing\",\"Static black-box reviews the specification (L2); reviews and standards for the code are static white-box.\"],[\"Static white box testing\",\"Correct. Lecture 3: the Static White Box Testing tree splits into Formal Review, Coding Standards and Guidelines, and Code Review Checklist (\\\"Examining the Design and Code\\\").\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"From the following, select the choice which is appropriate to standards and guidelines\",\"o\":[[\"Scalability\",\"Not one of the three reasons (L3).\"],[\"Reliability\",\"Correct. Lecture 3: the 3 reasons for standards and guidelines are reliability, readability/maintainability and portability. Only reliability is among the options.\"],[\"Usability\",\"Not one of the three reasons; usability is a user-facing quality (L3).\"],[\"Compatibility\",\"Sounds like portability, but the slide's word is portability, and compatibility is not listed (L3).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"Which name is NOT another name for white-box testing?\",\"o\":[[\"Behavioral testing\",\"Correct. Behavioral testing is another name for BLACK-box testing.\"],[\"Glass box testing\",\"Listed as another name for white-box testing.\"],[\"Structural testing\",\"Listed as another name for white-box testing.\"],[\"Clear box testing\",\"Listed as another name for white-box testing.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT one of the 4 essential elements of a formal review?\",\"o\":[[\"Identify problems in the design and code\",\"Identifying problems in the design and code is element 1 of the 4 essential elements of a formal review, so it IS one of them.\"],[\"Follow rules, e.g. lines of code reviewed per day\",\"Following rules (e.g. lines of code reviewed per day, time spent) is element 2 of a formal review, so it IS one of them.\"],[\"Write a report summarizing the results\",\"Writing a report summarizing the results for the development team is element 4 of a formal review, so it IS one of them.\"],[\"Rewrite the faulty code during the meeting\",\"Correct. The elements are identify problems, follow rules, prepare, and write a report; fixing code is not one of them.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A team lead who did NOT write a module presents its code, and the reviewers examine it from the user's and tester's perspective. Which type of formal review is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Walkthrough\",\"In a walkthrough the programmer who WROTE the code presents it.\"],[\"Peer review\",\"A peer review involves a programmer who helped design the code, with other programmers or testers reviewing.\"],[\"Inspection\",\"Correct. In an inspection the presenter is not the real programmer and reviewers take the user's and tester's view.\"],[\"Code coverage\",\"Code coverage is dynamic white-box testing, not a review.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"During a code review you find a variable <code>tempTotal</code> that is declared but never used. Which error class from the checklist is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Computation errors\",\"Computation errors are about wrong calculations (mixed types, overflow, zero divisor).\"],[\"Comparison errors\",\"Comparison errors are about &lt;, &gt;, =, ≠ and Boolean operands.\"],[\"Data declaration errors\",\"Correct. \\\"Declared but never used or used only once\\\" is a data declaration check.\"],[\"Input/Output errors\",\"I/O errors concern files, keyboard/mouse input and writing to file or screen.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"A reviewer notices a <code>while</code> loop whose exit condition can never become true. Which error class is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Data reference errors\",\"Those are uninitialized variables, out-of-bounds subscripts and off-by-one errors.\"],[\"Data declaration errors\",\"Those are about declaring variables and constants with the right type, length and initial value.\"],[\"Input/Output errors\",\"Those concern reading and writing files and devices.\"],[\"Control flow errors\",\"Correct. Control flow checks that loops terminate and branching is correct.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT one of the types of data coverage listed in the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"Statement coverage\",\"Correct. Statement coverage is a type of CODE coverage.\"],[\"Data flow\",\"Data flow is one of the three types of data coverage (data flow, sub-boundaries, error forcing), so it IS listed (L3).\"],[\"Sub-boundaries\",\"Sub-boundaries is one of the three types of data coverage, so it IS listed; statement coverage is code coverage.\"],[\"Error forcing\",\"Error forcing is one of the three types of data coverage, so it IS listed; statement coverage belongs to code coverage.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"<pre>1: READ X\\n2: IF X &gt; 10 THEN\\n3:    PRINT \\\"Big\\\"\\n4: END IF\\n5: PRINT \\\"Done\\\"</pre>What is the minimum number of test cases for <b>statement</b> coverage and for <b>path (branch)</b> coverage, respectively?\",\"o\":[[\"2 and 2\",\"One test with X &gt; 10 already runs every statement, so statement coverage needs only 1.\"],[\"1 and 2\",\"Correct. X = 20 runs every line (1); path coverage also needs a false case such as X = 5 (2), like the Christmas example.\"],[\"1 and 1\",\"One test cannot take both the true and the false branch of the IF.\"],[\"2 and 4\",\"There is only one simple condition, so path coverage needs 2, not 4.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"<pre>1: IF Age &gt;= 18 AND Member$ = \\\"YES\\\" THEN\\n2:    PRINT \\\"Discount\\\"\\n3: END IF</pre>Following the lecture's condition-coverage example, each sub-condition is made true and false in every combination. How many test cases does that give?\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"One case cannot make each sub-condition both true and false.\"],[\"2\",\"2 is enough for branch coverage (whole IF true once, false once), not the lecture's condition coverage table.\"],[\"8\",\"8 = 2×2×2 would need three sub-conditions; this IF has only two (Age and Member$), giving 2×2 = 4 combinations.\"],[\"4\",\"Correct. Two sub-conditions × (true, false) = 4 combinations, as in the Date$/Time$ table.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"\\\"In data coverage, the code is divided into data and states just like in black-box testing, so it is easy to map white-box and black-box test cases.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture says exactly this about data coverage.\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture states this directly in the data coverage section.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Code written to standards and guidelines can run on different hardware and different compilers. Which of the 3 reasons for following standards is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Reliability\",\"Reliability means the code is more reliable and secure.\"],[\"Readability / Maintainability\",\"This means the code is easier to understand and maintain.\"],[\"Portability\",\"Correct. Portability means the code runs on different hardware and compilers.\"],[\"Usability\",\"Usability is not one of the 3 reasons for standards (reliability, readability/maintainability, portability); running on other hardware is portability.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"……… testing is also called glass box, structural, open box or clear box testing, because it examines internal workings.\",\"o\":[[\"Black-box\",\"Black-box testing looks only at inputs and outputs, not internal workings.\"],[\"White-box\",\"Correct. White-box testing has these four other names because it looks inside the code. (L3, What white-box testing does)\"],[\"Integration\",\"Integration testing is part of testing the pieces, not another name for white-box testing.\"],[\"Acceptance\",\"Acceptance testing is the top of the V-model's validation side, not a name for white-box testing.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: White-box testing tests logical correctness by checking both the true and the false conditions.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Testing logical correctness (both true and false conditions) is one of the 4 things white-box testing helps with. (L3, What white-box testing does)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong: the lecture lists logical correctness, both true and false conditions, as a white-box goal.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Peer review, walkthrough and inspection are the three types of:\",\"o\":[[\"Code review checklist\",\"The code review checklist is a list of error classes (data reference, computation, ...), not review meetings.\"],[\"Coding standards and guidelines\",\"Standards and guidelines are rules for writing code, not types of review.\"],[\"Data coverage\",\"Data coverage is dynamic white-box testing (data flow, sub-boundaries, error forcing).\"],[\"Formal review\",\"Correct. The figure shows Formal Review splitting into Peer Review, Walkthrough and Inspection. (L3, Formal review)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT one of the 3 parts of static white-box testing?\",\"o\":[[\"Formal review\",\"Formal review is one of the 3 parts of static white-box testing.\"],[\"Code coverage\",\"Correct. Code coverage (statement, path, condition) belongs to dynamic white-box testing, not static. (L3, What white-box testing does)\"],[\"Coding standards and guidelines\",\"Coding standards and guidelines are one of the 3 static parts.\"],[\"Code review checklist\",\"The code review checklist is one of the 3 static parts.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In a formal review, having a fixed set of rules (e.g. the lines of code reviewed per day and the time spent) is the essential element called:\",\"o\":[[\"Follow rules\",\"Correct. \\\"Follow rules\\\" means a fixed set of rules such as lines reviewed per day and time spent. (L3, Formal review)\"],[\"Prepare\",\"Prepare means every participant prepares and contributes.\"],[\"Identify problems\",\"Identify problems means finding problems in the design and code.\"],[\"Write a report\",\"Write a report means summarizing the results for the development team.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: In a formal review, the report summarizing the results is kept by the testers and is not made available to the development team.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong: the lecture says the report is made available to the development team.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The fourth element is \\\"Write a report\\\" summarizing results, made available to the development team. (L3, Formal review)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In a walkthrough, the programmer who wrote the code presents it to a group of ……… programmers and testers.\",\"o\":[[\"2 or 3\",\"Too small: the lecture gives a group of 5 or 6.\"],[\"3 or 4\",\"Not the lecture's number; the group is 5 or 6.\"],[\"5 or 6\",\"Correct. The author reads through the code line by line to a group of 5 or 6 programmers and testers. (L3, Formal review)\"],[\"10 or 12\",\"Too large: the lecture gives a group of 5 or 6.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In an inspection, the inspectors review the code from the ……… perspective.\",\"o\":[[\"user's and tester's\",\"Correct. In an inspection the presenter is not the real programmer and inspectors review from the user's and tester's perspective. (L3, Formal review)\"],[\"programmer's and designer's\",\"This fits a peer review, where the programmer who helped design the code is involved.\"],[\"manager's and customer's\",\"The lecture does not mention managers or customers in an inspection.\"],[\"compiler's and hardware\",\"Compilers and hardware relate to portability, a reason for standards, not to inspection.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: In an inspection, the presenter is the programmer who wrote the code.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong: that describes a walkthrough; in an inspection the presenter is not the real programmer.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. In an inspection the presenter is not the real programmer. (L3, Formal review)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: Guidelines are rules that must be adhered to, while standards are not really mandatory.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong: it is the other way round.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Standards are mandatory rules; guidelines help follow the standards and are not really mandatory. (L3, Coding standards and guidelines)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Code written based on standards and guidelines is easier to understand and maintain. Which of the 3 reasons for following them is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Portability\",\"Portability means the code runs on different hardware and compilers.\"],[\"Reliability\",\"Reliability means the code is more reliable and secure.\"],[\"Usability\",\"Usability is not one of the 3 reasons in the lecture.\"],[\"Readability / Maintainability\",\"Correct. Readability/maintainability: such code is easier to understand and maintain. (L3, Coding standards and guidelines)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A reviewer checks whether array subscript values are within the array's bounds. This check belongs to:\",\"o\":[[\"Data declaration errors\",\"Data declaration covers length, type, storage class, initialization and similar names.\"],[\"Data reference errors\",\"Correct. Subscripts within the array bounds and off-by-one indexing are data reference checks. (L3, Generic code review checklist)\"],[\"Computation errors\",\"Computation covers mixed types, overflow and division by zero.\"],[\"Control flow errors\",\"Control flow covers improper behaviour of loops and control structures.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A calculation in which the divisor could become zero is listed under:\",\"o\":[[\"Comparison errors\",\"Comparison errors are about &lt; vs &lt;= and Boolean operands.\"],[\"Data reference errors\",\"Data reference is about un-initialized variables and array bounds.\"],[\"Computation errors\",\"Correct. \\\"If it is ever possible for a divisor to be zero\\\" is a computation-error check. (L3, Generic code review checklist)\"],[\"Input/output errors\",\"Input/output errors are about files, keyboard/mouse input and the screen.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Checking whether an integer variable containing integer values is used in a Boolean calculation is a check for:\",\"o\":[[\"Comparison errors\",\"Correct. Wrong operands of a Boolean operator are a comparison-error check. (L3, Generic code review checklist)\"],[\"Computation errors\",\"Computation is about arithmetic: mixed types, size, overflow, division by zero.\"],[\"Data declaration errors\",\"Data declaration is about how variables are declared and initialized.\"],[\"Control flow errors\",\"Control flow is about loops and control structures behaving improperly.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"An error when accepting input from the keyboard or writing to the screen belongs to:\",\"o\":[[\"Control flow errors\",\"Control flow errors come from loops and control structures.\"],[\"Data reference errors\",\"Data reference errors come from variables not properly declared or initialized.\"],[\"Input/output errors\",\"Correct. Reading from a file, keyboard/mouse input and writing to a file or screen are input/output errors. (L3, Generic code review checklist)\"],[\"Computation errors\",\"Computation errors come from wrong calculations.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: Referencing an un-initialized variable is a computation error.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong: computation errors are about calculations (mixed types, overflow, division by zero).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Un-initialized variables being referenced is a data reference error. (L3, Generic code review checklist)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: Possible overflow in the middle of a numeric calculation is checked under computation errors.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Overflow in the middle of a calculation is one of the 4 computation checks. (L3, Generic code review checklist)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong: the lecture lists overflow mid-calculation under computation errors.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In dynamic white-box testing, \\\"testing the pieces\\\" means:\",\"o\":[[\"Formal review and inspection\",\"These are static white-box (formal review) activities.\"],[\"Data coverage and code coverage\",\"These are separate dynamic white-box topics, not \\\"testing the pieces\\\".\"],[\"Debugging and fixing\",\"Debugging fixes bugs; it is compared with testing, not part of testing the pieces.\"],[\"Unit and integration testing\",\"Correct. Testing the pieces = unit and integration testing. (L3, Dynamic white-box testing)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Data flow, sub-boundaries and error forcing are types of:\",\"o\":[[\"Code coverage\",\"Code coverage types are statement, path and condition coverage.\"],[\"Data coverage\",\"Correct. The lecture lists data flow, sub-boundaries and error forcing as the types of data coverage. (L3, Data coverage and code coverage)\"],[\"Integration testing\",\"Integration strategies are bottom-up, top-down and umbrella.\"],[\"Formal review\",\"Formal review types are peer review, walkthrough and inspection.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Statement coverage, path (branch) coverage and condition coverage are the 3 types of:\",\"o\":[[\"Code coverage\",\"Correct. The Code Coverage figure splits into Statement, Path and Condition Coverage. (L3, Data coverage and code coverage)\"],[\"Data coverage\",\"Data coverage types are data flow, sub-boundaries and error forcing.\"],[\"Static white-box testing\",\"Static white-box is formal review, standards and guidelines, and the checklist.\"],[\"Integration testing\",\"Integration testing strategies are bottom-up, top-down and umbrella.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"<code>if (X&lt;5) and (Y&gt;2) then B=B*X;</code> The statement-coverage test case is:\",\"o\":[[\"X=6, Y=3\",\"X&lt;5 is false, so B=B*X never runs.\"],[\"X=3, Y=1\",\"Y&gt;2 is false, so the AND is false and B=B*X never runs.\"],[\"X=3, Y=4\",\"Correct. 3&lt;5 and 4&gt;2 are both true, so B=B*X runs and every statement is covered. (L3, Data coverage and code coverage)\"],[\"All answers\",\"Only X=3, Y=4 makes both parts of the AND true.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"<code>if (A=1) or (B&lt;0) then C=C-1;</code> The statement-coverage test cases are:\",\"o\":[[\"A=1, B=5\",\"This works (A=1 is true), but it is not the only one.\"],[\"A=0, B=-2\",\"This works (B&lt;0 is true), but it is not the only one.\"],[\"A=1, B=-1\",\"This works (both parts true), but it is not the only one.\"],[\"All answers\",\"Correct. With OR, one true part is enough, and each option makes at least one part true, so C=C-1 runs for all of them. (L3, Data coverage and code coverage)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"<code>if (X&gt;2) and (Y=1) then A=A-X;</code><br><code>if (X=4) or (A&lt;0) then A=A*2;</code><br>The statement-coverage test case (one test that runs both THEN statements) is:\",\"o\":[[\"X=3, Y=1, A=10\",\"The first IF runs (A=7), but X=4 is false and A&lt;0 is false, so A=A*2 is skipped.\"],[\"X=4, Y=1, A=10\",\"Correct. 4&gt;2 and Y=1 → A=10−4=6; then X=4 is true → A=12. Both statements run. (L3, Data coverage and code coverage)\"],[\"X=4, Y=0, A=5\",\"Y=1 is false, so A=A-X is skipped; only the second IF runs.\"],[\"All answers\",\"Only X=4, Y=1, A=10 runs both THEN statements.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the lecture's path-coverage example (<code>IF Date$ = \\\"25-12-2008\\\"</code>), which lines run when Date$ = \\\"01-01-2009\\\"?\",\"o\":[[\"1, 2, 5, 6, 7\",\"Correct. Any date other than 25-12-2008 makes the IF false, so lines 3 and 4 are skipped. (L3, Data coverage and code coverage)\"],[\"1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7\",\"This is the path for Date$ = \\\"25-12-2008\\\" only.\"],[\"1, 3, 5, 7\",\"Line 2 (the IF) always runs, and lines 5 and 6 both run.\"],[\"1, 2, 3, 7\",\"Line 3 needs the IF to be true, and lines 5 and 6 always run.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Following the lecture's full condition coverage method (every combination of the sub-conditions), how many test cases does <code>IF A AND B AND C THEN ...</code> need?\",\"o\":[[\"3\",\"That would test each sub-condition once, not every true/false combination.\"],[\"4\",\"4 is for 2 sub-conditions (2 × 2), like the lecture's Date/Time example.\"],[\"2\",\"2 is branch coverage (whole IF true once, false once), not full condition coverage.\"],[\"8\",\"Correct. Each of the 3 sub-conditions is true or false: 2 × 2 × 2 = 8 combinations. (L3, Why condition coverage needs more cases)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: In the condition coverage example, the test case Date$ = \\\"25-12-2008\\\", Time$ = \\\"11:11:11\\\" runs line 3 (PRINT \\\"MERRY CHRISTMAS\\\").\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong: the Time part is false, so the AND is false and line 3 is skipped.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Only (25-12-2008, 00:00:00) runs line 3; this case runs 1, 2, 5, 6, 7. (L3, Test cases for full condition coverage)\"]],\"a\":1}]},{\"n\":4,\"title\":\"Cyclomatic Complexity, Triangle Problem and OOP Testing\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"V-model: verification and validation\",\"pts\":[\"The opening figure repeats the V-model: the left side going <b>down</b> (requirements spec → functional spec → system design → unit design → code) is <b>verification</b>. The right side going <b>up</b> (unit test → integration test → system test → acceptance test) is <b>validation</b>.\",\"Test plans link the two sides: acceptance test plan, system test plan, integration test plan and unit test cases.\",\"Figure 1.3 (V Model):<br>• <b>Left column</b>: goes down with solid arrows: <b>Requirements Specifications → Functional Specifications → System Design → Unit Design → Code</b>.<br>• <b>Right column</b>: goes up from Code with solid arrows: <b>Unit Test → Integration Test → System Test → Acceptance Test</b>.<br>• <b>Dotted arrows</b> cross from left to right: Requirements Specifications → Acceptance Test, labelled <b>Acceptance Test Plan</b>; Functional Specifications → System Test, labelled <b>System Test Plan</b>; System Design → Integration Test, labelled <b>Integration Test Plan</b>.<br>• The words <b>Test Cases</b> sit between Unit Design and Unit Test.<br>• A big arrow pointing down on the left is labelled <b>Verification</b>, and a big arrow pointing up on the right is labelled <b>Validation</b>.\"],\"fig\":\"qa-v-verify\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-v-verify\":2},\"figH\":{\"qa-v-verify\":\"Figure 1.3: the V Model, verification down the left and validation up the right\"}},{\"h\":\"Worked example: foo()\",\"code\":\"public float foo(int a, int b, float x) {\\n1.  if (a>1 && b==0) {\\n2.     x = x/a;\\n    }\\n3.  if (a==2 || x>1) {\\n4.     x = x+1;\\n    }\\n    else\\n5.     x = x*a;\\n6.  return (a+x);\\n}\"},{\"h\":\"Control flow graph of foo\",\"table\":[[\"Edge\",\"Meaning\"],[\"1 → 2\",\"True branch of (a&gt;1 && b==0)\"],[\"1 → 3\",\"False branch of the first IF\"],[\"2 → 3\",\"Sequential\"],[\"3 → 4\",\"True branch of (a==2 || x&gt;1)\"],[\"3 → 5\",\"False branch (else)\"],[\"4 → 6\",\"Sequential\"],[\"5 → 6\",\"Sequential\"]],\"pts\":[\"Figure (control flow graph of foo):<br>• <b>Nodes</b>: six circles: 1 at the top, 2 to its lower right, 3 below 1, then 5 (left) and 4 (right), and 6 at the bottom.<br>• Thick solid arrows 1 → 2 and 3 → 4 are <b>True Branch</b>.<br>• Dotted arrows 1 → 3 and 3 → 5 are <b>False Branch</b>.<br>• Thin arrows 2 → 3, 4 → 6 and 5 → 6 are <b>Sequential Branch</b>.<br>• A legend at the top right lists True Branch, Sequential Branch and False Branch; the caption reads \\\"control flow graph\\\".\"],\"fig\":\"qa-foo-cfg\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-foo-cfg\":0},\"figH\":{\"qa-foo-cfg\":\"The control flow graph of foo() and its cyclomatic complexity\"}},{\"h\":\"Cyclomatic complexity and basis paths\",\"formula\":[\"V(G) = E − N + 2P = 7 − 6 + 2(1) = 3\",\"Check: V(G) = predicate nodes + 1 = 2 (nodes 1 and 3) + 1 = 3\",\"Check: regions of the planar graph = 2 inner regions + 1 outer = 3\",\"Basis path 1: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6   → a=2, b=0, x=0 (any x)\",\"Basis path 2: 1, 3, 4, 6      → a=2, b=1, x=0 (any x)\",\"Basis path 3: 1, 3, 5, 6      → a=0, b=0, x=0\"]},{\"h\":\"Checking the test cases\",\"pts\":[\"a=2, b=0: a&gt;1 && b==0 is true → x=x/2; then a==2 is true → x=x+1. Path 1,2,3,4,6 ✔.\",\"a=2, b=1: b==0 is false → skip 2; a==2 true → node 4. Path 1,3,4,6 ✔.\",\"a=0, b=0, x=0: a&gt;1 false → skip 2; a==2 false and x&gt;1 false → else node 5. Path 1,3,5,6 ✔.\",\"Cyclomatic complexity = the <b>number of independent (basis) paths</b>, so it gives the number of test cases needed to execute every basis path (<b>3 for foo()</b>: one test case per basis path, as on the slide). ‡ It is not the minimum number of tests for branch coverage: foo() reaches branch coverage with only 2 tests (a=2, b=0 makes both IFs true; a=0, b=0, x=0 makes both false).\"]},{\"h\":\"Triangle problem: functional (black-box) testing\",\"pts\":[\"Exercise: Apply black box and white box on the triangle problem. Show <b>Function testing, statement, and Path (branch) coverage</b> of the triangle problem.\",\"Pseudocode:<br><code>read a,b,c;</code><br><code>type=\\\"scalene\\\";</code><br><code>if (a==b or a==c or b==c) type=\\\"isosceles\\\";</code><br><code>if (a==b and a==c) type=\\\"equilateral\\\";</code><br><code>if (a&gt;=b+c or b&gt;=a+c or c&gt;=a+b) type=\\\"not a triangle\\\";</code><br><code>if (a&lt;=0 or b&lt;=0 or c&lt;=0) type=\\\"bad inputs\\\";</code><br><code>print type</code>\",\"Domain split into 3 subdomains:<br>• <b>scalene</b>: no sides equal.<br>• <b>isosceles</b>: two equal.<br>• <b>equilateral</b>: all equal.<br>Plus 2 error subdomains: <b>bad inputs</b> and <b>not a triangle</b>.<br>Each test case must state the expected output.\"]},{\"h\":\"Triangle functional test cases\",\"table\":[[\"Subdomain\",\"Case\",\"Test (a,b,c) → output\"],[\"Scalene\",\"Increasing size\",\"(3,4,5) scalene\"],[\"Scalene\",\"Decreasing size\",\"(5,4,3) scalene\"],[\"Scalene\",\"Largest as second\",\"(4,5,3) scalene\"],[\"Isosceles\",\"a=b, other larger / smaller\",\"(5,5,8) / (8,8,5) isosceles\"],[\"Isosceles\",\"a=c, other larger / smaller\",\"(5,8,5) / (8,5,8) isosceles\"],[\"Isosceles\",\"b=c, other larger / smaller\",\"(8,5,5) / (5,8,8) isosceles\"],[\"Equilateral\",\"All sides equal\",\"(5,5,5) equilateral\"],[\"Not a triangle\",\"Largest first / second / third\",\"(6,4,2) / (4,6,2) / (1,2,3) not a triangle\"],[\"Bad inputs\",\"One / two / three bad inputs\",\"(1,2,4) ‡ / (3,2,5) ‡ / (0,0,0) bad inputs\"]]},{\"h\":\"Note on the bad-inputs row\",\"pts\":[\"‡ The slide lists (1,2,4) and (3,2,5) as \\\"bad inputs\\\", but running the pseudocode on them gives <b>\\\"not a triangle\\\"</b> (4 ≥ 1+2 and 5 ≥ 3+2); no side is ≤ 0. Correct examples would be (−1,2,4) for one bad input and (0,−2,5) for two. (0,0,0) is correct: the last IF sets \\\"bad inputs\\\".\"]},{\"h\":\"Triangle: statement and branch coverage\",\"table\":[[\"Node\",\"Source line\",\"3,4,5\",\"3,5,3\",\"0,1,0\",\"4,4,4\"],[\"A\",\"read a,b,c\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\"],[\"B\",\"type = scalene\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\"],[\"C\",\"if (a==b || b==c || a==c)\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\"],[\"D\",\"type = isosceles\",\"\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\"],[\"E\",\"if (a==b && b==c)\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\"],[\"F\",\"type = equilateral\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"*\"],[\"G\",\"if (a&gt;=b+c || b&gt;=a+c || c&gt;=a+b)\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\"],[\"H\",\"type = not a triangle\",\"\",\"\",\"*\",\"\"],[\"I\",\"if (a&lt;=0 || b&lt;=0 || c&lt;=0)\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\"],[\"J\",\"type = bad inputs\",\"\",\"\",\"*\",\"\"],[\"K\",\"print type\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\",\"*\"]],\"fig\":\"qa-triangle-run\",\"figH\":{\"qa-triangle-run\":\"Try it: run the triangle pseudocode with any a, b and c\"}},{\"h\":\"Minimal coverage sets\",\"pts\":[\"Question (slide): Find the <b>minimal set of test cases</b> that would achieve <b>statement and branch coverage</b> of the triangle problem pseudocode.\",\"(0,1,0): a==c → isosceles (D); not all equal; b ≥ a+c (1 ≥ 0) → not a triangle (H); a ≤ 0 → bad inputs (J).\",\"(4,4,4): isosceles (D), equilateral (F), valid triangle, positive sides.\",\"<b>Using the test cases of the slide's table</b>, <b>statement coverage minimum = 2 tests</b>: (4,4,4) + (0,1,0) run every line A–K (only (4,4,4) reaches F, and only (0,1,0) reaches H and J).\",\"<b>Using the test cases of the slide's table</b>, <b>branch coverage minimum = 3 tests</b>: add (3,4,5), the only column that makes C false. (0,1,0) makes E false and G, I true; (4,4,4) makes E true and G, I false. So every IF is both true and false.\",\"‡ The slide gives no answer. With <b>any</b> input (not only the table's columns) the minimums are smaller: (0,0,0) alone runs every line A to K (0==0 sets isosceles and equilateral, 0 ≥ 0+0 sets not a triangle, 0 ≤ 0 sets bad inputs), so <b>statement coverage needs 1 test</b>. (0,0,0) makes every IF true and (3,4,5) makes every IF false, so <b>branch coverage needs 2 tests</b> (one test cannot make an IF both true and false).\",\"Figure (Control flow graph for triangle program):<br>• <b>Chain</b>: a chain of nodes <b>a → c → e → g → i</b> joined by straight arrows down.<br>• <b>Branches</b>: a also branches to <b>isosceles</b>, c to <b>equilateral</b>, e to <b>not a triangle</b> and g to <b>bad inputs</b>.<br>• <b>Arrows back</b>: each side node has an arrow back into the next chain node (isosceles → c, equilateral → e, not a triangle → g, bad inputs → i).\"],\"fig\":\"qa-triangle-cfg\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-triangle-cfg\":6},\"figH\":{\"qa-triangle-cfg\":\"The control flow graph of the triangle program, branch by branch\"}},{\"h\":\"Functional tests: area of a triangle from 3 points\",\"table\":[[\"Point 1\",\"Point 2\",\"Point 3\",\"Expected area\"],[\"1,1\",\"1,5\",\"5,1\",\"8\"],[\"1,1\",\"1,5\",\"1,10\",\"Not a triangle (collinear, x=1)\"],[\"10,10\",\"0,10\",\"10,0\",\"50\"],[\"0,0\",\"0,10\",\"10,10\",\"50\"]],\"pts\":[\"Example: A program calculates the area of a triangle. The inputs are <b>three sets of x,y coordinates</b>. Construct functional tests; the answer completes the table below.\"]},{\"h\":\"OOP testing\",\"pts\":[\"Testing object-oriented software presents <b>some new challenges</b>. <b>Many conventional techniques are still appropriate</b>.\",\"Functional testing of OO software is <b>no different</b> from conventional software: test cases come from the required functionality in the requirements document. <b>Structural</b> testing of OOP is very different.\",\"Two structural testing approaches will be covered: <b>Method-message (MM) testing</b> and <b>function pair testing</b>.\",\"Conventional software uses coverage criteria (statement, branch, data flow). These can be applied to OO, but statement and branch coverage <b>do not seem appropriate</b> for OO complexity: the <b>interactions between methods</b> must be tested.\",\"<b>MM (Method-Message) testing</b>: every method call must be tested at least once. If a method calls another several times, each call is tested only once. It is the most basic criterion and <b>does not subsume statement coverage</b>.\",\"<b>Function Pair (FP) testing</b>: all possible sequences of method executions <b>of length two</b> must be tested, usually based on a <b>state machine diagram</b>.\"]},{\"h\":\"Stack example: MM and FP\",\"pts\":[\"States: <b>Empty, Normal, Full</b>.<br>• <b>new</b> → Empty.<br>• <b>push</b>: Empty→Normal, Normal→Normal, Normal→Full, Full→error.<br>• <b>pop</b>: Empty→error, Normal→Normal, Normal→Empty, Full→Normal.\",\"Figure (stack state machine; slide: \\\"Find MM and FP of the stack in the following figure\\\"):<br>• Three states <b>Empty</b>, <b>Normal</b> and <b>Full</b>.<br>• A black start dot has an arrow <b>New</b> into Empty.<br>• <b>Push</b> goes Empty → Normal and Normal → Full; <b>Pop</b> goes Full → Normal and Normal → Empty.<br>• Under Normal, a loop labelled <b>Push pop</b> leaves Normal and comes back to it.<br>• A <b>Pop</b> arrow leaves Empty and a <b>Push</b> arrow leaves Full, both pointing to no state (the error cases).\",\"<b>MM</b>: a simple sequence of <b>create, push and pop</b> might achieve MM testing.\",\"<b>FP (14 pairs)</b>:<br>(1) new-pop (empty, error)<br>(2) new-push<br>(3) push(from empty)-push<br>(4) push(from empty)-pop<br>(5) push(normal→normal)-push (still normal)<br>(6) push(normal→normal)-push (into full)<br>(7) push(normal→normal)-pop<br>(8) push(normal→full)-push (error)<br>(9) push(normal→full)-pop<br>(10) pop(normal→normal)-push <b>(still in normal)</b><br>(11) pop(normal→normal)-pop (still normal)<br>(12) pop(normal→normal)-pop (into empty)<br>(13) pop(into empty)-push<br>(14) pop(into empty)-pop (error).\"],\"fig\":\"qa-stack-fp\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-stack-fp\":3},\"figH\":{\"qa-stack-fp\":\"Try it: push and pop the stack and collect the 14 function pairs\"}},{\"h\":\"Lecture questions (answers)\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Q1</b> How is functional testing of OO software done? The same as conventional: test cases from the required functionality in the requirements document.\",\"<b>Q2</b> Is statement coverage useful for OO? Limited: it can be applied but does not test interactions between methods.\",\"<b>Q3</b> Does MM subsume statement coverage? <b>No</b> (lecture: \\\"MM testing does not subsume every-statement coverage\\\"). The lec-4 student summary says \\\"Yes\\\"; that contradicts the slide.\",\"<b>Q4</b> Advantage of function pair coverage? It tests sequences of two method executions, so it catches interaction faults and state-dependent bugs (e.g. push on full, pop on empty) that single-call MM testing misses.\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Cyclomatic complexity formula\",\"V(G) = E − N + 2P (P = connected components, usually 1). Also = predicate nodes + 1 = number of regions.\"],[\"V(G) of foo()\",\"7 edges − 6 nodes + 2 = 3.\"],[\"Basis paths of foo()\",\"1,2,3,4,6; 1,3,4,6; 1,3,5,6.\"],[\"Cyclomatic complexity determines\",\"The number of independent (basis) paths.\"],[\"V-model: left side / right side\",\"Left (down) = verification; right (up) = validation.\"],[\"Triangle subdomains\",\"Scalene, isosceles, equilateral, plus the error cases bad inputs and not a triangle.\"],[\"Minimal statement coverage for the triangle\",\"Using the slide table's test cases: (4,4,4) and (0,1,0). ‡ With any input: (0,0,0) alone (1 test).\"],[\"Minimal branch coverage for the triangle\",\"Using the slide table's test cases: (3,4,5), (4,4,4) and (0,1,0). ‡ With any input: (0,0,0) and (3,4,5) (2 tests).\"],[\"MM testing\",\"Every method call is tested at least once.\"],[\"Does MM subsume statement coverage?\",\"No.\"],[\"Function pair testing\",\"All sequences of method executions of length two, based on a state machine.\"],[\"MM sequence for a stack\",\"create, push, pop.\"],[\"Stack states\",\"Empty, Normal, Full.\"],[\"Number of function pairs for the stack\",\"14.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Find the cyclomatic complexity of foo() and its basis paths with test cases.\",\"Nodes 1 to 6, edges 1-2, 1-3, 2-3, 3-4, 3-5, 4-6, 5-6 give E=7, N=6, so V(G)=7-6+2=3 (also 2 predicates + 1). Basis paths: 1,2,3,4,6 (a=2,b=0,x=0); 1,3,4,6 (a=2,b=1,x=0); 1,3,5,6 (a=0,b=0,x=0).\"],[\"Differentiate between MM testing and function pair testing.\",\"MM testing requires every method call to be tested at least once (each call once even if repeated). It is the most basic criterion and does not subsume statement coverage. Function pair testing requires every possible sequence of two method executions, usually derived from a state machine diagram, so it tests interactions and state-dependent behaviour.\"],[\"Give the minimal test sets for statement and branch coverage of the triangle program.\",\"Using the test cases of the slide's table (3,4,5 / 3,5,3 / 0,1,0 / 4,4,4): Statement: (4,4,4) and (0,1,0), which reach isosceles, equilateral, not a triangle and bad inputs (2 tests). Branch: add (3,4,5) so the first IF is also false (3 tests). Then every decision is true and false at least once. ‡ The slide gives no answer; with any input, (0,0,0) alone runs every line A to K, so statement coverage needs 1 test, and (0,0,0) (every IF true) plus (3,4,5) (every IF false) give branch coverage with 2 tests.\"],[\"List the function pairs for the stack.\",\"new-pop (error), new-push, push(from empty)-push, push(from empty)-pop, push(normal)-push (still normal), push(normal)-push (into full), push(normal)-pop, push(to full)-push (error), push(to full)-pop, pop(normal)-push (still in normal), pop(normal)-pop (still normal), pop(normal)-pop (into empty), pop(into empty)-push, pop(into empty)-pop (error).\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"A simple sequence that achieves method-message coverage in stack testing is:\",\"o\":[[\"create, push &amp; pop\",\"Correct. MM testing only needs every method call tested at least once; the lecture says a simple sequence of create, push and pop achieves it for the stack (L4, Stack example).\"],[\"create, insert &amp; pop\",\"A stack's methods are new/create, push and pop; \\\"insert\\\" is not one of them, so this sequence does not exercise push.\"],[\"create &amp; push\",\"pop is never called, so one of the stack's methods is untested; MM needs every method call at least once: create, push and pop.\"],[\"create &amp; insert\",\"\\\"insert\\\" is not a stack method and pop is missing, so neither push nor pop is exercised; the lecture's sequence is create, push and pop.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"For foo() with E = 7 and N = 6, the cyclomatic complexity is:\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"V(G) = 1 only for straight-line code with no decisions; foo() has two predicate nodes (1 and 3), so V(G) = 3.\"],[\"2\",\"2 is the number of predicate nodes (1 and 3); V(G) = predicates + 1 = 3, or E − N + 2 = 7 − 6 + 2 = 3.\"],[\"3\",\"Correct. V(G) = E − N + 2P = 7 − 6 + 2(1) = 3, which matches predicate nodes + 1 = 2 + 1 and the 3 regions of the graph (L4, foo()).\"],[\"13\",\"13 = E + N = 7 + 6, which is not the formula; V(G) = E − N + 2P = 7 − 6 + 2 = 3.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Cyclomatic complexity is used to determine the number of:\",\"o\":[[\"Paths\",\"Correct. It gives the number of independent (basis) paths.\"],[\"States\",\"States and transitions come from a state machine diagram (used for function pair testing), not from cyclomatic complexity.\"],[\"Lines\",\"Cyclomatic complexity is computed from the flow graph's edges, nodes and decisions, not lines; it gives the number of independent paths.\"],[\"Statements\",\"Many statements can lie on one path; V(G) counts independent (basis) paths, i.e. the minimum tests for basis path coverage.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"Method-message testing does NOT subsume:\",\"o\":[[\"Statement coverage\",\"Correct. The lecture says MM testing is the most basic OO criterion and does not subsume statement coverage: calling every method once does not run every statement (L4, OOP testing).\"],[\"All the above\",\"The lecture only mentions statement coverage.\"],[\"Data coverage\",\"The lecture does not discuss data coverage in relation to MM testing; it states only that MM does not subsume statement coverage.\"],[\"State coverage\",\"State coverage is not compared with MM in the lecture (states belong to function pair testing); the stated fact is about statement coverage.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"Which input set gives the path 1,3,5,6 of foo()?\",\"o\":[[\"a=2, b=0, x=0\",\"a=2, b=0 makes the first IF true (node 2), then a==2 goes to node 4: path 1,2,3,4,6, basis path 1 in the lecture.\"],[\"a=2, b=1, x=0\",\"b=1 skips node 2, but a==2 makes the second IF true, so node 4 runs: path 1,3,4,6, basis path 2.\"],[\"a=0, b=0, x=0\",\"Correct. First IF false, then a≠2 and x=0 is not &gt;1, so the else (5) runs.\"],[\"a=3, b=0, x=9\",\"a=3, b=0 makes the first IF true (node 2: x=9/3=3), then x&gt;1 is true, so node 4 runs: path 1,2,3,4,6.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Using only the four test cases in the slide's table (3,4,5 / 3,5,3 / 0,1,0 / 4,4,4), what is the minimum number of tests for STATEMENT coverage of the triangle pseudocode?\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"Among the table's four columns, only (4,4,4) reaches equilateral (F) and only (0,1,0) reaches not a triangle (H) and bad inputs (J), so no single column runs every line. (‡ With any input, (0,0,0) alone would do it.)\"],[\"2\",\"Correct. (4,4,4) runs isosceles and equilateral; (0,1,0) runs isosceles, not a triangle and bad inputs. Together they run every line A–K (L4, Minimal coverage sets).\"],[\"4\",\"The slide table uses 4 columns, but 2 are enough.\"],[\"16\",\"16 is far above the minimum; statement coverage only needs every line run once, and (4,4,4) plus (0,1,0) already do that.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"The triple (0,1,0) finally prints:\",\"o\":[[\"isosceles\",\"type is set to isosceles first (a==c, 0==0), but later IFs overwrite it: not a triangle (1 ≥ 0+0), then bad inputs (a ≤ 0).\"],[\"not a triangle\",\"\\\"not a triangle\\\" is set because b ≥ a+c (1 ≥ 0), but the last IF (a ≤ 0) overwrites it with bad inputs before printing.\"],[\"bad inputs\",\"Correct. The last IF (a ≤ 0) overwrites the type.\"],[\"scalene\",\"scalene is only the initial value; a==c changes it to isosceles, and later IFs leave it as bad inputs.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Function pair coverage requires testing:\",\"o\":[[\"Every method call once\",\"Testing every method call once is MM (method-message) coverage; FP coverage tests every sequence of two method executions.\"],[\"All sequences of method executions of length two\",\"Correct. Usually derived from a state machine diagram.\"],[\"Every statement\",\"Every statement is statement coverage, a conventional criterion the lecture says is not enough for OO; FP tests pairs of method executions.\"],[\"Only error states\",\"FP includes error pairs (e.g. new-pop, push on full) but requires ALL length-two sequences, 14 pairs in the stack example.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Functional testing of object-oriented software is:\",\"o\":[[\"Very different from conventional software\",\"That is true for STRUCTURAL testing, not functional.\"],[\"No different from functional testing of conventional software\",\"Correct. Test cases come from the required functionality.\"],[\"Impossible without source code\",\"Functional (black-box) testing derives test cases from the requirements document, so no source code is needed.\"],[\"Only statement coverage\",\"Statement coverage is a structural (code-based) criterion; functional testing of OO uses the required functionality, as for conventional software.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the V-model figure, the left (downward) side is labelled:\",\"o\":[[\"Validation\",\"Validation is the right, upward side of the V (unit test up to acceptance test); the left, downward side is verification.\"],[\"Verification\",\"Correct. The left side going down (requirements spec → functional spec → system design → unit design → code) is verification; the right side going up is validation (L4, V-model).\"],[\"Debugging\",\"Debugging is not a label in the V-model figure; the two sides are verification (left, down) and validation (right, up).\"],[\"Maintenance\",\"Maintenance is a Waterfall phase, not a V-model label; the figure's left side is verification and the right side is validation.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Expected area for points (1,1), (1,5), (5,1)?\",\"o\":[[\"8\",\"Correct. Right triangle with legs 4 and 4: ½·4·4 = 8.\"],[\"16\",\"16 = 4 × 4 forgets the ½: a right triangle with legs 4 and 4 has area ½ × 4 × 4 = 8.\"],[\"Not a triangle\",\"Not a triangle needs collinear points, like (1,1), (1,5), (1,10) on x=1; here (5,1) is off the line, so the triangle is real.\"],[\"50\",\"50 is the expected area of the 10-by-10 cases, e.g. (10,10), (0,10), (10,0); these points have legs of 4, so area 8.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A simple sequence of achieve method-message in Queue testing are\",\"o\":[[\"create, insert, and delete\",\"Correct. Method-message (MM) testing needs every method call tested at least once. For the stack, Lecture 4 says \\\"a simple sequence of create, push and pop might achieve MM testing.\\\" The queue's state diagram (Empty, Normal, Full) uses new, insert and delete, so its sequence is create, insert and delete (L4, Stack example: MM and FP).\"],[\"create, insert, and pop\",\"Mixes the two: pop is the stack's removal method; the queue's is delete.\"],[\"create, push, and pop\",\"This is the stack's sequence from the slide, word for word; push and pop are not the queue's methods.\"],[\"create, push, and delete\",\"Mixes the two: push is the stack's insertion method; the queue's is insert.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Cyclomatic Complexity equals\",\"o\":[[\"no. of Edges - no. of nodes + 3\",\"Wrong constant: foo() would give 7 − 6 + 3 = 4, not the lecture's 3 (L4).\"],[\"no. of nodes - no. of Edges + 2\",\"Reversed: foo() would give 6 − 7 + 2 = 1 (L4).\"],[\"no. of Edges - no. of nodes + 2\",\"Correct. Lecture 4: V(G) = E − N + 2P, with P = 1 for one program, so V(G) = E − N + 2. For foo(): 7 − 6 + 2 = 3, the same as predicate nodes + 1 = 2 + 1 (L4, Cyclomatic complexity and basis paths).\"],[\"no. of Edges - no. of nodes + 3\",\"Same as the first option (the quiz lists it twice): the constant is wrong (L4).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Function pair is used to test\",\"o\":[[\"State machine diagram\",\"The slide says FP is based on a state machine diagram: the diagram is where the pairs come from, not what is being tested (L4).\"],[\"Data flow Diagram\",\"Not connected to function pair testing in the lecture; data flow is a type of data coverage (L3).\"],[\"All answers\",\"Cannot be right, because the data flow diagram is wrong.\"],[\"The interactions between methods\",\"Correct. Lecture 4: in OO code \\\"the interactions between methods must be tested,\\\" and MM and function pair testing are the two approaches for it. Function pair testing tests \\\"all possible sequences of method executions of length two,\\\" usually based on a state machine diagram (L4, OOP testing).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The method message testing does not subsume\",\"o\":[[\"Data coverage\",\"L4 does not compare data coverage (an L3 white-box idea) with MM testing.\"],[\"State coverage\",\"States belong to function pair testing; the slide does not compare state coverage with MM (L4).\"],[\"Statement coverage\",\"Correct. Lecture 4: MM testing \\\"is the most basic criterion and does not subsume statement coverage\\\": calling every method once does not run every statement inside them (L4, OOP testing).\"],[\"All answers\",\"The slide names only statement coverage (L4).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Cyclomatic Complexity is used to determine\",\"o\":[[\"number of states\",\"States come from a state machine diagram, used for function pair testing (L4).\"],[\"number of lines\",\"V(G) counts decisions, not lines: straight-line code has V(G) = 1 however long it is.\"],[\"number of statements\",\"Statements do not change V(G) unless they add a decision; statement coverage is a different idea (L3).\"],[\"number of paths\",\"Correct. Lecture 4: cyclomatic complexity is \\\"the number of independent (basis) paths,\\\" so it gives the number of test cases needed to run every basis path. foo() has V(G) = 3 and 3 basis paths: 1-2-3-4-6, 1-3-4-6, 1-3-5-6.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"A control flow graph has <b>8 edges</b>, <b>7 nodes</b> and is one connected component (P = 1). What is its cyclomatic complexity?\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"1 = 8 − 7 forgets the +2P term; V(G) = E − N + 2P = 8 − 7 + 2(1) = 3.\"],[\"3\",\"Correct. V(G) = E − N + 2P = 8 − 7 + 2(1) = 3, so the graph has 3 independent paths (L4, Cyclomatic complexity).\"],[\"5\",\"This uses P = 2 (8 − 7 + 4); the graph is one component.\"],[\"15\",\"15 = E + N = 8 + 7; the nodes are subtracted, not added: V(G) = 8 − 7 + 2 = 3.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"<pre>read(x, y, z);\\nif (x &gt; 0) { y = y + 1; }\\nif (z &gt; 0) { y = y * 2; }\\nif (y &gt; 10) { print(\\\"big\\\"); }\\nprint(y);</pre>Using V(G) = predicate nodes + 1, what is the cyclomatic complexity?\",\"o\":[[\"4\",\"Correct. There are 3 simple decisions (predicate nodes), so V(G) = 3 + 1 = 4.\"],[\"3\",\"3 is the number of predicates; you must add 1.\"],[\"5\",\"5 would need 4 predicate nodes; the code has only 3 IFs (x&gt;0, z&gt;0, y&gt;10), so V(G) = 3 + 1 = 4.\"],[\"8\",\"V(G) is not 2^3; it grows by one per decision.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"For the lecture's <code>foo(a, b, x)</code>, which path is taken with <b>a = 3, b = 0, x = 2</b>?\",\"o\":[[\"1, 2, 3, 5, 6\",\"Correct. a&gt;1 && b==0 is true so x = 2/3 ≈ 0.67; then a==2 is false and x&gt;1 is false, so the else (node 5) runs.\"],[\"1, 2, 3, 4, 6\",\"After node 2, x ≈ 0.67, so x&gt;1 is false, and a is not 2; node 4 is not reached.\"],[\"1, 3, 5, 6\",\"The first IF is true (3 &gt; 1 and b = 0), so node 2 runs.\"],[\"1, 3, 4, 6\",\"The first IF is true, so node 2 cannot be skipped.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Running the lecture's triangle pseudocode on <b>(2, 2, 4)</b>, what is finally printed?\",\"o\":[[\"isosceles\",\"It is set first, but the later IF (4 &gt;= 2+2) overwrites it.\"],[\"scalene\",\"Two sides are equal, so scalene is overwritten.\"],[\"bad inputs\",\"All sides are positive, so the last IF is false.\"],[\"not a triangle\",\"Correct. a==b sets isosceles, but c &gt;= a+b (4 &gt;= 4) then sets \\\"not a triangle\\\"; no side is ≤ 0.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Using only the four test cases in the slide's table (3,4,5 / 3,5,3 / 0,1,0 / 4,4,4), what is the minimum number of tests for <b>branch</b> coverage of the triangle pseudocode?\",\"o\":[[\"2\",\"(4,4,4) and (0,1,0) are both needed (only they reach equilateral, not a triangle and bad inputs), and both make the first IF true; among the table's columns only (3,4,5) makes it false, so 2 is not enough. (‡ With any input, (0,0,0) + (3,4,5) would give 2.)\"],[\"4\",\"The slide table has 4 columns, but 3 tests are enough.\"],[\"3\",\"Correct. (4,4,4) and (0,1,0) plus (3,4,5), which makes the first IF false.\"],[\"5\",\"One test per subdomain is not required for branch coverage.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Under Method-Message (MM) testing, method A calls method B <b>three times</b>. How many times must the A → B call be tested?\",\"o\":[[\"Three times\",\"The lecture says each call is tested only once even if it happens several times.\"],[\"Once\",\"Correct. Each method call is tested at least once; repeated calls are tested only once.\"],[\"Twice\",\"MM has no two-test rule: each call is tested at least once, and repeated calls of the same method are tested only once.\"],[\"It does not need testing\",\"MM requires every method call to be tested at least once.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the stack function-pair example, what happens in the pair <b>push (Normal → Full) followed by push</b>?\",\"o\":[[\"The stack moves to Normal\",\"A push never moves Full to Normal; pop does that.\"],[\"The stack stays Full with no error\",\"Push from Full is an error state in the diagram.\"],[\"Error, because the stack is full\",\"Correct. This is pair 8: push on a full stack gives an error.\"],[\"The stack moves to Empty\",\"Push never empties a stack; Empty is reached only by pop from Normal. Push on a Full stack is an error (pair 8).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Following the lecture's \\\"area of a triangle from 3 points\\\" functional tests, which input should have the expected output <b>\\\"Not a triangle\\\"</b>?\",\"o\":[[\"(2,2), (4,4), (6,6)\",\"Correct. The three points lie on the same line (y = x), so they are collinear, like (1,1), (1,5), (1,10).\"],[\"(0,0), (4,0), (0,6)\",\"(0,0), (4,0), (0,6) are not on one line; they form a right triangle with legs 4 and 6, area ½ × 4 × 6 = 12.\"],[\"(1,1), (5,1), (1,4)\",\"(1,1), (5,1), (1,4) are not collinear; they form a right triangle with legs 4 and 3, area ½ × 4 × 3 = 6.\"],[\"(0,0), (3,0), (3,3)\",\"(0,0), (3,0), (3,3) are not collinear; they form a right triangle with legs 3 and 3, area ½ × 3 × 3 = 4.5.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"Statement and branch coverage are fully appropriate for object-oriented software because they test the interactions between methods.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The lecture says statement and branch coverage do not seem appropriate for OO complexity, because the interactions between methods must be tested.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. They can be applied, but they do not test interactions between methods; MM and FP testing are used for that.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A planar flow graph divides the plane into <b>3 inner regions</b> plus the outer region. What is V(G)?\",\"o\":[[\"3\",\"3 counts only the inner regions; the outer region also counts, so V(G) = 3 + 1 = 4 (foo: 2 inner + 1 outer = 3).\"],[\"2\",\"This subtracts instead of adding the outer region.\"],[\"5\",\"5 adds two outer regions; a planar graph has exactly one outer region, so V(G) = 3 + 1 = 4.\"],[\"4\",\"Correct. V(G) = number of regions = 3 inner + 1 outer = 4 (foo had 2 + 1 = 3).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In the V-model figure, the dotted arrow from Requirements Specifications to Acceptance Test is labelled:\",\"o\":[[\"Acceptance Test Plan\",\"Correct. Requirements Specifications → Acceptance Test is labelled Acceptance Test Plan. (L4, V-model: verification and validation)\"],[\"System Test Plan\",\"System Test Plan links Functional Specifications to System Test.\"],[\"Integration Test Plan\",\"Integration Test Plan links System Design to Integration Test.\"],[\"Test Cases\",\"\\\"Test Cases\\\" sits between Unit Design and Unit Test.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: In the V-model figure, the words \\\"Test Cases\\\" sit between Unit Design and Unit Test.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The figure places \\\"Test Cases\\\" between Unit Design and Unit Test. (L4, V-model: verification and validation)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong: that is exactly where the figure puts \\\"Test Cases\\\"; the other links are test plans.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A control flow graph has <b>10 edges</b> and <b>8 nodes</b> and is one connected component (P = 1). What is V(G)?\",\"o\":[[\"2\",\"This is E − N without adding 2P.\"],[\"4\",\"Correct. V(G) = E − N + 2P = 10 − 8 + 2(1) = 4. (L4, Cyclomatic complexity and basis paths)\"],[\"6\",\"This uses P = 2 (10 − 8 + 4); the graph is one component, so P = 1 and V(G) = 4.\"],[\"18\",\"This adds E and N instead of subtracting.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A flow graph has <b>9 edges</b>, <b>9 nodes</b> and <b>2 connected components</b> (P = 2). Using V(G) = E − N + 2P, what is V(G)?\",\"o\":[[\"2\",\"This uses P = 1 (9 − 9 + 2); here P = 2.\"],[\"0\",\"This forgets the 2P term (9 − 9).\"],[\"4\",\"Correct. V(G) = 9 − 9 + 2(2) = 4. (L4, Cyclomatic complexity and basis paths)\"],[\"11\",\"This adds 2 to E alone (9 + 2); N must be subtracted and P = 2.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"A flow graph has <b>4 predicate (decision) nodes</b>. Using V(G) = predicate nodes + 1, what is V(G)?\",\"o\":[[\"3\",\"This subtracts 1 instead of adding it.\"],[\"4\",\"This forgets the + 1.\"],[\"8\",\"This doubles the predicates; the formula adds 1.\"],[\"5\",\"Correct. V(G) = 4 + 1 = 5. (L4, Cyclomatic complexity and basis paths)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"The planar control flow graph of the lecture's <code>foo()</code> divides the plane into how many regions (inner + outer)?\",\"o\":[[\"3\",\"Correct. 2 inner regions + 1 outer = 3, matching V(G) = 3. (L4, Cyclomatic complexity and basis paths)\"],[\"2\",\"2 is only the inner regions; the outer region also counts.\"],[\"4\",\"foo() has 2 inner regions, not 3.\"],[\"7\",\"7 is the number of edges, not regions.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"<pre>public int bar(int a, int b) {\\n1.  if (a&gt;0 &amp;&amp; b&gt;0) {\\n2.     b = b-a;\\n    }\\n3.  if (b==0 || a&gt;5) {\\n4.     a = a+1;\\n    }\\n    else\\n5.     a = a*2;\\n6.  return (a+b);\\n}</pre>The control flow graph of <code>bar()</code> is built like <code>foo()</code>. Which path does <b>bar(3, 3)</b> follow?\",\"o\":[[\"1, 2, 3, 4, 6\",\"Correct. 3&gt;0 and 3&gt;0 → b = 3−3 = 0 (node 2); b==0 is true → node 4. (L4, Checking the test cases)\"],[\"1, 3, 4, 6\",\"The first IF is true (a&gt;0 and b&gt;0), so node 2 is not skipped.\"],[\"1, 2, 3, 5, 6\",\"After node 2, b = 0, so b==0 is true and the path goes to node 4, not the else.\"],[\"1, 3, 5, 6\",\"Both IFs are true here, so neither node 2 nor node 4 is skipped.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"<pre>public int bar(int a, int b) {\\n1.  if (a&gt;0 &amp;&amp; b&gt;0) {\\n2.     b = b-a;\\n    }\\n3.  if (b==0 || a&gt;5) {\\n4.     a = a+1;\\n    }\\n    else\\n5.     a = a*2;\\n6.  return (a+b);\\n}</pre>What does <b>bar(2, 5)</b> return?\",\"o\":[[\"6\",\"This takes node 4 (a = 3), but b = 3 is not 0 and a = 2 is not &gt; 5, so the else runs.\"],[\"7\",\"Correct. 2&gt;0 and 5&gt;0 → b = 5−2 = 3; b==0 false and a&gt;5 false → a = 2*2 = 4; return 4 + 3 = 7 (path 1,2,3,5,6). (L4, Checking the test cases)\"],[\"9\",\"This skips node 2 (b stays 5): 4 + 5 = 9. But the first IF is true.\"],[\"3\",\"This returns b alone; the function returns a + b.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"For the lecture's <code>foo(a, b, x)</code>, which path does <b>a = 4, b = 1, x = 3</b> follow?\",\"o\":[[\"1, 2, 3, 4, 6\",\"Node 2 needs b==0; here b = 1, so node 2 is skipped.\"],[\"1, 3, 5, 6\",\"x = 3 &gt; 1 makes the second IF true, so the path goes to node 4, not the else.\"],[\"1, 3, 4, 6\",\"Correct. b==0 is false → skip node 2; a==2 is false but x&gt;1 is true → node 4. (L4, Checking the test cases)\"],[\"1, 2, 3, 5, 6\",\"Both decisions are wrong here: node 2 is skipped and node 4 is taken.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"What does the lecture's <code>foo(2, 0, 4)</code> return? (<code>return (a+x)</code>)\",\"o\":[[\"7\",\"This skips node 2 (x = 4 + 1 = 5, 2 + 5 = 7), but a&gt;1 &amp;&amp; b==0 is true.\"],[\"3\",\"3 is the final x, not a + x.\"],[\"6\",\"This takes the else (x = 2 * 2 = 4, 2 + 4 = 6), but a==2 is true so node 4 runs.\"],[\"5\",\"Correct. x = 4/2 = 2 (node 2); a==2 → x = 2+1 = 3 (node 4); return 2 + 3 = 5. (L4, Checking the test cases)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: Since V(G) of foo() is 3, foo() needs at least 3 test cases to achieve branch coverage.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong: V(G) = 3 is the number of basis-path tests; branch coverage of foo() needs only 2.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. a=2, b=0 makes both IFs true and a=0, b=0, x=0 makes both false, so 2 tests give branch coverage. (L4, Checking the test cases)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the control flow graph of <code>foo()</code>, the predicate (decision) nodes are:\",\"o\":[[\"Nodes 1 and 3\",\"Correct. Nodes 1 and 3 are the two IFs, so V(G) = 2 + 1 = 3. (L4, Cyclomatic complexity and basis paths)\"],[\"Nodes 2 and 4\",\"Nodes 2 and 4 are assignments (x = x/a and x = x+1).\"],[\"Nodes 1 and 6\",\"Node 6 is the return statement, not a decision.\"],[\"Nodes 3 and 5\",\"Node 5 is the else assignment x = x*a, not a decision.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: In V(G) = E − N + 2P, P is the number of predicate nodes.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong: predicate nodes appear in the other formula, V(G) = predicate nodes + 1.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. P is the number of connected components, usually 1. (L4, Cyclomatic complexity and basis paths)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Running the lecture's triangle pseudocode on <b>(4, 4, 7)</b>, what is finally printed?\",\"o\":[[\"not a triangle\",\"7 ≥ 4+4 = 8 is false, so the \\\"not a triangle\\\" IF is not taken.\"],[\"isosceles\",\"Correct. a==b sets isosceles; not all equal; no side ≥ the sum of the others (7 &lt; 8); all positive. (L4, Triangle problem: functional (black-box) testing)\"],[\"equilateral\",\"Equilateral needs a==b and a==c; c = 7 differs.\"],[\"scalene\",\"Two sides are equal (a==b), so the type changes from scalene to isosceles.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"For the triangle test case <b>(3, 4, 5)</b>, which nodes of the statement-coverage table are NOT executed?\",\"o\":[[\"C, E, G, I\",\"C, E, G, I are the IF tests; every test case executes them.\"],[\"A, B, K\",\"A (read), B (type = scalene) and K (print) run for every test case.\"],[\"D, F, H, J\",\"Correct. (3,4,5) makes every IF false, so it skips isosceles (D), equilateral (F), not a triangle (H) and bad inputs (J). (L4, Triangle: statement and branch coverage)\"],[\"F only\",\"Only (4,4,4) reaches F, but (3,4,5) also skips D, H and J.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"If ANY input is allowed (not only the slide table's columns), the minimum number of test cases for <b>statement</b> coverage of the triangle pseudocode is:\",\"o\":[[\"3\",\"3 is the branch-coverage minimum using the slide table's tests.\"],[\"2\",\"2 is the statement-coverage minimum when limited to the slide table's tests.\"],[\"4\",\"4 is the number of columns in the slide table, not a minimum.\"],[\"1\",\"Correct. (0,0,0) alone sets isosceles, equilateral, not a triangle (0 ≥ 0+0) and bad inputs (0 ≤ 0), running every line A to K. (L4, Minimal coverage sets)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Following the \\\"area of a triangle from 3 points\\\" functional tests, what is the expected area for the points <b>(0,0), (6,0), (0,4)</b>?\",\"o\":[[\"24\",\"This is base × height without halving.\"],[\"12\",\"Correct. Base 6, height 4: area = ½ × 6 × 4 = 12. (L4, Functional tests: area of a triangle from 3 points)\"],[\"10\",\"This adds the sides 6 + 4 instead of using the area.\"],[\"Not a triangle\",\"The points are not collinear (not all on one line), so they form a triangle.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: The slide lists (1,2,4) as a \\\"bad inputs\\\" case, but running the pseudocode on it prints \\\"not a triangle\\\".\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. No side is ≤ 0, and 4 ≥ 1+2, so the last IF that is true sets \\\"not a triangle\\\". (L4, Note on the bad-inputs row)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong: none of 1, 2, 4 is ≤ 0, so the bad-inputs IF is false; 4 ≥ 3 sets \\\"not a triangle\\\".\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"The two structural testing approaches for object-oriented software covered in the lecture are:\",\"o\":[[\"Statement and branch coverage\",\"These are conventional criteria that do not seem appropriate for OO complexity.\"],[\"Data flow and error forcing\",\"These are data coverage types from dynamic white-box testing.\"],[\"Method-message testing and function pair testing\",\"Correct. The lecture covers MM testing and function pair testing. (L4, OOP testing)\"],[\"Peer review and inspection\",\"These are formal review types (static white-box).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: Testing object-oriented software presents some new challenges, but many conventional techniques are still appropriate.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture says exactly this. (L4, OOP testing)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong: the lecture says many conventional techniques are still appropriate.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Function pair (FP) testing is usually based on a:\",\"o\":[[\"Control flow graph\",\"The control flow graph is used for cyclomatic complexity and basis paths.\"],[\"Requirements document\",\"The requirements document is the basis of functional testing.\"],[\"V-model\",\"The V-model links development phases to test levels, not method pairs.\"],[\"State machine diagram\",\"Correct. FP testing covers all sequences of two method executions, usually based on a state machine diagram. (L4, OOP testing)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Which of the following stack function pairs ends in an <b>error</b>?\",\"o\":[[\"new-push\",\"new gives Empty, and push from Empty goes to Normal: no error.\"],[\"push(from empty)-pop\",\"After push from Empty the stack is Normal; pop from Normal is valid.\"],[\"pop(into empty)-pop\",\"Correct. After popping into Empty, another pop is an error (pair 14). (L4, Stack example: MM and FP)\"],[\"pop(normal→normal)-push\",\"After pop the stack is still Normal; push from Normal is valid (still in normal).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"How many function pairs does the lecture list for the stack example?\",\"o\":[[\"3\",\"3 is the number of stack states (Empty, Normal, Full), or the MM sequence length.\"],[\"8\",\"The lecture lists more than 8 pairs.\"],[\"16\",\"The lecture lists 14, not 16.\"],[\"14\",\"Correct. The lecture lists 14 pairs, from new-pop (error) to pop(into empty)-pop (error). (L4, Stack example: MM and FP)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In the stack state machine, a <b>pop</b> from the <b>Normal</b> state can lead to:\",\"o\":[[\"Normal or Empty\",\"Correct. pop: Normal→Normal or Normal→Empty. (L4, Stack example: MM and FP)\"],[\"Full only\",\"pop never leads to Full; push from Normal can.\"],[\"An error\",\"pop gives an error only from Empty.\"],[\"Normal or Full\",\"Normal or Full is where push from Normal leads.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: Statement and branch coverage cannot be applied to object-oriented software at all.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong: the lecture says they can be applied to OO software.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. They can be applied, but they do not seem appropriate because the interactions between methods must be tested. (L4, OOP testing)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"What is the advantage of function pair coverage over MM testing?\",\"o\":[[\"It guarantees every statement is executed\",\"Neither criterion is about every statement; MM does not even subsume statement coverage.\"],[\"It tests sequences of two method executions, catching state-dependent faults like push on Full\",\"Correct. FP tests pairs of method executions, so it catches interaction and state-dependent bugs (push on full, pop on empty) that single-call MM misses. (L4, Lecture questions (answers))\"],[\"It needs fewer test cases than MM testing\",\"FP needs more tests (14 pairs for the stack) than the simple MM sequence create, push, pop.\"],[\"It does not need a state machine diagram\",\"FP is usually based on a state machine diagram.\"]],\"a\":1}]},{\"n\":5,\"title\":\"Web Site Testing\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Introduction and why web testing matters\",\"pts\":[\"Objectives:<br>• fundamentals of web-page testing<br>• black-box testing of web pages<br>• white-box and gray-box testing<br>• configuration and compatibility testing.\",\"A <b>web site</b> is a collection of one or more web pages (text, graphics, links, sounds…) grouped under the <b>same domain</b>. It must have a <b>domain name</b> and a <b>web host</b>.\",\"Importance: a site built for Internet Explorer may fail in Mozilla, Chrome or on Linux, so testing makes sure the site works on <b>every browser and platform</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-web-site\",\"qa-browsers\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-web-site\":1,\"qa-browsers\":2},\"figH\":{\"qa-web-site\":\"A web site: pages under one domain, with a domain name and a web host\",\"qa-browsers\":\"Built for Internet Explorer, untested elsewhere: why every browser and platform is tested\"}},{\"h\":\"Web page fundamentals\",\"pts\":[\"<b>1. Home page</b>: the default page. It usually has a header at the top with the <b>source name</b> of the site (text only, or designs with graphics).\",\"<b>2. Links</b> connect local and remote pages to the home page. Used to:<br>• navigate to other pages<br>• direct the user to a different location<br>• download files<br>• open other Internet tools such as the default e-mail client (e.g. Outlook).\",\"<b>3. Content</b> is the <b>most important</b> part. Forms:<br>• text documents<br>• graphics<br>• sounds<br>• downloadable movie clips<br>• fields for user data<br>• rotating advertisements<br>• dynamically changing text.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-page-parts\",\"qa-link-uses\",\"qa-content-forms\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-page-parts\":0,\"qa-link-uses\":1,\"qa-content-forms\":2},\"figH\":{\"qa-page-parts\":\"The three components of a web page: home page, links, content\",\"qa-link-uses\":\"The four uses of links: click each one\",\"qa-content-forms\":\"The seven forms content takes, live on one page\"}},{\"h\":\"13 elements of web site testing\",\"table\":[[\"#\",\"Element\",\"What to check\"],[\"1\",\"User interface\",\"The site is simple to use\"],[\"2\",\"Instructions\",\"All relevant instructions are present\"],[\"3\",\"Site map / navigational map\",\"Map exists and is appropriate; every link on it exists and works; navigation bar on every page\"],[\"4\",\"Content\",\"Professional: no plagiarism; minimal bold, big fonts, blinking text; web references are hyperlinked\"],[\"5\",\"Colors/backgrounds\",\"Content remains easy to read\"],[\"6\",\"Images\",\"\\\"A picture is worth a thousand words\\\": use them to convey messages\"],[\"7\",\"Tables\",\"Positioned so users do not keep scrolling\"],[\"8\",\"Wrap-around\",\"Text wraps correctly around images\"],[\"9\",\"Functionality\",\"All links, database connection and forms\"],[\"10\",\"Links\",\"The vehicle that navigates from page to page\"],[\"11\",\"Forms\",\"Information is accepted and the submit button works\"],[\"12\",\"Data verification\",\"Input matches the defined business rules\"],[\"13\",\"Cookies\",\"Cookies work; login information in cookies is encrypted\"]],\"pts\":[\"2. Instructions: make sure the web site contains all relevant instructions. Since many people use web sites, some may require some clarification.\",\"3. Site map or navigational mapping: test whether the site has a map, because experienced users generally know exactly where they want to go and avoid lengthy instructions. Example site map on the slide: http://www.apple.com/sitemap/\",\"4. Content: check plagiarism and keep bold text, big fonts and blinking texts minimal, as they may not appeal to the customer, who might thus not revisit the web site.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-site-map\",\"qa-13-elements\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-site-map\":1},\"figH\":{\"qa-site-map\":\"Site map testing: checks (a) to (d) run on a small site\",\"qa-13-elements\":\"The 13 elements: each one as a page that fails and a page that passes\"}},{\"h\":\"Black-box testing of a web page\",\"pts\":[\"Example screen: the Infosys web site shows the fundamental elements: <b>text, graphics and hyperlinks</b> (plus forms).\",\"Figure 8.5 (Website with Texts, Hyperlinks, and Graphics): a screen image of the Infosys home page.<br>• <b>Top left</b>: the Infosys logo with the slogan \\\"Building Tomorrow's Enterprise\\\".<br>• <b>Top right</b>: links Global [Change] | Contact Us | Careers | Investors | Blogs | Microsites, and a Search box.<br>• <b>Navigation bar</b>: Home, Building Tomorrow's Enterprise, Offerings, Global Sourcing, Infosys Labs, Sustainability, Newsroom, About Us.<br>• <b>Below it</b>: a banner picture (hands shaping a clay pot) with the text \\\"TRANSFORM. Build Tomorrow's Enterprise Today\\\".\",\"<b>Text</b>: target audience level, terminology, depth of content, subject matter, accuracy, spelling, punctuation, correct contact info (phones, addresses).\",\"<b>Hyperlinks</b>: each goes to the correct destination and opens in the same tab or a new window as specified (if no spec, check that it works). Links must be <b>obvious</b> (underlined text, mouse pointer changes). For an e-mail link, send an e-mail and verify you get a response.\",\"<b>Graphics</b>: all load and display properly. A missing or wrongly named graphic will not load, and the page shows an error in its place.\",\"<b>Forms</b> are text boxes, list boxes, and other fields that allow a user to <b>enter or select information</b> on a Web page (e.g. Google's Gmail sign-up form). Check:<br>• Are the form fields positioned properly?<br>• Are the fields the correct size?<br>• Do they accept correct data?<br>• Do they reject bad data?\",\"Figure 8.6 (Google's Form): the \\\"Get started with Gmail\\\" sign-up form. Fields:<br>• First name<br>• Last name<br>• Desired Login Name (followed by @gmail.com, with \\\"Examples: JSmith, John.Smith\\\" and a \\\"check availability!\\\" button)<br>• Choose a password (\\\"Minimum of 8 characters in length\\\", with a \\\"Password strength\\\" indicator)<br>• Re-enter password<br>• and two ticked checkboxes, \\\"Stay signed in\\\" and \\\"Enable Web History\\\" (with a \\\"Learn More\\\" link).\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-web-parts\",\"qa-gmail-form\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-web-parts\":4,\"qa-gmail-form\":6},\"figH\":{\"qa-web-parts\":\"Figure 8.5: a web site's text, hyperlinks and graphics, and what to test in each\",\"qa-gmail-form\":\"Figure 8.6: Google's form and the four checks for form fields\"}},{\"h\":\"White-box and gray-box testing of a web site\",\"pts\":[\"<b>White-box</b>: the tester knows the internal design. Elements to test:\",\"1. <b>Dynamic content</b>: graphics and text that vary with conditions (time of day, weather, stock tickers).\",\"2. <b>Database-driven pages</b>: catalogs and inventories (e-commerce).\",\"3. <b>Programmatically created pages</b>: the designer types entries in a database, drags and drops elements in a layout program and presses a button to generate the HTML.\",\"4. <b>Server performance and loading</b>: popular sites get millions of hits per day, so simulate millions of connections and downloads.\",\"5. <b>Security</b>: denial of service and buffer overflow attacks.\",\"<b>Gray-box testing</b>: a mix of black-box and white-box. Its purpose is to <b>isolate defects related to bad design or bad implementation</b> of the web. ‡ (not on the slides) It is possible for web sites because the tester works from the user's view but can also see the page's HTML/scripts.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-dynamic\",\"qa-db-pages\",\"qa-load\",\"qa-security\",\"qa-box-views\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-dynamic\":1,\"qa-db-pages\":3,\"qa-load\":4,\"qa-security\":5,\"qa-box-views\":6},\"figH\":{\"qa-dynamic\":\"Dynamic content: change the time of day and the weather, cover every branch\",\"qa-db-pages\":\"From database rows to a catalog page: database-driven and programmatically created pages\",\"qa-load\":\"Load testing: simulate from one to millions of connections\",\"qa-security\":\"The two security tests: denial of service and buffer overflow\",\"qa-box-views\":\"Black, white and gray box on one page: gray box isolates the defect\"}},{\"h\":\"Configuration and compatibility testing\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Hardware configurations</b>:<br>(1) CPU types<br>(2) RAM<br>(3) graphics cards<br>(4) video capture cards<br>(5) audio cards<br>(6) monitors/display devices<br>(7) network cards.\",\"<b>Compatibility</b>:<br>(1) various font sizes<br>(2) browsers with CSS<br>(3) various screen resolutions<br>(4) various memory sizes<br>(5) different network environments.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-config-count\",\"qa-compat-view\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-config-count\":0,\"qa-compat-view\":1},\"figH\":{\"qa-config-count\":\"How the configurations multiply: set the variants of each factor\",\"qa-compat-view\":\"One page under different font sizes, CSS, resolutions, memory and networks\"}},{\"h\":\"Automatic web testing tools\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Automatic web testing tools</b> check:<br>• browser compatibility<br>• load, stress and performance<br>• broken hyperlinks<br>• web functional/GUI testing<br>• spelling<br>• security<br>• database<br>• OOP<br>• network<br>• web applications (<b>Selenium</b> and <b>Cypress</b>).\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-web-tools\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-web-tools\":0},\"figH\":{\"qa-web-tools\":\"The ten checks of automatic tools, ending with a Cypress test\"}}],\"cards\":[[\"Web site (definition)\",\"A collection of one or more web pages under the same domain; needs a domain name and a web host.\"],[\"3 fundamental components of a web page\",\"Home page, links, content.\"],[\"Most important part of a web site\",\"Content.\"],[\"Site-map testing checks\",\"Map appropriate, every link exists, navigation bar on every page, each link works on each page.\"],[\"Cookies testing\",\"Cookies work and stored login info is encrypted.\"],[\"Data verification\",\"User input must match the business rules defined for the system.\"],[\"Black-box elements of a web page\",\"Text, hyperlinks, graphics, forms.\"],[\"Obvious hyperlinks\",\"Underlined text and the mouse pointer changes.\"],[\"Dynamic content\",\"Graphics/text that vary with conditions: time of day, weather, stock tickers.\"],[\"White-box web elements\",\"Dynamic content, database-driven pages, programmatically created pages, server performance and loading, security.\"],[\"Security tests for web sites\",\"Denial of service and buffer overflow attacks.\"],[\"Gray-box testing purpose\",\"Isolate defects related to bad design or bad implementation of the web.\"],[\"Web application test tools\",\"Selenium and Cypress.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"What basic elements of a web page can easily be tested with a black-box approach?\",\"Text (audience, terminology, accuracy, spelling, contact info), hyperlinks (correct destination, same tab or new window, obvious, e-mail links), graphics (load and display, no missing or misnamed images) and forms (positioned, right size, accept good data, reject bad data).\"],[\"What basic elements can easily be tested with a white-box approach?\",\"Dynamic content, database-driven pages, programmatically created pages, server performance and loading, and security (denial of service, buffer overflow).\"],[\"Why is gray-box testing possible with web site testing?\",\"‡ (not on the slides) Web pages are built from HTML and scripts that the tester can see (view source) while still testing from the user's view, so black-box and white-box can be mixed. The aim (on the slide) is to isolate defects caused by bad design or bad implementation.\"],[\"List what configuration and compatibility testing check for a web site.\",\"Hardware: CPU types, RAM, graphics cards, video capture cards, audio cards, monitors, network cards. Compatibility: font sizes, browsers with CSS, screen resolutions, memory sizes, network environments.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"Which testing strategy checks that a web site works properly across different hardware and software environments?\",\"o\":[[\"White box testing\",\"White-box web testing uses knowledge of the internal design (dynamic content, server load, security); it does not target different hardware and browsers.\"],[\"Black box testing\",\"Black-box web testing checks text, hyperlinks, graphics and forms from the user's view on one setup; running across environments is compatibility testing.\"],[\"Gray box testing\",\"That isolates design or implementation defects.\"],[\"Compatibility testing\",\"Correct. Configuration and compatibility testing covers hardware and browsers, resolutions and networks.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"Which is the MOST important part of a web site?\",\"o\":[[\"Home page\",\"The home page is the site's default page with the header and source name, one of three fundamentals, but the lecture calls content the most important part.\"],[\"Links\",\"Links connect pages and open downloads or e-mail, one of the three fundamentals, but the lecture names content as the most important part.\"],[\"Content\",\"Correct. The lecture calls content the most important part.\"],[\"Cookies\",\"Cookies are only element 13 of the web testing list (check they work and login info is encrypted); content is the most important part.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Graphics and text that vary depending on conditions (time of day, weather, stock tickers) are called:\",\"o\":[[\"Database-driven pages\",\"Database-driven pages show catalogs and inventories from a database (e-commerce); they are not defined by changing with time or weather.\"],[\"Dynamic content\",\"Correct. The lecture's first white-box element, dynamic content, is graphics and text that vary with conditions such as the time of day, weather or stock tickers (L5, White-box testing).\"],[\"Programmatically created pages\",\"Those are pages generated from a layout program.\"],[\"Wrap-around\",\"Wrap-around is web testing element 8: checking that text wraps correctly around images, not content that changes with conditions.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Security testing of a web site (white-box) includes:\",\"o\":[[\"Spelling and punctuation\",\"Spelling and punctuation are checked in black-box TEXT testing of a page, not in white-box security testing.\"],[\"Denial of service and buffer overflow attacks\",\"Correct. Security is the fifth white-box web element, and the lecture names denial of service and buffer overflow attacks as what to test (L5, White-box testing).\"],[\"Font sizes\",\"Font sizes are item 1 of the compatibility testing list, not a security concern.\"],[\"Site map\",\"The site map is element 3 of web testing (navigation: links on the map exist and work); it has nothing to do with attacks.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Dynamic content is graphics and text that vary depending on some conditions.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture defines dynamic content, the first white-box web element, as graphics and text that vary depending on conditions such as time of day, weather or stock tickers (L5).\"],[\"False\",\"The statement matches the lecture's definition of dynamic content word for word (time of day, weather, stock tickers), so it is true.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Summary T/F\"},{\"q\":\"\\\"A navigational map helps the user to go straight to the information they want.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Experienced users know where they want to go and avoid long instructions.\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture supports it: experienced users know where they want to go, and a site map lets them reach it directly instead of reading long instructions.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Summary T/F\"},{\"q\":\"The purpose of gray-box web site testing is to:\",\"o\":[[\"Replace all black-box tests\",\"Gray-box testing mixes black-box and white-box methods; it does not replace black-box tests.\"],[\"Isolate defects related to bad design or bad implementation\",\"Correct. Gray-box testing mixes black- and white-box, and its stated purpose is to isolate defects related to bad design or bad implementation of the web (L5, Gray-box testing).\"],[\"Only test the server load\",\"Server performance and loading is a white-box element (simulating millions of connections); gray-box aims to isolate design and implementation defects.\"],[\"Check spelling\",\"Spelling is checked in black-box text testing; gray-box testing aims to isolate defects from bad design or implementation.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT in the lecture's list of hardware configuration checks?\",\"o\":[[\"CPU types\",\"CPU types are item 1 of the 7 hardware configuration checks, so they ARE in the list (L5, Configuration testing).\"],[\"Audio cards\",\"Audio cards are item 5 of the 7 hardware configuration checks, so they ARE in the list.\"],[\"Keyboard layout\",\"Correct. The list is CPU, RAM, graphics, video capture, audio, monitors, network cards.\"],[\"Network cards\",\"Network cards are item 7, the last of the hardware configuration checks, so they ARE in the list.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"When testing cookies used for storing login information, you must make sure that:\",\"o\":[[\"They are never used\",\"The lecture allows cookies; element 13 says to test that they work, not to ban them.\"],[\"The cookies work and the information is encrypted\",\"Correct. Element 13 of web testing says to make sure cookies work and that login information stored in them is encrypted (L5, Web site testing elements).\"],[\"They are stored as plain text for speed\",\"Plain-text login data is the opposite of the requirement; the lecture says login information in cookies must be encrypted.\"],[\"They are blinking\",\"Blinking relates to text content (minimise blinking text), not to cookies; the cookie check is that they work and are encrypted.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which tools are named for testing web applications?\",\"o\":[[\"Selenium and Cypress\",\"Correct. The lecture's list of automatic web testing tools ends with web applications, naming Selenium and Cypress (L5, Tools).\"],[\"Excel and Word\",\"Excel and Word are office programs for spreadsheets and documents, not web testing tools; the lecture names Selenium and Cypress.\"],[\"Git and Docker\",\"Git (version control) and Docker (containers) are not testing tools and are not in the lecture; it names Selenium and Cypress.\"],[\"Figma and Balsamiq\",\"Figma and Balsamiq are design and wireframing tools; the lecture's web application testing tools are Selenium and Cypress.\"]],\"a\":0}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"According to the lecture, a web site must have:\",\"o\":[[\"A database and a login form\",\"Many sites have these, but the lecture's requirement is a domain name and a web host.\"],[\"A domain name and a web host\",\"Correct. A web site is pages grouped under the same domain, and it must have a domain name and a web host.\"],[\"Cookies and rotating advertisements\",\"These are optional elements, not requirements.\"],[\"At least 13 pages\",\"13 is the number of testing elements, not a page count.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT one of the uses of links listed in the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"Downloading files\",\"Downloading files IS one of the lecture's uses of links, alongside navigating, redirecting and opening Internet tools.\"],[\"Encrypting the user's login information\",\"Correct. Encrypting login data is checked under cookies, not a use of links.\"],[\"Opening the default e-mail client\",\"Listed: links can open other Internet tools such as Outlook.\"],[\"Directing the user to a different location\",\"Directing the user to a different location IS one of the lecture's listed uses of links.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A tester checks that paragraphs flow correctly around a product photo instead of overlapping it. Which of the 13 web-testing elements is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Images\",\"The images element is about using pictures to convey a message, not text flowing around them.\"],[\"Tables\",\"Tables are about positioning so users do not keep scrolling.\"],[\"Colors/backgrounds\",\"That checks content stays easy to read on the chosen colors.\"],[\"Wrap-around\",\"Correct. Wrap-around checks that text wraps correctly around images.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"On a registration page, a tester checks that a \\\"date of birth\\\" entry follows the company's defined business rules. Which web-testing element is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Data verification\",\"Correct. Data verification checks that input matches the defined business rules.\"],[\"Cookies\",\"Cookies are about stored data working and login info being encrypted.\"],[\"Instructions\",\"That checks that all relevant instructions are present.\"],[\"Site map\",\"That checks the navigational map and its links.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"When black-box testing an <b>e-mail link</b> on a web page, the lecture says you should:\",\"o\":[[\"Check only that the link text is underlined\",\"Being obvious is required for all links, but an e-mail link must also actually work.\"],[\"Read the server's HTML code\",\"That is white-box or gray-box work, not the black-box check.\"],[\"Send an e-mail through it and verify that you get a response\",\"Correct. This is the lecture's specific check for e-mail links.\"],[\"Simulate millions of connections to it\",\"That is server performance and loading testing.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"\\\"If a graphic is missing or wrongly named, it will not load and the page shows an error in its place.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. This is why black-box testing checks that all graphics load and display properly.\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture states this in the graphics part of black-box web testing.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"An online shop's catalog and inventory pages are filled from stored product records. Which white-box web element is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Dynamic content\",\"Dynamic content varies with conditions such as time of day, weather or stock tickers.\"],[\"Programmatically created pages\",\"Those are generated by pressing a button in a layout program after entering data and dragging elements.\"],[\"Security\",\"Security is about attacks such as denial of service and buffer overflow.\"],[\"Database-driven pages\",\"Correct. The lecture's examples are catalogs and inventories (e-commerce).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Which white-box web element requires simulating millions of connections and downloads?\",\"o\":[[\"Dynamic content\",\"Dynamic content is graphics and text that vary with conditions (time, weather, stock tickers); it does not need millions of simulated connections.\"],[\"Server performance and loading\",\"Correct. Popular sites get millions of hits per day, so the load must be simulated.\"],[\"Programmatically created pages\",\"That is about how pages are generated, not about load.\"],[\"Gray-box testing\",\"Gray-box is a mix of black- and white-box aimed at design/implementation defects.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT in the lecture's compatibility testing list?\",\"o\":[[\"Various screen resolutions\",\"Various screen resolutions is item 3 of the compatibility list, so it IS listed.\"],[\"Various font sizes\",\"Various font sizes is item 1 of the compatibility list, so it IS listed.\"],[\"Different printer models\",\"Correct. The list is font sizes, browsers with CSS, screen resolutions, memory sizes and network environments.\"],[\"Different network environments\",\"Different network environments is item 5 of the compatibility list, so it IS listed.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"A designer types entries into a database, drags and drops elements in a layout program, then presses a button to generate the HTML. These are:\",\"o\":[[\"Programmatically created pages\",\"Correct. This is the lecture's description of programmatically created pages.\"],[\"Database-driven pages\",\"Those display catalogs and inventories from a database.\"],[\"Dynamic content\",\"Dynamic content changes with conditions like weather or time.\"],[\"Home page\",\"The home page is the site's default page with a header and source name; generating HTML from a layout program describes programmatically created pages.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A web site is a collection of one or more web pages grouped under the same ………\",\"o\":[[\"domain\",\"Correct. The lecture defines a web site as pages grouped under the same domain. (L5, Introduction)\"],[\"browser\",\"Wrong. A site must work on every browser; the browser does not define what groups the pages.\"],[\"site map\",\"Wrong. A site map is one of the 13 testing elements, a navigation aid, not what groups the pages.\"],[\"cookie\",\"Wrong. Cookies store information such as login data; they do not define a web site.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: A site that works in Internet Explorer will also work in Mozilla, Chrome and on Linux, so cross-browser testing is optional.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong. The lecture says a site built for Internet Explorer may fail in Mozilla, Chrome or on Linux.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. That is exactly why testing must make sure the site works on every browser and platform. (L5, Introduction)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"The default page of a web site, usually with a header at the top showing the source name of the site, is the:\",\"o\":[[\"Site map\",\"Wrong. The site map is a map of the site's pages and links, not the default page.\"],[\"Home page\",\"Correct. The home page is the default page and usually has a header with the source name. (L5, Web page fundamentals)\"],[\"Content\",\"Wrong. Content is the most important part (text, graphics, sounds…), not the default page.\"],[\"Navigation bar\",\"Wrong. A navigation bar should appear on every page; it is not itself the default page.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT one of the forms of web content listed in the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"Rotating advertisements\",\"Wrong. Rotating advertisements are listed as a form of content.\"],[\"Downloadable movie clips\",\"Wrong. Downloadable movie clips are listed as a form of content.\"],[\"Dynamically changing text\",\"Wrong. Dynamically changing text is listed as a form of content.\"],[\"Buffer overflow attacks\",\"Correct. Buffer overflow attacks belong to white-box security testing, not to the forms of content. (L5, Web page fundamentals)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: Links can be used to download files and to open other Internet tools such as the default e-mail client.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture lists downloading files and opening the default e-mail client (e.g. Outlook) as uses of links. (L5, Web page fundamentals)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. Both uses are in the lecture's list for links.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Why does the lecture say a web site should be tested for a site map?\",\"o\":[[\"Because search engines need it to index the site\",\"Wrong. The lecture does not mention search engines; its reason is about experienced users.\"],[\"Because it encrypts the user's login information\",\"Wrong. Encrypting login information is checked under cookies.\"],[\"Because experienced users know where they want to go and avoid lengthy instructions\",\"Correct. That is the reason the lecture gives for checking the site map. (L5, 13 elements)\"],[\"Because it makes the graphics load faster\",\"Wrong. Graphics loading is a black-box graphics check, not the purpose of a site map.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Under the <b>Content</b> element, bold text, big fonts and blinking text should be kept minimal because:\",\"o\":[[\"They may not appeal to the customer, who might not revisit the site\",\"Correct. The lecture says such text may not appeal to the customer, who might not revisit the web site. (L5, 13 elements)\"],[\"They break the database connection\",\"Wrong. The database connection is part of the Functionality element and is unrelated to fonts.\"],[\"They prevent the submit button from working\",\"Wrong. The submit button is checked under the Forms element.\"],[\"They count as plagiarism\",\"Wrong. Plagiarism is a separate content check about copied material, not about formatting.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which of the 13 web-testing elements makes sure the content remains easy to read?\",\"o\":[[\"Wrap-around\",\"Wrong. Wrap-around checks that text wraps correctly around images.\"],[\"Colors/backgrounds\",\"Correct. Colors and backgrounds are checked so the content remains easy to read. (L5, 13 elements)\"],[\"Tables\",\"Wrong. Tables are checked so users do not keep scrolling.\"],[\"Instructions\",\"Wrong. Instructions checks that all relevant instructions are present.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A tester checks that a long price list is placed on the page so that users do not have to keep scrolling. Which element is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Images\",\"Wrong. Images are about conveying messages (\\\"a picture is worth a thousand words\\\").\"],[\"User interface\",\"Wrong. User interface checks that the site is simple to use in general.\"],[\"Tables\",\"Correct. Tables must be positioned so users do not keep scrolling. (L5, 13 elements)\"],[\"Data verification\",\"Wrong. Data verification checks input against the business rules.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"\\\"A picture is worth a thousand words\\\" is the lecture's note for which web-testing element?\",\"o\":[[\"Content\",\"Wrong. Content is about plagiarism, minimal bold or blinking text, and hyperlinked references.\"],[\"Graphics loading\",\"Wrong. Graphics loading is a black-box check that images load and display properly.\"],[\"Wrap-around\",\"Wrong. Wrap-around is about text wrapping around images, not using them to convey messages.\"],[\"Images\",\"Correct. The Images element says to use pictures to convey messages. (L5, 13 elements)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: In the 13 elements, Functionality covers all the links, the database connection and the forms.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture's Functionality row lists all links, database connection and forms. (L5, 13 elements)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. Those three are exactly what the Functionality element lists.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which of the 13 elements checks that the user interface is <b>simple to use</b>?\",\"o\":[[\"User interface\",\"Correct. The first element, User interface, checks that the site is simple to use. (L5, 13 elements)\"],[\"Site map\",\"Wrong. The site map checks that a map exists, its links work and a navigation bar is on every page.\"],[\"Cookies\",\"Wrong. Cookies checks that cookies work and login information is encrypted.\"],[\"Links\",\"Wrong. Links are the vehicle that navigates from page to page.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a black-box check of the <b>text</b> of a web page?\",\"o\":[[\"Spelling and punctuation\",\"Wrong. Spelling and punctuation are listed text checks.\"],[\"Simulating millions of connections\",\"Correct. That is white-box server performance and loading, not a text check. (L5, Black-box testing)\"],[\"Correct contact information\",\"Wrong. Correct phones and addresses are listed text checks.\"],[\"Target audience level\",\"Wrong. Audience level is the first text check listed.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"According to the lecture, a hyperlink is <b>obvious</b> when:\",\"o\":[[\"It opens in a new window\",\"Wrong. Same tab or new window depends on the spec; it is not what makes a link obvious.\"],[\"It is listed on the site map\",\"Wrong. Site-map links are checked to exist and work, which is a different check.\"],[\"The text is underlined and the mouse pointer changes\",\"Correct. Those are the lecture's signs of an obvious link. (L5, Black-box testing)\"],[\"It is written in bold, big fonts\",\"Wrong. The lecture says bold and big fonts should be kept minimal.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: If there is no specification for whether a hyperlink opens in the same tab or a new window, the tester checks that the link works.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. With no spec, the lecture says to check that the link works. (L5, Black-box testing)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. The lecture explicitly covers the no-spec case: check that it works.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"……… are text boxes, list boxes and other fields that allow a user to enter or select information on a web page.\",\"o\":[[\"Hyperlinks\",\"Wrong. Hyperlinks take the user to a destination; they are not fields for entering data.\"],[\"Graphics\",\"Wrong. Graphics are images that must load and display properly.\"],[\"Cookies\",\"Wrong. Cookies store information such as login data; the user does not type into them.\"],[\"Forms\",\"Correct. This is the lecture's definition of forms. (L5, Black-box testing)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"On the Gmail sign-up form (Figure 8.6) the password must be a minimum of 8 characters. A tester types a 5-character password and checks that the form refuses it. Which form check is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Do the fields reject bad data?\",\"Correct. A 5-character password breaks the 8-character minimum, so the tester is checking that bad data is rejected. (L5, Black-box testing)\"],[\"Are the fields the correct size?\",\"Wrong. Field size is about how big the box is on the page, not the length rule.\"],[\"Are the form fields positioned properly?\",\"Wrong. Positioning is about where the fields sit on the page.\"],[\"Do the fields accept correct data?\",\"Wrong. That check would use a valid password of 8 or more characters.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"The Infosys home page (Figure 8.5) is used in the lecture to show which fundamental elements of a web page?\",\"o\":[[\"Dynamic content and database-driven pages\",\"Wrong. Those are white-box elements, not what the Infosys screen illustrates.\"],[\"Text, graphics and hyperlinks\",\"Correct. Figure 8.5 is titled \\\"Website with Texts, Hyperlinks, and Graphics\\\". (L5, Black-box testing)\"],[\"CPU types and RAM\",\"Wrong. Those are hardware configuration checks.\"],[\"Cookies and data verification\",\"Wrong. Those are two of the 13 testing elements, not what the figure shows.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: In white-box testing of a web site, the tester knows the internal design.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture defines white-box web testing as the tester knowing the internal design. (L5, White-box and gray-box)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. Knowing the internal design is exactly what makes it white-box.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT one of the white-box elements of a web site?\",\"o\":[[\"Security\",\"Wrong. Security (denial of service, buffer overflow) is white-box element 5.\"],[\"Dynamic content\",\"Wrong. Dynamic content is white-box element 1.\"],[\"Wrap-around\",\"Correct. Wrap-around is one of the 13 general testing elements, not a white-box element. (L5, White-box and gray-box)\"],[\"Server performance and loading\",\"Wrong. Server performance and loading is white-box element 4.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: Gray-box testing is a mix of black-box and white-box testing.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture defines gray-box testing as a mix of the two. (L5, White-box and gray-box)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. The lecture says gray-box is exactly that mix.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"Various screen resolutions\\\" appears in which list of the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"Hardware configurations\",\"Wrong. Hardware lists CPU types, RAM, cards and monitors, not screen resolutions.\"],[\"White-box elements\",\"Wrong. White-box elements are dynamic content, database-driven and programmatic pages, server load and security.\"],[\"Black-box elements\",\"Wrong. Black-box elements are text, hyperlinks, graphics and forms.\"],[\"Compatibility\",\"Correct. Screen resolutions are compatibility item 3. (L5, Configuration and compatibility)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: RAM and video capture cards belong to the compatibility list, not to the hardware configuration list.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong. RAM and video capture cards are in the hardware configuration list.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Both are hardware configurations; the compatibility list has memory sizes, not RAM. (L5, Configuration and compatibility)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT listed as something automatic web testing tools check?\",\"o\":[[\"Domain name registration\",\"Correct. Registering a domain name is not in the tools list. (L5, Automatic tools)\"],[\"Broken hyperlinks\",\"Wrong. Broken hyperlinks are in the tools list.\"],[\"Load, stress and performance\",\"Wrong. Load, stress and performance are in the tools list.\"],[\"Browser compatibility\",\"Wrong. Browser compatibility is the first item in the tools list.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: Browsers with CSS is one of the items checked in compatibility testing.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Browsers with CSS is compatibility item 2. (L5, Configuration and compatibility)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. The compatibility list includes browsers with CSS.\"]],\"a\":0}]},{\"n\":6,\"title\":\"Software Quality Assurance (SQA)\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"What is quality?\",\"pts\":[\"Definition: quality means <b>conformance to requirements</b>. It is the set of attributes <b>valued by end-users</b>.\",\"It is affected by <b>directly measurable factors</b> (e.g. defects) and <b>indirectly measurable factors</b> (e.g. maintainability, usability).\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-measurable\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-measurable\":1},\"figH\":{\"qa-measurable\":\"Quality is affected by directly and indirectly measurable factors: sort the slide's examples\"}},{\"h\":\"8 software quality factors\",\"table\":[[\"Factor\",\"Meaning\"],[\"Reliability\",\"Performs its intended function accurately\"],[\"Efficiency\",\"Optimal use of computing resources\"],[\"Integrity\",\"Control over access and security\"],[\"Usability\",\"Effort required to learn and operate the program\"],[\"Maintainability\",\"Effort required to locate and fix errors\"],[\"Flexibility\",\"Effort required to change the program\"],[\"Portability\",\"Effort required to transfer the program to another system\"],[\"Reusability\",\"Which parts can be used in other applications\"]],\"fig\":[\"qa-eight-factors\"],\"figH\":{\"qa-eight-factors\":\"The 8 quality factors: explore each meaning, then match meanings to factors\"}},{\"h\":\"SQA vs SQC; testing vs QA\",\"table\":[[\"\",\"SQA (Assurance)\",\"SQC (Control)\"],[\"Idea\",\"Prevention\",\"Detection\"],[\"Focus\",\"Process (proactive)\",\"Product (reactive)\"],[\"Goal\",\"Ensure standards and procedures are applied correctly\",\"Evaluate product quality and screen out non-compliant items\"]],\"fig\":[\"qa-sqa-sqc\"],\"figH\":{\"qa-sqa-sqc\":\"SQA prevents on the process, SQC detects on the product and screens out non-compliant items\"}},{\"h\":\"Testing vs QA; roles and tasks\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Software testing</b>:<br>• part of SQA<br>• detection-oriented<br>• evaluates spec, design and coding<br>• performed under specified (normal and abnormal) conditions<br>• main goal: find and report bugs<br>• done by testers.\",\"<b>QA</b>:<br>• broader than testing<br>• prevents defects<br>• ensures correct processes, standards and methodologies<br>• uses <b>ISO / CMM</b> models.\",\"<b>Tasks of testing</b>:<br>(1) choose the test implementation approach<br>(2) execute tests to find bugs<br>(3) prepare verification and validation reports<br>(4) attend project and design review meetings.\",\"<b>Role of the tester</b>:<br>• take responsibility for bugs found<br>• track bugs through their life cycle<br>• convince the development team to fix them.\",\"<b>QA responsibilities</b>:<br>• inspect the development process<br>• find improvements that prevent bugs<br>• scope is larger than the testing team<br>• monitor and evaluate processes and testing activities.\",\"<b>QA task examples</b>:<br>• develop standard processes<br>• create guidelines (requirements, design, coding)<br>• use checklists for each stage<br>• define quality metrics and criteria.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-test-in-qa\",\"qa-who-does-it\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-test-in-qa\":1,\"qa-who-does-it\":5},\"figH\":{\"qa-test-in-qa\":\"Software testing is part of QA: detection inside, prevention around it\",\"qa-who-does-it\":\"Testing or QA? Sort the 14 tasks, roles and responsibilities\"}},{\"h\":\"Quality management in IT and in organizations\",\"pts\":[\"Quality management in IT is often called <b>ITSM (IT Service Management)</b>. Goal: deliver IT services at an <b>agreed-upon level of quality</b>.\",\"Categories:<br>• <b>IT Service Support</b> = processes for the <b>efficient delivery of IT operational services</b>.<br>• <b>IT Service Delivery</b> = processes for <b>long-term planning, control and management</b> of IT services.\",\"SQA is not just testing. It includes establishing standards and methodologies, monitoring and evaluating the development process, and correcting errors during development while preventing their recurrence. Quality metrics assess how far quality requirements are met.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-itsm\",\"qa-not-just-testing\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-itsm\":1,\"qa-not-just-testing\":2},\"figH\":{\"qa-itsm\":\"ITSM and its two categories: Service Support and Service Delivery\",\"qa-not-just-testing\":\"SQA is not just testing: what else it includes, and what metrics assess\"}},{\"h\":\"Test management and organization structures\",\"table\":[[\"Structure\",\"Reports to\",\"Pros\",\"Cons\"],[\"1. Small team\",\"Development manager (testers + programmers under them)\",\"Fast communication between developers and testers\",\"An inexperienced dev manager may ignore bug reports to push delivery\"],[\"2. Independent test team\",\"Project manager (test manager/lead + development manager)\",\"Testing team's feedback is considered in decisions\",\"Final decision lies with the project manager; may hurt quality in critical projects\"],[\"3. Test group reporting to executive management\",\"Executive manager (QA/test, development and project managers)\",\"Independent quality team; quality reports reach senior management directly\",\"–\"]],\"pts\":[\"Org chart of the <b>small team structure</b> (slide 10): the <b>Development Manager</b> is at the top, with two boxes under him: <b>Testers</b> and <b>Programmers</b>.\",\"Org chart of the independent test team (slide 11): the Project Manager is at the top, with two branches under him: the Test Manager/Test Lead, with the Testers under him, and the Development Manager, with the Programmers under him.\",\"Org chart of the <b>test group reporting to executive management</b> (slide 12): the <b>Executive Manager</b> is at the top, with three boxes under him: <b>QA/Test Managers</b>, <b>Development Managers</b> and <b>Project Managers</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-test-orgs\",\"qa-bug-path\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-bug-path\":2},\"figH\":{\"qa-bug-path\":\"Follow a bug report up each of the three test organization structures\",\"qa-test-orgs\":\"The three test organization structures, with their pros and cons\"}},{\"h\":\"SQA metrics and indicators\",\"pts\":[\"• <b>Metrics</b>: rules for measuring software attributes (code size, complexity).<br>• <b>Indicators</b>: variables reflecting process outcomes (number of detected bugs).\",\"• <b>Product metrics</b> measure the <b>quality of the final product</b> (e.g. code size, number of documented pages).<br>• <b>Process metrics</b> measure the development process (e.g. time taken, development methodology).\",\"<b>9 common metrics</b>:<br>• code coverage<br>• bugs per line of code<br>• cyclomatic complexity<br>• function point analysis<br>• number of classes and interfaces<br>• cohesion<br>• coupling<br>• order of growth<br>• source lines of code.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-metric-tree\",\"qa-pick-metrics\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-metric-tree\":1,\"qa-pick-metrics\":2},\"figH\":{\"qa-metric-tree\":\"Product metric, process metric or indicator? Sort the slide's examples\",\"qa-pick-metrics\":\"Pick the 9 common metrics out of a mix of metrics and quality indicators\"}},{\"h\":\"Software quality indicators\",\"table\":[[\"Indicator\",\"Measures\"],[\"Progress\",\"Work done by the developer in each phase\"],[\"Stability\",\"Whether each phase's products are stable enough to proceed\"],[\"Process compliance\",\"Developer's obedience to the procedures approved at project start\"],[\"Quality evaluation effort\",\"Effort spent on internal quality evaluation\"],[\"Defect detection efficiency\",\"How many defects were detected in a phase\"],[\"Defect removal rate\",\"Defects detected AND resolved over a period\"],[\"Defect age profile\",\"Defects NOT resolved over a period\"],[\"Defect density\",\"Identifies defect-prone parts of the system\"],[\"Complexity\",\"Complexity of the code in a program\"]],\"fig\":[\"qa-indicator-map\",\"qa-defect-board\"],\"figH\":{\"qa-indicator-map\":\"Where each of the 9 quality indicators looks: phases, process, time, code\",\"qa-defect-board\":\"Log your own defects and watch the defect indicators update\"}}],\"cards\":[[\"Quality (definition)\",\"Conformance to requirements; the set of attributes valued by end-users.\"],[\"Directly vs indirectly measurable factors\",\"Direct: defects. Indirect: maintainability, usability.\"],[\"Reliability\",\"Performs its intended function accurately.\"],[\"Integrity\",\"Control over access and security.\"],[\"Maintainability\",\"Effort required to locate and fix errors.\"],[\"Flexibility\",\"Effort required to change the program.\"],[\"Portability\",\"Effort required to transfer the program to another system.\"],[\"SQA vs SQC\",\"SQA: process, prevention, proactive. SQC: product, detection, reactive.\"],[\"ITSM\",\"IT Service Management: delivering IT services at an agreed-upon level of quality.\"],[\"IT service support vs delivery\",\"Support: efficient delivery of operational services. Delivery: long-term planning, control and management.\"],[\"Metrics vs indicators\",\"Metrics: rules for measuring attributes. Indicators: variables reflecting process outcomes.\"],[\"Product vs process metrics\",\"Product: final product (code size, pages). Process: development (time, methodology).\"],[\"Defect removal rate vs defect age profile\",\"Removal rate: detected and resolved. Age profile: not resolved, over a period.\"],[\"Defect density\",\"Identifies the defect-prone parts of the system.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"What are the most commonly used software quality metrics?\",\"Code coverage, bugs per line of code, cyclomatic complexity, function point analysis, number of classes and interfaces, cohesion, coupling, order of growth, source lines of code.\"],[\"Differentiate between software testing and QA.\",\"Testing is part of SQA, detection-oriented, evaluates spec, design and code under normal and abnormal conditions, finds and reports bugs, and is done by testers. QA is broader, prevents defects, ensures correct processes, standards and methodologies, and uses ISO/CMM models.\"],[\"Compare the three test organization structures.\",\"Small team under the development manager: fast communication, but bug reports may be ignored. Independent team under the project manager: test feedback counts, but the PM decides, which is risky for critical projects. Test group reporting to executive management: independent QA team and quality reports go directly to senior management.\"],[\"List the software quality indicators.\",\"Progress, stability, process compliance, quality evaluation effort, defect detection efficiency, defect removal rate, defect age profile, defect density, complexity.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"The activity to ensure that an organization is providing the best product to the customer:\",\"o\":[[\"Quality Assurance\",\"Correct. QA ensures the processes, standards and methodologies that deliver quality.\"],[\"Quality Control\",\"QC inspects the product and screens out defects; QA is the broader organizational activity.\"],[\"Code coverage\",\"Code coverage is a white-box testing technique and one of the 9 SQA metrics, not an organization-wide activity that assures quality.\"],[\"Data coverage\",\"Data coverage is a dynamic white-box technique that tracks data through the code (L3); it is not the organizational activity of assuring quality.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"The goal of a software tester is to find:\",\"o\":[[\"Bugs\",\"Incomplete. The tester must also make sure they get fixed.\"],[\"Fix bugs\",\"Developers fix; debugging is not the tester's role.\"],[\"Bugs and make sure they get fixed\",\"Correct. The role: take responsibility for bugs, track them and convince the team to fix them.\"],[\"Write code\",\"Writing code is the developers' job; the tester's role is to find bugs, track them through their life cycle and convince the team to fix them.\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Which guarantees that the principles, methods and procedures are suitable for the project and applied properly?\",\"o\":[[\"Software quality control\",\"SQC is product-focused and reactive: it evaluates the product and screens out non-compliant items, rather than guaranteeing the procedures.\"],[\"Software quality assurance\",\"Correct. SQA ensures standards and procedures are applied correctly.\"],[\"Software testing\",\"Software testing is a detection-oriented part of SQA that finds and reports bugs; it does not guarantee that methods and procedures are applied properly.\"],[\"Software metrics\",\"Metrics are rules for measuring attributes such as code size and complexity; they measure quality, they do not guarantee procedures are applied.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"Which task is NOT performed under Software Quality Assurance?\",\"o\":[[\"Developing standard processes\",\"Developing standard processes is the first of the lecture's QA task examples, so it IS performed under SQA.\"],[\"Setting guidelines\",\"Creating guidelines (for requirements, design and coding) is one of the lecture's QA task examples, so it IS an SQA task.\"],[\"Creating checklists\",\"Using checklists for each stage is one of the lecture's QA task examples, so it IS an SQA task.\"],[\"Choosing the implementation approach for a given test\",\"Correct. Choosing the test implementation approach is task 1 of software TESTING, not one of the QA task examples (processes, guidelines, checklists, metrics) (L6, Tasks of testing).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"SQC is described as:\",\"o\":[[\"Process-focused and preventive\",\"Process-focused and preventive (proactive) describes SQA; SQC is product-focused and detection-oriented.\"],[\"Product-focused and detection-oriented (reactive)\",\"Correct. SQC focuses on the product and is reactive: it detects defects, evaluating product quality and screening out non-compliant items, while SQA prevents them (L6, SQA vs SQC).\"],[\"Only about standards\",\"Establishing standards and checking they are applied correctly is the goal of SQA; SQC evaluates the product itself.\"],[\"The same as SQA\",\"The lecture contrasts them: SQA = process, prevention, proactive; SQC = product, detection, reactive.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Effort required to locate and fix errors\\\" defines:\",\"o\":[[\"Flexibility\",\"Flexibility is the effort to change the program.\"],[\"Maintainability\",\"Correct. In the lecture's 8 quality factors, maintainability is the effort required to locate and fix errors (L6, Software quality factors).\"],[\"Reliability\",\"Reliability is performing the intended function accurately.\"],[\"Portability\",\"Portability is transferring to another system.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Number of detected bugs\\\" is an example of:\",\"o\":[[\"A metric\",\"Metrics are rules for measuring attributes like size and complexity.\"],[\"An indicator\",\"Correct. Indicators reflect process outcomes.\"],[\"A standard\",\"A standard is a mandatory rule to follow; the number of detected bugs is a value that reflects a process outcome, i.e. an indicator.\"],[\"A KPA\",\"KPAs (key process areas) are CMM's groups of activities for each maturity level (L7), not measures such as a bug count.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which ITSM category covers long-term planning, control and management of IT services?\",\"o\":[[\"IT Service Support\",\"Support is the efficient delivery of operational services.\"],[\"IT Service Delivery\",\"Correct. ITSM has two categories: IT Service Support (efficient delivery of operational services) and IT Service Delivery (long-term planning, control and management) (L6, Quality management in IT).\"],[\"Quality Control\",\"Quality control is product inspection (SQC), not one of the two ITSM categories, which are IT Service Support and IT Service Delivery.\"],[\"Configuration testing\",\"Configuration testing checks hardware setups for web sites (L5); it is not an ITSM category.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In which organization structure may an inexperienced development manager ignore bug reports to push the team to deliver?\",\"o\":[[\"Small team structure\",\"Correct. Testers report to the development manager.\"],[\"Independent test team\",\"Its con is that the project manager has the final decision.\"],[\"Test group reporting to executive management\",\"Reporting to executive management makes the quality team independent and sends its reports straight to senior management, so bug reports are not buried.\"],[\"None\",\"One structure does have this con: in the small team, testers report to the development manager, who may ignore bug reports.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"Quality Assurance is the same as Quality Control.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"QA is process and prevention; QC is product and detection.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. QA is process-focused and preventive (proactive); QC is product-focused and detection-oriented (reactive), so they are not the same (L6, SQA vs SQC).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Summary T/F\"},{\"q\":\"Which indicator measures the total number of defects NOT resolved over a period of time?\",\"o\":[[\"Defect removal rate\",\"The defect removal rate counts defects detected AND resolved over a period; unresolved defects are the defect age profile.\"],[\"Defect age profile\",\"Correct. The defect age profile measures defects NOT resolved over a period of time; the removal rate counts those that were resolved (L6, Quality indicators).\"],[\"Defect density\",\"Defect density identifies the defect-prone parts of the system; it does not track how many remain unresolved over time.\"],[\"Stability\",\"That judges readiness to move to the next phase.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"The goal of a software tester is to find\",\"o\":[[\"None of the above\",\"Option c matches the slide.\"],[\"Bugs\",\"Incomplete: testing's main goal is to find and report bugs, but the tester must also track them and get them fixed (L6).\"],[\"Bugs and make sure they get fixed\",\"Correct. Lecture 6, role of the tester: take responsibility for the bugs found, track bugs through their life cycle, and convince the development team to fix them. The 2025/26 final keyed the same question this way.\"],[\"Fix Bugs\",\"Developers fix bugs; the tester convinces the team to fix them (L6).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The activity to ensure that an organization is providing the best product or services to customer\",\"o\":[[\"Code coverage\",\"Code coverage is a white-box technique (L3), not an organizational activity.\"],[\"Quality Control\",\"QC inspects the finished product and screens out defects (reactive); it is not the organization-wide assurance activity (L6).\"],[\"Quality Assurance\",\"Correct. Lecture 6: QA is process-focused and preventive and covers the whole organization: establishing standards and methodologies, monitoring and evaluating the development process, and preventing errors from recurring. QC is product-focused and detection-oriented. The 2025/26 final keyed the same question as Quality Assurance.\"],[\"Data coverage\",\"Data coverage is a white-box technique (L3), not an organizational activity.\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"The lecture defines quality as:\",\"o\":[[\"Conformance to requirements\",\"Correct. It is also described as the set of attributes valued by end-users.\"],[\"The absence of any testing\",\"Testing is a way to check quality, not its definition.\"],[\"The number of lines of code\",\"Lines of code is a metric, not the definition of quality.\"],[\"Fast delivery of the product\",\"Speed of delivery is not the lecture's definition.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"The number of defects is an example of an indirectly measurable quality factor.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Defects are the lecture's example of a DIRECTLY measurable factor; maintainability and usability are indirect.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Defects are directly measurable; maintainability and usability are the indirect examples.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A team measures how much effort it takes to move their program from one system to another. Which quality factor is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Flexibility\",\"Flexibility is the effort required to change the program.\"],[\"Reusability\",\"Reusability is which parts can be used in other applications.\"],[\"Portability\",\"Correct. Portability = effort required to transfer the program to another system.\"],[\"Efficiency\",\"Efficiency is the optimal use of computing resources.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"A hospital system must ensure that only authorized staff can open patient records. Which quality factor is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Reliability\",\"Reliability is performing the intended function accurately.\"],[\"Integrity\",\"Correct. Integrity = control over access and security.\"],[\"Usability\",\"Usability is the effort required to learn and operate the program.\"],[\"Maintainability\",\"Maintainability is the effort required to locate and fix errors.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which of these is listed as a task of <b>software testing</b> (not of QA)?\",\"o\":[[\"Develop standard processes\",\"Developing standard processes is a QA task example, not one of the four tasks of software testing.\"],[\"Define quality metrics and criteria\",\"Defining quality metrics and criteria is a QA task example; testing tasks are choosing the approach, executing tests, reports and review meetings.\"],[\"Create guidelines for requirements, design and coding\",\"Creating guidelines for requirements, design and coding is a QA task example, not a task of software testing.\"],[\"Attend project and design review meetings\",\"Correct. The four testing tasks are choose the test approach, execute tests, prepare V&amp;V reports and attend project and design review meetings, task 4 (L6, Tasks of testing).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In a company, the test lead and the development manager both report to the project manager, who makes the final decision on releases. Which organization structure is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Small team\",\"In a small team the testers report to the development manager.\"],[\"Independent test team\",\"Correct. Testing feedback is considered, but the final decision lies with the project manager.\"],[\"Test group reporting to executive management\",\"There the QA, development and project managers all report to an executive manager.\"],[\"IT Service Delivery\",\"That is an ITSM category, not an organization structure.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Time taken to develop\\\" and \\\"the methodology used\\\" are examples of:\",\"o\":[[\"Process metrics\",\"Correct. Process metrics measure the development process.\"],[\"Product metrics\",\"Product metrics measure the final product, e.g. code size or number of documented pages.\"],[\"Quality factors\",\"Quality factors are attributes like reliability and usability.\"],[\"Defect indicators\",\"Defect indicators (removal rate, age profile, density) count or locate defects; development time and methodology are process metrics.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT one of the 9 common SQA metrics listed in the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"Coupling\",\"Coupling IS one of the 9 common SQA metrics, alongside cohesion, so it is in the list.\"],[\"Function point analysis\",\"Function point analysis IS one of the 9 common SQA metrics listed in the lecture.\"],[\"Order of growth\",\"Order of growth IS one of the 9 common SQA metrics, listed just before source lines of code.\"],[\"Number of team meetings held\",\"Correct. The 9 metrics are code coverage, bugs per line, cyclomatic complexity, function points, classes and interfaces, cohesion, coupling, order of growth and SLOC; meetings held is not one (L6).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A manager wants to know whether developers are following the procedures approved at the start of the project. Which quality indicator measures this?\",\"o\":[[\"Progress\",\"Progress measures the work done by the developer in each phase, not whether approved procedures are being followed.\"],[\"Stability\",\"Stability judges whether a phase's products are stable enough to proceed.\"],[\"Process compliance\",\"Correct. It measures the developer's obedience to the approved procedures.\"],[\"Defect density\",\"Defect density identifies defect-prone parts of the system.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"What is the goal of quality management in IT (ITSM)?\",\"o\":[[\"To replace all testers with automated tools\",\"Replacing testers with tools is not in the lecture; ITSM's goal is delivering IT services at an agreed-upon level of quality.\"],[\"To certify the company at CMM Level 5\",\"CMM levels belong to Lecture 7's maturity model; ITSM's goal is delivering IT services at an agreed-upon level of quality.\"],[\"To deliver IT services at an agreed-upon level of quality\",\"Correct. This is the lecture's stated ITSM goal.\"],[\"To write the source code of IT services\",\"ITSM is about managing service quality, not writing code.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Quality is the set of attributes valued by ………\",\"o\":[[\"developers\",\"Wrong. The lecture says the attributes are those valued by end-users, not developers.\"],[\"testers\",\"Wrong. Testers find and report bugs; the definition is about end-users.\"],[\"end-users\",\"Correct. Quality is the set of attributes valued by end-users. (L6, What is quality)\"],[\"project managers\",\"Wrong. Project managers make release decisions in one structure; they are not in the definition.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Maintainability and usability are examples of:\",\"o\":[[\"Indirectly measurable factors\",\"Correct. The lecture gives maintainability and usability as indirectly measurable factors. (L6, What is quality)\"],[\"Directly measurable factors\",\"Wrong. The directly measurable example is defects.\"],[\"Software quality indicators\",\"Wrong. Indicators are things like progress, stability and defect density.\"],[\"Process metrics\",\"Wrong. Process metrics are things like time taken and development methodology.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"Optimal use of computing resources\\\" defines which quality factor?\",\"o\":[[\"Reliability\",\"Wrong. Reliability means performing the intended function accurately.\"],[\"Efficiency\",\"Correct. Efficiency is the optimal use of computing resources. (L6, Quality factors)\"],[\"Reusability\",\"Wrong. Reusability is about which parts can be used in other applications.\"],[\"Integrity\",\"Wrong. Integrity is control over access and security.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A payroll program must always compute the exact salary it is meant to compute. Which quality factor is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Efficiency\",\"Wrong. Efficiency is about using computing resources optimally, not accuracy.\"],[\"Usability\",\"Wrong. Usability is the effort to learn and operate the program.\"],[\"Flexibility\",\"Wrong. Flexibility is the effort required to change the program.\"],[\"Reliability\",\"Correct. Reliability means the program performs its intended function accurately. (L6, Quality factors)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"\\\"Effort required to change the program\\\" defines:\",\"o\":[[\"Maintainability\",\"Wrong. Maintainability is the effort to locate and fix errors.\"],[\"Portability\",\"Wrong. Portability is the effort to transfer the program to another system.\"],[\"Flexibility\",\"Correct. Flexibility is the effort required to change the program. (L6, Quality factors)\"],[\"Usability\",\"Wrong. Usability is the effort to learn and operate the program.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"New employees need only an hour to learn how to operate a company's system. This describes good:\",\"o\":[[\"Usability\",\"Correct. Usability is the effort required to learn and operate the program. (L6, Quality factors)\"],[\"Integrity\",\"Wrong. Integrity is control over access and security.\"],[\"Maintainability\",\"Wrong. Maintainability is about locating and fixing errors.\"],[\"Efficiency\",\"Wrong. Efficiency is the optimal use of computing resources.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A team takes the login module of one project and uses it unchanged in a new application. Which quality factor does this show?\",\"o\":[[\"Portability\",\"Wrong. Portability is moving the whole program to another system, not reusing a part in another application.\"],[\"Reusability\",\"Correct. Reusability is about which parts can be used in other applications. (L6, Quality factors)\"],[\"Flexibility\",\"Wrong. Flexibility is the effort to change the program.\"],[\"Reliability\",\"Wrong. Reliability is performing the intended function accurately.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: SQA is process-focused and proactive, and its main idea is prevention.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture's table gives SQA: prevention, process (proactive). (L6, SQA vs SQC)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. Prevention and a proactive process focus are exactly how the lecture describes SQA.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"The goal of <b>SQC</b> is to:\",\"o\":[[\"Ensure standards and procedures are applied correctly\",\"Wrong. That is the goal of SQA.\"],[\"Deliver IT services at an agreed-upon level of quality\",\"Wrong. That is the goal of ITSM.\"],[\"Develop standard processes and guidelines\",\"Wrong. Those are QA task examples.\"],[\"Evaluate product quality and screen out non-compliant items\",\"Correct. SQC focuses on the product and screens out non-compliant items. (L6, SQA vs SQC)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: Software testing is part of SQA and is detection-oriented.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture lists testing as part of SQA and detection-oriented. (L6, Testing vs QA)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. Both points are in the lecture's description of software testing.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which models does QA use, according to the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"ISO / CMM\",\"Correct. The lecture says QA uses ISO / CMM models. (L6, Testing vs QA)\"],[\"Selenium / Cypress\",\"Wrong. Selenium and Cypress are tools for testing web applications (L5).\"],[\"ITSM support / delivery\",\"Wrong. These are the two ITSM categories, not QA models.\"],[\"Cohesion / coupling\",\"Wrong. Cohesion and coupling are two of the common metrics.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: Software testing is performed only under normal conditions.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong. The lecture says testing is performed under specified normal and abnormal conditions.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Testing covers both normal and abnormal conditions. (L6, Testing vs QA)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is part of the <b>role of the tester</b>?\",\"o\":[[\"Develop standard processes\",\"Wrong. Developing standard processes is a QA task example.\"],[\"Track bugs through their life cycle\",\"Correct. The tester tracks bugs through their life cycle and convinces developers to fix them. (L6, Roles and tasks)\"],[\"Define quality metrics and criteria\",\"Wrong. Defining quality metrics is a QA task example.\"],[\"Inspect the development process\",\"Wrong. Inspecting the development process is a QA responsibility.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which statement about QA responsibilities is correct?\",\"o\":[[\"QA only executes tests to find bugs\",\"Wrong. Executing tests is a testing task; QA is broader than testing.\"],[\"QA's scope is smaller than the testing team's\",\"Wrong. The lecture says QA's scope is larger than the testing team.\"],[\"QA monitors and evaluates processes and testing activities\",\"Correct. That is one of the listed QA responsibilities. (L6, Roles and tasks)\"],[\"QA is reactive and focuses on the product\",\"Wrong. That describes SQC; QA is proactive and process-focused.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a QA task example (not a testing task)?\",\"o\":[[\"Execute tests to find bugs\",\"Wrong. Executing tests is a task of testing.\"],[\"Prepare verification and validation reports\",\"Wrong. V&amp;V reports are a task of testing.\"],[\"Choose the test implementation approach\",\"Wrong. Choosing the implementation approach is a task of testing.\"],[\"Use checklists for each stage\",\"Correct. Checklists for each stage is one of the QA task examples. (L6, Roles and tasks)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: Preparing verification and validation reports is one of the tasks of testing.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. It is testing task 3 in the lecture. (L6, Roles and tasks)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. The lecture lists V&amp;V reports among the tasks of testing.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Quality management in IT is often called ………\",\"o\":[[\"ITSM (IT Service Management)\",\"Correct. The lecture says quality management in IT is often called ITSM. (L6, Quality management in IT)\"],[\"SQC (Software Quality Control)\",\"Wrong. SQC is product-focused detection, not the name of IT quality management.\"],[\"CMM\",\"Wrong. CMM is a model QA uses, not IT quality management's name.\"],[\"Function point analysis\",\"Wrong. Function point analysis is one of the common metrics.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Processes for the <b>efficient delivery of IT operational services</b> form the ITSM category:\",\"o\":[[\"IT Service Delivery\",\"Wrong. Service Delivery covers long-term planning, control and management.\"],[\"IT Service Support\",\"Correct. IT Service Support is the efficient delivery of IT operational services. (L6, Quality management in IT)\"],[\"Software Quality Control\",\"Wrong. SQC is about detecting non-compliant products, not an ITSM category.\"],[\"Process compliance\",\"Wrong. Process compliance is a quality indicator.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: SQA includes correcting errors during development and preventing their recurrence, not just testing.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture says SQA is not just testing and includes correcting errors while preventing recurrence. (L6, Quality management in IT)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. The lecture explicitly says SQA is more than testing and includes this.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"The main advantage of the <b>small team structure</b> is:\",\"o\":[[\"Quality reports reach senior management directly\",\"Wrong. That is the advantage of the test group reporting to executive management.\"],[\"The testing team's feedback is considered in decisions\",\"Wrong. That is the advantage of the independent test team.\"],[\"Fast communication between developers and testers\",\"Correct. In the small team, testers and programmers sit under one development manager, so communication is fast. (L6, Organization structures)\"],[\"The final decision lies with the project manager\",\"Wrong. That is a disadvantage of the independent test team.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In which structure is there an independent quality team whose reports reach senior management directly?\",\"o\":[[\"Small team structure\",\"Wrong. In the small team, testers report to the development manager.\"],[\"Independent test team\",\"Wrong. Here the test lead reports to the project manager, who makes the final decision.\"],[\"ITSM service support\",\"Wrong. That is an ITSM category, not a test organization structure.\"],[\"Test group reporting to executive management\",\"Correct. The QA/test managers report to the executive manager, so quality reports reach senior management directly. (L6, Organization structures)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: In the independent test team structure, the final decision lies with the test manager.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Wrong. The lecture says the final decision lies with the project manager.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The project manager decides, which may hurt quality in critical projects. (L6, Organization structures)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Code size\\\" and \\\"number of documented pages\\\" are examples of:\",\"o\":[[\"Product metrics\",\"Correct. Product metrics measure the quality of the final product, e.g. code size and documented pages. (L6, Metrics and indicators)\"],[\"Process metrics\",\"Wrong. Process metrics are time taken and development methodology.\"],[\"Quality indicators\",\"Wrong. Indicators reflect process outcomes such as the number of detected bugs.\"],[\"Indirectly measurable factors\",\"Wrong. The lecture's indirect examples are maintainability and usability.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Rules for measuring software attributes such as code size and complexity are called:\",\"o\":[[\"Indicators\",\"Wrong. Indicators are variables reflecting process outcomes.\"],[\"Metrics\",\"Correct. Metrics are rules for measuring software attributes. (L6, Metrics and indicators)\"],[\"Guidelines\",\"Wrong. Guidelines are a QA task (for requirements, design, coding).\"],[\"Quality factors\",\"Wrong. Quality factors are the 8 attributes like reliability and portability.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Over one month a team detects 40 defects and resolves 30 of them. How many defects does the <b>defect age profile</b> count for that month?\",\"o\":[[\"30\",\"Wrong. 30 are detected and resolved, which is what the defect removal rate counts.\"],[\"40\",\"Wrong. 40 is all detected defects, related to defect detection efficiency.\"],[\"10\",\"Correct. The age profile counts defects NOT resolved: 40 − 30 = 10. (L6, Quality indicators)\"],[\"70\",\"Wrong. Adding detected and resolved (40 + 30) has no meaning for any indicator.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which quality indicator identifies the defect-prone parts of the system?\",\"o\":[[\"Defect removal rate\",\"Wrong. Removal rate counts defects detected and resolved over a period.\"],[\"Stability\",\"Wrong. Stability checks whether each phase's products are stable enough to proceed.\"],[\"Progress\",\"Wrong. Progress measures the work done by the developer in each phase.\"],[\"Defect density\",\"Correct. Defect density identifies defect-prone parts of the system. (L6, Quality indicators)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Before starting the coding phase, a manager checks whether the design documents are settled enough to build on. Which indicator is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Stability\",\"Correct. Stability measures whether each phase's products are stable enough to proceed. (L6, Quality indicators)\"],[\"Quality evaluation effort\",\"Wrong. That measures the effort spent on internal quality evaluation.\"],[\"Complexity\",\"Wrong. Complexity measures how complex the program's code is.\"],[\"Defect age profile\",\"Wrong. Age profile counts defects not resolved over a period.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: The Progress indicator measures the work done by the developer in each phase.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. That is the lecture's meaning for Progress. (L6, Quality indicators)\"],[\"False\",\"Wrong. The lecture defines Progress exactly this way.\"]],\"a\":0}]},{\"n\":7,\"title\":\"Maturity Model (CMM) and Quality Standards (ISO 9000, SPICE)\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Introduction\",\"pts\":[\"Unit objectives:<br>• explain CMM<br>• describe the elements of ISO 9000<br>• state the software engineering standards.\",\"Organizations seek sustainable excellence in their IT operations, <b>requiring a flexible framework to meet evolving business needs</b>. However, challenges like poor IT application design, high costs and delayed projects obstruct progress. Quality assurance, through <b>maturity models</b> and systematic procedures, helps define organizational structures and improve performance.\",\"• <b>CMM</b> helps manage complex IT systems efficiently.<br>• A <b>QMS</b> (quality management system) gives a structured way to improve process capability, manage risk and ensure customer satisfaction.<br>• <b>ISO 9000</b> promotes continuous improvement, product reliability and international recognition.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-qm-intro\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-qm-intro\":2},\"figH\":{\"qa-qm-intro\":\"From IT challenges to maturity models and quality standards\"}},{\"h\":\"Capability Maturity Model (CMM)\",\"pts\":[\"A <b>standard framework for assessing and improving the maturity</b> of a software company's development process.\",\"Developed by the <b>Software Engineering Institute (SEI)</b> and <b>Carnegie Mellon University</b> under the <b>U.S. Department of Defense</b>, CMM provides <b>structured guidance for enhancing software quality</b>.\",\"A <b>five-level incremental</b> approach: organizations progress gradually in process maturity <b>without skipping levels</b>. This structured growth helps companies <b>adapt to changes efficiently</b>.\",\"Designed mainly for software development organizations, but also applies to software/system engineering, project management, IT, risk management and personnel management.\",\"A <b>maturity model</b> provides structured levels showing how well <b>processes and behaviors</b> achieve desired outcomes. It can be used for <b>both evaluation and improvement</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-cmm-climb\",\"qa-cmm-glance\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-cmm-climb\":2,\"qa-cmm-glance\":4},\"figH\":{\"qa-cmm-climb\":\"Try it: climb the CMM levels without skipping\",\"qa-cmm-glance\":\"CMM: who made it, what it is, what it is for, where it applies\"}},{\"h\":\"CMM structure: 6 components\",\"pts\":[\"<b>1. Maturity levels</b>: define an organization's process capability. The CMM model consists of <b>five levels</b>:<br>• the initial level (Level 1) is characterized by ad-hoc processes with minimal structure;<br>• the top level (Level 5) represents an ideal state where processes are systematically managed, optimized and continuously improved.<br>This structured progression helps organizations <b>enhance their process maturity over time</b>.\",\"<b>2. Process capability</b>: the organization's ability to meet quality expectations through its current processes; it helps <b>predict</b> the outcomes of future software projects, <b>ensuring consistency and reliability</b>.\",\"<b>3. Key Process Areas (KPA)</b>: groups of related activities that achieve goals when performed together; they define capability at each level. Example: software project delivery planning.\",\"<b>4. Goals</b>: evaluate essential practices within a process area to determine if an <b>organization or project</b> has successfully implemented it, <b>highlighting its importance and purpose</b>.\",\"<b>5. Common features</b>: characteristics showing whether a KPA's implementation is successful and sustainable.\",\"<b>6. Key practices</b>: define the necessary actions and infrastructure to support a KPA, <b>ensuring effective application</b>. Example: a software delivery schedule follows a documented procedure.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-cmm-structure\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-cmm-structure\":5},\"figH\":{\"qa-cmm-structure\":\"How the 6 CMM components fit together\"}},{\"h\":\"Five levels of CMM\",\"table\":[[\"Level\",\"Name (figure label)\",\"Description\",\"Improvements implemented (Table 1)\"],[\"1\",\"Initial (inconsistent management)\",\"Unstructured, reactive, inconsistent, ad-hoc; struggle to meet goals\",\"No required processes\"],[\"2\",\"Repeatable (project management)\",\"Basic project management gives repeatable success: planning, requirement control, product modifications; better cost and schedule\",\"Plan and track projects; manage suppliers; manage product configurations; measure projects; assure policy compliance\"],[\"3\",\"Defined (process management)\",\"Standardized processes across the organization; teams tailor them\",\"Enhancement infrastructure; identify required processes; organize/manage processes; collect process data; organization-wide training; manage with non-software groups\"],[\"4\",\"Quantitatively Managed (capability management); the slide's figure labels it \\\"Level 4: Managed (Capability Management)\\\"\",\"Processes controlled with statistical and quantitative techniques; predict performance; minimize variation\",\"Supervise processes quantitatively; create capability baselines\"],[\"5\",\"Optimizing (change management)\",\"Continuous process improvement through incremental and innovative advances using quantitative goals\",\"Develop change infrastructure; assess and organize improvements; remove causes of defects\"]],\"pts\":[\"<b>Level 1 (Initial)</b>: processes are unstructured, reactive and inconsistent, <b>leading to unstable project management</b>. Organizations at this level struggle to meet goals reliably.\",\"<b>Level 2 (Repeatable)</b>: basic project management practices are implemented, allowing for repeatable success. Organizations establish <b>project planning, requirement control and product modifications</b>, ensuring better cost and schedule management.\",\"<b>Level 3 (Defined)</b>: standardized processes are <b>developed and refined over time</b>. <b>Management ensures process consistency across the organization</b>, and project teams tailor processes based on these standards.\",\"<b>Level 4 (Quantitatively Managed)</b>: processes are controlled through statistical and quantitative techniques, allowing organizations to <b>predict and manage project performance accurately</b>. This ensures <b>stable software quality and maintenance</b> while minimizing variations.\",\"<b>Level 5 (Optimizing)</b>: focuses on <b>continuous process improvement</b> through incremental and innovative advancements. Organizations at this level <b>systematically refine their processes</b>, using <b>quantitative goals</b> to drive enhancements and <b>achieve long-term business objectives</b>.\",\"Figure (CMM levels): five ellipses stacked from bottom to top, joined by upward arrows:<br><b>Level 1: Initial (Inconsistent Management)</b><br>↑ arrow \\\"Basic Management Control\\\" (Level 1→2)<br><b>Level 2: Repeatable (Project Management)</b><br>↑ arrow \\\"Process Definition\\\" (Level 2→3)<br><b>Level 3: Defined (Process Management)</b><br>↑ arrow \\\"Process Measurement\\\" (Level 3→4)<br><b>Level 4: Managed (Capability Management)</b><br>↑ arrow \\\"Process Control\\\" (Level 4→5)<br><b>Level 5: Optimizing (Change Management)</b>.<br>The figure labels Level 4 just \\\"Managed\\\"; the slide text and Table 1 call it \\\"Quantitatively Managed\\\".\",\"<b>Table 1: Capability Maturity Model Process Areas</b> (Maturity Level: Improvements Implemented):<br><b>5. Optimizing</b>: develop change infrastructure; assess and organize improvements; remove causes of defects.<br><b>4. Quantitatively Managed</b>: supervise processes quantitatively; create capability baselines.<br><b>3. Defined</b>: create enhancement infrastructure; identify required software processes; organize and manage processes; collect process-level data; provide organization-wide training; manage with non-software groups.<br><b>2. Repeatable</b>: plan and track projects; manage suppliers; manage product configurations; measure projects; assist and assure policy compliance.<br><b>1. Initial</b>: no required processes.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-cmm-levels\",\"qa-cmm-assess\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-cmm-levels\":5,\"qa-cmm-assess\":6},\"figH\":{\"qa-cmm-assess\":\"Try it: find an organization's maturity level from Table 1\",\"qa-cmm-levels\":\"The five levels of CMM, from Initial up to Optimizing\"}},{\"h\":\"Key Process Areas (KPA) and benefits of CMM\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Within each of the maturity levels are Key Process Areas (KPA)</b> which explain about that level, and each KPA has <b>5 aspects</b>:<br>(1) <b>Goals</b>: each KPA sets several goals, which include the different objectives that help the organization climb to the next CMM level<br>(2) <b>Commitment</b>: various requirements that must be met to achieve the specified goals<br>(3) <b>Ability</b>: the activities carried out to enable the organization to meet the commitments<br>(4) <b>Measurement</b>: <b>the activities performed should be monitored</b>, and hence there are methods that have to be followed to implement the same<br>(5) <b>Verification</b>: various methods that need to be followed that define the verification.\",\"<b>Benefits of CMM</b>:<br>(1) simple and easy to analyze<br>(2) many executives already know it<br>(3) used to explain major IT issues to senior executives<br>(4) helps get project funding (e.g. enterprise data asset management, metadata repository)<br>(5) large companies and governments use it to compare themselves with others.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-kpa-aspects\",\"qa-cmm-benefits\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-kpa-aspects\":0,\"qa-cmm-benefits\":1},\"figH\":{\"qa-kpa-aspects\":\"The 5 aspects of a Key Process Area\",\"qa-cmm-benefits\":\"The 5 benefits of CMM and who they help\"}},{\"h\":\"ISO 9000\",\"pts\":[\"A set of internationally recognized standards published by the <b>International Organization for Standardization (ISO)</b>, defining universal best practices so organizations meet quality requirements and client expectations.\",\"<b>Highly generic</b>: applies to <b>any organization</b> providing products or services.\",\"\\\"ISO\\\" comes from the Greek word <b>isos = \\\"equal\\\"</b>, reflecting a global standard for quality assurance.\",\"ISO 9000 consists of <b>20 key elements</b> that outline the necessary measures for organizations to comply with its quality assurance guidelines. Each element must be <b>clearly and thoroughly documented</b> to demonstrate adherence to ISO 9000 standards.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-iso-overview\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-iso-overview\":3},\"figH\":{\"qa-iso-overview\":\"ISO 9000 at a glance: generic, global, 20 documented elements\"}},{\"h\":\"20 elements of ISO 9000\",\"table\":[[\"#\",\"Element\",\"#\",\"Element\"],[\"1\",\"Management responsibility (quality policy, resources)\",\"11\",\"Inspection/measuring equipment (calibrate)\"],[\"2\",\"Quality systems (documented quality manual)\",\"12\",\"Inspection & test status\"],[\"3\",\"Contract review (customer needs)\",\"13\",\"Control of non-conforming product (rejected items)\"],[\"4\",\"Design control\",\"14\",\"Corrective & preventive action (root causes)\"],[\"5\",\"Documentation & data control\",\"15\",\"Handling/storage/packaging/delivery\"],[\"6\",\"Purchasing (supplier requirements)\",\"16\",\"Quality records (audit trails)\"],[\"7\",\"Customer-supplied product control\",\"17\",\"Internal quality audits\"],[\"8\",\"Product identification & traceability\",\"18\",\"Training (records, qualifications)\"],[\"9\",\"Process control (work instructions, plans)\",\"19\",\"Servicing (after delivery)\"],[\"10\",\"Inspection & testing (receiving, in-process, final)\",\"20\",\"Statistical techniques (control charts)\"]],\"pts\":[\"<b>Elements 1 to 10</b>:<br>(1) <b>Management Responsibility</b>: management must set the quality policy and provide resources.<br>(2) <b>Quality Systems</b>: maintain a documented quality manual and procedures.<br>(3) <b>Contract Review</b>: clearly state and verify customer needs.<br>(4) <b>Design Control</b>: document and control design and engineering changes.<br>(5) <b>Documentation &amp; Data Control</b>: strict control of document creation and modification.<br>(6) <b>Purchasing</b>: define supplier requirements and compliance processes.<br>(7) <b>Customer Supplied Product Control</b>: handle and protect customer-provided materials.<br>(8) <b>Product Identification &amp; Traceability</b>: track products from start to finish.<br>(9) <b>Process Control</b>: use work instructions, quality plans and standards.<br>(10) <b>Inspection &amp; Testing</b>: perform receiving, in-process and final inspections.\",\"<b>Elements 11 to 20</b>:<br>(11) <b>Inspection/Measuring Equipment</b>: calibrate instruments regularly.<br>(12) <b>Inspection &amp; Test Status</b>: clearly identify inspected products.<br>(13) <b>Control of Non-Conforming Product</b>: separate and manage rejected items.<br>(14) <b>Corrective &amp; Preventive Action</b>: identify root causes and prevent future defects.<br>(15) <b>Handling/Storage/Packaging/Delivery</b>: protect products throughout production and shipping.<br>(16) <b>Quality Records</b>: maintain records for audit trails.<br>(17) <b>Internal Quality Audits</b>: conduct periodic audits and share results.<br>(18) <b>Training</b>: maintain training records and qualifications of all employees.<br>(19) <b>Servicing</b>: ensure after-delivery support meets requirements (if applicable).<br>(20) <b>Statistical Techniques</b>: use data, control charts and analysis for quality improvement.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-iso-elements\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-iso-elements\":1},\"figH\":{\"qa-iso-elements\":\"Tour the 20 elements, then quiz yourself\"}},{\"h\":\"ISO 8 principles\",\"pts\":[\"ISO is built on <b>8 key principles</b> that guide organizations toward high-quality performance:<br>• <b>Customer Focus</b>: understand and exceed customer needs to gain loyalty.<br>• <b>Leadership</b>: leaders create an environment where people are fully involved.<br>• <b>Involvement of People</b>: employees at all levels contribute to the organization's success.<br>• <b>Process Approach</b>: manage activities as processes for consistent results.<br>• <b>System Approach to Management</b>: align interconnected processes as one system.<br>• <b>Continuous Improvement</b>: ongoing improvement is a permanent objective.<br>• <b>Factual Decision Making</b>: base decisions on reliable data and analysis.<br>• <b>Mutually Beneficial Supplier Relationships</b>: build strong, value-creating relationships with suppliers.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-iso-principles\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-iso-principles\":0},\"figH\":{\"qa-iso-principles\":\"Tour the 8 principles, then quiz yourself\"}},{\"h\":\"Software engineering standards and SPICE\",\"pts\":[\"• Software engineering standards ensure proper development, testing and maintenance of software <b>by following specific guidelines</b>.<br>• <b>Various organizations, including international and national bodies, establish and enforce Software Quality Assurance (SQA) standards.</b><br>• Organizations are increasingly shifting from <b>local to international</b> standards.<br>• The <b>IEEE and IEEE Computer Society</b> are at the forefront of this movement; since <b>1976</b> IEEE has contributed significantly to standardization, leading to the <b>IEEE Software Engineering Standard Collection</b>, which <b>serves as a foundation for many international project process standards</b>.\",\"<b>SPICE</b> = <b>Software Process Improvement for Capability Determination</b>; the SPICE project and <b>ISO/IEC 15504</b> are joint initiatives by ISO and IEC to establish a standard software process assessment methodology. Introduced in <b>1993</b>, SPICE aims to <b>improve international software process standards</b>.\",\"<b>3 objectives of SPICE</b>:<br>(1) establish a standard framework for software process assessment<br>(2) conduct organizational assessments for emerging standards<br>(3) facilitate global adoption of software process assessment in industry.\"],\"fig\":[\"qa-std-timeline\",\"qa-spice\"],\"figAt\":{\"qa-std-timeline\":1,\"qa-spice\":2},\"figH\":{\"qa-std-timeline\":\"Software engineering standards: from local to international, 1976 to 1993\",\"qa-spice\":\"SPICE: the acronym and its 3 objectives\"}}],\"cards\":[[\"CMM\",\"Capability Maturity Model: a standard framework for assessing and improving the maturity of a software company's development process.\"],[\"Who developed CMM?\",\"SEI and Carnegie Mellon University, under the U.S. Department of Defense.\"],[\"Number of CMM maturity levels\",\"Five, and levels cannot be skipped.\"],[\"CMM levels in order\",\"Initial, Repeatable, Defined, Quantitatively Managed, Optimizing.\"],[\"CMM Level 1\",\"Initial: ad-hoc, unstructured, reactive. No required processes.\"],[\"CMM Level 4\",\"Quantitatively managed: processes controlled with statistical/quantitative techniques; capability baselines.\"],[\"CMM Level 5\",\"Optimizing: continuous process improvement; remove causes of defects.\"],[\"KPA\",\"Key Process Area: related activities that achieve goals together (e.g. delivery planning).\"],[\"5 aspects of a KPA\",\"Goals, commitment, ability, measurement, verification.\"],[\"ISO name origin\",\"Greek \\\"isos\\\" = equal.\"],[\"Number of ISO 9000 elements\",\"20.\"],[\"ISO element for rejected materials\",\"Control of non-conforming product (element 13).\"],[\"ISO element related to testing\",\"Inspection and testing (element 10).\"],[\"SPICE\",\"Software Process Improvement for Capability Determination (ISO/IEC 15504), introduced in 1993.\"],[\"IEEE standardization since\",\"1976 (IEEE Software Engineering Standard Collection).\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Briefly discuss the levels of CMM.\",\"Level 1 Initial: processes are unstructured, reactive and inconsistent, leading to unstable project management; goals are met unreliably. Level 2 Repeatable: basic project management practices give repeatable success; organizations establish project planning, requirement control and product modifications, ensuring better cost and schedule management. Level 3 Defined: standardized processes are developed and refined over time; management ensures consistency across the organization and teams tailor them. Level 4 Quantitatively Managed: statistical and quantitative control; project performance predicted and managed accurately, stable quality and minimal variation. Level 5 Optimizing: continuous incremental and innovative improvement with quantitative goals to achieve long-term business objectives.\"],[\"List the 20 elements of the ISO 9000 standard.\",\"Management responsibility, quality systems, contract review, design control, documentation and data control, purchasing, customer-supplied product control, product identification and traceability, process control, inspection and testing, inspection/measuring equipment, inspection and test status, control of non-conforming product, corrective and preventive action, handling/storage/packaging/delivery, quality records, internal quality audits, training, servicing, statistical techniques.\"],[\"What are the 8 quality management principles of ISO?\",\"Customer focus, leadership, involvement of people, process approach, system approach to management, continuous improvement, factual decision making, mutually beneficial supplier relationships.\"],[\"State the three objectives of SPICE.\",\"Establish a standard framework for software process assessment; conduct organizational assessments for emerging standards; facilitate global adoption of software process assessment in industry.\"],[\"What are the benefits of CMM?\",\"Simple and easy to analyze; familiar to executives; explains IT issues to senior executives; helps get funding for key initiatives; used by large companies and governments to benchmark themselves.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"............ is the widely used and preferred software method of evaluation.\",\"o\":[[\"CMM\",\"Correct. The summary wording: \\\"CMM is the widely used and preferred software method of evaluation.\\\"\"],[\"ISO 9000\",\"A generic quality standard, not the preferred evaluation method.\"],[\"6 Sigma\",\"Six Sigma is a statistical, data-driven methodology for reducing defects and variation (DMAIC, L8), not the preferred software evaluation method.\"],[\"SPICE\",\"A process-assessment initiative, but not the answer here.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"............ are maturity levels in the CMM.\",\"o\":[[\"3\",\"Three levels is too few; CMM has five: Initial, Repeatable, Defined, Quantitatively Managed and Optimizing (L7).\"],[\"5\",\"Correct. Initial, Repeatable, Defined, Quantitatively Managed, Optimizing.\"],[\"4\",\"Four levels misses one; CMM is a five-level incremental model, ending with Level 5 Optimizing (L7).\"],[\"6\",\"There is no sixth level; CMM stops at Level 5 Optimizing. 6 is the number of CMM structure components, not levels.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"There are 20 crucial elements in the ............ standard.\",\"o\":[[\"CMM\",\"CMM is built from 5 maturity levels and 6 structure components, not 20 elements; the 20 elements belong to ISO 9000.\"],[\"6 Sigma\",\"Six Sigma is organized around the DMAIC phases, not 20 elements; the 20 key elements belong to ISO 9000.\"],[\"ISO 9000\",\"Correct. ISO 9000 has 20 key elements (management responsibility … statistical techniques), each clearly and thoroughly documented to show compliance (L7, ISO 9000).\"],[\"SPICE\",\"SPICE (ISO/IEC 15504) is defined by 3 objectives for software process assessment, not 20 elements.\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"SPICE stands for:\",\"o\":[[\"Software Process Improvement for Capability Determination\",\"Correct. SPICE = Software Process Improvement for Capability Determination, the joint ISO/IEC initiative (ISO/IEC 15504) for software process assessment (L7).\"],[\"Capability Evaluation\",\"\\\"Capability Evaluation\\\" drops most of the name, and the lecture says Determination; SPICE = Software Process Improvement for Capability Determination.\"],[\"Software Procedure Improvement\",\"SPICE says Process, not Procedure, and this drops \\\"for Capability Determination\\\"; the full name is Software Process Improvement for Capability Determination.\"],[\"Software Procedure Improvement for Determination\",\"It says Procedure instead of Process and drops \\\"Capability\\\"; SPICE = Software Process Improvement for Capability Determination.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"When was the SPICE project introduced?\",\"o\":[[\"1985\",\"1985 is too early; the lecture dates the SPICE initiative (ISO/IEC 15504) to 1993.\"],[\"1995\",\"1995 is two years late; the lecture says SPICE was introduced in 1993.\"],[\"2005\",\"2005 is far too late; the lecture says SPICE was introduced in 1993.\"],[\"1993\",\"Correct. The lecture says the SPICE project, a joint ISO and IEC initiative with ISO/IEC 15504 for software process assessment, was introduced in 1993 (L7).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"ISO stands for:\",\"o\":[[\"International Organization for Standardization\",\"Correct. The name comes from Greek \\\"isos\\\" = equal.\"],[\"International Software Organization\",\"ISO is not limited to software: it is the International Organization for Standardization, and ISO 9000 applies to any organization.\"],[\"Indian Software Operations\",\"ISO is an international body, not Indian; it is the International Organization for Standardization (from Greek isos = equal).\"],[\"Indian Standard Operations\",\"ISO is not Indian; it stands for the International Organization for Standardization, whose ISO 9000 standards are recognized worldwide.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"At which CMM level are processes controlled through statistical and quantitative techniques?\",\"o\":[[\"Level 2 Repeatable\",\"Level 2 Repeatable adds basic project management (planning, tracking, requirement control), not statistical control of processes.\"],[\"Level 3 Defined\",\"Level 3 Defined standardizes processes across the organization; statistical and quantitative control comes at Level 4.\"],[\"Level 4 Quantitatively Managed\",\"Correct. At Level 4 Quantitatively Managed, processes are controlled with statistical and quantitative techniques to predict performance and minimize variation (L7, Five levels).\"],[\"Level 5 Optimizing\",\"Continuous improvement (uses quantitative goals, but control by statistics is level 4).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT one of the five aspects of a Key Process Area?\",\"o\":[[\"Commitment\",\"Commitment (requirements to meet the goals) IS one of the five KPA aspects: goals, commitment, ability, measurement, verification.\"],[\"Ability\",\"Ability (activities that enable meeting the commitments) IS one of the five KPA aspects.\"],[\"Marketing\",\"Correct. The aspects are goals, commitment, ability, measurement, verification.\"],[\"Verification\",\"Verification (methods defining verification) IS the fifth KPA aspect, after goals, commitment, ability and measurement.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"\\\"A maturity model can be utilized as a model for evaluation.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. It can be used for both evaluation and improvement.\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture says a maturity model can be used for both evaluation and improvement, so the statement is true.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Summary T/F\"},{\"q\":\"If you must develop procedures for rejected materials, which ISO 9000 element is it?\",\"o\":[[\"Purchasing\",\"Purchasing (element 6) sets requirements for suppliers; procedures for rejected materials are element 13, control of non-conforming product.\"],[\"Control of non-conforming product\",\"Correct. Element 13 of ISO 9000, control of non-conforming product, requires procedures to identify, separate and manage rejected items (L7, 20 elements).\"],[\"Servicing\",\"Servicing (element 19) covers support after delivery, not handling rejected materials.\"],[\"Training\",\"Training (element 18) keeps records of employee qualifications; rejected materials fall under control of non-conforming product.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"CMM was developed by:\",\"o\":[[\"ISO and IEC\",\"ISO and IEC jointly produced SPICE / ISO/IEC 15504; CMM came from SEI and Carnegie Mellon under the U.S. DoD.\"],[\"SEI and Carnegie Mellon University (U.S. DoD)\",\"Correct. CMM was developed by the Software Engineering Institute (SEI) and Carnegie Mellon University under the U.S. Department of Defense (L7, CMM).\"],[\"IEEE in 1976\",\"IEEE made the Software Engineering Standard Collection.\"],[\"Motorola\",\"The lecture does not name Motorola as CMM's developer; it credits SEI and Carnegie Mellon University under the U.S. DoD.\"]],\"a\":1}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"A company plans and tracks each project, manages its suppliers and product configurations, and measures projects. However, there is no organization-wide standard process. Which CMM level is it at?\",\"o\":[[\"Level 1 – Initial\",\"Level 1 has no required processes; this company already does project management.\"],[\"Level 3 – Defined\",\"Level 3 requires standardized processes across the organization.\"],[\"Level 4 – Quantitatively Managed\",\"Level 4 uses statistical and quantitative control of processes.\"],[\"Level 2 – Repeatable\",\"Correct. These are Level 2 improvements (basic project management); organization-wide standard processes would be Level 3.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A company has standard processes across the whole organization that each team tailors, collects process data, and runs organization-wide training, but does not yet control processes statistically. Which CMM level?\",\"o\":[[\"Level 3 – Defined\",\"Correct. Standardized, tailored processes and organization-wide training are Level 3.\"],[\"Level 2 – Repeatable\",\"Level 2 is basic project management without organization-wide standards.\"],[\"Level 4 – Quantitatively Managed\",\"Statistical control is missing, so it has not reached Level 4.\"],[\"Level 5 – Optimizing\",\"Level 5 is continuous improvement with change infrastructure.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A company already controls its processes quantitatively and now develops a change infrastructure, assesses improvements and removes the causes of defects for continuous improvement. Which CMM level?\",\"o\":[[\"Level 4 – Quantitatively Managed\",\"Level 4 is supervising processes quantitatively and creating capability baselines.\"],[\"Level 5 – Optimizing\",\"Correct. These are the Level 5 (change management) improvements.\"],[\"Level 3 – Defined\",\"Level 3 is standardizing processes across the organization.\"],[\"Level 1 – Initial\",\"Level 1 is ad-hoc with no required processes.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the CMM figure, which label is on the arrow from Level 2 to Level 3?\",\"o\":[[\"Basic management control\",\"Basic management control is the label on the arrow from Level 1 to Level 2, not from Level 2 to Level 3.\"],[\"Process measurement\",\"Process measurement labels the arrow from Level 3 to Level 4, one step further up.\"],[\"Process control\",\"Process control labels the last arrow, from Level 4 to Level 5.\"],[\"Process definition\",\"Correct. 1→2 basic management control, 2→3 process definition, 3→4 process measurement, 4→5 process control.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Which CMM component is the organization's ability to meet quality expectations with its current processes, helping to predict future project outcomes?\",\"o\":[[\"Maturity levels\",\"Maturity levels are the 5 stages that define process capability.\"],[\"Common features\",\"Common features show whether a KPA's implementation is successful and sustainable.\"],[\"Process capability\",\"Correct. This is the lecture's definition of process capability.\"],[\"Goals\",\"Goals evaluate essential practices to see if a process area is implemented successfully.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"\\\"A software delivery schedule follows a documented procedure\\\" is the lecture's example of which CMM component?\",\"o\":[[\"Key Process Areas\",\"The KPA example is \\\"software project delivery planning\\\".\"],[\"Key practices\",\"Correct. Key practices are the actions and infrastructure needed to support a KPA.\"],[\"Maturity levels\",\"Maturity levels are the 5 stages, not an action.\"],[\"Process capability\",\"Process capability is the ability to meet quality expectations.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"A company at CMM Level 2 can jump directly to Level 4 if it starts using statistical techniques.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"CMM is a five-level incremental approach: organizations progress gradually without skipping levels.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Organizations progress gradually and cannot skip levels.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A quality team investigates the root cause of a recurring defect so that it does not happen again. Which ISO 9000 element is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Corrective & preventive action\",\"Correct. Element 14, corrective &amp; preventive action, deals with finding root causes of problems so they do not recur (L7, 20 elements of ISO 9000).\"],[\"Control of non-conforming product\",\"Control of non-conforming product (element 13) handles rejected items already found, not the root cause that prevents recurrence.\"],[\"Internal quality audits\",\"Audits check the quality system; root-cause action is element 14.\"],[\"Statistical techniques\",\"Statistical techniques (element 20) means using tools such as control charts, not acting on a defect's root cause.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT one of ISO's 8 quality management principles?\",\"o\":[[\"Maximizing short-term profit\",\"Correct. ISO's 8 principles are customer focus, leadership, involvement of people, process approach, system approach, continuous improvement, factual decisions and supplier relations; profit is not one (L7).\"],[\"Customer focus\",\"Customer focus IS one of ISO's 8 quality management principles (the first in the list).\"],[\"Factual decision making\",\"Factual decision making IS one of ISO's 8 quality management principles.\"],[\"Mutually beneficial supplier relationships\",\"Mutually beneficial supplier relationships IS one of ISO's 8 principles (the last in the list).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"SPICE is linked with which standard as a joint ISO and IEC initiative for software process assessment?\",\"o\":[[\"ISO 9000\",\"ISO 9000 is the generic quality standard with 20 elements.\"],[\"The IEEE Software Engineering Standard Collection\",\"That is IEEE's collection (since 1976), not SPICE.\"],[\"CMM\",\"CMM is from SEI and Carnegie Mellon, not ISO and IEC.\"],[\"ISO/IEC 15504\",\"Correct. SPICE with ISO/IEC 15504, introduced in 1993.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"……… is a standard framework for assessing and improving the maturity of a software company's development process.\",\"o\":[[\"ISO 9000\",\"ISO 9000 is a set of generic quality standards with 20 elements for any organization, not a maturity framework.\"],[\"SPICE\",\"SPICE (ISO/IEC 15504) is the ISO/IEC software process assessment initiative, not the maturity framework described here.\"],[\"CMM\",\"Correct. CMM (Capability Maturity Model) is exactly this framework. (L7, CMM)\"],[\"DMAIC\",\"DMAIC is the Six Sigma methodology (Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control), from Lecture 8.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In the development of CMM, \\\"SEI\\\" stands for:\",\"o\":[[\"Software Engineering Institute\",\"Correct. CMM was developed by the Software Engineering Institute (SEI) and Carnegie Mellon University. (L7, CMM)\"],[\"Software Evaluation Index\",\"No such body is in the lecture; SEI is the Software Engineering Institute.\"],[\"Standard Engineering Institute\",\"Wrong first word: the S is for Software.\"],[\"Systems Engineering International\",\"Not the lecture's expansion; SEI is the Software Engineering Institute.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: CMM is designed only for software development organizations and cannot be applied to project management or risk management.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"It is designed mainly for software development, but not only for it.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. It also applies to software/system engineering, project management, IT, risk management and personnel management. (L7, CMM)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the CMM levels figure, Level 2 (Repeatable) is labelled with which kind of management?\",\"o\":[[\"Inconsistent Management\",\"That is the label of Level 1 (Initial).\"],[\"Project Management\",\"Correct. The figure reads \\\"Level 2: Repeatable (Project Management)\\\". (L7, Five levels of CMM)\"],[\"Process Management\",\"That is the label of Level 3 (Defined).\"],[\"Change Management\",\"That is the label of Level 5 (Optimizing).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the CMM levels figure, the arrow from Level 3 to Level 4 is labelled:\",\"o\":[[\"Basic Management Control\",\"That arrow goes from Level 1 to Level 2.\"],[\"Process Definition\",\"That arrow goes from Level 2 to Level 3.\"],[\"Process Control\",\"That arrow goes from Level 4 to Level 5.\"],[\"Process Measurement\",\"Correct. Level 3 (Defined) moves to Level 4 through \\\"Process Measurement\\\". (L7, Five levels of CMM)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"According to Table 1, \\\"create capability baselines\\\" is an improvement implemented at which CMM level?\",\"o\":[[\"Level 2 Repeatable\",\"Level 2 plans and tracks projects, manages suppliers and configurations and measures projects.\"],[\"Level 3 Defined\",\"Level 3 identifies and organizes processes, collects process data and gives organization-wide training.\"],[\"Level 4 Quantitatively Managed\",\"Correct. Level 4 supervises processes quantitatively and creates capability baselines. (L7, Table 1)\"],[\"Level 5 Optimizing\",\"Level 5 develops change infrastructure, organizes improvements and removes causes of defects.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"According to Table 1, \\\"manage with non-software groups\\\" is implemented at which CMM level?\",\"o\":[[\"Level 2 Repeatable\",\"Level 2 is about project-level control: plan and track projects, manage suppliers and configurations.\"],[\"Level 3 Defined\",\"Correct. Managing with non-software groups is in the Level 3 (Defined) list, with organization-wide training and process data. (L7, Table 1)\"],[\"Level 4 Quantitatively Managed\",\"Level 4 only supervises processes quantitatively and creates capability baselines.\"],[\"Level 1 Initial\",\"Level 1 has no required processes.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: According to Table 1, CMM Level 1 (Initial) has no required processes.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Table 1 lists \\\"no required processes\\\" for Level 1. (L7, Table 1)\"],[\"False\",\"Level 1 is the ad-hoc level; Table 1 lists nothing required for it.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which CMM component is described as \\\"groups of related activities that achieve goals when performed together\\\", for example software project delivery planning?\",\"o\":[[\"Key Process Areas (KPA)\",\"Correct. A KPA is a group of related activities that achieve goals together; the example is delivery planning. (L7, CMM structure)\"],[\"Key practices\",\"Key practices are the actions and infrastructure that support a KPA (example: a delivery schedule follows a documented procedure).\"],[\"Goals\",\"Goals evaluate the essential practices of a process area to see if it was implemented successfully.\"],[\"Common features\",\"Common features show whether a KPA's implementation is successful and sustainable.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which CMM component consists of characteristics showing whether a KPA's implementation is successful and sustainable?\",\"o\":[[\"Goals\",\"Goals evaluate essential practices within a process area and highlight its importance and purpose.\"],[\"Key practices\",\"Key practices define the actions and infrastructure needed to support a KPA.\"],[\"Process capability\",\"Process capability is the organization's ability to meet quality expectations with its current processes.\"],[\"Common features\",\"Correct. Common features are the characteristics that show whether a KPA's implementation is successful and sustainable. (L7, CMM structure)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Which aspect of a Key Process Area covers \\\"the activities carried out to enable the organization to meet the commitments\\\"?\",\"o\":[[\"Commitment\",\"Commitment is the set of requirements that must be met to achieve the goals.\"],[\"Ability\",\"Correct. Ability is the activities that enable the organization to meet the commitments. (L7, KPA)\"],[\"Measurement\",\"Measurement means the activities performed must be monitored.\"],[\"Verification\",\"Verification is the methods that define how the work is verified.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: In a KPA, \\\"Commitment\\\" means the activities carried out to enable the organization to meet its goals.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"That description belongs to another aspect.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Commitment is the requirements that must be met to achieve the goals; the enabling activities are \\\"Ability\\\". (L7, KPA)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is listed in the lecture as a benefit of CMM?\",\"o\":[[\"It reduces defects to 3.4 per million opportunities\",\"That is the Six Sigma target (Lecture 8).\"],[\"It consists of 20 key elements that must be documented\",\"That describes ISO 9000.\"],[\"It helps get project funding, e.g. for a metadata repository\",\"Correct. Helping get funding (enterprise data asset management, metadata repository) is one of the five benefits of CMM. (L7, Benefits of CMM)\"],[\"It was introduced in 1993 as ISO/IEC 15504\",\"That describes SPICE.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"The word \\\"ISO\\\" comes from the Greek word \\\"isos\\\", which means:\",\"o\":[[\"Equal\",\"Correct. isos = \\\"equal\\\", reflecting a global standard for quality assurance. (L7, ISO 9000)\"],[\"Standard\",\"Standard is the S in the full name, not the meaning of isos.\"],[\"Quality\",\"Quality is what the standard is about, not the meaning of isos.\"],[\"International\",\"International is part of the organization's name, not the Greek word's meaning.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: ISO 9000 is highly generic and applies to any organization providing products or services.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture calls ISO 9000 \\\"highly generic\\\", for any organization with products or services. (L7, ISO 9000)\"],[\"False\",\"It is not limited to software companies.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which ISO 9000 element requires instruments to be calibrated regularly?\",\"o\":[[\"Inspection &amp; Testing\",\"Element 10 performs receiving, in-process and final inspections.\"],[\"Inspection/Measuring Equipment\",\"Correct. Element 11 is about calibrating instruments regularly. (L7, 20 elements of ISO 9000)\"],[\"Inspection &amp; Test Status\",\"Element 12 clearly identifies the products that were inspected.\"],[\"Process Control\",\"Element 9 uses work instructions, quality plans and standards.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"An auditor asks a company to show records that prove its quality checks for an audit trail. Which ISO 9000 element covers keeping them?\",\"o\":[[\"Internal Quality Audits\",\"Element 17 is conducting periodic audits and sharing results, not keeping the records.\"],[\"Documentation &amp; Data Control\",\"Element 5 is strict control of document creation and modification.\"],[\"Training\",\"Element 18 keeps training records and qualifications of employees only.\"],[\"Quality Records\",\"Correct. Element 16 maintains records for audit trails. (L7, 20 elements of ISO 9000)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"\\\"Base decisions on reliable data and analysis\\\" is which of ISO's 8 principles?\",\"o\":[[\"Process Approach\",\"Process approach manages activities as processes for consistent results.\"],[\"Continuous Improvement\",\"Continuous improvement makes ongoing improvement a permanent objective.\"],[\"Factual Decision Making\",\"Correct. Factual decision making bases decisions on reliable data and analysis. (L7, ISO 8 principles)\"],[\"System Approach to Management\",\"System approach aligns interconnected processes as one system.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: The ISO principle \\\"System Approach to Management\\\" means managing each activity as a separate process to get consistent results.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"That wording mixes up two principles.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Managing activities as processes is the \\\"Process Approach\\\"; the system approach aligns interconnected processes as one system. (L7, ISO 8 principles)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Since ……… IEEE has contributed significantly to standardization, leading to the IEEE Software Engineering Standard Collection.\",\"o\":[[\"1976\",\"Correct. IEEE has worked on standardization since 1976. (L7, Software engineering standards)\"],[\"1993\",\"1993 is the year SPICE was introduced.\"],[\"1985\",\"This year is not in the lecture; IEEE's year is 1976.\"],[\"2005\",\"This year is not in the lecture.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: Organizations are increasingly shifting from international to local software engineering standards.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The direction is reversed.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The shift is from local to international standards. (L7, Software engineering standards)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is one of the three objectives of SPICE?\",\"o\":[[\"Reduce process variation to 3.4 defects per million\",\"That is the Six Sigma goal.\"],[\"Define 20 key elements that must be clearly documented\",\"That is ISO 9000.\"],[\"Provide five maturity levels that cannot be skipped\",\"That is CMM.\"],[\"Facilitate global adoption of software process assessment in industry\",\"Correct. This is SPICE objective 3, with a standard assessment framework and organizational assessments for emerging standards. (L7, SPICE)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: SPICE (ISO/IEC 15504) is a joint initiative of ISO and IEEE.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"IEEE is the body behind the Software Engineering Standard Collection, not SPICE.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. SPICE is a joint initiative of ISO and IEC, as the name ISO/IEC 15504 shows. (L7, SPICE)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A company's processes are unstructured and reactive; each project is handled differently and goals are met unreliably. Which CMM level is it at?\",\"o\":[[\"Level 1 Initial\",\"Correct. Unstructured, reactive, inconsistent processes and unreliable goals describe Level 1. (L7, Five levels of CMM)\"],[\"Level 2 Repeatable\",\"Level 2 already has basic project management giving repeatable success.\"],[\"Level 3 Defined\",\"Level 3 has standardized processes across the organization.\"],[\"Level 4 Quantitatively Managed\",\"Level 4 controls processes statistically, the opposite of ad-hoc.\"]],\"a\":0}]},{\"n\":8,\"title\":\"Six Sigma\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Introduction and definitions\",\"pts\":[\"Six Sigma is one of the most famous management concepts in <b>total quality management</b>. It emerged in the <b>late 1970s and early 1980s</b> and became popular as major global companies adopted it.\",\"Six Sigma leads companies to avoid the highest possible percentage of errors <b>in all their activities</b> and to reduce quality defects as much as possible. Quality control programs have been designed to focus on correcting <b>design, industrial and commercial</b> defects.\",\"<b>Standard deviation (σ)</b> is <b>a statistical method and an indicator</b> used to describe deviation, variance, dispersion or asymmetry in a certain process with respect to the desired objectives. σ = square root of variance.\",\"6 Sigma is a <b>systematic method</b> for using the collection of critically important information and statistical analysis to identify sources of errors and ways to eliminate them. 6 Sigma focuses on performance measurements combined with statistical analysis of errors <b>present in quality programs</b>.\",\"It is a <b>statistical management system</b> focusing on customer satisfaction, reducing waste, improving quality, and improving financial and time performance.\",\"It reduces process variation until the result is <b>3.4 defects per million</b> samples (opportunities) or less. The aim is <b>zero defects</b> (\\\"zero default\\\" on the slide), offering customers a zero-defect product.\"]},{\"h\":\"Normal distribution (figure)\",\"table\":[[\"Range\",\"Inside the limits\",\"Out of spec (ppm)\"],[\"±1σ\",\"68.27%\",\"317,300\"],[\"±2σ\",\"95.45%\",\"45,500\"],[\"±3σ\",\"99.73%\",\"2,700\"],[\"±4σ\",\"99.9937%\",\"63\"],[\"±5σ\",\"99.999943%\",\"0.57\"],[\"±6σ\",\"99.9999998%\",\"0.002\"]],\"pts\":[\"Figure (<b>Normal Distribution - Gaussian Curve</b>): a bell-shaped curve centred on 0 (a vertical centre line), with the horizontal axis marked from -6σ to +6σ; one σ is marked as the distance from the centre line to +1σ, with the label <b>Sigma = σ = Deviation (Square root of variance)</b>. Below the curve, arrows show the share of values between ±1σ, ±2σ … ±6σ and the result in ppm out of spec (table below).\"],\"fig\":\"qa-sigma-curve\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-sigma-curve\":0},\"figH\":{\"qa-sigma-curve\":\"Try it: the normal curve from ±1σ to ±6σ, and how many defects per million each allows\"}},{\"h\":\"3.4 vs 0.002 ppm\",\"pts\":[\"The figure shows the centred curve: ±6σ leaves only 0.002 ppm outside. The famous <b>3.4 defects per million</b> comes from allowing the process mean to <b>drift by 1.5σ</b> in the long term (6 − 1.5 = 4.5σ on one side). This last point is general Six Sigma background, not on the slide.\",\"<b>Background formula</b> (not on the slides, useful if asked): DPMO = defects ÷ (units × opportunities per unit) × 1,000,000. Example: 25 defects in 500 units with 10 opportunities → 25 ÷ 5,000 × 10⁶ = <b>5,000 DPMO</b> (yield 99.5%, about 4.1σ with the 1.5σ shift). At 6σ, DPMO = 3.4.\"]},{\"h\":\"DMAIC methodology\",\"pts\":[\"<b>6 Sigma Objective</b>: the 6 Sigma methodology is <b>DMAIC</b>: Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control.\",\"<b>D – Define</b>: identify what needs to be <b>improved and developed in the organization's work and activities</b> through the precise identification of the problems <b>that obstacle this improvement</b>. The problem statement should generally include the <b>level</b> of the problem, its <b>location</b> and its <b>financial impact</b>. Also, <b>form a team</b> to solve the problem.\",\"<b>M – Measure</b>: it mainly focuses on planning and implementing performance measurement against the requirements and expectations of customers, based on a set of criteria for measuring defects through the use of statistical control with <b>various scientific and statistical tools (6 σ)</b>.\",\"<b>A – Analyze</b>: analyze the collected data with the aim of identifying and determining the <b>root causes</b> of all problems <b>and obstacles to improvement</b>, by analyzing the chain of failure causes, then ranking them according to their impact, categorizing them, and then controlling and managing them.\",\"<b>I – Improve</b>: changes in processes occur to ensure the elimination of product defects, excessive costs, waste, etc., <b>confirming the precise identification of customer needs and expectations</b>. Studying the causes of failure, finding solutions for them, <b>testing these solutions on a sample</b> of products, <b>determining the results</b>, and making the necessary corrections.\",\"<b>C – Control</b>: focus on monitoring all of these processes and do not stop, <b>as there are always areas in need of improvement</b>. The following should be taken into consideration:<br>• continuously measuring performance standards<br>• conducting measurement processes<br>• correcting any deviations, if found, at the specified time.\"],\"fig\":\"qa-dmaic\",\"figH\":{\"qa-dmaic\":\"DMAIC step by step: Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control\"}},{\"h\":\"DMADV methodology\",\"pts\":[\"The slide also shows <b>DMADV</b> (Define, Measure, Analyze, Design, Verify). ‡ (not on the slides) DMADV is used for designing <b>new</b> processes/products; DMAIC improves <b>existing</b> ones.\",\"Figure (DMADV Methodology): five curved arrows forming a cycle around a central circle labelled \\\"DMADV\\\": <b>Define</b> → <b>Measure</b> → <b>Analyze</b> → <b>Design</b> → <b>Verify</b> → back to Define. It covers part of a DMAIC figure behind it, whose visible fragments read \\\"Control: Maint… soluti…\\\" and \\\"Improve: Implement … the solution\\\".\"],\"fig\":\"qa-dmadv\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-dmadv\":1},\"figH\":{\"qa-dmadv\":\"The DMADV cycle: Define, Measure, Analyze, Design, Verify\"}},{\"h\":\"Principles and implementation phases\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Principles</b>:<br>• focus on customers<br>• making decisions based on facts and accurate data<br>• focusing on internal operations and activities<br>• effective management based on advance planning<br>• collaborative teamwork and avoiding competition <b>in order to achieve the desired goals</b>.\",\"<b>Implementation phases</b>:<br>(1) identifying and selecting the important projects <b>in which the Six Sigma methodology will be applied</b><br>(2) assigning the organization's employees <b>who have good experience</b> to improvement tasks<br>(3) preparing a written document for the problem or project, <b>including everything related to the project</b>, its reasons for being chosen, its objectives and its scope<br>(4) training on <b>requirements related to Six Sigma</b>, such as measurement, analysis, process redesign, planning and problem-solving<br>(5) <b>implementing the process</b>, providing practical solutions and applying them<br>(6) submission of solutions.\"]},{\"h\":\"Case: failure cases in the preparatory certificate\",\"pts\":[\"Table on the slide: <b>Six Sigma for failure cases in the preparatory certificate</b> (the slide's column headings are in Arabic: المواد, المتوسط الحسابي, الإنحراف المعياري; the overall row المؤشر الكلي is in bold).\",\"A table of 10 subjects (X1–X10) gives each subject's mean, standard deviation and a \\\"Six sigma\\\" value. The Six Sigma column is <b>6 × standard deviation</b>: e.g. X1: 6 × 1.22 = 7.32; X2: 6 × 0.98 = 5.88; overall: 6 × 1.07 = 6.42 (overall mean 2.99).\",\"Applying the Six Sigma methodology, which can be summarized as:<br>• <b>Define</b>: identify these causes;<br>• then <b>Measure</b>: measure the impact of the magnitude of these causes;<br>• then <b>Analyze</b>: the sources and elements, etc.;<br>• then <b>Improve</b>: <b>derive appropriate solutions through the previous (DMAIC) steps</b>, which is useful for reducing failure cases and thereby improving and increasing the level of success;<br>• and finally <b>Control</b>: <b>continuously monitor the improvement processes</b>.\"],\"table\":[[\"Subject (المواد)\",\"Mean (المتوسط الحسابي)\",\"Standard deviation (الإنحراف المعياري)\",\"Six sigma\"],[\"X1\",\"2.08\",\"1.22\",\"7.32\"],[\"X2\",\"3.00\",\"0.98\",\"5.88\"],[\"X3\",\"3.30\",\"1.04\",\"6.24\"],[\"X4\",\"2.87\",\"0.96\",\"5.76\"],[\"X5\",\"3.37\",\"1.17\",\"7.02\"],[\"X6\",\"3.00\",\"1.05\",\"6.30\"],[\"X7\",\"3.06\",\"1.27\",\"7.62\"],[\"X8\",\"3.00\",\"1.06\",\"6.36\"],[\"X9\",\"3.11\",\"0.91\",\"5.46\"],[\"X10\",\"2.51\",\"1.03\",\"6.18\"],[\"<b>Overall indicator (المؤشر الكلي)</b>\",\"<b>2.99</b>\",\"<b>1.07</b>\",\"<b>6.42</b>\"]],\"fig\":\"qa-sigma-case\",\"figH\":{\"qa-sigma-case\":\"Six sigma = 6 × standard deviation, subject by subject\"}},{\"h\":\"Six Sigma belts (levels of certification)\",\"table\":[[\"Belt\",\"Applied to\"],[\"White Belt\",\"Step 1 in the certification figure (entry level)\"],[\"Yellow Belt\",\"All employees\"],[\"Green Belt\",\"Executive employees\"],[\"Black Belt\",\"Middle and senior management\"],[\"Master Black Belt\",\"Senior management\"]],\"pts\":[\"Figure (<b>Levels of Six Sigma Certification</b>): a man with a briefcase climbs a staircase toward a target with an arrow in its bullseye. The steps from bottom to top:<br>• <b>Step 1: White Belt</b><br>• <b>Step 2: Yellow Belt</b><br>• <b>Step 3: Green Belt</b><br>• <b>Step 4: Black Belt</b><br>• <b>Step 5: Master Black Belt</b> (the top step, next to the target).\"],\"fig\":\"qa-belts\",\"figAt\":{\"qa-belts\":0},\"figH\":{\"qa-belts\":\"The five levels of Six Sigma certification, White Belt to Master Black Belt\"}},{\"h\":\"Six Sigma tools and techniques\",\"pts\":[\"• <b>5S</b><br>• <b>Seven Wastes</b><br>• <b>Value stream mapping</b><br>• <b>Visual workspace</b><br>• <b>Voice of Customer (VOC)</b><br>• <b>Kaizen</b><br>• <b>Kanban</b><br>• <b>Regression analysis</b>\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Six Sigma target\",\"3.4 defects per million opportunities or less (aim: zero defects).\"],[\"Sigma (σ)\",\"Standard deviation = square root of variance.\"],[\"When did Six Sigma emerge?\",\"Late 1970s and early 1980s.\"],[\"DMAIC\",\"Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control.\"],[\"DMADV\",\"Define, Measure, Analyze, Design, Verify (‡ not on the slides: for new designs).\"],[\"Define phase includes\",\"Precise problem identification (level, location, financial impact) and forming a team.\"],[\"Analyze phase\",\"Find root causes, rank them by impact, categorize, control.\"],[\"Improve phase\",\"Change processes; test solutions on a sample of products; correct.\"],[\"Control phase\",\"Continuously measure performance, conduct measurement, correct deviations on time.\"],[\"±3σ coverage\",\"99.73% (2,700 ppm out of spec).\"],[\"Yellow Belt\",\"All employees.\"],[\"Green Belt\",\"Executive employees.\"],[\"Black Belt\",\"Middle and senior management.\"],[\"Master Black Belt\",\"Senior management.\"],[\"DPMO formula (background)\",\"Defects ÷ (units × opportunities) × 1,000,000.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Briefly discuss the Six Sigma methodology.\",\"Six Sigma is a statistical management methodology that reduces process variation to 3.4 defects per million or less, aiming for zero defects and customer satisfaction. It uses DMAIC: Define the problem (level, location, financial impact) and form a team; Measure performance against customer requirements with statistical tools; Analyze data to find and rank root causes; Improve processes and test solutions on a sample; Control by continuously monitoring and correcting deviations.\"],[\"What are the principles of Six Sigma?\",\"Focus on customers; decisions based on facts and accurate data; focus on internal operations; effective management based on advance planning; collaborative teamwork without competition.\"],[\"List the Six Sigma belts and who they are for.\",\"Yellow: all employees. Green: executive employees. Black: middle and senior management. Master Black: senior management. (The figure also starts with a White Belt.)\"],[\"Calculate DPMO for 25 defects found in 500 units with 10 opportunities each.\",\"DPMO = 25 / (500 x 10) x 1,000,000 = 25 / 5,000 x 1,000,000 = 5,000 DPMO, about 4.1 sigma. This is background practice, not on the slides.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"............ reduce process variation to reach zero default.\",\"o\":[[\"6 Sigma\",\"Correct. \\\"Six Sigma reduces process variation… The aim of six sigma is to reach zero default.\\\"\"],[\"SPICE\",\"SPICE (ISO/IEC 15504) is a standard for software process assessment (L7); it does not set a goal of reducing variation to zero defects.\"],[\"CMM\",\"CMM is a five-level maturity framework for assessing and improving the development process (L7), not a variation-reduction method.\"],[\"ISO 9000\",\"ISO 9000 is a generic set of 20 documented quality elements (L7); reducing process variation to reach zero defects is Six Sigma.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Six Sigma aims to reduce defects to:\",\"o\":[[\"3.4 per thousand\",\"The Six Sigma target is 3.4 defects per MILLION opportunities; 3.4 per thousand would be a thousand times worse.\"],[\"3.4 per million or less\",\"Correct. Six Sigma reduces process variation until the result is 3.4 defects per million samples (opportunities) or less, aiming for zero defects (L8, Definitions).\"],[\"2,700 per million\",\"2,700 ppm is the share outside ±3σ in the normal distribution figure, far above Six Sigma's target of 3.4 per million.\"],[\"68.27%\",\"68.27% is the share of values inside ±1σ in the normal distribution figure, not a defect rate.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"What does DMAIC stand for?\",\"o\":[[\"Design, Measure, Analyze, Implement, Check\",\"Only M and A are right; D is Define (not Design), I is Improve (not Implement) and C is Control (not Check).\"],[\"Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control\",\"Correct. DMAIC = Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control, the Six Sigma method (‡ for improving existing processes, not on the slides) (L8, DMAIC).\"],[\"Define, Manage, Audit, Inspect, Control\",\"Only D and C are right; M is Measure (not Manage), A is Analyze (not Audit) and I is Improve (not Inspect).\"],[\"Develop, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Close\",\"D is Define (not Develop) and C is Control (not Close); Six Sigma's last phase never stops monitoring, so it is not \\\"close\\\".\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which DMAIC phase forms the team and states the problem's level, location and financial impact?\",\"o\":[[\"Define\",\"Correct. In Define the problem is identified precisely, with its level, location and financial impact, and a team is formed to solve it (L8, DMAIC).\"],[\"Measure\",\"Measure plans and implements performance measurement.\"],[\"Analyze\",\"Analyze studies the data to find and rank the root causes; stating the problem and forming the team happen earlier, in Define.\"],[\"Control\",\"Control is the last phase: continuous monitoring and correcting deviations after improvement, not forming the team.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In which phase are solutions tested on a sample of products?\",\"o\":[[\"Measure\",\"Measure plans and implements performance measurement against customer requirements; solutions are not yet found, let alone tested.\"],[\"Analyze\",\"Analyze finds and ranks the root causes of problems; finding solutions and testing them on a sample come in Improve.\"],[\"Improve\",\"Correct. Improve changes processes to remove defects and waste: study failure causes, find solutions, test them on a sample of products, check results and correct (L8, DMAIC).\"],[\"Define\",\"Define identifies the problem (level, location, financial impact) and forms the team; no solutions exist yet to test.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"What percentage of values lies within ±3σ?\",\"o\":[[\"68.27%\",\"68.27% lies within ±1σ (317,300 ppm outside), not ±3σ.\"],[\"95.45%\",\"95.45% lies within ±2σ (45,500 ppm outside), not ±3σ.\"],[\"99.73%\",\"Correct. The normal distribution figure gives 99.73% inside ±3σ, leaving 2,700 ppm out of spec (L8, Normal distribution).\"],[\"99.9937%\",\"99.9937% lies within ±4σ (63 ppm outside), one step beyond ±3σ.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"The Green Belt is applied to:\",\"o\":[[\"All employees\",\"All employees take the Yellow Belt; the Green Belt is for executive employees (L8, Belts).\"],[\"Executive employees\",\"Correct. In the belts table the Green Belt is applied to executive employees; Yellow is for all employees and Black for middle and senior management (L8, Belts).\"],[\"Middle and senior management\",\"Middle and senior management take the Black Belt, one level above the Green Belt.\"],[\"Senior management only\",\"Senior management takes the Master Black Belt, the highest belt; the Green Belt is for executive employees.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT listed as a Six Sigma tool/technique?\",\"o\":[[\"Kaizen\",\"Kaizen (continuous small improvements) IS in the lecture's list of Six Sigma tools and techniques.\"],[\"Voice of Customer\",\"Voice of Customer (VOC) IS in the lecture's list of Six Sigma tools, alongside 5S and Kaizen.\"],[\"Cyclomatic complexity\",\"Correct. That is a software metric (L6), not a Six Sigma tool.\"],[\"Value stream mapping\",\"Value stream mapping IS in the lecture's list of Six Sigma tools and techniques.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In the preparatory-certificate table, a subject has SD = 0.98. Its \\\"Six sigma\\\" value is:\",\"o\":[[\"0.98\",\"0.98 is σ itself; the table's Six Sigma column is 6 × σ = 6 × 0.98 = 5.88.\"],[\"5.88\",\"Correct. The case study's Six Sigma column is 6 × standard deviation: 6 × 0.98 = 5.88, like X1: 6 × 1.22 = 7.32 (L8, Case study).\"],[\"6.98\",\"6.98 = 6 + 0.98 adds instead of multiplying; Six Sigma = 6 × 0.98 = 5.88.\"],[\"2.94\",\"2.94 = 3 × 0.98 is three sigma; the table uses six: 6 × 0.98 = 5.88.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A process has 25 defects in 500 units, each with 10 defect opportunities. DPMO = ?\",\"o\":[[\"50,000\",\"You forgot the 10 opportunities per unit (25/500 × 10⁶).\"],[\"5,000\",\"Correct. DPMO = defects ÷ (units × opportunities) × 10⁶ = 25 ÷ (500 × 10) × 10⁶ = 25 ÷ 5,000 × 10⁶ = 5,000 (L8 notes, background formula).\"],[\"500\",\"500 is ten times too small (e.g. multiplying by 10⁵, not 10⁶): 25 ÷ 5,000 × 1,000,000 = 5,000.\"],[\"3.4\",\"3.4 is the Six Sigma target DPMO, not this process's value; 25 ÷ 5,000 × 10⁶ = 5,000.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which is a Six Sigma principle?\",\"o\":[[\"Competition between teams\",\"The principle is collaborative teamwork, avoiding competition.\"],[\"Decisions based on facts and accurate data\",\"Correct. The Six Sigma principles are focus on customers, decisions based on facts and accurate data, focus on internal operations, advance planning and collaborative teamwork (L8, Principles).\"],[\"Decisions based on intuition\",\"Intuition is the opposite of the principle: Six Sigma decisions must be based on facts and accurate data.\"],[\"Ignoring customers\",\"Focus on customers is the first Six Sigma principle, and the method aims at customer satisfaction.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"During which Phase of DMAIC is the solution monitored for long-term stability?\",\"o\":[[\"Measure\",\"Measure measures performance against customer requirements with statistical tools, before any solution exists (L8).\"],[\"Control\",\"Correct. Lecture 8: \\\"C – Control: focus on monitoring all of these processes and do not stop\\\": continuously measure performance and correct any deviations. The summary repeats it: \\\"finally Control: continuously monitor the improvement processes\\\" (L8, DMAIC).\"],[\"Improve\",\"Improve finds and tests solutions and puts them in place; Control keeps them there (L8).\"],[\"Define\",\"Define identifies the problem (level, location, financial impact) and forms the team (L8).\"],[\"Analyze\",\"Analyze finds the root causes from the collected data (L8).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"In which among the following is the Lean Six Sigma process not applicable?\",\"o\":[[\"Selecting the best employee of the year\",\"Correct. Lean Six Sigma improves repeatable processes: it cuts waste (Lean) and reduces defects and variation (Six Sigma) with DMAIC. Lecture 8 says Six Sigma targets errors \\\"in all their activities\\\". Choosing the employee of the year is a one-off judgement, not a repeatable process with defects to measure, so DMAIC has nothing to work on.\"],[\"Business administration\",\"Administrative processes (approvals, invoicing, HR workflows) repeat and can be measured, so Lean Six Sigma applies.\"],[\"Healthcare\",\"Healthcare is one of its biggest users, e.g. reducing waiting times and medication errors.\"],[\"Supply Chain\",\"Supply chain (inventory, logistics, delivery) is a classic Lean use.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is true about variation?\",\"o\":[[\"It decreases cycle time\",\"Variation causes rework and delays, so it makes cycle time longer, not shorter (general Lean knowledge).\"],[\"It increases the likelihood of failing to meet customer requirements\",\"Correct. Lecture 8: Six Sigma focuses on customer satisfaction and \\\"reduces process variation until the result is 3.4 defects per million.\\\" More variation means more output falls outside the customer's limits, so more defects and more failures to meet requirements; the slide's normal-curve table shows the wider the spread, the more parts per million out of spec (L8).\"],[\"It is one of the 8 Wastes\",\"The usual 8 wastes are transport, inventory, motion, waiting, overproduction, overprocessing, defects and unused skills; variation is not one of them (general knowledge; L8 only names the \\\"Seven Wastes\\\" tool).\"],[\"It is only part of manufacturing processes\",\"L8 says Six Sigma targets errors \\\"in all their activities\\\": design, industrial and commercial.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"During which Phase of DMAIC is the baseline of the problem determined?\",\"o\":[[\"Control\",\"Control comes last and monitors the solution so it stays stable (L8).\"],[\"Define\",\"Define describes the problem (level, location, financial impact) and forms the team; Measure puts numbers on it (L8).\"],[\"Measure\",\"Correct. Lecture 8: Measure is \\\"planning and implementing performance measurement against the requirements and expectations of customers,\\\" measuring defects with statistical tools. Measuring current performance before changing anything is the baseline, which later improvements are compared against (L8, DMAIC).\"],[\"Improve\",\"Improve finds and tests solutions, judged against the baseline from Measure (L8).\"],[\"Analyze\",\"Analyze finds root causes using the data Measure collected (L8).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"What is the percentage accuracy in the six sigma process?\",\"o\":[[\"99.99966%\",\"Correct. Lecture 8: Six Sigma reduces variation until the result is 3.4 defects per million opportunities. 3.4 / 1,000,000 = 0.00034% defective, so 100% − 0.00034% = 99.99966% defect-free. (The slide's ±6σ = 99.9999998% is for a perfectly centred process; 3.4 per million allows a 1.5σ drift.)\"],[\"99.1%\",\"About 9,000 defects per million, roughly 3.9 sigma.\"],[\"99.8%\",\"About 2,000 defects per million, roughly 4.4 sigma.\"],[\"99.05%\",\"About 9,500 defects per million, roughly 3.8 sigma.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is not an advantage of using Lean Six Sigma?\",\"o\":[[\"Product improvement\",\"A real advantage: improving quality and offering a zero-defect product (L8).\"],[\"A decrease in customer satisfaction\",\"Correct. Lecture 8: Six Sigma is \\\"a statistical management system focusing on customer satisfaction, reducing waste, improving quality,\\\" and reduces defects to 3.4 per million. It aims to raise customer satisfaction, so a decrease cannot be an advantage.\"],[\"Defect reduction\",\"A real advantage: the core goal, 3.4 defects per million or fewer (L8).\"],[\"Process improvement\",\"A real advantage: DMAIC's Improve phase changes processes to remove defects, costs and waste (L8).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"According to the normal distribution figure, how many parts per million (ppm) fall OUTSIDE the ±2σ limits?\",\"o\":[[\"317,300\",\"317,300 ppm fall outside ±1σ (68.27% inside), not ±2σ.\"],[\"2,700\",\"2,700 ppm fall outside ±3σ (99.73% inside), not ±2σ.\"],[\"45,500\",\"Correct. ±2σ covers 95.45%, leaving 45,500 ppm outside.\"],[\"63\",\"63 ppm fall outside ±4σ (99.9937% inside), not ±2σ.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"A process has a variance of <b>0.25</b>. Using σ = √variance and the case study's \\\"Six sigma = 6 × standard deviation\\\", what is its Six Sigma value?\",\"o\":[[\"1.5\",\"This multiplies the variance by 6 instead of σ.\"],[\"3\",\"Correct. σ = √variance = √0.25 = 0.5, and the case study's Six Sigma value is 6 × σ = 6 × 0.5 = 3 (L8, Case study).\"],[\"0.5\",\"0.5 = √0.25 is σ itself; the Six Sigma value is 6 × 0.5 = 3.\"],[\"6.25\",\"6.25 = 6 + 0.25 adds 6 to the variance; take σ = √0.25 = 0.5, then 6 × 0.5 = 3.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A Six Sigma team analyzes the chain of failure causes, ranks the causes by their impact and categorizes them. Which DMAIC phase is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Define\",\"Define identifies the problem (level, location, financial impact) and forms the team.\"],[\"Improve\",\"Improve changes processes and tests solutions on a sample.\"],[\"Analyze\",\"Correct. Analyze finds the root causes, ranks them by impact and categorizes them.\"],[\"Control\",\"Control keeps monitoring and correcting deviations.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"After the improvements are made, the team continuously measures performance standards and corrects deviations at the specified time, and never stops monitoring. Which DMAIC phase?\",\"o\":[[\"Control\",\"Correct. Control means continuous monitoring and correcting deviations.\"],[\"Measure\",\"Measure plans and implements performance measurement against customer requirements before improvement.\"],[\"Analyze\",\"Analyze finds and ranks the root causes of problems before improvement; continuous monitoring after improvement is Control.\"],[\"Define\",\"Define states the problem and forms the team.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A company wants to design a completely NEW product process using Six Sigma. Which approach shown on the slide fits?\",\"o\":[[\"DMAIC\",\"DMAIC (Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control) improves EXISTING processes; a new design uses DMADV (‡ not on the slides).\"],[\"ISO 9000\",\"ISO 9000 is a quality standard, not a Six Sigma approach.\"],[\"CMM\",\"CMM is a maturity model, not a Six Sigma approach.\"],[\"DMADV (Define, Measure, Analyze, Design, Verify)\",\"Correct. DMADV is for designing new processes/products (‡ not on the slides; the slide only shows the DMADV cycle).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"When did Six Sigma emerge, according to the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"Early 1950s\",\"Too early; the lecture says late 1970s and early 1980s.\"],[\"Late 1970s and early 1980s\",\"Correct. The lecture says Six Sigma emerged in the late 1970s and early 1980s and became popular as major global companies adopted it (L8, Introduction).\"],[\"1993\",\"1993 is the year SPICE was introduced (L7); Six Sigma emerged in the late 1970s and early 1980s.\"],[\"After 2010\",\"Too late; it was already popular with major global companies long before.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which belt is applied to <b>middle and senior management</b>?\",\"o\":[[\"Yellow Belt\",\"The Yellow Belt is for all employees, not specifically middle and senior management.\"],[\"Black Belt\",\"Correct. In the belts table the Black Belt is applied to middle and senior management; Master Black Belt is for senior management only (L8, Belts).\"],[\"Green Belt\",\"The Green Belt is for executive employees, one level below the Black Belt.\"],[\"White Belt\",\"The White Belt is step 1, the entry level in the certification figure, not for middle and senior management.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"What is the FIRST phase of implementing Six Sigma?\",\"o\":[[\"Train on measurement, analysis and process redesign\",\"Training on measurement, analysis and process redesign is phase 4, after projects are selected, staff assigned and the problem documented.\"],[\"Implement and apply practical solutions\",\"Implementing practical solutions is phase 5, near the end, just before submitting solutions (phase 6).\"],[\"Prepare a written document of the problem\",\"Preparing a written problem document (reasons, objectives, scope) is phase 3, after selecting projects and assigning employees.\"],[\"Identify and select important projects\",\"Correct. The implementation phases are<br>(1) identify and select important projects<br>(2) assign experienced employees<br>(3) document the problem<br>(4) train<br>(5) implement<br>(6) submit (L8).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"\\\"In the centred normal distribution figure, only 0.002 ppm fall outside the ±6σ limits.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. ±6σ covers 99.9999998%, leaving 0.002 ppm outside in the figure.\"],[\"False\",\"The figure gives 99.9999998% inside ±6σ, which is 0.002 ppm outside.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In the preparatory-certificate case study, which DMAIC step \\\"measures the size of the impact\\\" of the failure causes?\",\"o\":[[\"Measure\",\"Correct. Define the causes → Measure their impact → Analyze sources → Improve → Control.\"],[\"Define\",\"In the case study, Define identifies the causes of failure; measuring the size of their impact is the next step, Measure.\"],[\"Improve\",\"Improve derives solutions to the failure causes; it comes after their impact is measured and their sources analyzed.\"],[\"Control\",\"Control is the last step, continuous monitoring after solutions are applied, not measuring the causes' impact.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Six Sigma is one of the most famous management concepts in ………\",\"o\":[[\"total quality management\",\"Correct. The lecture opens by placing Six Sigma in total quality management. (L8, Introduction)\"],[\"capability maturity\",\"Capability maturity is the CMM idea from Lecture 7.\"],[\"software process assessment\",\"Software process assessment is what SPICE standardizes.\"],[\"configuration management\",\"Configuration management is not how the lecture classifies Six Sigma.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"……… is a statistical management system focusing on customer satisfaction, reducing waste, improving quality, and improving financial and time performance.\",\"o\":[[\"CMM\",\"CMM is a five-level maturity framework for software processes.\"],[\"SPICE\",\"SPICE is a standard software process assessment methodology.\"],[\"ISO 9000\",\"ISO 9000 is a set of 20-element generic quality standards.\"],[\"Six Sigma\",\"Correct. The lecture defines Six Sigma as this statistical management system. (L8, Introduction)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: The standard deviation (σ) is the square of the variance.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The relation is the other way round.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. σ is the square root of the variance (the variance is σ squared). (L8, Introduction)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A process has a variance of 0.81. Using σ = √variance and the case-study rule \\\"Six sigma = 6 × standard deviation\\\", its Six Sigma value is:\",\"o\":[[\"4.86\",\"This is 6 × 0.81: it multiplies the variance, forgetting to take the square root first.\"],[\"5.4\",\"Correct. σ = √0.81 = 0.9, then 6 × 0.9 = 5.4. (L8, Case study)\"],[\"0.9\",\"This is only σ; it still has to be multiplied by 6.\"],[\"6.81\",\"This adds 6 to the variance instead of multiplying σ by 6.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A new subject X11 is added to the preparatory-certificate table with standard deviation 1.15. Its \\\"Six sigma\\\" value is:\",\"o\":[[\"5.75\",\"This is 5 × 1.15; the rule multiplies by 6.\"],[\"7.15\",\"This adds 6 to the standard deviation instead of multiplying.\"],[\"6.90\",\"Correct. Six sigma = 6 × SD = 6 × 1.15 = 6.90. (L8, Case study)\"],[\"1.15\",\"This is only the standard deviation itself.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In the preparatory-certificate table, which subject has the largest \\\"Six sigma\\\" value?\",\"o\":[[\"X1\",\"X1 has SD 1.22, so 6 × 1.22 = 7.32, smaller than X7.\"],[\"X5\",\"X5 has SD 1.17, so 6 × 1.17 = 7.02, smaller than X7.\"],[\"X7\",\"Correct. X7 has the largest SD (1.27), so 6 × 1.27 = 7.62 is the largest value. (L8, Case study)\"],[\"X9\",\"X9 has the smallest SD (0.91), giving the smallest value 5.46.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In the case table, a subject's \\\"Six sigma\\\" value is 6.24. What is its standard deviation?\",\"o\":[[\"1.04\",\"Correct. SD = 6.24 ÷ 6 = 1.04 (subject X3). (L8, Case study)\"],[\"1.24\",\"This subtracts 5 instead of dividing by 6.\"],[\"0.24\",\"This subtracts 6 instead of dividing by 6.\"],[\"3.12\",\"This divides by 2 instead of 6.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: In the preparatory-certificate table, the overall indicator has mean 2.99 and standard deviation 1.07, giving a Six Sigma value of 6.42.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Overall row: mean 2.99, SD 1.07, and 6 × 1.07 = 6.42. (L8, Case study)\"],[\"False\",\"All three numbers match the bold overall row.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"According to the normal distribution table, how many ppm fall outside the ±4σ limits?\",\"o\":[[\"2,700\",\"2,700 ppm is outside ±3σ.\"],[\"63\",\"Correct. ±4σ keeps 99.9937% inside and leaves 63 ppm out of spec. (L8, Normal distribution)\"],[\"0.57\",\"0.57 ppm is outside ±5σ.\"],[\"45,500\",\"45,500 ppm is outside ±2σ.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A process keeps 99.73% of its values inside the limits. How many parts per million (ppm) are out of spec?\",\"o\":[[\"2,700\",\"Correct. (100 − 99.73)% = 0.27%, and 0.27 × 10,000 = 2,700 ppm (the ±3σ row). (L8, Normal distribution)\"],[\"270\",\"This multiplies 0.27 by 1,000 instead of 10,000 (1% = 10,000 ppm).\"],[\"27,000\",\"This multiplies 0.27 by 100,000; 1% is 10,000 ppm.\"],[\"317,300\",\"317,300 ppm is outside ±1σ (68.27% inside).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: The famous 3.4 defects per million comes from letting the process mean drift by 1.5σ in the long term, leaving 4.5σ on one side.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture note explains 3.4 by a 1.5σ long-term drift: 6 − 1.5 = 4.5σ (general background, not on the slide). (L8, 3.4 vs 0.002 ppm)\"],[\"False\",\"The centred ±6σ curve gives only 0.002 ppm; the 1.5σ drift is what gives 3.4.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Using the background DPMO formula from the notes: 18 defects are found in 300 units, each with 5 defect opportunities. DPMO = ?\",\"o\":[[\"60,000\",\"This divides by units only (18 ÷ 300 × 10⁶), ignoring the 5 opportunities per unit.\"],[\"1,200\",\"This is 18 ÷ 15,000 × 10⁶: the denominator is ten times too big; 300 × 5 = 1,500.\"],[\"3.4\",\"3.4 is the DPMO at 6σ, not this process.\"],[\"12,000\",\"Correct. DPMO = 18 ÷ (300 × 5) × 1,000,000 = 18 ÷ 1,500 × 10⁶ = 12,000. (L8, DPMO background formula)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In DMAIC, which phase comes directly after Analyze?\",\"o\":[[\"Measure\",\"Measure comes before Analyze (D, M, A, I, C).\"],[\"Control\",\"Control is the last phase, after Improve.\"],[\"Improve\",\"Correct. The order is Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control. (L8, DMAIC)\"],[\"Design\",\"Design follows Analyze in DMADV, not in DMAIC.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which DMAIC phase plans and implements performance measurement against customers' requirements and expectations using statistical tools?\",\"o\":[[\"Measure\",\"Correct. Measure plans and implements performance measurement against customer requirements with statistical tools (6 σ). (L8, DMAIC)\"],[\"Define\",\"Define identifies the problems, states level, location and financial impact, and forms a team.\"],[\"Analyze\",\"Analyze looks for the root causes in the collected data.\"],[\"Control\",\"Control keeps monitoring the processes and corrects deviations.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In which DMAIC phase do changes in processes occur to eliminate product defects, excessive costs and waste?\",\"o\":[[\"Define\",\"Define only identifies the problem and forms the team.\"],[\"Measure\",\"Measure measures performance; it does not change processes.\"],[\"Analyze\",\"Analyze finds and ranks root causes; the changes come next.\"],[\"Improve\",\"Correct. In Improve, processes change to eliminate defects, excessive costs and waste. (L8, DMAIC)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: The Control phase ends the Six Sigma work: once deviations are corrected, monitoring stops.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The lecture says the opposite about stopping.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Control monitors the processes and does not stop, because there are always areas in need of improvement. (L8, DMAIC)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"What does the \\\"V\\\" in DMADV stand for?\",\"o\":[[\"Validate\",\"Close, but the slide's word is Verify.\"],[\"Verify\",\"Correct. DMADV = Define, Measure, Analyze, Design, Verify. (L8, DMADV)\"],[\"Value\",\"Value appears in \\\"value stream mapping\\\", a tool, not in DMADV.\"],[\"Variation\",\"Variation is what Six Sigma reduces, not a DMADV phase.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: One of the principles of Six Sigma is effective management based on advance planning.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. It is one of the five principles listed. (L8, Principles)\"],[\"False\",\"The list includes it, with customer focus, facts and data, internal operations and teamwork.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which is the LAST of the six Six Sigma implementation phases?\",\"o\":[[\"Training on requirements related to Six Sigma\",\"Training is phase 4.\"],[\"Implementing the process and applying practical solutions\",\"Implementing the process is phase 5.\"],[\"Submission of solutions\",\"Correct. Phase 6, the last, is the submission of solutions. (L8, Implementation phases)\"],[\"Preparing a written document for the problem or project\",\"The written document is phase 3.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which Six Sigma belt is applied to all employees?\",\"o\":[[\"White Belt\",\"White Belt is step 1 in the certification figure, but the lecture assigns \\\"all employees\\\" to Yellow.\"],[\"Yellow Belt\",\"Correct. Yellow Belt is applied to all employees. (L8, Belts)\"],[\"Green Belt\",\"Green Belt is for executive employees.\"],[\"Master Black Belt\",\"Master Black Belt is for senior management.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: The Master Black Belt is applied to senior management and is the top step (step 5) in the certification figure.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Step 5, next to the target, is the Master Black Belt, applied to senior management. (L8, Belts)\"],[\"False\",\"Both parts match the figure and the belts table.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is listed as a Six Sigma tool or technique?\",\"o\":[[\"Contract review\",\"Contract review is ISO 9000 element 3 (Lecture 7).\"],[\"Key Process Areas\",\"Key Process Areas are a CMM component (Lecture 7).\"],[\"Capability baselines\",\"Capability baselines are a CMM Level 4 improvement (Lecture 7).\"],[\"Kanban\",\"Correct. Kanban is in the list with 5S, Seven Wastes, value stream mapping, visual workspace, VOC, Kaizen and regression analysis. (L8, Tools and techniques)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: Six Sigma quality control programs were designed to focus on correcting design, industrial and commercial defects.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture names these three kinds of defects. (L8, Introduction)\"],[\"False\",\"The statement matches the lecture word for word.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: According to the normal distribution table, ±5σ leaves 0.57 ppm outside the limits.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. ±5σ keeps 99.999943% inside and leaves 0.57 ppm out of spec. (L8, Normal distribution)\"],[\"False\",\"The table row for ±5σ is 99.999943% inside, 0.57 ppm outside.\"]],\"a\":0}]}],\"exams\":[{\"title\":\"Final Exam 2025/2026\",\"meta\":\"First term · 2 hours · 60 marks · 5 questions · Code 22H211-CS-EN-T1 · Prof. Arabi Keshk, Prof. Mahmoud Gadallah\",\"note\":\"Transcribed from two scanned pages. There is no official key, so all answers are worked out from the lectures. Q1 #4 is ambiguous: the answer given is ISO 9000, matching the student-summary wording (\\\"consistent processes, reduces errors, increases customer trust\\\"). Q1 #12 follows the summary key (\\\"All answers\\\"). The whole paper was readable. Question 5 is printed with parts (a) and (c) only; there is no (b) on the paper.\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Question 1 · Select the correct answer\",\"marks\":\"20 marks · 1 each\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"1- ............ is the widely used and preferred software method of evaluation.\",\"o\":[\"CMM\",\"ISO 9000\",\"6 Sigma\",\"SPICE\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"CMM is the standard framework for assessing process maturity; the summary states \\\"CMM is the widely used and preferred software method of evaluation\\\" (L7).\",\"ow\":[null,\"ISO 9000 is a generic set of 20 documented quality elements for any organization, not the preferred method of evaluating software processes (L7).\",\"Six Sigma is a statistical method for reducing process variation and defects (L8), not a software evaluation method.\",\"SPICE (ISO/IEC 15504) is a process-assessment initiative from 1993, but the lecture calls CMM the widely used and preferred method.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"2- ............ are maturity levels in the CMM.\",\"o\":[\"3\",\"5\",\"4\",\"6\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Initial, Repeatable, Defined, Quantitatively Managed, Optimizing (L7).\",\"ow\":[\"Three levels is too few; CMM has five: Initial, Repeatable, Defined, Quantitatively Managed, Optimizing (L7).\",null,\"Four levels misses one; CMM is a five-level incremental model ending with Level 5 Optimizing (L7).\",\"There is no sixth level; 6 is the number of CMM structure components, while the maturity levels number five (L7).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"3- There are 20 crucial elements in ............ standard.\",\"o\":[\"CMM\",\"6 Sigma\",\"ISO 9000\",\"SPICE\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"ISO 9000 has 20 key elements (management responsibility, quality systems, … statistical techniques), and each must be clearly and thoroughly documented to show compliance (L7).\",\"ow\":[\"CMM is built from 5 maturity levels and 6 structure components, not 20 elements (L7).\",\"Six Sigma is organized around DMAIC phases, not 20 elements (L8).\",null,\"SPICE is defined by 3 objectives for software process assessment, not 20 elements (L7).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"4- ............ improve processes, reduce errors, and boost customer trust.\",\"o\":[\"CMM\",\"SPICE\",\"6 Sigma\",\"ISO 9000\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"ISO 9000 \\\"promotes continuous improvement, product reliability and international recognition\\\", and the summary lists \\\"ensures consistent processes, reduces errors and risks, increases customer trust\\\". Six Sigma could also be argued (customer satisfaction), but it is the answer to #5, so ISO 9000 fits best here.\",\"ow\":[\"CMM assesses and improves process maturity in five levels; the lecture does not describe it in terms of reducing errors and boosting customer trust.\",\"SPICE is a standard framework for software process assessment; it is not credited with reducing errors and building customer trust.\",\"Six Sigma does improve processes for customer satisfaction, but its defining aim (question 5) is reducing variation to zero defects; the summary links this wording to ISO 9000.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"5- ............ reduce process variation to reach zero default.\",\"o\":[\"6 Sigma\",\"SPICE\",\"CMM\",\"ISO 9000\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L8: Six Sigma reduces process variation to 3.4 defects per million; its aim is zero defects (\\\"zero default\\\").\",\"ow\":[null,\"SPICE (ISO/IEC 15504) is a software process-assessment standard (L7); it does not aim at zero defects by reducing variation.\",\"CMM is a five-level maturity framework (L7), not a statistical method for reducing process variation.\",\"ISO 9000 is a set of 20 documented quality elements (L7); reducing variation to 3.4 defects per million is Six Sigma (L8).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"6- The process is confirmed that software meets its specification.\",\"o\":[\"Verification\",\"Validation\",\"Design\",\"Requirements\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Verification: \\\"are we building the product right?\\\", checked against the specification (L2).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Validation confirms the software meets the USER's requirements (\\\"are we building the right product?\\\"), not its specification (L2).\",\"Design is a development activity that defines the software structure; it is not a checking process.\",\"Requirements are what the software is checked against, not a checking process; checking against the specification is verification.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"7- The activity to ensure that an organization is providing the best product to customer.\",\"o\":[\"Quality Assurance\",\"Quality Control\",\"Code coverage\",\"Data coverage\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"QA is the organization-wide activity (processes, standards, prevention) that ensures quality products (L6). QC only screens the product.\",\"ow\":[null,\"QC is product-focused and reactive: it inspects the product and screens out defects, not the organization-wide assurance activity (L6).\",\"Code coverage is a white-box technique and one of the SQA metrics (L3, L6), not an organizational quality activity.\",\"Data coverage is a dynamic white-box technique that tracks data through the code (L3), not an organizational activity.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"8- The process is confirmed that software meets the user's requirement.\",\"o\":[\"Validation\",\"Verification\",\"Design\",\"Requirements\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Validation: \\\"are we building the right product?\\\", e.g. user acceptance testing (L2).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Verification checks the product against its SPECIFICATION (\\\"are we building the product right?\\\"), not the user's requirements (L2).\",\"Design is a development activity that defines the software structure; it is not a checking process.\",\"Requirements are what the product is checked against, not the checking process; checking against user needs is validation.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"9- Which of the following stages do inspections come under?\",\"o\":[\"Formal review\",\"Peer review\",\"Code coverage\",\"Data coverage\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Formal review = peer review, walkthrough, inspection (L3).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Peer review is one of the three formal reviews alongside inspection, not the stage that contains inspections (L3).\",\"Code coverage (statement, path, condition) is dynamic white-box testing, which runs the code; inspection is static (L3).\",\"Data coverage (data flow, sub-boundaries, error forcing) is dynamic white-box testing; inspection is a formal review (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"10- From the following, select the choice which is appropriate to standards and guidelines.\",\"o\":[\"Scalability\",\"Reliability\",\"Usability\",\"Compatibility\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The 3 reasons for standards and guidelines: reliability, readability/maintainability, portability (L3).\",\"ow\":[\"Scalability is not one of the 3 reasons for standards and guidelines: reliability, readability/maintainability, portability (L3).\",null,\"Usability is not one of the 3 reasons for standards and guidelines: reliability, readability/maintainability, portability (L3).\",\"Compatibility is not on the list; the related reason is portability (code runs on different hardware and compilers) (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"11- The goal of a software tester is to find\",\"o\":[\"Bugs\",\"Fix bugs\",\"Bugs and make sure they get fixed\",\"Write code\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"The tester's role: take responsibility for bugs, track them, and convince the developers to fix them (L6).\",\"ow\":[\"Finding bugs is only half the role: the tester must also track them and convince the team to fix them (L6).\",\"Fixing bugs is debugging, done by developers; the tester finds and tracks bugs and makes sure they get fixed (L3, L6).\",null,\"Writing code is the developers' job; the tester's goal is to find bugs and make sure they get fixed (L6).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"12- The program shall accept an input value of integer > 9000. Determine the test case.\",\"o\":[\"9000\",\"9001\",\"9999\",\"All answers\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"BVA/EP: 9000 = invalid boundary (must be rejected), 9001 = valid boundary, 9999 = valid value. All are useful test cases, which matches the summary key.\",\"ow\":[\"9000 is a needed test (the invalid boundary, must be rejected) but not the only one; 9001 and 9999 are also valid test cases.\",\"9001 is the valid left boundary, but it is not the only useful case; 9000 (invalid boundary) and 9999 (valid value) are also tests.\",\"9999 is a valid value from the accepted class, but 9000 (invalid boundary) and 9001 (valid boundary) are also needed tests.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"13- if (X>1) and (Y=0) then A=A/X; the statement coverage test cases are\",\"o\":[\"All answer\",\"X=3, y=1\",\"X=2, y=1\",\"X=2, y=0\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Only X=2, Y=0 makes both parts of the AND true, so A=A/X executes.\",\"ow\":[\"With AND both parts must be true; X=3,y=1 and X=2,y=1 have Y≠0 and skip A=A/X, so not all options work.\",\"Y=1 makes (Y=0) false, so the AND fails and A=A/X never runs; statement coverage needs every statement executed.\",\"X&gt;1 holds but Y=1 makes (Y=0) false, so the AND fails and A=A/X is skipped.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"14- if (X=2) or (y>1) then A=A+1; the statement coverage test cases are\",\"o\":[\"X=2, y=0\",\"X=2, y=2\",\"X=1, y=2\",\"All answers\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"With OR, each option makes at least one part true, so A=A+1 runs in all three.\",\"ow\":[\"This works (X=2 makes the OR true), but so do the other two cases, so \\\"All answers\\\" is the complete choice.\",\"This works (both parts true), but the other cases also run A=A+1, so \\\"All answers\\\" is the complete choice.\",\"This works (y=2 &gt; 1 makes the OR true), but the other cases also run A=A+1, so \\\"All answers\\\" is the complete choice.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"15- if (X>1) and (Y=0) then A=A/X; if (X=2) or (A>1) then A=A+1; the statement coverage are\",\"o\":[\"X=3, Y=0, A=1\",\"X=0, Y=0, A=4\",\"All answers\",\"X=2, Y=0, A=4\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"X=2,Y=0,A=4: A=4/2=2, then X=2 is true so A=3, and both statements run. (A) gives A=1/3 and the second IF is false. (B) makes the first IF false.\",\"ow\":[\"First IF true: A = 1/3. Second IF: X≠2 and A=0.33 is not &gt;1, so A=A+1 is skipped; not every statement runs.\",\"X=0 makes X&gt;1 false, so A=A/X is skipped (even though A=4 later runs A=A+1).\",\"Only X=2,Y=0,A=4 runs both statements; the other two each skip one, so not all answers work.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"16- Which type of software testing refers to testing the running software?\",\"o\":[\"Requirement testing\",\"Static testing\",\"Analysis testing\",\"Dynamic testing\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Dynamic testing = running and using the software (L2).\",\"ow\":[\"\\\"Requirement testing\\\" is not a lecture term; the lecture splits testing into static (not running) and dynamic (running) (L2).\",\"Static testing examines something that is NOT running, such as a spec or code review (L2).\",\"\\\"Analysis testing\\\" is not a lecture term; testing the running software is dynamic testing (L2).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"17- What are instructions to be followed which are not really mandatory?\",\"o\":[\"Standards and Guidelines\",\"Standards\",\"Guidelines\",\"Not all\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Guidelines help follow standards and are not mandatory (L3).\",\"ow\":[\"Standards are mandatory rules that must be adhered to, so pairing them with guidelines is wrong (L3).\",\"Standards are the mandatory rules; the not-really-mandatory instructions are guidelines (L3).\",null,\"The lecture gives a direct answer: guidelines help you follow the standards and are not really mandatory (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"18- What are instructions to be followed which are mandatory?\",\"o\":[\"Guidelines\",\"Standards\",\"Standards and Guidelines\",\"Not all\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Standards are rules that must be adhered to (L3).\",\"ow\":[\"Guidelines only help a person follow the standards and are not really mandatory (L3).\",null,\"Only standards are mandatory; guidelines are not really mandatory (L3).\",\"The lecture gives a direct answer: standards are rules that must be adhered to (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"19- A simple sequence to achieve method-message in Stack testing are\",\"o\":[\"create, push & pop\",\"create, insert & pop\",\"create & push\",\"create & insert\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L4: \\\"a simple sequence of create, push, and pop might achieve MM testing.\\\"\",\"ow\":[null,\"A stack has push, not insert, so push is never exercised; MM needs every method call once: create, push, pop (L4).\",\"pop is never called, so one method is untested; MM testing needs create, push and pop (L4).\",\"insert is not a stack method and pop is missing; the lecture's MM sequence is create, push and pop (L4).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"20- Formal review, Coding standard and guidelines, and Code review are phases of\",\"o\":[\"Static white box\",\"Dynamic white box\",\"Static Black box\",\"Dynamic Black box\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"These are the three parts of static white-box testing (L3).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Dynamic white-box covers testing the pieces, data coverage and code coverage, which run the code; reviews are static (L3).\",\"Static black-box reviews the specification (e.g. user manual) without code knowledge; these phases examine the code (L3).\",\"Dynamic black-box runs the software without knowing the code; formal and code reviews examine the code without running it (L3).\"]}]},{\"title\":\"Question 2\",\"marks\":\"10 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(a) Briefly discuss the levels of Capability Maturity Model (CMM).\",\"ans\":\"<b>Level 1 – Initial</b> (inconsistent management): processes are ad-hoc, unstructured, reactive and inconsistent; project management is unstable; no required processes.<br><b>Level 2 – Repeatable</b> (project management): basic project management (planning, requirement control, product modifications) gives repeatable success; plan and track projects, manage suppliers and configurations.<br><b>Level 3 – Defined</b> (process management): standardized processes are defined and refined across the organization; teams tailor them; organization-wide training.<br><b>Level 4 – Quantitatively Managed</b> (capability management): processes are controlled with statistical and quantitative techniques; performance is predictable; capability baselines; minimal variation.<br><b>Level 5 – Optimizing</b> (change management): continuous process improvement through incremental and innovative advances with quantitative goals; remove causes of defects.<br>Levels are climbed in order and none can be skipped.\",\"why\":\"L7 \\\"Five Levels of CMM\\\" plus Table 1. Mention all five names in order.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(b) Briefly discuss 6 sigma methodology.\",\"ans\":\"Six Sigma is a statistical management methodology that reduces process variation to <b>3.4 defects per million</b> or less (aim: zero defects). It uses <b>DMAIC</b>:<br><b>Define</b>: identify the problem precisely (level, location, financial impact) and form a team.<br><b>Measure</b>: measure performance against customer requirements with statistical tools.<br><b>Analyze</b>: find the root causes, rank them by impact, categorize them.<br><b>Improve</b>: change processes to remove defects, cost and waste; test solutions on a sample.<br><b>Control</b>: continuously monitor, measure and correct deviations on time.\",\"why\":\"L8 DMAIC slides. You can add the principles or the belts for extra detail.\"}]},{\"title\":\"Question 3\",\"marks\":\"10 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(a) List the 20 Elements of ISO 9000 Series Standards.\",\"ans\":\"1 Management responsibility · 2 Quality systems · 3 Contract review · 4 Design control · 5 Documentation & data control · 6 Purchasing · 7 Customer-supplied product control · 8 Product identification & traceability · 9 Process control · 10 Inspection & testing · 11 Inspection/measuring equipment · 12 Inspection & test status · 13 Control of non-conforming product · 14 Corrective & preventive action · 15 Handling/storage/packaging/delivery · 16 Quality records · 17 Internal quality audits · 18 Training · 19 Servicing · 20 Statistical techniques.\",\"why\":\"L7. Mnemonic by groups: management (1–3), design/docs/buying (4–7), product and process (8–13), correction and logistics (14–15), records/audits/people (16–18), after-sale and statistics (19–20).\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(b) What are the most commonly used software quality metrics?\",\"ans\":\"1 Code coverage · 2 Bugs per line of code · 3 Cyclomatic complexity · 4 Function point analysis · 5 Number of classes and interfaces · 6 Cohesion · 7 Coupling · 8 Order of growth · 9 Source lines of code.<br>(Metrics are rules for measuring software attributes. Product metrics measure the final product; process metrics measure the development process.)\",\"why\":\"L6 \\\"Examples of Common Metrics\\\".\"}]},{\"title\":\"Question 4\",\"marks\":\"10 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(a) Find method message and function pair of the Queue in the next figure. [Figure: New → Empty; insert: Empty→Normal, Normal→Normal, Normal→Full, Full→(error); delete: Empty→(error), Normal→Normal, Normal→Empty, Full→Normal.]\",\"ans\":\"<b>MM</b>: the simple sequence <b>create (new), insert, delete</b> calls every method at least once.<br><b>Function pairs</b> (same pattern as the stack, 14 pairs):<br>1. new – delete (on empty, error)<br>2. new – insert<br>3. insert (from empty) – insert<br>4. insert (from empty) – delete<br>5. insert (normal→normal) – insert (still normal)<br>6. insert (normal→normal) – insert (into full)<br>7. insert (normal→normal) – delete<br>8. insert (normal→full) – insert (error)<br>9. insert (normal→full) – delete<br>10. delete (normal→normal) – insert (still normal)<br>11. delete (normal→normal) – delete (still normal)<br>12. delete (normal→normal) – delete (into empty)<br>13. delete (into empty) – insert<br>14. delete (into empty) – delete (error)\",\"why\":\"The lecture's stack example with push→insert and pop→delete. The queue diagram has the same states (Empty, Normal, Full) and transitions.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(b) 1- What basic elements of a web page can easily be tested with a black-box approach? 2- Why is gray-box testing possible with website testing? 3- What basic elements of a web page can easily be tested with a white-box approach?\",\"ans\":\"<b>1. Black-box</b>: <b>text</b> (audience, terminology, accuracy, spelling, contact info), <b>hyperlinks</b> (right destination, same/new window, obvious, e-mail links), <b>graphics</b> (load and display correctly), <b>forms</b> (positioned, correct size, accept good data, reject bad data).<br><b>2. Gray-box</b> is possible because web pages are built from HTML and scripts the tester can see (view source) while still testing from the user's side, so black-box and white-box can be mixed. Its purpose is to isolate defects related to bad design or bad implementation of the web.<br><b>3. White-box</b>: <b>dynamic content</b> (time of day, weather, stock tickers), <b>database-driven pages</b> (e-commerce catalogs), <b>programmatically created pages</b>, <b>server performance and loading</b> (millions of hits), <b>security</b> (denial of service, buffer overflow).\",\"why\":\"L5 sections 2, 8.3.\"}]},{\"title\":\"Question 5\",\"marks\":\"10 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(a) Write all test cases to achieve Branch and Condition coverage of the next code.<br><pre>1: PRINT \\\"Hello World\\\"\\n2: IF Date$ = \\\"01-01-2026\\\" AND Time$ = \\\"00:00:00\\\" THEN\\n3: PRINT \\\"Happy New Year\\\"\\n4: END IF\\n5: PRINT \\\"The date is: \\\"; Date$\\n6: PRINT \\\"The time is: \\\"; Time$\\n7: END</pre>\",\"ans\":\"<b>Branch coverage</b> (the IF true once, false once), 2 tests:<table><tr><th>Date$</th><th>Time$</th><th>Lines</th></tr><tr><td>01-01-2026</td><td>00:00:00</td><td>1,2,3,4,5,6,7</td></tr><tr><td>01-01-2025</td><td>11:11:11</td><td>1,2,5,6,7</td></tr></table><b>Condition coverage</b> (each condition true and false; all combinations as in the lecture), 4 tests:<table><tr><th>Date$</th><th>Time$</th><th>Lines</th></tr><tr><td>01-01-2025</td><td>11:11:11</td><td>1,2,5,6,7</td></tr><tr><td>01-01-2025</td><td>00:00:00</td><td>1,2,5,6,7</td></tr><tr><td>01-01-2026</td><td>11:11:11</td><td>1,2,5,6,7</td></tr><tr><td>01-01-2026</td><td>00:00:00</td><td>1,2,3,4,5,6,7</td></tr></table>\",\"why\":\"Copy of the L3 \\\"Test cases for a full condition coverage\\\" table, with the new date. Only the true/true case prints \\\"Happy New Year\\\".\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(c) Design test cases for function testing and statement coverage of the triangle in the next pseudocode.<br><pre>read a,b,c;\\ntype = \\\"scalene\\\";\\nif (a == b or a == c or b == c) type = \\\"isosceles\\\";\\nif (a == b and a == c) type = \\\"equilateral\\\";\\nif (a >= b+c or b >= a+c or c >= a+b) type = \\\"not a triangle\\\";\\nif (a &lt;= 0 or b &lt;= 0 or c &lt;= 0) type = \\\"bad inputs\\\";\\nprint type;</pre>\",\"ans\":\"<b>Function (black-box) testing</b>: subdomains scalene, isosceles, equilateral, not a triangle, bad inputs:<br>Scalene: (3,4,5), (5,4,3), (4,5,3).<br>Isosceles: (5,5,8), (5,8,5), (8,5,5), (8,8,5), (8,5,8), (5,8,8).<br>Equilateral: (5,5,5).<br>Not a triangle: (6,4,2), (4,6,2), (1,2,3).<br>Bad inputs: (−1,2,4), (0,−2,5), (0,0,0).<br><b>Statement coverage</b> (lecture table with (3,4,5), (3,5,3), (0,1,0), (4,4,4)):<br>(3,4,5) → scalene; (3,5,3) → isosceles; (4,4,4) → equilateral; (0,1,0) → not-a-triangle and bad-inputs lines both run, prints \\\"bad inputs\\\".<br>Using the lecture table’s test cases, the minimal set is <b>(4,4,4) and (0,1,0)</b>, which together execute every statement A–K.<br>‡ With any inputs, fewer are enough: <b>(0,0,0)</b> alone executes every statement A–K (0==0 → isosceles and equilateral; 0 ≥ 0+0 → not a triangle; 0 ≤ 0 → bad inputs).\",\"why\":\"L4 triangle slides. The slide lists (1,2,4) and (3,2,5) as \\\"bad inputs\\\", but by the pseudocode they print \\\"not a triangle\\\" (no side ≤ 0), so negative/zero sides are used here.\"}]}]},{\"title\":\"Practice paper (from student summaries, not an official exam)\",\"meta\":\"Built from the \\\"(SQA) True or False\\\" file and the \\\"ليلة الفاينل – MSQ\\\" bank · checked against the lectures\",\"note\":\"Practice only. Where the summary key contradicts the lectures, the lecture answer is used and the \\\"key\\\" line shows the summary's answer.\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Part A · True or False\",\"marks\":\"practice\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"The year 1988–2000 followed a prevention-oriented approach.\",\"a\":0,\"key\":\"Summary key: False\",\"why\":\"L1 table: 1988–2000 = prevention-oriented. The summary key is wrong.\",\"ow\":[null,\"The L1 evolution table puts 1988–2000 in the prevention-oriented period (prevent defects through early test design and planning), so the statement is true.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Software testing identifies the areas of weakness in an application or product.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Identifying areas of weakness is objective 4 of the six objectives of testing in L1 (with business requirements, catching bugs, stability, degree of quality and user acceptability).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Identifying areas of weakness is explicitly listed as objective 4 of software testing in L1, so the statement is true.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"The analysis phase defines the software and the hardware requirements relevant for the software development process.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"That is System Engineering. Analysis = feasibility, goals, performance and interface requirements (L1).\",\"ow\":[\"Defining both software and hardware requirements is the System Engineering phase of Waterfall; Analysis covers feasibility, goals, performance and interface requirements (L1).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"While performing a software test, the tester should first begin with test-to-fail.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Start with test-to-pass, then test-to-fail (L2).\",\"ow\":[\"The lecture says to start with test-to-pass (confirm it minimally works with normal input), and only then test-to-fail (L2).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"White box testing is also known as clear box testing or glass box testing.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L3 lists the other names of white-box testing: glass box, structural, open box and clear box testing, because it examines the internal workings.\",\"ow\":[null,\"L3 gives clear box and glass box (plus structural and open box) as names for white-box testing, so the statement is true.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Black box testing is also known as glass box testing because it examines the internal code structure.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Black-box (behavioral) testing works without knowing the internal logic (L2); glass box is a name for WHITE-box testing, which examines the code (L3).\",\"ow\":[\"Black-box testing does not look at the code at all (L2); glass box is another name for white-box testing (L3).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"The participants in a walkthrough are more than the participants in a peer review.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"A walkthrough uses a group of 5 or 6 programmers and testers (L3).\",\"ow\":[null,\"A walkthrough is presented to a group of 5 or 6 programmers and testers, while a peer review involves a programmer with a few reviewers, so walkthroughs have more (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Standards are structured and must be adhered to.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L3: standards are rules that must be adhered to (mandatory), while guidelines are instructions that help follow them and are not really mandatory.\",\"ow\":[null,\"The lecture defines standards as rules that must be adhered to; it is guidelines that are not really mandatory (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Boundary value analysis can only be used for white-box testing.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"BVA is taught as a black-box technique (L2) and can also be used in white-box testing.\",\"ow\":[\"Boundary testing is taught as a black-box technique in L2 (e.g. testing 0 and 256 for a 1–255 field), so it is not limited to white-box.\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Equivalence testing divides the input domain into classes from which test cases are derived, reducing the total number of test cases.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"The definition of equivalence partitioning (L2).\",\"ow\":[null,\"This is the L2 definition of equivalence partitioning: one case from each class is enough, cutting test cases without lowering quality.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Bugs found and fixed early cost more than bugs found later.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The cost rises from requirements (low) to production (extremely costly) (L1).\",\"ow\":[\"The reverse is true: cost rises from low at requirements to moderate at coding, very costly in testing and extremely costly in production (L1).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Quality Assurance is the same as Quality Control.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"QA is process and prevention; QC is product and detection (L2, L6).\",\"ow\":[\"QA is process-focused and preventive; QC is product-focused and corrective (detection), so they are different (L2, L6).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Dynamic content is graphics and text that vary depending on some conditions.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L5 defines dynamic content, the first white-box web element, as graphics and text that vary depending on conditions such as time of day, weather or stock tickers.\",\"ow\":[null,\"The statement repeats the L5 definition of dynamic content (time of day, weather, stock tickers), so it is true.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"A maturity model can be utilized as a model for evaluation.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L7 says a maturity model gives structured levels of how well processes achieve outcomes and can be used for both evaluation and improvement.\",\"ow\":[null,\"L7 says a maturity model can be used for both evaluation and improvement, so using it for evaluation is correct.\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Testing is the process of executing a program with the intent of finding errors (Myers).\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"This is Myers' definition from the destruction-oriented era (1979–1982), when tests were designed to find errors (L1, Evolution of testing).\",\"ow\":[null,\"This is Myers' definition, the basis of destruction-oriented testing (1979–1982) in L1, where tests are designed to find errors.\"]}]},{\"title\":\"Part B · Multiple choice\",\"marks\":\"practice\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Identify the testing technique used to test how the actual code works.\",\"o\":[\"Structural testing\",\"Functional testing\",\"Static testing\",\"Black box testing\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Structural testing is another name for white-box testing, which examines the internal workings of the actual code (L3).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Functional testing is black-box: it checks inputs and outputs against requirements without looking at the code (L2).\",\"Static testing examines something that is not running (e.g. a spec review); it does not test how the code actually works (L2).\",\"Black-box testing works without knowledge of the internal logic, so it cannot test how the code works (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"What testing is carried out after integrating the units to ensure that specifications are met?\",\"o\":[\"Agile testing\",\"Unit testing\",\"System testing\",\"Acceptance testing\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"System testing tests the complete system against its specifications (L2).\",\"ow\":[\"Agile testing is early, customer-focused testing within agile cycles (sprint-based validation), not a level after integration (L2).\",\"Unit testing tests the smallest components (functions or modules) before integration, not the complete system (L2).\",null,\"Acceptance testing is done by the user/customer to confirm business requirements; checking the integrated system against its specifications is system testing (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Alternative name for white box testing is\",\"o\":[\"Structural analysis\",\"Gray box testing\",\"Glass box testing\",\"Functional testing\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"L3 lists the other names of white-box testing: glass box, structural, open box and clear box testing.\",\"ow\":[\"\\\"Structural analysis\\\" is not the lecture's name; white-box is called structural TESTING, glass box, open box or clear box (L3).\",\"Gray-box testing is a MIX of black- and white-box (L5), not another name for white-box.\",null,\"Functional testing is black-box (behavioral) testing, the opposite of white-box (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following refers to data declaration errors?\",\"o\":[\"Improper declaration of variables and constants\",\"Improper initialization\",\"Improper calculations\",\"Loop behavior errors\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L3 definition. Calculations = computation errors; loops = control flow.\",\"ow\":[null,\"Uninitialized variables are checked under data reference errors (variables not properly declared or initialized); the data declaration definition is improper declaration (L3).\",\"Improper calculations are computation errors (mixed types, overflow, divide by zero) in the L3 checklist.\",\"Improper behaviour of loops and control structures is a control flow error in the L3 checklist.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"How many CMM levels are there?\",\"o\":[\"One\",\"Two\",\"Three\",\"Five\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"CMM has five maturity levels: Initial, Repeatable, Defined, Quantitatively Managed and Optimizing, climbed without skipping levels (L7).\",\"ow\":[\"One level is not a model; CMM has five levels from Initial to Optimizing (L7).\",\"Two is only up to Repeatable; CMM continues to Defined, Quantitatively Managed and Optimizing (five levels) (L7).\",\"Three stops at Defined; CMM also has Level 4 Quantitatively Managed and Level 5 Optimizing (L7).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is a strategy of integration testing (from the lecture figure)?\",\"o\":[\"Bottom-up testing\",\"Static white box testing\",\"Black box testing\",\"Code review\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"The figure shows bottom-up, top-down and umbrella approach.\",\"ow\":[null,\"Static white-box testing (reviews, standards, checklists) examines code without running it; it is not an integration strategy (L3).\",\"Black-box testing is a general approach without code knowledge, not one of the integration strategies (bottom-up, top-down, umbrella) (L3).\",\"Code review (checklist) is part of static white-box testing, not an integration testing strategy (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which type of software testing examines and reviews something that is not running?\",\"o\":[\"Dynamic testing\",\"System testing\",\"Integration testing\",\"Static testing\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"L2 defines static testing as examining and reviewing something that is not running, while dynamic testing means running and using the software.\",\"ow\":[\"Dynamic testing is the opposite: running and using the software (L2).\",\"System testing runs the complete integrated system to check it meets its specifications (L2).\",\"Integration testing runs combined modules to test the interfaces between them (L2), so the software is running.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"In which testing type is the size of a collection tested (sub-boundaries)?\",\"o\":[\"Data coverage testing\",\"Code coverage testing\",\"Inspections\",\"Walkthrough\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Data coverage includes data flow, sub-boundaries and error forcing (L3).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Code coverage (statement, path, condition) checks which code runs; sub-boundaries are a type of data coverage (L3).\",\"Inspections are a static formal review of code by someone other than its author, not a dynamic test of data sizes (L3).\",\"A walkthrough is a static review where the author presents the code; it does not test sub-boundaries (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Main quality tools used by Six Sigma include:\",\"o\":[\"Kaizen and Kanban\",\"Selenium and Cypress\",\"Peer review\",\"SPICE\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L8 tools: 5S, seven wastes, value stream mapping, visual workspace, VOC, Kaizen, Kanban, regression analysis.\",\"ow\":[null,\"Selenium and Cypress are web application testing tools (L5), not Six Sigma tools.\",\"Peer review is a formal review in static white-box testing (L3), not a Six Sigma tool.\",\"SPICE is a software process-assessment standard (ISO/IEC 15504, L7), not a Six Sigma tool.\"]}]}]},{\"title\":\"Midterm 2026/2027\",\"meta\":\"Software Quality Assurance · Midterm, 1st term 2026/2027 · online quiz · 40 questions (MCQ) · Lectures 1 to 8 · Prof. Dr. Arabi Keshk, Prof. Mahmoud Gadallah\",\"note\":\"Confirmed by the doctor (November 2026), except one question: \\\"Which stage of SDLC typically consumes the maximum cost and effort?\\\" The quiz's key for it is Preventive Maintenance, but the answer here is the standard one, Perfective Maintenance (Lientz and Swanson, quoted in Pressman and Sommerville). The answers are worked out from Lectures 1 to 8; the four maintenance types and Lean Six Sigma are not in the lectures and come from the standard textbooks. The first question is edited: the quiz said \\\"integer >= 9000\\\", which leaves no invalid option, so the site uses \\\"> 9000\\\". The other questions are kept as they appeared, typos included (\\\"Test plane\\\", \\\"Bing Bang\\\", the same formula listed twice in the cyclomatic complexity question).\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Choose the correct answer\",\"marks\":\"40 questions\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The program shall accept an input value of integer > 9000. Determine the invalid test case for this program.\",\"o\":[\"9000\",\"9999\",\"10000\",\"All answers\"],\"a\":0,\"key\":\"Edited for the site: the quiz said \\\"integer >= 9000\\\", which makes 9000, 9999 and 10000 all valid, so no option was an invalid test case. With \\\"> 9000\\\", exactly one option works: 9000.\",\"why\":\"With \\\"> 9000\\\" the valid class starts at 9001, so 9000 is the invalid boundary: the value just outside the range, which must be rejected. Lecture 2's example is the same: for \\\"≥ 10,000\\\", 09999 is the invalid left boundary and 10000 the valid left boundary (L2, Equivalence partitioning, Boundary value analysis).\",\"ow\":[null,\"9999 is greater than 9000, so it is a valid input from the accepted class (L2).\",\"10000 is greater than 9000, so it is a valid input (L2).\",\"9999 and 10000 are valid, so not every option is an invalid test case.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which type of software testing refers to testing the running software\",\"o\":[\"Static testing\",\"Requirement testing\",\"Dynamic testing\",\"Analysis testing\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Lecture 2: \\\"Static testing = testing something that is not running (examining and reviewing it). Dynamic testing = running and using the software.\\\"\",\"ow\":[\"The opposite: static testing examines and reviews software that is not running (L2).\",\"Not a lecture term; L2 splits testing into static (not running) and dynamic (running).\",null,\"Not a lecture term; \\\"Analysis\\\" is only a Waterfall/Spiral phase in L1, not a type of testing.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The reason for adhering to standards and guidelines are\",\"o\":[\"Portability\",\"Reliability\",\"Maintainability\",\"All answers\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Lecture 3 gives 3 reasons to follow coding standards and guidelines: (1) reliability, more reliable and secure code; (2) readability/maintainability; (3) portability, running on different hardware and compilers (L3, Coding standards and guidelines).\",\"ow\":[\"A real reason, but only one of the three; reliability and maintainability are also listed (L3).\",\"A real reason, but only one of the three (L3).\",\"A real reason (readability/maintainability), but only one of the three (L3).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"What is the main focus of Preventive Maintenance?\",\"o\":[\"Redesigning the entire user interface.\",\"Fixing immediate runtime errors.\",\"Moving data from legacy databases to cloud storage.\",\"Modifying software to detect and correct latent faults before they become actual errors.\"],\"a\":3,\"key\":\"Confirmed by the doctor. Not in the lectures: the four maintenance types come from the standard classification (ISO/IEC 14764, Pressman, Sommerville).\",\"why\":\"Preventive maintenance modifies software to detect and correct latent faults before they become effective faults (ISO/IEC 14764). The lectures only describe Maintenance as the last Waterfall phase, which \\\"fixes issues and updates the software as new needs arise\\\" (L1).\",\"ow\":[\"Improving or redesigning working software is perfective maintenance.\",\"Fixing faults that have already happened is corrective maintenance, the opposite of acting before errors appear.\",\"Fitting software or data to a new environment is adaptive maintenance (migration).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"A simple sequence of achieve method-message in Queue testing are\",\"o\":[\"create, insert, and delete\",\"create, insert, and pop\",\"create, push, and pop\",\"create, push, and delete\"],\"a\":0,\"key\":\"Confirmed by the doctor. The lecture works the stack (create, push, pop); the queue's methods (new, insert, delete) come from the queue diagram in the 2025/26 final.\",\"why\":\"Method-message (MM) testing needs every method call tested at least once. For the stack, Lecture 4 says \\\"a simple sequence of create, push and pop might achieve MM testing.\\\" The queue's state diagram (Empty, Normal, Full) uses new, insert and delete, so its sequence is create, insert and delete (L4, Stack example: MM and FP).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Mixes the two: pop is the stack's removal method; the queue's is delete.\",\"This is the stack's sequence from the slide, word for word; push and pop are not the queue's methods.\",\"Mixes the two: push is the stack's insertion method; the queue's is insert.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Upgrading a software application to make it compatible with a new version of an operating system is an example of:\",\"o\":[\"Perfective Maintenance\",\"Corrective Maintenance\",\"Preventive Maintenance\",\"Adaptive Maintenance\"],\"a\":3,\"key\":\"Confirmed by the doctor. Not in the lectures: the four maintenance types come from the standard classification (ISO/IEC 14764, Pressman, Sommerville).\",\"why\":\"Adaptive maintenance changes software so it keeps working in a changed environment: a new operating system, hardware, database or regulations. Making an application compatible with a new OS version is the textbook example (ISO/IEC 14764).\",\"ow\":[\"Perfective maintenance adds or improves features or performance; the environment changing is not its reason.\",\"Corrective maintenance fixes faults that have appeared; nothing is broken here.\",\"Preventive maintenance fixes latent faults before they become errors; it is not triggered by a new environment.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"During which Phase of DMAIC is the solution monitored for long-term stability?\",\"o\":[\"Measure\",\"Control\",\"Improve\",\"Define\",\"Analyze\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Lecture 8: \\\"C – Control: focus on monitoring all of these processes and do not stop\\\": continuously measure performance and correct any deviations. The summary repeats it: \\\"finally Control: continuously monitor the improvement processes\\\" (L8, DMAIC).\",\"ow\":[\"Measure measures performance against customer requirements with statistical tools, before any solution exists (L8).\",null,\"Improve finds and tests solutions and puts them in place; Control keeps them there (L8).\",\"Define identifies the problem (level, location, financial impact) and forms the team (L8).\",\"Analyze finds the root causes from the collected data (L8).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Code coverage have the next type\",\"o\":[\"All answers\",\"Condition coverage\",\"Line coverage\",\"Branch coverage\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Lecture 3: \\\"Code coverage has 3 types: Statement coverage, Path coverage (branch), Condition coverage.\\\" Condition coverage is on the slide, branch coverage is the slide's name for path coverage, and line coverage is the usual other name for statement coverage (every line runs at least once) (L3, Data coverage and code coverage).\",\"ow\":[null,\"A real type, but only one of the three (L3).\",\"A real type (statement coverage), but only one of the three (L3).\",\"A real type (path coverage), but only one of the three (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"In which among the following is the Lean Six Sigma process not applicable?\",\"o\":[\"Selecting the best employee of the year\",\"Business administration\",\"Healthcare\",\"Supply Chain\"],\"a\":0,\"key\":\"Confirmed by the doctor. Lecture 8 does not mention Lean Six Sigma or these fields; answered from general knowledge.\",\"why\":\"Lean Six Sigma improves repeatable processes: it cuts waste (Lean) and reduces defects and variation (Six Sigma) with DMAIC. Lecture 8 says Six Sigma targets errors \\\"in all their activities\\\". Choosing the employee of the year is a one-off judgement, not a repeatable process with defects to measure, so DMAIC has nothing to work on.\",\"ow\":[null,\"Administrative processes (approvals, invoicing, HR workflows) repeat and can be measured, so Lean Six Sigma applies.\",\"Healthcare is one of its biggest users, e.g. reducing waiting times and medication errors.\",\"Supply chain (inventory, logistics, delivery) is a classic Lean use.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following stages do inspections come under?\",\"o\":[\"Code coverage\",\"Data coverage\",\"Formal review\",\"Peer review\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Lecture 3: static white-box testing splits into Formal Review, Coding Standards and Guidelines, and Code Review Checklist, and Formal Review branches into Peer Review, Walkthrough and Inspection (L3, Static white-box figure).\",\"ow\":[\"Code coverage (statement, path, condition) is dynamic white-box testing, which runs the code; an inspection is a static review (L3).\",\"Data coverage (data flow, sub-boundaries, error forcing) is dynamic white-box testing (L3).\",null,\"Peer review is a sibling of inspection, one of the three formal reviews, not the stage above it (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"What are instructions to be followed which are not really mandatory.\",\"o\":[\"None of the above\",\"Standards\",\"Standards and Guidelines\",\"Guidelines\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Lecture 3: \\\"Standards are rules that must be adhered to (mandatory). Guidelines are instructions which enable a person to follow a set of standards (not really mandatory).\\\"\",\"ow\":[\"Guidelines fit the definition exactly (L3).\",\"The opposite: standards are the mandatory rules (L3).\",\"Standards are mandatory, so the pair does not fit \\\"not really mandatory\\\" (L3).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The meeting for inspection of the software's design and code is called\",\"o\":[\"All the above\",\"Code review\",\"Data review\",\"Formal review\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Lecture 3 defines formal review as \\\"formal meetings between programmers and testers to inspect the software's design and code\\\" (L3, Formal review).\",\"ow\":[\"Only formal review matches the definition (L3).\",\"In L3, \\\"code review\\\" is the Code Review Checklist, a list of error types, not the meeting; and the meeting covers the design too.\",\"Not a lecture term; data coverage is dynamic testing, not a meeting.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of Software Maintenance?\",\"o\":[\"Stopping the software from running on older machines.\",\"Writing the initial application code from scratch.\",\"Modifying software, improve performance, or adapt it to a changed environment.\",\"Designing the system architecture during the requirement gathering phase.\"],\"a\":2,\"key\":\"Confirmed by the doctor. Lecture 1 only names Maintenance as the last Waterfall phase; the option's wording follows the IEEE definition.\",\"why\":\"Maintenance is the last Waterfall phase, after delivery: it \\\"fixes issues and updates the software as new needs arise\\\" (L1). The IEEE definition (IEEE 1219, ISO/IEC 14764): modification of a software product after delivery to correct faults, to improve performance or other attributes, or to adapt it to a modified environment.\",\"ow\":[\"The opposite: adaptive maintenance keeps software working when the environment changes.\",\"That is the Coding phase, before Testing and Maintenance (L1, Waterfall).\",null,\"That is the early Analysis and Design phases, long before maintenance (L1, Waterfall).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which stage of SDLC typically consumes the maximum cost and effort?\",\"o\":[\"Perfective Maintenance\",\"Adaptive Maintenance\",\"Preventive Maintenance\",\"Corrective Maintenance\"],\"a\":0,\"key\":\"Not confirmed by the doctor: the quiz's key is Preventive Maintenance. The answer here is the standard one: Lientz and Swanson's survey, quoted in Pressman and Sommerville, puts perfective at about 50-60% of maintenance effort and preventive at about 4%, the smallest. Not in the lectures.\",\"why\":\"The options are maintenance types, and maintenance is the costliest stage, so the question asks which type takes most of it. Lientz, Swanson and Tompkins (1978): 60.3% perfective, 18.2% adaptive, 17.4% corrective, 4.1% other; the rounded textbook version is 50% perfective, 25% adaptive, 21% corrective, 4% preventive.\",\"ow\":[null,\"About 18-25% of maintenance effort, well below perfective (Lientz and Swanson).\",\"The smallest share, about 4% (Lientz and Swanson). This is the quiz's key, which goes against the standard sources.\",\"About 17-21%: most effort goes into changing working software, not fixing faults (Lientz and Swanson).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Data coverage have the next type\",\"o\":[\"sub-boundaries\",\"Data flow\",\"Error forcing\",\"All answers\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Lecture 3: data coverage types are \\\"Data flow, Sub-boundaries, Error forcing.\\\" All three options are on the list (L3, Data coverage and code coverage).\",\"ow\":[\"A real type, but only one of the three (L3).\",\"A real type, but only one of the three (L3).\",\"A real type, but only one of the three (L3).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"if(X=2) or (y>1) then A=A+1; the statement coverage test cases are\",\"o\":[\"X=2, y=0\",\"X=1, y=2\",\"X=2, y=2\",\"All answers\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Statement coverage means every statement runs at least once (L3); the only statement is A=A+1, so the OR condition must be true. X=2,y=0 (X=2 true), X=1,y=2 (y>1 true) and X=2,y=2 (both true) each run it. The 2025/26 final had the same question, keyed \\\"All answers\\\".\",\"ow\":[\"Works (X=2 makes the OR true), but so do the other two.\",\"Works (y=2 > 1 makes the OR true), but so do the other two.\",\"Works (both parts true), but so do the other two.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The process is confirmed that software meets the customer's Requirement\",\"o\":[\"Design\",\"Requirements\",\"Validation\",\"Verification\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Lecture 2: validation = \\\"Are we building the right product?\\\", checking against what the customer needs (e.g. user acceptance testing); verification = \\\"Are we building the product right?\\\", checking against the specification. The 2025/26 final keyed the same question as Validation.\",\"ow\":[\"Design is a development activity that defines the structure; it does not check the product.\",\"Requirements are what the product is checked against, not the checking process.\",null,\"Verification checks against the specification; checking against the customer's requirement is validation (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is true about variation?\",\"o\":[\"It decreases cycle time\",\"It increases the likelihood of failing to meet customer requirements\",\"It is one of the 8 Wastes\",\"It is only part of manufacturing processes\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Lecture 8: Six Sigma focuses on customer satisfaction and \\\"reduces process variation until the result is 3.4 defects per million.\\\" More variation means more output falls outside the customer's limits, so more defects and more failures to meet requirements; the slide's normal-curve table shows the wider the spread, the more parts per million out of spec (L8).\",\"ow\":[\"Variation causes rework and delays, so it makes cycle time longer, not shorter (general Lean knowledge).\",null,\"The usual 8 wastes are transport, inventory, motion, waiting, overproduction, overprocessing, defects and unused skills; variation is not one of them (general knowledge; L8 only names the \\\"Seven Wastes\\\" tool).\",\"L8 says Six Sigma targets errors \\\"in all their activities\\\": design, industrial and commercial.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Cyclomatic Complexity equals\",\"o\":[\"no. of Edges - no. of nodes + 3\",\"no. of nodes - no. of Edges + 2\",\"no. of Edges - no. of nodes + 2\",\"no. of Edges - no. of nodes + 3\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Lecture 4: V(G) = E − N + 2P, with P = 1 for one program, so V(G) = E − N + 2. For foo(): 7 − 6 + 2 = 3, the same as predicate nodes + 1 = 2 + 1 (L4, Cyclomatic complexity and basis paths).\",\"ow\":[\"Wrong constant: foo() would give 7 − 6 + 3 = 4, not the lecture's 3 (L4).\",\"Reversed: foo() would give 6 − 7 + 2 = 1 (L4).\",null,\"Same as the first option (the quiz lists it twice): the constant is wrong (L4).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The goal of a software tester is to find\",\"o\":[\"None of the above\",\"Bugs\",\"Bugs and make sure they get fixed\",\"Fix Bugs\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Lecture 6, role of the tester: take responsibility for the bugs found, track bugs through their life cycle, and convince the development team to fix them. The 2025/26 final keyed the same question this way.\",\"ow\":[\"Option c matches the slide.\",\"Incomplete: testing's main goal is to find and report bugs, but the tester must also track them and get them fixed (L6).\",null,\"Developers fix bugs; the tester convinces the team to fix them (L6).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The process is confirmed that software meets its specification\",\"o\":[\"Validation\",\"Design\",\"Requirements\",\"Verification\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Lecture 2: verification = \\\"Are we building the product right?\\\", checking against the specification (code reviews, design checks). In the V-model, the left side going down (requirements spec to code) is verification (L1, L2).\",\"ow\":[\"Validation checks against the customer's requirements; checking against the specification is verification (L2).\",\"Design is a development activity; it does not confirm anything against the spec.\",\"Requirements are what is checked against, not the checking process.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Perfective maintenance is mainly concerned with:\",\"o\":[\"Fixing hidden software crashes.\",\"Restructuring code to make it easier to read without changing its functionality.\",\"Porting code to a new computer hardware platform.\",\"Enhancing system performance, execution speed, or adding new features.\"],\"a\":3,\"key\":\"Confirmed by the doctor. Not in the lectures: the four maintenance types come from the standard classification (ISO/IEC 14764, Pressman, Sommerville).\",\"why\":\"Perfective maintenance improves software that already works: better performance and speed, or new features users ask for. It takes the largest share of maintenance effort (Lientz and Swanson).\",\"ow\":[\"Fixing faults is corrective maintenance; catching latent faults early is preventive.\",\"Refactoring for readability is preventive maintenance in ISO/IEC 14764 (it improves maintainability); older IEEE wording puts maintainability under perfective, but the main concern of perfective is performance and features.\",\"Fitting a new hardware platform is adaptive maintenance.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"During which Phase of DMAIC is the baseline of the problem determined?\",\"o\":[\"Control\",\"Define\",\"Measure\",\"Improve\",\"Analyze\"],\"a\":2,\"key\":\"Confirmed by the doctor. Lecture 8 does not use the word \\\"baseline\\\"; in standard Six Sigma the baseline is set in Measure.\",\"why\":\"Lecture 8: Measure is \\\"planning and implementing performance measurement against the requirements and expectations of customers,\\\" measuring defects with statistical tools. Measuring current performance before changing anything is the baseline, which later improvements are compared against (L8, DMAIC).\",\"ow\":[\"Control comes last and monitors the solution so it stays stable (L8).\",\"Define describes the problem (level, location, financial impact) and forms the team; Measure puts numbers on it (L8).\",null,\"Improve finds and tests solutions, judged against the baseline from Measure (L8).\",\"Analyze finds root causes using the data Measure collected (L8).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is a strategy of integration testing?\",\"o\":[\"Bottom-up testing\",\"Black box\",\"Bing Bang testing\",\"Static white box testing\"],\"a\":0,\"key\":\"Confirmed by the doctor. \\\"Bing Bang\\\" (Big Bang) is also an integration strategy in textbooks, but the slides list only bottom-up, top-down and umbrella.\",\"why\":\"Lecture 3: \\\"Integration testing strategies: Bottom-up, Top-down, Umbrella approach.\\\" In the figure, Integration testing splits into Bottom Up, Top Down and Umbrella Approach Testing (L3, Testing the pieces).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Black box is a testing approach (from the spec, without the code; L2), not an integration strategy.\",\"Not in the lecture's list. (Big Bang, putting all modules together at once, is a textbook integration strategy, but not on the slides.)\",\"Static white-box reviews design and code without running it; integration testing runs the combined pieces (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Umbrella approach is a type of\",\"o\":[\"Bottom up testing\",\"Static testing\",\"Integration Testing\",\"Top down testing\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Lecture 3's figure: Integration testing splits into Bottom Up Testing, Top Down Testing and Umbrella Approach Testing (L3, Testing the pieces).\",\"ow\":[\"A sibling of the umbrella approach, not its parent (L3).\",\"Static testing does not run the code; integration testing runs the combined modules (L2, L3).\",null,\"A sibling of the umbrella approach, not its parent (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Function pair is used to test\",\"o\":[\"State machine diagram\",\"Data flow Diagram\",\"All answers\",\"The interactions between methods\"],\"a\":3,\"key\":\"Confirmed by the doctor. \\\"State machine diagram\\\" is close: the slide says function pair testing is based on one, but the diagram is the source of the pairs, not what is tested.\",\"why\":\"Lecture 4: in OO code \\\"the interactions between methods must be tested,\\\" and MM and function pair testing are the two approaches for it. Function pair testing tests \\\"all possible sequences of method executions of length two,\\\" usually based on a state machine diagram (L4, OOP testing).\",\"ow\":[\"The slide says FP is based on a state machine diagram: the diagram is where the pairs come from, not what is being tested (L4).\",\"Not connected to function pair testing in the lecture; data flow is a type of data coverage (L3).\",\"Cannot be right, because the data flow diagram is wrong.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which type of software maintenance is performed to fix bugs and errors discovered after the product is released?\",\"o\":[\"Corrective Maintenance\",\"Perfective Maintenance\",\"Preventive Maintenance\",\"Adaptive Maintenance\"],\"a\":0,\"key\":\"Confirmed by the doctor. Not in the lectures: the four maintenance types come from the standard classification (ISO/IEC 14764, Pressman, Sommerville).\",\"why\":\"Corrective maintenance fixes faults (bugs and errors) found after the software is delivered, usually reported by users (ISO/IEC 14764).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Perfective maintenance improves performance or adds features; nothing is broken.\",\"Preventive maintenance fixes latent faults before they become errors; here the bugs have already appeared.\",\"Adaptive maintenance fits the software to a changed environment (new OS or hardware).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The method message testing does not subsume\",\"o\":[\"Data coverage\",\"State coverage\",\"Statement coverage\",\"All answers\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Lecture 4: MM testing \\\"is the most basic criterion and does not subsume statement coverage\\\": calling every method once does not run every statement inside them (L4, OOP testing).\",\"ow\":[\"L4 does not compare data coverage (an L3 white-box idea) with MM testing.\",\"States belong to function pair testing; the slide does not compare state coverage with MM (L4).\",null,\"The slide names only statement coverage (L4).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Document with test objectives and scope is called\",\"o\":[\"Test plane\",\"Random test\",\"Dynamic test\",\"Test case\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Lecture 2's table of testing terms: \\\"Test Plan: document with test objectives and scope\\\" (example: describes the login testing scope). \\\"Test plane\\\" is the quiz's spelling of test plan.\",\"ow\":[null,\"A random test is a way of choosing inputs, not a document.\",\"Dynamic testing is a type of testing (running the software), not a document (L2).\",\"A test case is one test: input, conditions and expected output; the test plan sets the objectives and scope for all of them.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"What is the percentage accuracy in the six sigma process?\",\"o\":[\"99.99966%\",\"99.1%\",\"99.8%\",\"99.05%\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Lecture 8: Six Sigma reduces variation until the result is 3.4 defects per million opportunities. 3.4 / 1,000,000 = 0.00034% defective, so 100% − 0.00034% = 99.99966% defect-free. (The slide's ±6σ = 99.9999998% is for a perfectly centred process; 3.4 per million allows a 1.5σ drift.)\",\"ow\":[null,\"About 9,000 defects per million, roughly 3.9 sigma.\",\"About 2,000 defects per million, roughly 4.4 sigma.\",\"About 9,500 defects per million, roughly 3.8 sigma.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Identify the boundary tests in order to test the field Day in DD/MM/YYYY.\",\"o\":[\"30, 31\",\"All answers\",\"28\",\"29\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Boundary value analysis tests the edges of each valid range (L2). The last valid day depends on the month and year: 28 (February), 29 (February in a leap year), 30 (April, June, September, November), 31 (the other months). All of them are boundaries to test.\",\"ow\":[\"Boundaries for 30- and 31-day months, but February's 28 and 29 are missing.\",null,\"Only February's boundary in a normal year.\",\"Only February's boundary in a leap year.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The errors that caused due to the variables, constants, and strings are related to\",\"o\":[\"Comparison Errors\",\"Control Flow Errors\",\"Computation Errors\",\"Data Reference Errors\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Lecture 3, Code Review Checklist: data reference errors are \\\"errors caused by the usage of variables, constants, arrays, strings or records which are not properly declared or initialized\\\" (e.g. uninitialized variables, subscripts out of bounds, off-by-one indexing).\",\"ow\":[\"Comparison errors happen at boundary conditions (&lt; vs &lt;=, wrong Boolean operands) (L3).\",\"Control flow errors are loops and control structures behaving wrongly (L3).\",\"Computation errors are wrong calculations, e.g. mixing data types (L3).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Adding long integers to short integers will cause\",\"o\":[\"Comparison Errors\",\"Computation Errors\",\"Data Reference Errors\",\"Control Flow Errors\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Lecture 3, Code Review Checklist, computation errors check (b): calculations using variables of \\\"the same data type but vary in size (ex: adding long integers to short integers).\\\"\",\"ow\":[\"Comparison errors are about comparison operators and Boolean operands, not arithmetic (L3).\",null,\"Data reference errors are about undeclared or uninitialized variables and out-of-bounds indexes (L3).\",\"Control flow errors are about loops and control structures (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"if (X>1) and (Y=0) then A=A/X; if(X=2) or (A>1) then A=A+1;the statement coverage test cases are\",\"o\":[\"X=0,Y=0, A=4\",\"X=2,Y=0, A=4\",\"All the above\",\"X=3,Y=0, A=1\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Statement coverage needs both A=A/X and A=A+1 to run (L3). X=2,Y=0,A=4: X>1 and Y=0 are true, so A = 4/2 = 2; then X=2 is true, so A = 3. Both statements run. The 2025/26 final keyed the same question this way.\",\"ow\":[\"X=0 makes X>1 false, so A=A/X is skipped.\",null,\"The other two each skip a statement.\",\"A = 1/3 ≈ 0.33; then X=2 is false and A>1 is false, so A=A+1 is skipped.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which type of software testing that not running, examining and reviewing it\",\"o\":[\"Dynamic testing\",\"Static testing\",\"integration testing\",\"System testing\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Lecture 2: \\\"Static testing = testing something that is not running (examining and reviewing it).\\\"\",\"ow\":[\"The opposite: dynamic testing runs and uses the software (L2).\",null,\"Integration testing runs the combined modules, so it is dynamic (L3).\",\"System testing runs the whole system, so it is dynamic (L1, V-model).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The activity to ensure that an organization is providing the best product or services to customer\",\"o\":[\"Code coverage\",\"Quality Control\",\"Quality Assurance\",\"Data coverage\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Lecture 6: QA is process-focused and preventive and covers the whole organization: establishing standards and methodologies, monitoring and evaluating the development process, and preventing errors from recurring. QC is product-focused and detection-oriented. The 2025/26 final keyed the same question as Quality Assurance.\",\"ow\":[\"Code coverage is a white-box technique (L3), not an organizational activity.\",\"QC inspects the finished product and screens out defects (reactive); it is not the organization-wide assurance activity (L6).\",null,\"Data coverage is a white-box technique (L3), not an organizational activity.\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Formal review, Coding standard and guidelines, and Code review are phases of\",\"o\":[\"Dynamic white box testing\",\"Dynamic Black box testing\",\"Static Black box testing\",\"Static white box testing\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Lecture 3: the Static White Box Testing tree splits into Formal Review, Coding Standards and Guidelines, and Code Review Checklist (\\\"Examining the Design and Code\\\").\",\"ow\":[\"Dynamic white-box runs the code: testing the pieces, data coverage and code coverage (L3).\",\"Dynamic black-box runs the software without seeing the code, e.g. equivalence partitioning and boundary values (L2).\",\"Static black-box reviews the specification (L2); reviews and standards for the code are static white-box.\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Cyclomatic Complexity is used to determine\",\"o\":[\"number of states\",\"number of lines\",\"number of statements\",\"number of paths\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Lecture 4: cyclomatic complexity is \\\"the number of independent (basis) paths,\\\" so it gives the number of test cases needed to run every basis path. foo() has V(G) = 3 and 3 basis paths: 1-2-3-4-6, 1-3-4-6, 1-3-5-6.\",\"ow\":[\"States come from a state machine diagram, used for function pair testing (L4).\",\"V(G) counts decisions, not lines: straight-line code has V(G) = 1 however long it is.\",\"Statements do not change V(G) unless they add a decision; statement coverage is a different idea (L3).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"From the following, select the choice which is appropriate to standards and guidelines\",\"o\":[\"Scalability\",\"Reliability\",\"Usability\",\"Compatibility\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Lecture 3: the 3 reasons for standards and guidelines are reliability, readability/maintainability and portability. Only reliability is among the options.\",\"ow\":[\"Not one of the three reasons (L3).\",null,\"Not one of the three reasons; usability is a user-facing quality (L3).\",\"Sounds like portability, but the slide's word is portability, and compatibility is not listed (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is not an advantage of using Lean Six Sigma?\",\"o\":[\"Product improvement\",\"A decrease in customer satisfaction\",\"Defect reduction\",\"Process improvement\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Lecture 8: Six Sigma is \\\"a statistical management system focusing on customer satisfaction, reducing waste, improving quality,\\\" and reduces defects to 3.4 per million. It aims to raise customer satisfaction, so a decrease cannot be an advantage.\",\"ow\":[\"A real advantage: improving quality and offering a zero-defect product (L8).\",null,\"A real advantage: the core goal, 3.4 defects per million or fewer (L8).\",\"A real advantage: DMAIC's Improve phase changes processes to remove defects, costs and waste (L8).\"]}]}]}],\"midterm\":{\"lecs\":[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8],\"scope\":\"Up to Six Sigma (L1–L8)\",\"sections\":[{\"lec\":1,\"h\":\"Testing: definition, aim, objectives and evolution\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Software testing</b> is an <b>incremental and iterative</b> process for detecting defects and verifying that a system meets its specified requirements; it is an <b>integral part of the SDLC</b>.\",\"<b>Miller's aim</b>: to <b>affirm the quality</b> of software systems by <b>systematically exercising</b> the software in <b>carefully controlled circumstances</b>.\",\"<b>6 objectives</b>: meet the business requirements (customer confidence), catch bugs, ensure the system is stable and ready, identify areas of weakness, establish the degree of quality, determine user acceptability.\",\"<b>Gelperin &amp; Hetzel</b> (by goals and phases): until 1956 <b>Debugging</b>-oriented; 1957–1978 <b>Demonstration</b> (meets specifications); 1979–1982 <b>Destruction</b> (find errors, Myers); 1983–1987 <b>Evaluation</b> (introduced V&amp;V); 1988–2000 <b>Prevention</b> (early test design and planning).\"]},{\"lec\":1,\"h\":\"Bugs: conditions, types, reasons, cost\",\"pts\":[\"A <b>bug</b> is anything that affects quality. <b>5 conditions</b> (calculator): (1) does not do what the spec says (+ key fails); (2) does what the spec says it should not (crash/freeze); (3) does something the spec does not mention (extra \\\"square\\\"); (4) does not do something it should although unmentioned (weak battery → wrong answers); (5) hard to understand, cumbersome or slow (buttons too small): the <b>customer perspective</b>.\",\"<b>6 bug types</b>: <b>Conceptual</b> (wrong syntax, misspelled keywords), <b>Math</b> (divide-by-zero, overflow, rounding), <b>Logical</b> (infinite loops, wrong conditions), <b>Resource</b> (buffer overflow, access violations, uninitialized variables), <b>Co-programming</b> (deadlocks, race conditions), <b>Team working</b> (outdated documentation, mismatched files, incorrect linking).\",\"<b>7 reasons</b>: human factor (logic/syntax mistakes), communication failure, unrealistic timeframe, poor design logic, poor coding practices (incl. faulty tools), lack of skilled testing, last-minute change requests.\",\"<b>Cost by stage found</b>: Requirement <b>low</b> → Coding <b>moderate</b> → Integration <b>higher</b> → Testing <b>very costly</b> → Production <b>extremely costly</b>. Example: <b>Patriot missile, 1991</b>, a <b>timing bug</b>, 28 soldiers died.\"]},{\"lec\":1,\"h\":\"Development models and their testing\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Waterfall</b> (oldest, sequential): System Engineering (software <i>and</i> hardware requirements) → Analysis (feasibility) → Design (e.g. database design) → Coding → Testing → Maintenance. Drawback: early errors carry over and must be <b>fixed at multiple points</b>.\",\"<b>Spiral</b> (iterative): Planning → Risk analysis → Design engineering → Customer evaluation, repeated in cycles; usually <b>2 or 3 prototypes</b>. Manages risk early; drawback: time of iterations → <b>time-consuming and costly</b>.\",\"<b>V-model</b>: testing in every stage; left side goes down (high → low level), right side goes up (low → high). Pairs: Requirements specs → <b>Acceptance</b> test plan; Functional specs → <b>System</b> test plan; System design → <b>Integration</b> test plan; Unit design → <b>Test cases</b> (unit test); Code at the bottom. Acceptance testing is done by the <b>customer</b>.\",\"<b>RAD</b>: linear sequential, <b>extremely short cycle</b>, <b>reusable components</b>; phases Requirements planning → Design (prototype) → Construction → Testing &amp; handover. Fails if a reusable component is missing; only small, simple projects.\",\"<b>Agile</b>: incremental, most widely used, continuous collaboration; approaches <b>DSDM</b> (Dynamic Systems Development Method), <b>SCRUM</b>, <b>XP</b> (Extreme Programming, most popular, pair programming). <b>TDD</b> writes tests <b>before</b> code: Create test code → Write/modify functional code → Create additional tests → Test functional code (loop back) → Refactor.\",\"Summary: Waterfall is sequential; <b>V and Spiral</b> use testing as an integral part, so they are more efficient than waterfall; <b>Agile</b> is the most advanced and efficient.\"]},{\"lec\":1,\"h\":\"What the questions ask (chapter summary MCQs on the site)\",\"pts\":[\"No past-exam question on this lecture is on the site yet; the summary MCQs ask: <i>period → orientation</i> (\\\"Demonstration-oriented was followed during…\\\" → 1957–1978), True/False on a period (\\\"1988–2000 prevention-oriented\\\" → True).\",\"<i>Reason for bugs from a description</i> (logic or syntax mistakes → Human factor) and <i>V-model stage → test plan</i> (acceptance test plan → Requirements specifications).\"]},{\"lec\":2,\"h\":\"Terms, black-box, static vs dynamic, pass vs fail\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Verification</b>: \\\"Are we building the product <i>right</i>?\\\" (meets its specification; code reviews, design checks). <b>Validation</b>: \\\"Are we building the <i>right</i> product?\\\" (meets the user's requirement; user acceptance testing).\",\"<b>QA</b>: process-focused, preventive (audits, checklists). <b>QC</b>: product-focused, corrective (unit and system testing). <b>Test plan</b> = objectives and scope; <b>test case</b> = input, conditions, expected output. Unit = smallest component; Integration = interfaces between modules; System = complete system; Acceptance = by user/customer.\",\"<b>Black-box</b> (behavioral) testing: functionality from inputs and expected outputs <b>without knowing internal logic</b> (ATM via the GUI). Advantages: no code knowledge, quick cases, <b>exposes ambiguities in specs</b>, GUI view. Disadvantages: cannot test all inputs, hard if requirements unclear, bugs in complex areas missed.\",\"<b>Static</b> = testing something <b>not running</b> (static black-box reviews the <b>specification</b>, e.g. user manual). <b>Dynamic</b> = running the software with data (dynamic black-box = <b>exploratory</b>/behavioral). Start with <b>test-to-pass</b> (normal cases, \\\"kindness dealing\\\", boundaries, sub-boundaries, default data), <b>then test-to-fail</b> (abnormal cases, force errors). Error-message test (save, no disk) is <b>probably both</b>.\"]},{\"lec\":2,\"h\":\"Equivalence partitioning and boundaries (method)\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Equivalence class (partition)</b>: a set of test cases that tests the same thing or reveals the same bug, so <b>one case per class</b> is enough. EP <b>reduces the number of test cases without lowering quality</b>. Classes are <b>valid</b> and <b>invalid</b>.\",\"Method for a range: one valid class + invalid below + invalid above (driving license 18 to 60 → <b>3 classes</b>). For a count 4 to 10 inputs, test <b>3</b> (invalid), <b>4, 7, 10</b> (valid), <b>11</b> (invalid).\",\"Worked example 10,000 to 99,999 → <b>6 test cases</b>: 00000 (invalid special value), 09999 (invalid left boundary), 10000 (valid left boundary), 50000 (valid mid), 99999 (valid right boundary), 100000 (invalid right boundary).\",\"<b>Boundary conditions</b>: situations at the <b>edge of the planned operational limits</b> (edge of a cliff); <b>bugs increase at boundaries</b>. Test valid edges and one past them: text field 1 to 255 → 1, 255 (254) valid, <b>0 and 256</b> invalid; pages per sheet → 1, max, 0, max+1. Software = <b>data</b> + <b>program</b>; data partitions use boundary, sub-boundary, nulls and bad data.\"]},{\"lec\":2,\"h\":\"State testing and the media player\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Software state</b>: the condition or mode the software is in (Paint pencil vs airbrush). A <b>state transition map</b> shows each unique state, the input/condition that moves between states, and conditions/output on entry or exit; draw it from the <b>user's view</b>. Check all <b>state variables</b> (Paint startup: pencil selected, black on white, \\\"untitled\\\").\",\"<b>5 ways to reduce</b>: visit each state at least once; most common transitions; least common paths; all error states and returning from them; random transitions.\",\"Media player (8 transitions): OFF→play→PLAY, OFF→ff→FAST FORWARD, PLAY→stop→OFF, PLAY→ff→FAST PLAY, FAST FORWARD→stop→OFF, FAST FORWARD→play→PLAY, <b>FAST PLAY→stop→PLAY</b>, FAST PLAY→ff→FAST FORWARD. All states: <b>1</b> test case; all 8 transitions: <b>2</b> test cases.\"]},{\"lec\":2,\"h\":\"What the doctor asks\",\"pts\":[\"Exam 2025/26: \\\"The process that confirms software meets its <b>specification</b> is ………\\\" → Verification; \\\"… meets the <b>user's requirement</b>\\\" → Validation; \\\"testing the <b>running</b> software\\\" → Dynamic testing.\",\"Exam 2025/26 boundary: \\\"accept an integer &gt; 9000, determine the test case\\\" with options 9000, 9001, 9999 → <b>All answers</b> (boundary, just inside, and a valid value). Summary MCQs: Day boundary tests in DD/MM/YYYY → all the above (28, 29, 30, 31); which technique pushes limits → test-to-fail; exposes ambiguities in specs → black box.\"]},{\"lec\":3,\"h\":\"White-box testing and static white-box (formal review)\",\"pts\":[\"<b>White-box testing</b> is also called <b>glass box</b>, <b>structural</b>, <b>open box</b> or <b>clear box</b> testing, because it examines internal workings. It tests: independent paths in a unit/module, <b>logical correctness</b> (true and false conditions), <b>loops at their boundaries</b> (operational boundary correctness) and <b>internal data structures</b>.\",\"<b>Static white-box</b> = examining the design and code, 3 parts: <b>Formal Review</b>, <b>Coding Standards and Guidelines</b>, <b>Code Review Checklist</b>. Formal review has 3 types: <b>peer review</b>, <b>walkthrough</b>, <b>inspection</b>.\",\"<b>Formal review</b>: formal meetings between programmers and testers to inspect design and code. <b>4 essential elements</b>: (1) <b>Identify problems</b>, (2) <b>Follow rules</b> (e.g. lines of code reviewed per day, time spent), (3) <b>Prepare</b> (every participant prepares and contributes), (4) <b>Write a report</b> available to the development team.\",\"<b>Peer review</b>: a programmer who helped design the architecture/code, with other programmers or testers as reviewers.<br><b>Walkthrough</b>: the programmer who <b>wrote</b> the code presents it line by line (or function by function) to a group of <b>5 or 6</b> programmers and testers and explains it.<br><b>Inspection</b>: the presenter is <b>not the real programmer</b>; inspectors review from the <b>user's and tester's perspective</b>.\"]},{\"lec\":3,\"h\":\"Standards, guidelines and the code review checklist\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Standards</b> = rules that <b>must be adhered to</b> (mandatory). <b>Guidelines</b> = instructions that help follow the standards, <b>not really mandatory</b>. <b>3 reasons</b> to follow them: <b>Reliability</b> (more reliable and secure), <b>Readability / Maintainability</b>, <b>Portability</b> (runs on different hardware and compilers).\",\"Checklist classes 1 and 2: <b>Data reference</b> = un-initialized variables referenced, subscripts outside the array bounds, <b>off-by-one</b> indexing, a variable used where a constant is better (array boundary).<br><b>Data declaration</b> = wrong length/type/storage class, initialization at declaration and consistent with type, <b>variables with similar names</b>, declared but never used or used once (should be a constant).\",\"<b>Computation</b> = mixed data types (int + float), same type different size (<b>long + short</b>), <b>overflow</b> mid-calculation, <b>divisor can be zero</b>.<br><b>Comparison</b> = boundary conditions, e.g. <code>&lt;</code> instead of <code>&lt;=</code>, wrong operands of a Boolean operator (an integer used in a Boolean calculation).<br><b>Control flow</b> = improper behaviour of loops and control structures.<br><b>Input/output</b> = reading files, keyboard/mouse input, writing to a file or screen.\",\"<b>Trap</b>: the comparison-errors slide says \\\"In the C language, zero is considered as true and non-zero as false\\\". Real C is the opposite, but if the exam repeats the slide sentence, answer <b>the C language</b>.\"]},{\"lec\":3,\"h\":\"Dynamic white-box: data coverage and code coverage\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Dynamic white-box testing</b> = a <b>validation check</b>: running test cases with input values to make sure the application works by the spec. Testing <b>finds</b> bugs, <b>debugging fixes</b> them. <b>Testing the pieces</b> = unit and integration testing; integration strategies: <b>bottom-up</b>, <b>top-down</b>, <b>umbrella approach</b>.\",\"<b>Data coverage</b> types: <b>data flow</b>, <b>sub-boundaries</b>, <b>error forcing</b> (code divided into data and states, like black-box). <b>Code coverage</b> types: <b>statement</b>, <b>path (branch)</b>, <b>condition</b>.\",\"<b>Statement coverage</b>: every statement runs at least once (4 sequential PRINTs → 1 test).<br><b>Path (branch) coverage</b>: both branches of every IF. Christmas example: Date = 25-12-2008 runs <code>1,2,3,4,5,6,7</code>; any other date runs <code>1,2,5,6,7</code> → <b>2 tests</b>.\",\"<b>Condition coverage</b> (<code>Date$ = \\\"25-12-2008\\\" AND Time$ = \\\"00:00:00\\\"</code>): test <b>every combination</b> of the sub-conditions (2 × 2) → <b>4 test cases</b>; only (25-12-2008, 00:00:00) runs line 3, the other 3 run <code>1,2,5,6,7</code>.\"]},{\"lec\":3,\"h\":\"What the doctor asks (Lecture 3)\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Which stage / which phase</b>: \\\"inspections come under?\\\" → <b>Formal review</b>; \\\"Formal review, coding standards and guidelines, and code review are phases of:\\\" → <b>Static white box</b>.\",\"<b>Standards vs guidelines</b>: \\\"mandatory\\\" → <b>Standards</b>; \\\"not really mandatory\\\" → <b>Guidelines</b>; \\\"appropriate to standards and guidelines\\\" → <b>Reliability</b> (not scalability, usability, compatibility).\",\"<b>Statement-coverage test case</b>: given <code>if (X&gt;1) and (Y=0) then A=A/X;</code>, choose the input that runs the THEN statement (<b>X=2, y=0</b>). With <code>or</code>, several inputs work → <b>All answers</b>. With two IFs, the input must make <b>both</b> true (X=2, Y=0, A=4).\",\"Also on the site, from <b>student summaries</b> (not the doctor): fixing bugs → <b>Debugging</b>; long + short integers → <b>Computation errors</b>; umbrella approach → <b>Integration testing</b>; validation check with code knowledge → <b>Dynamic white box</b>; \\\"zero is true\\\" → <b>C</b>.\"]},{\"lec\":4,\"h\":\"V-model and cyclomatic complexity\",\"pts\":[\"<b>V-model</b>: the left side going <b>down</b> (requirements spec → functional spec → system design → unit design → code) is <b>verification</b>; the right side going <b>up</b> (unit → integration → system → acceptance test) is <b>validation</b>. Dotted links: requirements ↔ <b>Acceptance Test Plan</b>, functional spec ↔ <b>System Test Plan</b>, system design ↔ <b>Integration Test Plan</b>, \\\"Test Cases\\\" between unit design and unit test.\",\"<b>Cyclomatic complexity</b> V(G) = the <b>number of independent (basis) paths</b>. 3 ways to compute it:<br><code>V(G) = E − N + 2P</code> (P = connected components, usually 1)<br><code>V(G) = predicate nodes + 1</code><br><code>V(G) = number of regions</code> (inner + outer).\",\"Worked example <code>foo()</code>: nodes 1 to 6, edges 1-2, 1-3, 2-3, 3-4, 3-5, 4-6, 5-6 → <code>V(G) = 7 − 6 + 2(1) = 3</code>; predicates are nodes 1 and 3 → 2 + 1 = 3; regions 2 inner + 1 outer = 3.\",\"Basis paths of <code>foo()</code> with one test case each: <code>1,2,3,4,6</code> (a=2, b=0, x=0), <code>1,3,4,6</code> (a=2, b=1, x=0), <code>1,3,5,6</code> (a=0, b=0, x=0). <b>Trap</b>: V(G) = 3 basis-path tests, but branch coverage of foo() needs only 2 tests.\"]},{\"lec\":4,\"h\":\"Triangle problem and functional tests\",\"pts\":[\"Triangle domain: 3 subdomains <b>scalene</b> (no sides equal), <b>isosceles</b> (two equal), <b>equilateral</b> (all equal), plus 2 error subdomains <b>bad inputs</b> and <b>not a triangle</b>. Every test case states its expected output, e.g. (3,4,5) scalene, (5,5,8) isosceles, (5,5,5) equilateral, (6,4,2) not a triangle, (0,0,0) bad inputs.\",\"Tracing the pseudocode: the IFs run in order and the <b>last true IF wins</b>. (0,1,0): isosceles (a==c) → not a triangle (1 ≥ 0+0) → <b>bad inputs</b> (a ≤ 0). <b>Slide error</b>: (1,2,4) and (3,2,5) are listed as bad inputs but the code prints <b>not a triangle</b>.\",\"Minimal sets (using the slide table's tests 3,4,5 / 3,5,3 / 0,1,0 / 4,4,4): <b>statement coverage = 2</b> → (4,4,4) + (0,1,0); <b>branch coverage = 3</b> → add (3,4,5), the only one making the first IF false. With any input: (0,0,0) alone gives statement coverage; (0,0,0) + (3,4,5) give branch coverage.\",\"Area of a triangle from 3 points (functional tests): (1,1),(1,5),(5,1) → <b>8</b>; (1,1),(1,5),(1,10) → <b>not a triangle</b> (collinear, x = 1); (10,10),(0,10),(10,0) → <b>50</b>; (0,0),(0,10),(10,10) → <b>50</b>.\"]},{\"lec\":4,\"h\":\"OOP testing: MM and function pair\",\"pts\":[\"OO testing brings <b>some new challenges</b>, but <b>many conventional techniques are still appropriate</b>. <b>Functional</b> testing of OO is <b>no different</b> (tests from the requirements document); <b>structural</b> testing is very different. Statement and branch coverage can be applied but <b>do not seem appropriate</b>: the <b>interactions between methods</b> must be tested.\",\"<b>MM (Method-Message) testing</b>: every method call tested <b>at least once</b> (a repeated call is tested only once). Most basic criterion; <b>does NOT subsume statement coverage</b>. Stack: <b>create, push and pop</b> may achieve MM.\",\"<b>Function Pair (FP) testing</b>: all sequences of method executions of <b>length two</b>, usually from a <b>state machine diagram</b>. Stack states <b>Empty, Normal, Full</b>; push on Full and pop on Empty → <b>error</b>. The lecture lists <b>14 pairs</b>; error pairs: new-pop, push(normal→full)-push, pop(into empty)-pop. Advantage: catches interaction and state-dependent faults that MM misses.\"]},{\"lec\":4,\"h\":\"What the doctor asks (Lecture 4)\",\"pts\":[\"\\\"A simple sequence that achieves method-message coverage in stack testing is:\\\" → <b>create, push &amp; pop</b> (not insert).\",\"From <b>student summaries</b> (not the doctor): cyclomatic complexity determines the number of <b>paths</b> (not states, lines or statements); method-message testing does NOT subsume <b>statement coverage</b>.\",\"The doctor's only Lecture 4 question is the MM one above, but the lecture is calculation-heavy, so expect: V(G) from E, N (and P), from predicate nodes or regions; tracing foo() and the triangle code for a given input; minimal test sets for statement/branch coverage.\"]},{\"lec\":5,\"h\":\"Web site basics and the 13 testing elements\",\"pts\":[\"A <b>web site</b> = a collection of one or more web pages (text, graphics, links, sounds…) under the <b>same domain</b>. It must have a <b>domain name</b> and a <b>web host</b>.\",\"<b>Why test</b>: a site built for Internet Explorer may fail in Mozilla, Chrome or on Linux, so testing makes sure it works on <b>every browser and platform</b>.\",\"The <b>3 fundamentals</b> of a web page: <b>home page</b> (the default page, header with the site's source name), <b>links</b> (navigate, redirect, download files, open the e-mail client) and <b>content</b>, the <b>most important</b> part (text, graphics, sounds, movie clips, user-data fields, rotating ads, dynamic text).\",\"The <b>13 elements</b>: user interface (simple), instructions, site map, content, colors/backgrounds (easy to read), images, tables (no endless scrolling), wrap-around (text around images), functionality (links, DB connection, forms), links, forms (submit works), data verification (business rules), cookies (work + login info <b>encrypted</b>).\",\"<b>Site map</b>: experienced users know where they want to go and avoid long instructions; check the map exists, every link on it works, and a navigation bar is on every page. <b>Content</b>: no plagiarism; keep bold, big fonts and blinking text minimal or the customer may not revisit.\"]},{\"lec\":5,\"h\":\"Black-box, white-box and gray-box web testing\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Black-box elements</b>: text, hyperlinks, graphics, forms (Infosys page, Figure 8.5). <b>Text</b>: audience level, terminology, accuracy, spelling, punctuation, correct contact info.\",\"<b>Hyperlinks</b>: correct destination, same tab or new window as the spec says (no spec → just check it works), <b>obvious</b> (underlined, pointer changes); e-mail link → send an e-mail and check for a response. <b>Graphics</b>: missing or wrongly named → won't load, error shown.\",\"<b>Forms</b> = text boxes, list boxes and other fields to <b>enter or select information</b> (Gmail sign-up, Figure 8.6). The 4 checks: positioned properly? correct size? accept correct data? reject bad data?\",\"<b>White-box</b> (tester knows the internal design), 5 elements: <b>dynamic content</b> (varies with time of day, weather, stock tickers), <b>database-driven pages</b> (catalogs, inventories), <b>programmatically created pages</b>, <b>server performance and loading</b> (simulate millions of connections), <b>security</b> (denial of service, buffer overflow).\",\"<b>Gray-box</b> = a mix of black-box and white-box; purpose: <b>isolate defects related to bad design or bad implementation</b> of the web.\"]},{\"lec\":5,\"h\":\"Configuration, compatibility and automatic tools\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Hardware configurations</b> (7): CPU types, RAM, graphics cards, video capture cards, audio cards, monitors/display devices, network cards.\",\"<b>Compatibility</b> (5): font sizes, browsers with CSS, screen resolutions, memory sizes, network environments. Trap: RAM is <i>hardware</i>, memory sizes is <i>compatibility</i>.\",\"<b>Automatic web testing tools</b> check browser compatibility, load/stress/performance, broken hyperlinks, functional/GUI, spelling, security, database, OOP, network, and web applications (<b>Selenium</b>, <b>Cypress</b>).\"]},{\"lec\":5,\"h\":\"What the doctor asks\",\"pts\":[\"No past-exam or sheet question from the doctor on this lecture is on the site yet; expect definitions and lists like the rest of the course.\",\"Student-summary questions on the site ask: <i>which strategy checks the site across hardware/software environments</i> → <b>Compatibility testing</b>; T/F <i>dynamic content varies with conditions</i> → True; T/F <i>a navigational map takes the user straight to the information</i> → True.\"]},{\"lec\":6,\"h\":\"Quality and the 8 quality factors\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Quality</b> = <b>conformance to requirements</b>; the set of attributes <b>valued by end-users</b>.\",\"<b>Directly measurable</b> factors: defects. <b>Indirectly measurable</b>: maintainability, usability.\",\"The 8 factors: <b>Reliability</b> (intended function accurately), <b>Efficiency</b> (optimal use of resources), <b>Integrity</b> (access control, security), <b>Usability</b> (effort to learn and operate), <b>Maintainability</b> (effort to locate and fix errors), <b>Flexibility</b> (effort to change), <b>Portability</b> (effort to transfer to another system), <b>Reusability</b> (parts usable in other applications).\",\"Trap: <b>Maintainability</b> = fix errors, <b>Flexibility</b> = change the program, <b>Portability</b> = move it to another system.\"]},{\"lec\":6,\"h\":\"SQA vs SQC, testing vs QA, ITSM\",\"pts\":[\"<b>SQA</b> = prevention, focus on the <b>process</b> (proactive), ensures standards and procedures are applied correctly. <b>SQC</b> = detection, focus on the <b>product</b> (reactive), evaluates quality and screens out non-compliant items.\",\"<b>Testing</b>: part of SQA, detection-oriented, evaluates spec/design/code under normal and abnormal conditions, finds and reports bugs, done by testers. <b>QA</b>: broader, prevents defects, ensures processes/standards/methodologies, uses <b>ISO / CMM</b>.\",\"<b>Testing tasks</b>: choose the test implementation approach, execute tests, prepare V&amp;V reports, attend review meetings. <b>Tester role</b>: own the bugs, track them through their life cycle, convince developers to fix them. <b>QA tasks</b>: standard processes, guidelines, checklists per stage, quality metrics and criteria.\",\"<b>ITSM</b> (IT Service Management) = quality management in IT, delivering services at an <b>agreed-upon level of quality</b>. <b>Service Support</b> = efficient delivery of operational services; <b>Service Delivery</b> = long-term planning, control and management.\"]},{\"lec\":6,\"h\":\"Test organization, metrics and indicators\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Small team</b>: testers + programmers under the development manager → fast communication, but an inexperienced manager may ignore bug reports. <b>Independent test team</b>: test lead + dev manager under the project manager → test feedback counts, but the PM decides. <b>Reporting to executive management</b>: independent QA team, reports reach senior management directly.\",\"<b>Metrics</b> = rules for measuring attributes (code size, complexity); <b>indicators</b> = variables reflecting process outcomes (number of detected bugs). <b>Product metrics</b>: code size, documented pages. <b>Process metrics</b>: time taken, methodology.\",\"<b>9 common metrics</b>: code coverage, bugs per line of code, cyclomatic complexity, function point analysis, number of classes and interfaces, cohesion, coupling, order of growth, source lines of code.\",\"<b>9 indicators</b>: progress, stability, process compliance, quality evaluation effort, defect detection efficiency, defect removal rate (<b>detected AND resolved</b>), defect age profile (<b>NOT resolved</b>), defect density (defect-prone parts), complexity. E.g. 40 detected, 30 resolved → removal rate counts 30, age profile counts 10.\"]},{\"lec\":6,\"h\":\"What the doctor asks\",\"pts\":[\"Exam 2025/26: <i>The activity to ensure that an organization is providing the best product to the customer</i> → <b>Quality Assurance</b> (not Quality Control).\",\"Exam 2025/26: <i>The goal of a software tester is to find ……</i> → <b>bugs and make sure they get fixed</b> (not just \\\"bugs\\\").\",\"Student-summary questions: what guarantees methods and procedures are suitable and applied → <b>SQA</b>; task NOT under SQA → <b>choosing the implementation approach for a test</b> (a testing task); T/F <i>QA is the same as QC</i> → False.\"]},{\"lec\":7,\"h\":\"CMM: definition, components, levels\",\"pts\":[\"<b>CMM (Capability Maturity Model)</b>: a standard framework for assessing and improving the maturity of a software company's development process. Developed by the <b>SEI</b> and <b>Carnegie Mellon University</b> under the <b>U.S. Department of Defense</b>.\",\"<b>Five-level incremental</b> approach: organizations climb gradually, <b>no skipping levels</b>. Made mainly for software development organizations, but also applies to system engineering, project management, IT, risk and personnel management. A <b>maturity model</b> is used for <b>both evaluation and improvement</b>.\",\"<b>6 components</b> of the CMM structure: (1) <b>maturity levels</b>, (2) <b>process capability</b> (ability to meet quality expectations with current processes; predicts future projects), (3) <b>Key Process Areas (KPA)</b> (related activities that achieve goals together, e.g. delivery planning), (4) <b>goals</b>, (5) <b>common features</b> (show if a KPA's implementation is successful and sustainable), (6) <b>key practices</b> (actions and infrastructure that support a KPA, e.g. a delivery schedule follows a documented procedure).\",\"<b>The 5 levels</b> (figure label in brackets):<br>1 <b>Initial</b> (inconsistent management): ad-hoc, reactive; no required processes.<br>2 <b>Repeatable</b> (project management): planning, requirement control, product modifications; plan/track projects, manage suppliers and configurations.<br>3 <b>Defined</b> (process management): standard processes across the organization, teams tailor them; organization-wide training.<br>4 <b>Quantitatively Managed</b> (capability management): statistical/quantitative control; capability baselines.<br>5 <b>Optimizing</b> (change management): continuous improvement; remove causes of defects.\",\"Arrows in the figure: 1→2 <b>Basic Management Control</b>, 2→3 <b>Process Definition</b>, 3→4 <b>Process Measurement</b>, 4→5 <b>Process Control</b>. Trap: the figure calls Level 4 just \\\"Managed\\\".\",\"Each KPA has <b>5 aspects</b>: <b>goals</b>, <b>commitment</b> (requirements to meet the goals), <b>ability</b> (activities that enable meeting the commitments), <b>measurement</b> (activities must be monitored), <b>verification</b>. <b>Benefits of CMM</b>: simple and easy to analyze; executives know it; explains IT issues to senior executives; helps get project funding; large companies and governments compare themselves with it.\"]},{\"lec\":7,\"h\":\"ISO 9000, ISO principles, standards and SPICE\",\"pts\":[\"<b>ISO 9000</b>: internationally recognized standards from the <b>International Organization for Standardization</b>; <b>highly generic</b> (any organization with products or services). \\\"ISO\\\" from Greek <b>isos = equal</b>. It has <b>20 key elements</b>, each must be clearly documented.\",\"Elements most asked: (1) <b>Management responsibility</b> (quality policy, resources), (3) <b>Contract review</b> (customer needs), (10) <b>Inspection &amp; testing</b> (receiving, in-process, final), (11) <b>Inspection/measuring equipment</b> (calibrate), (13) <b>Control of non-conforming product</b> (rejected items), (14) <b>Corrective &amp; preventive action</b> (root causes), (16) <b>Quality records</b> (audit trails), (20) <b>Statistical techniques</b> (control charts).\",\"<b>ISO 8 principles</b>: customer focus, leadership, involvement of people, <b>process approach</b> (activities as processes), <b>system approach to management</b> (interconnected processes as one system), continuous improvement, <b>factual decision making</b>, mutually beneficial supplier relationships.\",\"<b>SE standards</b>: shift from <b>local to international</b>; <b>IEEE</b> since <b>1976</b> (IEEE Software Engineering Standard Collection). <b>SPICE</b> = <b>Software Process Improvement for Capability Determination</b>, with <b>ISO/IEC 15504</b> a joint <b>ISO and IEC</b> initiative, introduced in <b>1993</b>. 3 objectives: standard framework for process assessment; organizational assessments for emerging standards; global adoption of process assessment in industry.\"]},{\"lec\":7,\"h\":\"What the doctor asks\",\"pts\":[\"Fill the blank with one of the four models (CMM, ISO 9000, 6 Sigma, SPICE): \\\"……… is the widely used and preferred software method of evaluation\\\" → <b>CMM</b>; \\\"There are 20 crucial elements in the ……… standard\\\" → <b>ISO 9000</b>. (Exam 2025/26)\",\"Numbers: \\\"……… are maturity levels in the CMM\\\" → <b>5</b>. Know every count: 5 levels, 6 components, 5 KPA aspects, 20 ISO elements, 8 ISO principles, 3 SPICE objectives, years 1976 (IEEE) and 1993 (SPICE). (Exam 2025/26)\"]},{\"lec\":8,\"h\":\"Six Sigma and σ\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Six Sigma</b>: a famous concept in <b>total quality management</b>, emerged in the <b>late 1970s and early 1980s</b>. A <b>systematic method</b> and a <b>statistical management system</b> (customer satisfaction, reducing waste, improving quality, financial and time performance). Quality control programs target <b>design, industrial and commercial</b> defects.\",\"It reduces process variation to <b>3.4 defects per million</b> opportunities or less; the aim is <b>zero defects</b> (the slide and the exam write \\\"zero default\\\").\",\"<b>Standard deviation (σ)</b>: a statistical method and indicator of deviation/dispersion from the desired objectives; <b>σ = √variance</b>.\",\"Normal (Gaussian) curve, share inside and ppm outside:<br>±1σ 68.27% (317,300) · ±2σ 95.45% (45,500) · ±3σ 99.73% (2,700) · ±4σ 99.9937% (63) · ±5σ 99.999943% (0.57) · ±6σ 99.9999998% (0.002).<br>Centred ±6σ leaves 0.002 ppm; the famous 3.4 comes from a long-term <b>1.5σ drift</b> (6 − 1.5 = 4.5σ on one side; general background, not on the slide).\"]},{\"lec\":8,\"h\":\"Six Sigma calculations\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Case table method</b> (failure cases in the preparatory certificate): <b>Six sigma = 6 × standard deviation</b>. X1: 6 × 1.22 = 7.32; X2: 6 × 0.98 = 5.88; overall: mean 2.99, SD 1.07, 6 × 1.07 = 6.42. Largest: X7 (1.27 → 7.62); smallest: X9 (0.91 → 5.46).\",\"From variance: first σ = √variance, then × 6. Example: variance 0.25 → σ = 0.5 → 6 × 0.5 = 3.\",\"From the table: ppm out of spec = (100% − % inside) × 10,000. Example ±3σ: (100 − 99.73) × 10,000 = 2,700 ppm.\",\"<b>DPMO</b> (background formula in the notes, not on the slides) = defects ÷ (units × opportunities per unit) × 1,000,000. Example: 25 ÷ (500 × 10) × 10⁶ = <b>5,000 DPMO</b>. At 6σ, DPMO = 3.4.\"]},{\"lec\":8,\"h\":\"DMAIC, DMADV, principles, phases, belts, tools\",\"pts\":[\"<b>DMAIC</b>: <b>Define</b> (problems that obstruct improvement; statement with <b>level, location, financial impact</b>; form a team) → <b>Measure</b> (performance vs customer requirements with statistical tools) → <b>Analyze</b> (root causes; chain of failure causes, rank by impact, categorize) → <b>Improve</b> (change processes, test solutions on a sample, correct) → <b>Control</b> (monitor and never stop; measure continuously, correct deviations on time).\",\"<b>DMADV</b> = Define, Measure, Analyze, <b>Design, Verify</b> (for new processes/products; DMAIC improves existing ones, a note not on the slides).\",\"<b>Principles</b>: focus on customers; decisions on facts and accurate data; focus on internal operations; management based on advance planning; collaborative teamwork, <b>avoiding competition</b>. <b>Implementation phases</b>: (1) select important projects, (2) assign experienced employees, (3) written document (reasons, objectives, scope), (4) training, (5) implement the process, (6) submission of solutions.\",\"<b>Belts</b> (steps 1 to 5): White → Yellow (<b>all employees</b>) → Green (<b>executive employees</b>) → Black (<b>middle and senior management</b>) → Master Black (<b>senior management</b>). <b>Tools</b>: 5S, Seven Wastes, value stream mapping, visual workspace, VOC, Kaizen, Kanban, regression analysis.\"]},{\"lec\":8,\"h\":\"What the doctor asks\",\"pts\":[\"Fill the blank choosing among CMM, ISO 9000, 6 Sigma, SPICE: \\\"……… reduce process variation to reach zero default\\\" → <b>6 Sigma</b>. (Exam 2025/26) Link each model to its keyword: 3.4 per million / zero defects / DMAIC → Six Sigma.\"]}]}}");
